window.QD={"src":["Comp 1 Practice (V1)","Comp 3 Practice","Comp Bible Part 3","Comp Bible Pt 3 – Set 2 & Exam 1 Recording","Class slides – Physiological Adaptation","Class slides – Safety & Infection Control","Class slides – Pharmacology & Parenteral","Class slides – Management of Care","Class slides – Psychosocial Integrity","Class slides – Reduction of Risk Potential","Class slides – OB & Newborn","Class slides – Health Promotion","Class slides – Basic Care & Comfort","Class slides – NCLEX Test-Taking","Class slides – Pediatrics","Class slides – Respiratory","Class slides – GI","Class slides – Cardiac","Class slides – Hematology","Class slides – Endocrine","Class slides – Neurological","Class slides – Musculoskeletal","Weekly quiz – Unit 1","Weekly quiz – Unit 2","Weekly quiz – Unit 3","Weekly quiz – Unit 4 (A)","Weekly quiz – Unit 4 (B)","Weekly quiz – Unit 5 (A)","Weekly quiz – Unit 5 (B)","Weekly quiz – Unit 5 (C)","Weekly quiz – Unit 6","Weekly quiz – Unit 7","Weekly quiz – Unit 8","Weekly quiz – Unit 9","Weekly quiz – Unit 10","Not Galen – Comp C Highlights","Not Galen – Comprehensive B","Not Galen – Comp 203 (set 1)","Not Galen – Comp 203 (set 2)","Not Galen – Comprehensive Topics A"],"top":["Prioritization & Delegation","Infection Control & Safety","Legal, Ethics & Management","Cardiovascular","Respiratory","Renal, Fluids & Acid-Base","Endocrine & Metabolic","GI & Nutrition","Neuro, Eyes & Ortho","Burns, Trauma & Emergencies","Pharmacology & Labs","Maternal-Newborn","Pediatrics","Mental Health","Health Promotion & Prevention","Oncology & Radiation","Skills & Procedures","Other study sets (not Galen)"],"bank":[{"id":1,"t":0,"s":0,"q":"The nurse on a medical-surgical unit has been made aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first plan to assess the client who","o":["has type 2 diabetes mellitus with a premeal glucose of 85 mg/dL and a postmeal glucose of 220 mg/dL.","is 24 hours postoperative from abdominal surgery and wound drainage has changed from sanguineous to serosanguineous over 2 hours.","is preparing for a colonoscopy and whose BP was 128/74 mm Hg and is now 106/60 mm Hg.","has acute pancreatitis and reports an unchanged pain level of 7/10, 30 minutes after an oral pain medication."],"a":2,"r":"Priority = unstable/changing physiology first. A falling BP during bowel prep suggests fluid volume deficit (hypovolemia) and is the only true change that threatens circulation. Sanguineous→serosanguineous drainage is the expected healing progression, and a postmeal glucose of 220 is an expected rise. Unchanged pain is a concern, but it is not as acutely unstable as dropping BP."},{"id":2,"t":0,"s":0,"q":"The nurse has received hand-off report and is assigning tasks to unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP). Which task should the nurse instruct the UAP to perform first?","o":["Obtain a sputum specimen from a client with suspected pulmonary TB.","Obtain a capillary blood glucose on a client who had a hypoglycemic episode 30 minutes ago and received IV dextrose.","Turn and reposition a client who was last turned 2 hours ago.","Deliver a lunch tray to a client returning from radiology who has been NPO since breakfast."],"a":2,"r":"Delegation: UAP perform routine tasks on STABLE clients. A client who just had hypoglycemia and IV dextrose is unstable; the RN must monitor and interpret that glucose. Of the remaining tasks, turning is time-critical now (q2h to prevent pressure injury). The sputum specimen is routine, and the NPO client should be cleared by the RN before eating."},{"id":3,"t":0,"s":0,"q":"The nurse on a pediatric unit is reviewing client data. Which client should the nurse plan to see first?","o":["The infant with pertussis receiving oxygen via nasal cannula.","The adolescent with sickle cell crisis who is being discharged.","The toddler with bilateral arm casts who needs breakfast fed to them.","The school-aged child with type 1 diabetes who requires blood glucose monitoring."],"a":0,"r":"ABCs: an infant with pertussis has a high risk of apnea and airway obstruction and is already requiring oxygen. The other three are stable, routine needs."},{"id":4,"t":2,"s":0,"q":"A newly hired nurse is discussing fiscally responsible practice with the preceptor. Which statement requires follow-up?","o":["“Involving the family in client care activities is an example of fiscal responsibility.”","“Engaging in safe clinical practice to avoid personal injuries is an example of fiscal responsibility.”","“Completing assigned nursing care during scheduled hours is a way to demonstrate fiscal responsibility.”","“Following evidence-based best practice guidelines demonstrates fiscal responsibility.”"],"a":0,"r":"Fiscal responsibility means using resources wisely: avoiding injury and errors, completing work within scheduled hours (no overtime), and following EBP to prevent costly complications. Involving family is family-centered care and does not directly relate to cost, so it is the statement that needs follow-up."},{"id":5,"t":2,"s":0,"q":"The preceptor observes a newly hired nurse teaching a client with pelvic inflammatory disease who does not speak English. Which action requires intervention?","o":["Contacting the charge nurse to obtain a facility-approved interpreter.","Providing written instructions in the client’s primary language.","Using a facility-approved telephone interpretation service.","Having a family member who speaks both languages interpret the instructions."],"a":3,"r":"Use a qualified, facility-approved interpreter. Family members may alter or omit information, and the topic is sensitive (confidentiality/HIPAA). Written materials in the client’s language and phone interpretation are acceptable."},{"id":6,"t":11,"s":0,"q":"A client in labor has just had spontaneous rupture of membranes. Which finding is a priority to report to the PHCP?","o":["Contractions lasting 60 seconds.","New appearance of late decelerations.","Maternal temperature of 99.5° F.","Four contractions in a 10-minute period."],"a":1,"r":"Late decelerations indicate uteroplacental insufficiency (fetal hypoxia). After rupture, cord prolapse/placental problems are also a concern. The other findings are normal for labor (contractions ≤5 in 10 min, 60 s duration, T <100.4° F)."},{"id":7,"t":16,"s":0,"q":"The preceptor observes a newly hired nurse caring for clients. Which action indicates correct care?","o":["Placing an N95 on a client with active TB before transport to a long-term care facility.","Administering oral pain medication to a client 30 minutes before discharge.","Using a 23-gauge, 1-inch needle for an IM injection in the deltoid.","Teaching a client with a new ileostomy to empty the bag 30 minutes after eating."],"a":2,"r":"IM injection: 22–25 gauge, 1–1.5 in (adult deltoid) is appropriate. The CLIENT with TB wears a surgical mask (the nurse wears the N95). Ostomy pouches are emptied when 1/3 full and not right after meals, when output peaks."},{"id":8,"t":0,"s":0,"q":"In an outpatient clinic, which client should the nurse have the PHCP see first?","o":["15-year-old with a bull’s-eye rash 2 days after a camping trip.","37-year-old with polycystic kidney disease and lumbar pain when ambulating.","45-year-old with pleurisy and worsening shortness of breath.","57-year-old with Graves’ disease who has heat intolerance and diaphoresis."],"a":2,"r":"Worsening shortness of breath is an airway/breathing (ABC) problem. Heat intolerance in Graves’ disease, flank pain with PKD, and an early Lyme rash are expected or less urgent."},{"id":9,"t":15,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first plan to see the client who has","o":["cervical cancer, receives internal radiation therapy, and whose partner has been at the bedside for 2 hours.","neutropenia (WBC 3,000/mm³) and is eating homemade cookies from a visitor.","bone marrow suppression, receiving epoetin alfa, with BP changing from 140/94 to 124/80.","thrombocytopenia and a family member has brought fresh fruit and flowers."],"a":0,"r":"Internal (sealed) radiation: visitors are limited (about 30 minutes/day) and must stay ~6 ft from the source. A partner at the bedside for 2 hours is being unsafely exposed, so the nurse intervenes. The other situations are not immediate hazards for those diagnoses."},{"id":10,"t":9,"s":0,"q":"A male client has full-thickness burns to 50% of the lower body. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has a","o":["hematocrit of 60%.","SpO₂ of 91%.","urinary output of 100 mL over the past 2 hours.","temperature of 104.2° F."],"a":3,"r":"A temperature of 104.2° F suggests sepsis/infection, the leading cause of death in burn clients. Hemoconcentration (high Hct) is an expected early finding, and 50 mL/hr urine output is adequate (≥30 mL/hr)."},{"id":11,"t":0,"s":0,"q":"Following an explosion at a local factory, the ED nurse triages clients. Which client is the priority for treatment?","o":["Closed tibia fracture with severe pain.","Type 1 diabetes who feels shaky with clammy skin.","Large head wound with exposed gray matter and RR of 6.","Second-degree burns to chest and arms with RR greater than 30."],"a":3,"r":"Triage: treat life-threatening but salvageable problems first. Burns of the chest with tachypnea suggest airway/inhalation injury. The head wound with exposed brain and RR 6 is likely expectant (unsurvivable)."},{"id":12,"t":5,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with severe hypocalcemia. Which should be included in the plan of care?","o":["Encourage foods high in phosphorus.","Measure urine output every 2 hours.","Place the client on seizure precautions.","Encourage increased fluid intake."],"a":2,"r":"Low calcium increases neuromuscular excitability: tetany, laryngospasm, seizures. Phosphorus is inversely related to calcium and would worsen the imbalance."},{"id":13,"t":1,"s":0,"q":"Which action by the nurse is appropriate?","o":["Placing a client who had a stem cell transplant in contact isolation.","Having a client with pertussis wear a surgical mask during transport.","Initiating airborne precautions for a client with epiglottitis.","Wearing an N95 when caring for a client with impetigo."],"a":1,"r":"Pertussis = droplet precautions; the client wears a surgical mask when transported. A transplant recipient needs protective (reverse) isolation, epiglottitis is droplet, and impetigo is contact."},{"id":14,"t":1,"s":0,"q":"In an ambulatory center, the nurse should immediately remove from the waiting room and place in an exam room the client who has","o":["heart failure and a 3 lb weight gain in 3 days.","a fever of 101.8° F, headache, vomiting, and photophobia.","myasthenia gravis, left ptosis, and a nasal-sounding voice.","a 1 mm flat, red area at the site of a Mantoux test given 48 hours ago."],"a":1,"r":"Fever, headache, vomiting and photophobia suggest meningitis (droplet precautions), so protect others and expedite care. A 1 mm area is a negative PPD; the HF and MG clients need follow-up but are not infectious."},{"id":15,"t":15,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with a sealed radiation implant. Which precaution should the nurse implement?","o":["Limit each visitor to 1 hour per day.","Keep dedicated equipment in the client’s room until the implant is removed.","Assign a different nurse to care for the client each day.","Place bed linens in a biohazard receptacle outside the client’s room."],"a":2,"r":"Limit staff exposure by rotating nurses (time, distance, shielding). Visitors should be limited to about 30 minutes/day, and linens stay in the room until the implant is removed. Source note: dedicated equipment is listed as correct on other versions of this question, so check your instructor’s key."},{"id":16,"t":1,"s":0,"q":"A fire with smoke is noted in the nurses’ station. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Move clients close to a window with unit doors closed.","Use a bag valve mask to move a client on a mechanical ventilator.","Transfer clients by elevator to another floor.","Extinguish the fire starting at the ceiling and moving down."],"a":1,"r":"RACE: Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish. Ventilator-dependent clients are evacuated while manually ventilated. Never use elevators in a fire, and extinguish by aiming at the BASE of the fire (PASS)."},{"id":17,"t":14,"s":0,"q":"Which action by the nurse is an example of PRIMARY prevention?","o":["Teaching foot care to a client with diabetes.","Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group.","Recommending helmets when riding bicycles or motorcycles.","Teaching monthly breast self-examination."],"a":2,"r":"Primary = prevent disease/injury before it occurs (vaccines, helmets, healthy diet). Screening/BSE is secondary; foot care and support groups limit progression/complications (tertiary)."},{"id":18,"t":10,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is about to give scheduled immunizations to an immunocompromised child. Which should the nurse question?","o":["Haemophilus influenzae type B.","Inactivated poliovirus.","Hepatitis B.","Live attenuated influenza."],"a":3,"r":"Live vaccines (LAIV, MMR, varicella, rotavirus) are contraindicated in immunocompromised clients. Inactivated vaccines are safe."},{"id":19,"t":12,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is teaching the mother of a 9-year-old child about age-appropriate toys. Which is appropriate?","o":["Finger paints and bubbles.","Board games and reading books.","Large building blocks and balls.","Wooden puzzles."],"a":1,"r":"School-age (6–12) children are industrious: cooperative play, rules, board games, collections, reading, and sports. The other toys suit toddlers and preschoolers."},{"id":20,"t":0,"s":0,"q":"At a health fair, for which client is it a priority to recommend follow-up with a PHCP?","o":["65 y/o with elevated HDL, decreased LDL, and who does not follow a low-cholesterol diet.","55 y/o with BP 164/96 mm Hg who stopped smoking 2 months ago.","50 y/o with a family history of ovarian cancer who has not had the HPV vaccine.","45 y/o with exercise-induced asthma and vesicular breath sounds in the peripheral lung fields."],"a":1,"r":"BP 164/96 is stage 2 hypertension and needs evaluation. High HDL and low LDL are desirable, and vesicular peripheral breath sounds are normal."},{"id":21,"t":12,"s":0,"q":"In a well-baby clinic, it is most important to suggest follow-up for the infant who is","o":["6 months old and unable to roll over.","4 months old and the posterior fontanel is closed.","3 months old and unable to switch objects between hands.","4 months old and sleeps up to 9 hours at night."],"a":0,"r":"By ~6 months infants roll both ways, so this is a gross-motor delay. The posterior fontanel closes at 2–3 months, hand-to-hand transfer comes around 6 months, and 9-hour sleep is normal."},{"id":22,"t":13,"s":0,"q":"The parent of a child with leukemia is sitting at the bedside crying and says, “I’m so worried about my child.” Which response is most therapeutic?","o":["“I’ll stay here and sit with you awhile.”","“Are you able to take good care of yourself?”","“Have you considered attending a support group?”","“Life must seem very scary right now.”"],"a":0,"r":"Offering presence is the most therapeutic response for a distressed parent. Other options are closed-ended, give advice prematurely, or put words in the parent’s mouth."},{"id":23,"t":13,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is planning care for a client with histrionic personality disorder. Which intervention is appropriate?","o":["Work with the client on ways to develop trusting relationships.","Communicate with the client using concrete and descriptive language.","Have the client remain quiet and observe during group therapy.","Use compassionate behavior to keep the client in control."],"a":1,"r":"Cluster B/histrionic clients are dramatic and vague. Use concrete, direct, descriptive communication and set clear limits."},{"id":24,"t":13,"s":0,"q":"On a mental health unit, which client should the nurse assess first?","o":["Bipolar disorder, manic, no sleep in 24 hours, pacing the hallways.","Major depressive disorder overheard saying, “I can’t take it anymore.”","Suicidal ideation, reluctantly discussing a suicide contract with staff.","Anorexia nervosa who went to the restroom 15 minutes after a meal."],"a":1,"r":"Safety first. A statement like “I can’t take it anymore” may signal suicidal intent, and no one has assessed it yet. Review sheet: assess the client with depression who says “I can’t do this anymore” first."},{"id":25,"t":12,"s":0,"q":"A parent of a 3-year-old says the child is in constant motion and cannot sit through a story, and asks if the child is hyperactive. Which response is most appropriate?","o":["“It is normal for parents to worry about hyperactivity.”","“I am not seeing any obvious signs of hyperactivity.”","“At 3 years of age, it is hard to tell.”","“Describe when and where this occurs.”"],"a":3,"r":"Use therapeutic communication: open-ended exploration to gather data. Reassurance and dismissive responses block communication."},{"id":26,"t":8,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is teaching the partner of a client who had a stroke. Which statement indicates a need for additional teaching?","o":["“I will make sure my partner has a snack 1 to 2 hours before going to bed.”","“I should have my partner alternate a bite of food with sips of water.”","“A change in my partner’s voice after swallowing may indicate dysphagia.”","“Hesitancy to eat could indicate difficulty swallowing.”"],"a":0,"r":"Aspiration risk: with dysphagia, avoid eating close to bedtime and stay upright after meals. Wet or gurgly voice after swallowing and reluctance to eat are signs of dysphagia. (Key per source.)"},{"id":27,"t":7,"s":0,"q":"A client with GERD has been taught diet and lifestyle changes. Which statement indicates a need for additional teaching?","o":["“I will sit upright for 1 to 2 hours after meals.”","“I will have a snack 1 hour before bedtime.”","“I will avoid tight clothing.”","“I will sleep with the head of my bed elevated 6 to 12 inches.”"],"a":1,"r":"Do not eat within 2–3 hours of lying down. Eating before bed increases reflux. The other statements are correct."},{"id":28,"t":16,"s":0,"q":"A newly hired nurse is caring for a client who died of pneumonia. Which action requires intervention by the charge nurse?","o":["Attaching 2 ID tags to the body and 1 on the outside of the shroud.","Leaving drainage tubes and IV lines in the body prior to autopsy.","Leaving any jewelry on the client to be transported to the funeral home.","Asking the family if they wish to participate in bathing the body."],"a":2,"r":"Remove jewelry and give it to the family, documenting the transfer (a wedding band may be left only per policy). Tubes/lines are left in if an autopsy is expected, and family participation is encouraged."},{"id":29,"t":3,"s":0,"q":"The client has a new order for graduated compression stockings. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Measure the size of the client’s foot.","Apply the stockings while the client is sitting in a chair.","Check the stockings for wrinkles or creases.","Remove the stockings once each day for 10 minutes."],"a":2,"r":"Wrinkles act like a tourniquet and impair circulation. Measure the LEG, apply before rising (supine, legs elevated), and remove for ~30 minutes at least every 8–24 hours per policy for skin care and assessment."},{"id":30,"t":16,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is preparing a Mantoux test. Which action indicates correct technique?","o":["Injecting in the subcutaneous tissue.","Drawing up 0.1 mL of PPD tuberculin.","Using a 20-gauge needle.","Inserting the needle at a 90-degree angle."],"a":1,"r":"Mantoux = intradermal injection of 0.1 mL PPD with a 25–27 gauge, ⅜–½ inch needle at 5–15° (bevel up) to raise a wheal."},{"id":31,"t":10,"s":0,"q":"A client is receiving O positive packed red blood cells. The client’s blood type is B positive. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Administer acetaminophen and diphenhydramine PRN.","Immediately stop the transfusion.","Document on an incident report.","Continue to assess the client."],"a":3,"r":"O-negative is the universal donor, and O blood is ABO-compatible with a B-positive recipient. The Rh factor matches, so the transfusion is compatible. Continue to monitor for reactions."},{"id":32,"t":10,"s":0,"q":"A PHCP prescribes vaccines for an older adult. Which should the nurse clarify?","o":["Inactivated polio (IPV) series.","Influenza (quadrivalent).","Herpes zoster (Zostavax).","Tetanus, diphtheria, pertussis (Tdap) booster."],"a":0,"r":"Older adults routinely receive influenza, pneumococcal, Td/Tdap and zoster. Polio vaccine is not routinely given to adults unless at risk or unvaccinated, so clarify it. (Zostavax was a live vaccine that has since been replaced by the recombinant Shingrix.)"},{"id":33,"t":10,"s":0,"q":"A client is prescribed warfarin for a prosthetic heart valve. Which finding indicates a therapeutic effect?","o":["PT of 11.6 seconds.","PTT of 20 seconds.","aPTT of 35 seconds.","INR of 3."],"a":3,"r":"Therapeutic INR on warfarin is 2–3 (2.5–3.5 for mechanical valves). A PT of 11.6 s is normal (not anticoagulated); PTT/aPTT monitor heparin."},{"id":34,"t":9,"s":0,"q":"The nurse starts LR for a burn client prescribed 5,200 mL over the first 24 hours (half in the first 8 hours). How many mL/hr for the first 8 hours?","o":["350 mL/hr","433 mL/hr","325 mL/hr","217 mL/hr"],"a":2,"r":"Half of 5,200 = 2,600 mL in 8 hours → 2,600 ÷ 8 = 325 mL/hr."},{"id":35,"t":16,"s":0,"q":"Which action is appropriate when preparing to administer insulin to an adult?","o":["Limit the dose to 1 mL when injecting into the deltoid muscle.","Insert the needle at a 15-degree angle.","Draw up the dose using a 25-gauge, ⅜-inch needle.","Administer the dose using the Z-track method."],"a":2,"r":"Insulin is subcutaneous: 25–30 gauge, ⅜–⅝ inch needle at 45–90°. Z-track and deltoid volume limits are IM techniques; 15° is intradermal."},{"id":36,"t":10,"s":0,"q":"A client is receiving streptomycin. Which finding indicates an adverse effect?","o":["Increased liver enzymes.","Staining of the teeth.","Creatinine of 2.2 mg/dL.","Photosensitivity."],"a":2,"r":"Aminoglycosides (“-mycin”) are nephrotoxic and ototoxic. Elevated creatinine shows kidney injury. Teeth staining/photosensitivity are tetracycline effects."},{"id":37,"t":11,"s":0,"q":"A client who had a spontaneous abortion is taught about Rho(D) immune globulin. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","o":["“This medication will allow for safe mixing of my blood with the baby’s in future pregnancies.”","“The medication could cause redness or mild pain at the injection site.”","“This medication will prevent potential loss of pregnancy in the future.”","“The medication will help my body fully remove the placenta lining.”"],"a":3,"r":"RhoGAM prevents an Rh-negative mother from forming antibodies against Rh-positive fetal cells. It has no effect on removing the placenta or uterine lining. Injection-site soreness is expected."},{"id":38,"t":7,"s":0,"q":"A client with a history of PUD in the ED has BP 110/72, P 90, yellow emesis, frequent belching, Hgb 9.2 g/dL. What is the priority?","o":["Determine if the client has any stressors.","Type and cross for a PRBC transfusion.","Administer an IV antiemetic.","Prepare the client for surgery."],"a":2,"r":"Stable vital signs with no signs of active hemorrhage mean the immediate problem is vomiting. Controlling it prevents aspiration, dehydration and electrolyte loss. Compare: with hypotension (e.g., 92/58) bleeding is suspected and the priority changes."},{"id":39,"t":11,"s":0,"q":"During labor with fetal monitoring, the FHR has been 180 for 12 minutes. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Assist the client with guided imagery.","Continue to monitor the FHR.","Turn the client to their left side.","Prepare the client for cesarean delivery."],"a":2,"r":"FHR >160 for 10 minutes is fetal tachycardia (possible hypoxia). First, use the least invasive intervention: left lateral position to relieve cord compression and improve placental perfusion, then give oxygen, IV fluids, and notify the provider."},{"id":40,"t":5,"s":0,"q":"It is a priority to notify the PHCP if a client who","o":["has community-acquired pneumonia has WBC 15,000/mm³.","takes warfarin has an INR of 2.","has end-stage renal disease and a creatinine of 2.7 mg/dL.","has heart failure and a sodium of 125 mEq/L."],"a":3,"r":"Sodium 125 is critically low (normal 135–145) and risks seizures and confusion. Elevated WBC in pneumonia, INR 2 on warfarin, and elevated creatinine in ESRD are all expected."},{"id":41,"t":5,"s":0,"q":"A client 4 hours post-op after general anesthesia has T 98.4° F, P 58, BP 98/64, shallow respirations at 26, pale dry skin; ABG pH 7.20, PaCO₂ 54, HCO₃ 26. Which findings relate to the ABG? 1 IV fluids; 2 BP; 3 pulse; 4 temperature; 5 urine output; 6 general anesthesia; 7 pale dry skin; 8 shallow respirations.","o":["2, 3, 6, 7, 8","2, 5, 6, 8","1, 2, 4, 5, 6","4, 5, 7, 8"],"a":0,"r":"This is acute respiratory acidosis (low pH, high CO₂, normal HCO₃). Anesthesia depresses respiration → shallow breathing → CO₂ retention, with hypotension, bradycardia, and pale skin."},{"id":42,"t":12,"s":0,"q":"In a pediatric cardiac unit, which child should the nurse assess first?","o":["2-year-old walking in the playroom with a pulse of 150.","6-year-old sitting and eating a snack with a pulse of 130.","16-year-old sleeping with a pulse of 55.","2-month-old crying with a pulse of 170."],"a":1,"r":"Know pediatric norms. A resting 6-year-old should be ~75–120 bpm, so 130 is abnormal. The others are acceptable for activity/crying/sleeping."},{"id":43,"t":15,"s":0,"q":"A client with lung cancer is receiving external radiation therapy. Which statement shows understanding?","o":["“I will need to avoid eating raw fruits and vegetables.”","“I should avoid close contact with anyone who is pregnant or children under 12.”","“I will need to avoid direct skin exposure to the sun during treatment and for at least 1 year afterward.”","“I should apply 50-SPF sunscreen to the radiation area before going out.”"],"a":2,"r":"Irradiated skin stays sensitive for a year or more, so protect it from sun. External radiation does not make the client radioactive. Don’t apply products to the site unless prescribed."},{"id":44,"t":5,"s":0,"q":"A client had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has","o":["fluid intake of 1,000 mL and urine output of 800 mL over 8 hours.","a temperature rise from 98.2 to 99.3° F over 4 hours.","a WBC decrease from 6,000 to 5,000/mm³.","a weight increase from 199 to 203 lb over 24 hours."],"a":3,"r":"A gain of 4 lb in 24 hours (about 2 L of fluid) signals fluid retention and possible acute rejection. The other values are minor."},{"id":45,"t":3,"s":0,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who develops the following rhythm (premature wide, bizarre QRS complexes with inverted T waves — PVCs). Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Determine if the client has an internal pacemaker.","Check the client’s potassium level.","Place a defibrillator at the bedside.","Notify the rapid response team."],"a":1,"r":"Isolated PVCs are commonly caused by electrolyte imbalance (especially low K⁺ or Mg²⁺), hypoxia, or ischemia. Check the potassium level and notify the provider if it is abnormal. (Rhythm strip was an image in your PDF.)"},{"id":46,"t":7,"s":0,"q":"A client had gastric surgery 8 hours ago and has an NG tube to low suction. Which interventions belong in the plan? 1 Turn down the suction when assessing for peristalsis. 2 Encourage IS every 4 hours while awake. 3 Maintain SCDs. 4 Irrigate the NG with warm tap water as needed. 5 Turn and reposition every 2 hours. 6 Place a sign: “Do not manipulate the NG tube.”","o":["1, 2, 3","3, 5, 6","2, 4, 5","1, 3, 5"],"a":1,"r":"After gastric surgery, the NG tube must not be moved or irrigated without a surgeon’s order (protect the suture line). SCDs and q2h turning prevent VTE and pressure injury. Suction should be turned OFF to auscultate. IS is needed every 1–2 hours, and irrigation uses sterile normal saline, never tap water."},{"id":47,"t":4,"s":0,"q":"After tracheostomy care, which finding requires intervention before leaving the room?","o":["The client places a finger over the tube opening when speaking.","The cuff can be easily inflated and deflated.","There is space for 1 finger between the tie and the neck.","The tube moves in and out of the stoma when the client coughs."],"a":3,"r":"Tube movement means the ties are too loose and the tube can dislodge or be expelled. Fix it. A one-finger gap is correct; occluding the opening to speak is expected (speaking valve/finger)."},{"id":48,"t":4,"s":0,"q":"A client on mechanical ventilation has a low-pressure alarm. Which action should the nurse take next?","o":["Shut the alarm off and call a code.","Assess the cuff of the client’s endotracheal tube.","Manually ventilate with a bag-mask-valve device.","Call respiratory therapy to check the ventilator settings."],"a":1,"r":"Low-pressure alarm = a leak: disconnected tubing, cuff leak, or flat pilot balloon. Assess the client and the cuff. Never silence the alarm without finding the cause."},{"id":49,"t":12,"s":0,"q":"The nurse provides discharge teaching to parents of a child admitted with sickle cell crisis. Which instruction should be included?","o":["“Monitor your child’s temperature daily.”","“Restrict play activity to 1 hour per day.”","“Avoid areas with low altitude.”","“Maintain a warm temperature in your home.”"],"a":3,"r":"Cold causes vasoconstriction and sickling, so keep the home warm and avoid cold exposure, dehydration, and HIGH altitude (low oxygen). Also report fever promptly. Don’t restrict normal activity."},{"id":50,"t":13,"s":0,"q":"An older adult with dementia often tells stories to cope with anxiety. The nurse recognizes confabulation when the client","o":["tells made-up stories.","refuses to see family members.","displays ritualistic behaviors.","reminisces about the past."],"a":0,"r":"Confabulation = unconsciously filling memory gaps with fabricated stories. Refusing family is withdrawal, rituals are compulsions, and reminiscing is normal life review."},{"id":51,"t":2,"s":1,"q":"The nurse on the CQI committee is developing a program to reduce medication errors after a sentinel event. Which strategy should the committee initiate first?","o":["Identify key performance indicators (KPIs) of the medication error.","Review the events that led to the error by creating a flow diagram.","Provide an inservice to staff on safe medication administration.","Develop a quality improvement program for nurses involved in the error."],"a":1,"r":"Understand the problem before acting. Mapping the process (flow diagram/root-cause analysis) shows where the system failed. Education and programs follow, and the focus is on systems, not blaming individuals."},{"id":52,"t":2,"s":1,"q":"The nurse attended a conference on informed consent. Which statement indicates correct understanding?","o":["Informed consent can be waived per facility policy for urgent treatment if the client is unconscious.","Informed consent may not be withdrawn once the client signs.","Informed consent can be signed by a client with dementia if oriented at the time of signing.","Informed consent is signed by the nurse to indicate the client understands."],"a":0,"r":"Emergency (implied) consent applies when delay would risk life or limb and the client cannot consent. Consent can be withdrawn at any time, the client must be competent, and the nurse witnesses the signature but the provider explains the procedure."},{"id":53,"t":0,"s":1,"q":"After hand-off report, the nurse should first see the client who","o":["has pancreatitis and reports mid-epigastric discomfort.","had a transesophageal echocardiogram 2 hours ago, BP 106/64, with a sore throat.","has cirrhosis, new ecchymosis, and PTT 76 seconds.","had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy 18 hours ago with bilateral shoulder pain."],"a":2,"r":"A PTT of 76 s (normal 25–35) with new bruising means active bleeding risk and a coagulation problem in cirrhosis. Epigastric discomfort, post-TEE sore throat, and shoulder pain from retained CO₂ after laparoscopy are expected."},{"id":54,"t":0,"s":1,"q":"Clients are 24 hours postoperative; the nurse assessed 4 hours ago. Which change is a priority?","o":["Received NPH insulin and capillary glucose is 110 mg/dL.","Wound drainage was sanguineous and is now serosanguineous.","BP was 138/86 and is now 136/84.","Reports pain increased to 8/10 after an opioid analgesic."],"a":3,"r":"Pain that worsens after an opioid can signal a complication (bleeding, ischemia, compartment syndrome, anastomotic leak). The other changes are normal or insignificant."},{"id":55,"t":2,"s":1,"q":"The preceptor is teaching legal principles. Which situation is an example of malpractice?","o":["An alert client leaves AMA; the nurse applies restraints to prevent leaving.","A competent client refuses an antidepressant; the nurse dissolves it in food and gives it without the client’s knowledge.","The nurse plugs in an alarming IV pump despite noticing a loose outlet cover.","A client on a low-sodium diet eats chips; the nurse threatens wrist restraints."],"a":1,"r":"Giving medication covertly violates the client’s right to refuse treatment and falls below the standard of care. Items A and D are false imprisonment/assault concerns. (Key per source.)"},{"id":56,"t":0,"s":1,"q":"It is a priority for the nurse to follow up with a client who","o":["is receiving hydrocortisone and has glucose 135 mg/dL.","has MS, feels tired, and has diplopia.","has active pulmonary TB, hemoptysis, and weight loss.","is receiving pyridostigmine and reports nausea and a congested cough."],"a":3,"r":"Nausea and increased secretions on an anticholinesterase suggest cholinergic crisis (overmedication). This can progress to respiratory failure. The other findings are expected in those diagnoses."},{"id":57,"t":12,"s":1,"q":"On a pediatric cardiac unit, which client should the nurse initially assess?","o":["5-year-old watching TV with a pulse of 78.","Toddler playing with toys with a pulse of 100.","1-month-old sleeping with a pulse of 180.","15-year-old reading with a pulse of 60."],"a":2,"r":"An infant’s normal rate is ~100–160, lower when asleep. 180 while sleeping is tachycardia. The other rates are normal."},{"id":58,"t":2,"s":1,"q":"Which child should receive an interdisciplinary conference?","o":["3 years old, epiglottitis, difficulty breathing.","4 years old, new Kawasaki disease, started on immunoglobulin.","8 years old, multiple compound fractures in Buck’s traction.","5 years old, cystic fibrosis since birth, hospitalized 4 times in 2 months."],"a":3,"r":"A chronic, complex illness with repeated admissions needs a team approach (respiratory therapy, dietitian, PT, social work, spiritual care). Others are acute or have a single-discipline focus."},{"id":59,"t":3,"s":1,"q":"A client with pericarditis has been hospitalized for 24 hours. WBC 13,000/mm³ and temperature 100.8° F. What is the most appropriate action?","o":["Update the nursing interventions as needed.","Notify the charge nurse that the plan of care is outdated.","Change the client’s goal to reflect current status.","Obtain an order for an infectious disease referral."],"a":0,"r":"These findings fit the inflammatory process of pericarditis. Evaluation of the plan means modifying interventions (e.g., fever/comfort measures), not changing goals or escalating."},{"id":60,"t":2,"s":1,"q":"Which charted note by a nurse indicates correct documentation?","o":["“Dressing change performed to coccyx wound with no adverse effects noted by the client. Client transferred to chair.”","“IV catheter site in left antecubital space is red and warm to touch. IV catheter removed intact. 20-gauge catheter placed in right forearm.”","“Found client sitting on floor. All 4 side rails upright. Client reports no pain. No abrasions or bleeding. PHCP notified. Incident report completed.”","“Reports of pain in abdomen with vomiting noted. Medications administered.”"],"a":1,"r":"Document objective, specific, factual details (site, assessment findings, gauge, location). Don’t mention incident reports in the chart, and avoid vague wording (“no adverse effects,” “medications administered”)."},{"id":61,"t":1,"s":1,"q":"A client brought in their own CPAP machine. What is the most important nurse action?","o":["Explain that the hospital must supply the CPAP machine.","Review the instruction booklet on how to operate the machine.","Ask the client when they last cleaned the machine.","Have the machine inspected by the facility’s engineering department."],"a":3,"r":"Client-owned electrical equipment must be checked for electrical safety by biomedical/engineering before use."},{"id":62,"t":2,"s":1,"q":"The facility’s emergency response plan is activated. Which client should the nurse recommend for discharge?","o":["DVT on warfarin with PT 28 seconds.","HIV positive, nonproductive cough, Mantoux 6 mm induration.","Suspected appendicitis with intense McBurney’s point pain, now reporting the pain has stopped.","COPD on IV steroids with SpO₂ 86% on room air."],"a":0,"r":"Discharge the most stable client. A PT of 28 s (about 2× normal) is therapeutic on warfarin. Sudden pain relief in appendicitis suggests rupture, 6 mm in an HIV+ client is positive, and SpO₂ 86% is unstable."},{"id":63,"t":1,"s":1,"q":"The nurse observes a newly hired UAP. Which action indicates correct care?","o":["Wearing a gown to enter the room of a client with influenza.","Removing protective eyewear before removing gloves.","Placing dedicated equipment in the room of a client with herpes zoster.","Donning sterile gloves to empty a urinary drainage device."],"a":2,"r":"Herpes zoster requires contact precautions, including dedicated equipment. Influenza = droplet (mask). Gloves come off FIRST (most contaminated), and clean gloves are used to empty drainage devices."},{"id":64,"t":1,"s":1,"q":"During a class on child car passenger safety, which parent statement indicates a need for further teaching?","o":["“Car seats should be rear-facing in the back seat until about 2 to 4 years.”","“With a booster seat, the lap belt should be across the child’s upper thighs.”","“The harness chest clip should be at the level of the infant’s armpits.”","“One hand should fit between the infant and the harness.”"],"a":3,"r":"The harness should be snug: no more than one to two fingers should fit beneath it. A whole hand means it is too loose."},{"id":65,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"Which new order requires follow-up by the nurse?","o":["Fluid bolus of 500 mL over 1 hour for a 12-year-old in sickle cell crisis.","High-potassium diet for a 5-year-old with acute glomerulonephritis.","Chelation therapy for an 8-year-old with lead poisoning.","IV hydrocortisone for a 10-year-old with adrenal insufficiency."],"a":1,"r":"Acute glomerulonephritis causes oliguria and hyperkalemia, so potassium, sodium, and fluids are restricted. The other orders are appropriate."},{"id":66,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"Which order should the nurse clarify?","o":["IV alteplase for ischemic stroke symptoms that began 2 hours ago.","Low-sodium, low-potassium diet for acute kidney injury.","Chest x-ray for a client with a permanent pacemaker placed 6 months ago.","1 unit of PRBCs for a client with polycythemia vera."],"a":3,"r":"Polycythemia vera is an excess of RBCs, so transfusing more would increase viscosity and clotting risk. X-rays are safe with pacemakers; MRI is the concern."},{"id":67,"t":11,"s":1,"q":"Oxytocin is infusing for labor augmentation. The nurse should discontinue it if the client has","o":["uterine relaxation between contractions.","an FHR of 68 for 90 seconds.","dark red streaks in the bloody show.","intermittent back pain with each contraction."],"a":1,"r":"Fetal bradycardia indicates fetal distress or uterine hyperstimulation. Stop oxytocin, turn the client left, give oxygen, and notify the provider. Relaxation between contractions is desired."},{"id":68,"t":11,"s":1,"q":"After ensuring a newborn has a patent airway, what should the nurse do next?","o":["Weigh the newborn and document the weight.","Dry the newborn’s skin and cover with a warm blanket.","Administer vitamin K.","Administer eye prophylaxis."],"a":1,"r":"After airway comes thermoregulation (ABC then temperature). Cold stress increases oxygen consumption and hypoglycemia. Weight, vitamin K and eye care follow."},{"id":69,"t":16,"s":1,"q":"The nurse is auscultating the aortic valve. The diagram shows four sites: (1) right 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border, (2) left 2nd intercostal space at the sternal border, (3) left 4th intercostal space at the sternal border, (4) left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line. Where should the stethoscope be placed?","o":["Site 1: right 2nd intercostal space, sternal border.","Site 2: left 2nd intercostal space, sternal border.","Site 3: left 4th intercostal space, sternal border.","Site 4: left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line."],"a":0,"r":"Aortic = right 2nd ICS (“Aortic Right”). Pulmonic = left 2nd ICS. Tricuspid = left lower sternal border. Mitral (apical) = 5th ICS midclavicular line. (Diagram was an image in your PDF.)"},{"id":70,"t":11,"s":1,"q":"A client with AB-negative blood delivered a baby 4 hours ago. Which action should the nurse be prepared to take?","o":["Assess the newborn for abnormal clotting.","Administer Rh(D) immune globulin to the infant after double-nurse verification.","Review the newborn’s blood type.","Administer Rh(D) immune globulin to the mother within 24 hours of birth."],"a":2,"r":"Rh(D) immune globulin is given to the Rh-NEGATIVE mother only if the newborn is Rh-POSITIVE, so check the newborn’s type first. It is given to the mother within 72 hours (not the infant)."},{"id":71,"t":11,"s":1,"q":"LMP began November 19. Using Naegele’s rule, what is the EDD?","o":["July 12","August 12","July 26","August 26"],"a":3,"r":"Naegele’s rule: add 7 days to the LMP and subtract 3 months. Nov 19 + 7 = Nov 26 → minus 3 months = August 26."},{"id":72,"t":13,"s":1,"q":"A client who had a below-the-knee amputation from diabetic circulation problems shows ineffective coping with defense mechanisms. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Determine what is causing the client to respond in such a way.","Obtain an order for a psychiatric consultation.","Tell the client their behavior is not acceptable.","Explain that their reactions are unproductive to getting better."],"a":0,"r":"Assess first (nursing process) with open, nonjudgmental exploration. Defense mechanisms are normal reactions to loss. Avoid judging or lecturing."},{"id":73,"t":13,"s":1,"q":"A client presents to the ED after an amitriptyline overdose. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Administer oxygen via nasal cannula.","Initiate gastric lavage with activated charcoal.","Determine the events leading to the overdose.","Document the number of pills ingested."],"a":0,"r":"ABCs come first: TCA overdose causes respiratory depression, arrhythmias, and seizures. Support airway/breathing before decontamination or history-taking."},{"id":74,"t":13,"s":1,"q":"On a mental health unit, the nurse should first assess the client who has","o":["paranoid schizophrenia and has not bathed in 3 days.","major depressive disorder stating, “I wish I could stop crying all the time.”","bulimia nervosa and went to the restroom 15 minutes after a meal.","suicidal ideation and is reluctantly discussing a suicide contract with staff."],"a":2,"r":"Active purging (vomiting) risks electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia) and cardiac arrhythmias. The suicidal client is already engaged with staff in safety planning, and the others are not urgent."},{"id":75,"t":13,"s":1,"q":"Which client is at highest risk for suicide?","o":["70-year-old man whose wife recently died and goes to weekly bingo with friends.","52-year-old man who lost his job 3 months ago and feels he will never find another.","39-year-old woman newly diagnosed with breast cancer seeking a second opinion.","15-year-old girl who just broke up with her boyfriend and spends more time with friends."],"a":1,"r":"Hopelessness plus a major loss in a middle-aged man is a strong risk profile. The others still have social connection and active coping."},{"id":76,"t":7,"s":1,"q":"A newly hired nurse is giving continuous tube feeding via a gastrostomy tube. Which action requires immediate intervention?","o":["Adding no more than 4 hours’ worth of feeding to the bag.","Keeping the HOB at 30–45 degrees.","Using an irrigation set opened 48 hours ago.","Returning the residual obtained from the tube."],"a":2,"r":"Irrigation sets are changed every 24 hours to prevent bacterial contamination. Limiting formula to 4 hours, a 30–45° HOB, and returning residuals are acceptable."},{"id":77,"t":5,"s":1,"q":"A client with urinary incontinence is at risk for skin breakdown. Which preventive action should the nurse implement?","o":["Check the skin every 8 hours.","Request an order for an indwelling catheter.","Clean the perineum with hot water after each episode.","Apply a moisture barrier ointment to the skin."],"a":3,"r":"Barrier ointment protects skin from urine. Check and cleanse after each episode with warm water and pH-balanced cleanser. Catheters are not used for convenience (CAUTI risk)."},{"id":78,"t":8,"s":1,"q":"Which action should the nurse take when caring for a client in Buck’s (skin) traction?","o":["Turn the client to a 30-degree angle every 2 hours.","Provide pin site care.","Assess pedal push strength every 4 hours.","Keep the foot of the bed elevated."],"a":3,"r":"Buck’s traction needs countertraction: elevate the foot of the bed so the client’s body weight pulls back against the traction. Pin care applies to skeletal traction."},{"id":79,"t":5,"s":1,"q":"When assessing an older adult for urinary incontinence, which question is important?","o":["“Do you take the herbal preparation ginkgo biloba?”","“Do you ever use pads or tissues in your underwear to catch urine?”","“Do you have a history of kidney stones?”","“Do you have a family history of polycystic kidney disease?”"],"a":1,"r":"Asking about pads/tissues reveals the extent, frequency, and impact of incontinence, which is essential for planning care."},{"id":80,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"A client with a UTI has been prescribed ciprofloxacin. Which instruction should the nurse give?","o":["“This medication provides an analgesic effect and decreases painful urination.”","“Antacids should be taken 30 minutes after this medication.”","“You should not operate heavy machinery or drive while taking this medication.”","“You could experience dizziness and should change positions slowly.”"],"a":3,"r":"Fluoroquinolones can cause dizziness, lightheadedness, and CNS effects, so teach safety and slow position changes. Also report tendon pain (rupture risk), and separate from antacids/iron/calcium by 2+ hours."},{"id":81,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"An older adult takes furosemide daily for heart failure. Which finding is a priority to follow up?","o":["Reports of ringing in the ears.","Orientation to person and place only.","Potassium level of 3.5 mEq/L.","Weight loss of 1 lb in 3 days."],"a":0,"r":"Tinnitus signals ototoxicity, which can become permanent with loop diuretics, so report it before further doses. Potassium 3.5 is low-normal and a 1 lb loss is expected. (Key per source.)"},{"id":82,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"A 22-year-old female is prescribed lithium. Which statement needs further teaching?","o":["“I should be sure to eat food that contains sodium.”","“I should take this medication with food.”","“This medication will make my urine turn reddish-orange.”","“I should avoid becoming dehydrated.”"],"a":2,"r":"Lithium does not discolor urine (that’s phenazopyridine/rifampin). Maintain steady sodium and fluid intake, because low sodium or dehydration raises lithium to toxic levels."},{"id":83,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"A client’s potassium is 2.4 mEq/L. Which order should the nurse question?","o":["Administer 20 mEq KCl IV over 15 minutes.","Withhold the regularly scheduled furosemide.","Place the client on telemetry.","Allow the client out of bed with assistance only."],"a":0,"r":"IV potassium must not exceed 10 mEq/hr on a general unit (rapid infusion can cause cardiac arrest). Telemetry, holding furosemide, and fall precautions are appropriate."},{"id":84,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"Which prescription should the nurse follow up with the PHCP?","o":["Aspirin sensitivity and prescribed acetaminophen.","Potassium 3.8 mEq/L and prescribed sodium polystyrene.","Decreased phenytoin level and prescribed seizure precautions.","History of rash with penicillin and prescribed an MAOI."],"a":1,"r":"Sodium polystyrene lowers potassium and is for hyperkalemia. A normal K⁺ of 3.8 could cause hypokalemia. The other orders are appropriate (the penicillin rash is unrelated to MAOIs)."},{"id":85,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"The nurse is teaching a client with anemia who is starting liquid ferrous sulfate. Which instruction should be included?","o":["“Avoid eating excessive amounts of beans and green leafy vegetables.”","“You will notice a change in your bowel habits and stool color.”","“The medication can be taken with milk to prevent GI irritation.”","“Do not mix the medication with any other medications, foods, or liquids.”"],"a":1,"r":"Iron causes dark green/black stools and constipation or GI upset. Dilute liquid iron, use a straw to avoid staining teeth, and avoid milk/antacids that reduce absorption."},{"id":86,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"The nurse will give subcutaneous enoxaparin. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Check the most current D-dimer.","Gently massage the area after administration.","Aspirate for blood before injecting.","Insert the needle at a 90-degree angle."],"a":3,"r":"Enoxaparin is given in the abdomen at 90°. Don’t aspirate, expel the air bubble, or massage (bruising/hematoma). Monitor platelets/CBC, not D-dimer."},{"id":87,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"A toddler who weighs 22 lb is prescribed ampicillin 150 mg PO three times daily. Available: suspension 250 mg/5 mL. What should the nurse do?","o":["Check for an allergy to sulfa medications.","Contact the PHCP for clarification.","Administer 3 mL by mouth 3 times per day.","Determine the toddler’s weight in kilograms."],"a":2,"r":"Do the math: 150 mg ÷ 250 mg × 5 mL = 3 mL per dose. The question supplies what is needed, so no clarification is required (22 lb ≈ 10 kg)."},{"id":88,"t":7,"s":1,"q":"After a paracentesis, it is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client develops","o":["pink-tinged urine.","vesicular breath sounds.","flatulence.","pruritus."],"a":0,"r":"Pink-tinged urine (hematuria) suggests bladder puncture/perforation. Vesicular breath sounds are normal, and expected findings include drainage at the site and decreased abdominal girth."},{"id":89,"t":5,"s":1,"q":"ABG: pH 7.52, PaCO₂ 26 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 24 mEq/L. Which acid-base imbalance?","o":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"a":2,"r":"High pH (alkalosis) with LOW CO₂ (respiratory cause) and normal bicarbonate = respiratory alkalosis, from hyperventilation (anxiety, pain, hypoxia)."},{"id":90,"t":13,"s":1,"q":"A client admitted for alcohol withdrawal now (1500) has T 102° F, P 120, BP 164/100, profuse diaphoresis, hallucinations, nausea and vomiting (earlier vitals were near-normal with mild agitation). What action is appropriate?","o":["Administer flumazenil.","Notify the PHCP.","Attempt to orient the client.","Increase the rate of the IV infusion."],"a":1,"r":"This pattern indicates delirium tremens, a life-threatening emergency. The client needs more benzodiazepines, so notify the provider immediately. Flumazenil (a benzodiazepine antagonist) can trigger seizures."},{"id":91,"t":7,"s":1,"q":"During a large-volume enema, the client’s abdomen becomes distended and rigid. What should the nurse do?","o":["Reposition the client for better instillation.","Lower the enema fluid bag.","Ask the client to rate their pain level.","Discontinue the enema treatment."],"a":3,"r":"A distended, rigid abdomen suggests bowel perforation. Stop immediately and notify the provider."},{"id":92,"t":16,"s":1,"q":"A female client was taught to collect a midstream urine specimen. Which statement requires additional teaching?","o":["“I will remove the container before stopping the flow of urine.”","“Since I’m right-handed, I will use my left hand to spread the labia.”","“I will use the towelette to cleanse the area using a back-and-forth motion.”","“I will start to urinate in the toilet before urinating into the container.”"],"a":2,"r":"Cleanse from front to back, using a new wipe each time. Back-and-forth spreads organisms toward the urethra."},{"id":93,"t":6,"s":1,"q":"A client with Addison disease is at risk for Addisonian crisis. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Restrict fluid intake.","Administer hydrocortisone.","Provide a low-carbohydrate diet.","Monitor the client’s ADH levels."],"a":1,"r":"Addison disease = cortisol/aldosterone deficiency; crisis causes hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia. Treatment is IV hydrocortisone and fluids, not restriction."},{"id":94,"t":12,"s":1,"q":"Which finding should the nurse instruct parents to report after their child’s tonsillectomy?","o":["Odor to the breath.","Persistent earache.","Mouth breathing.","Temperature of 99.4° F."],"a":1,"r":"Bad breath and mouth breathing are expected after tonsillectomy, and a low-grade temp is normal. Persistent earache may signal infection and needs to be reported (also report frequent swallowing and bright red bleeding)."},{"id":95,"t":3,"s":1,"q":"A telemetry client continues to develop the rhythm shown (irregularly irregular, narrow QRS, no distinct P waves — atrial fibrillation). What should the nurse plan?","o":["Prepare to administer digoxin.","Initiate transcutaneous pacing.","Defibrillate the client.","Administer IV atropine."],"a":0,"r":"Atrial fibrillation with a stable client is treated with rate control (digoxin, β-blockers, calcium channel blockers) and anticoagulation. Pacing/atropine are for bradycardia, and defibrillation is for VF/pulseless VT. (Strip was an image.)"},{"id":96,"t":5,"s":1,"q":"Which signs and symptoms of peritonitis in peritoneal dialysis should the nurse discuss? 1 Hypoactive bowel sounds 2 Rebound abdominal tenderness 3 Amber urine 4 Nausea and vomiting 5 Cloudy or opaque outflow 6 Diarrhea 7 Bradycardia","o":["2, 5, 6, 7","1, 4, 7","4","1, 2, 4, 5"],"a":3,"r":"Peritonitis: cloudy effluent, abdominal pain/rebound tenderness, fever, N/V, hypoactive bowel sounds (ileus), and tachycardia. Not bradycardia."},{"id":97,"t":8,"s":1,"q":"A client suspected of having a retinal detachment of the LEFT eye. Which statement is consistent?","o":["“I feel as if a curtain has been pulled down over my right eye.”","“I have lost the central part of my vision.”","“I am experiencing a sharp pain in my eye.”","“I see floating dark spots in my left eye.”"],"a":3,"r":"Retinal detachment is painless with floaters, flashes, and a curtain/veil in the affected eye. Central vision loss suggests macular degeneration."},{"id":98,"t":16,"s":1,"q":"The nurse has an order to remove intermittent sutures from an abdominal wound. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Remove each suture in order from top to bottom.","Cut the suture by lifting it with forceps and cutting as close to the skin as possible.","Send the removed sutures to the lab for culture and sensitivity.","Apply adhesive strips after each suture is removed."],"a":1,"r":"Lift the knot, cut close to the skin on the side opposite the knot, and pull gently so no exposed (contaminated) suture is dragged through tissue. Remove alternating sutures first, then apply strips as needed."},{"id":99,"t":10,"s":1,"q":"A client with a pulmonary embolism on heparin. Which lab value requires immediate attention?","o":["PT 12 seconds","aPTT 60 seconds","Hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL","Platelets 100,000/mm³"],"a":3,"r":"A falling platelet count (<100,000 or >50% drop) suggests heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Stop heparin and notify the provider. aPTT 60 s is therapeutic (1.5–2.5× control)."},{"id":100,"t":9,"s":1,"q":"A client has deep partial-thickness burns to the lower torso, both forearms, and thighs from a hot grease spill. Which action is a priority?","o":["Maintaining a warm environment.","Inserting an indwelling urinary catheter.","Preparing to intubate the client.","Inspecting for signs of inhalation injury."],"a":1,"r":"This is a large burn (~30%+ TBSA) needing fluid resuscitation, and hourly urine output (≥30 mL/hr) is the best indicator that resuscitation is adequate. A scald from hot grease has no inhalation mechanism. (Key per source.)"},{"id":101,"t":2,"s":2,"q":"A client scheduled for ECT tomorrow asks, “Can I refuse the ECT treatment tomorrow?” Which response should the nurse make?","o":["“You should consider discussing this decision with your family first.”","“Having this treatment can be scary, but it is going to help you feel better.”","“Let’s talk with your doctor before you make a final decision.”","“You have a right to refuse even though you have signed the consent.”"],"a":3,"r":"Clients can withdraw consent at any time. The nurse is a client advocate and must not coerce, reassure falsely, or delay the client’s decision."},{"id":102,"t":2,"s":2,"q":"Which statement indicates correct understanding of informed consent?","o":["It is an agreement to receive medical treatment that cannot be revoked once signed.","Forms must be signed when a client is competent to make an informed decision.","It is signed by the nurse to indicate the client understands the procedure.","It is the nurse’s responsibility to explain the risks, benefits, and alternatives."],"a":1,"r":"The client must be competent (capacity) to consent, and consent can be revoked. The provider explains risks/benefits/alternatives, and the nurse only witnesses the signature."},{"id":103,"t":0,"s":2,"q":"After change-of-shift report, the nurse should first see the client who","o":["had laparoscopic surgery 12 hours ago with right shoulder pain.","had a cardiac catheterization 1 hour ago and has been drowsy since the procedure.","is scheduled for an echocardiogram in 1 hour and is eating food from a visitor.","had an ERCP 1 hour ago and is reporting difficulty swallowing."],"a":3,"r":"Difficulty swallowing after ERCP (sedation, throat anesthesia, or duodenal perforation) threatens the airway — ABCs. Referred shoulder pain after laparoscopy is expected, and a drowsy client after sedation is not unusual."},{"id":104,"t":11,"s":2,"q":"In L&D, several clients are in second-stage labor on external monitors. Which client is a priority for follow-up?","o":["Cervix dilated to 10 cm with moderately intense contractions of 40–50 seconds.","FHR consistent at 132 bpm.","Bloody show that is dark red with active pushing.","Contractions lasting 60 seconds with a 3-minute interval."],"a":2,"r":"Bloody show should be pink or red-tinged mucus. Dark red bleeding could indicate abruptio placentae or uterine problems. The other findings are normal for second stage."},{"id":105,"t":0,"s":2,"q":"The RN is assigning tasks. Which is best to assign to the LPN/VN?","o":["Collection of past medical history for a client admitted for cellulitis.","Recording output for a client who urinated in the toilet collection container.","Assisting with delivery of breakfast trays.","Developing a plan of care for a client postoperative from a cholecystectomy."],"a":0,"r":"LPN/VNs may collect data and contribute to the plan of care. UAPs record output and deliver trays. The RN is responsible for developing the care plan."},{"id":106,"t":2,"s":2,"q":"Which client information requires the ED nurse to report to an outside agency?","o":["18-year-old at 4 months pregnant with abdominal pain.","27-year-old with a history of gonorrhea and chlamydia from 1 year ago.","45-year-old injured in a factory fire with 50% TBSA burns.","72-year-old living with their daughter who has several stage 3 pressure ulcers."],"a":3,"r":"Several stage 3 pressure ulcers in a dependent older adult suggest neglect/abuse, which is mandated reporting (Adult Protective Services). Past STIs aren’t reportable now."},{"id":107,"t":0,"s":2,"q":"On the pediatric unit, which child should the nurse assess first?","o":["15-year-old with acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and crackles with neck vein distention.","13-year-old with hemophilia requiring prolonged pressure after a venipuncture.","10-year-old with sickle cell disease reporting pain of 7/10 and due for pain medication.","7-year-old with celiac disease and 3 episodes of diarrhea in 8 hours."],"a":0,"r":"Crackles and JVD signal fluid overload/heart failure and pulmonary edema in PSGN. This threatens breathing and needs immediate assessment. Others are expected or being managed."},{"id":108,"t":5,"s":2,"q":"A client had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has","o":["urine that continues to be slightly pink since surgery.","an increase in weight from 199 to 201 lb over 72 hours.","a BUN change from 10 to 16 mg/dL over 48 hours.","a BP change from 122/78 to 92/58 mm Hg over 4 hours."],"a":3,"r":"A significant BP drop suggests hypovolemia, hemorrhage, or sepsis, which threatens graft perfusion. Pink urine is expected early after surgery, and a 2 lb gain over 72 hours is minor."},{"id":109,"t":2,"s":2,"q":"After assessing a newly admitted client with pericarditis, which action should the nurse take first when preparing the plan of care?","o":["Prioritize the client’s reported concerns.","Determine any referrals that need to be initiated.","Formulate short-term and long-term goals.","Review client data to identify areas of concern."],"a":3,"r":"Nursing process: analyze the data first, identify the problems/nursing diagnoses, and then set priorities, goals, and interventions."},{"id":110,"t":2,"s":2,"q":"Which documentation by a staff nurse indicates correct charting?","o":["“IV site in antecubital space is red and warm. IV removed. New IV started.”","“Found client sitting on floor. Rails up. No pain. PHCP notified. Incident report completed.”","“Returned from radiology. VS obtained with slight increase in BP.”","“Ambulated from bed to doorway without assistance. No shortness of breath or diaphoresis noted. VS remain within baseline after ambulating.”"],"a":3,"r":"Good documentation is specific, objective, and measurable (activity, response, vitals). Avoid vague terms like “slight increase,” omitted details, and references to incident reports."},{"id":111,"t":2,"s":2,"q":"Which situation requires the nurse to complete an incident report?","o":["A client developed phlebitis from an IV catheter and is now receiving antibiotics.","A staff member called a code for a client in respiratory distress.","A client reports having an advance directive but has no copy on the chart.","The nurse finds a PRN prescription for restraints in the medical record."],"a":0,"r":"Incident reports document unexpected events/adverse outcomes (e.g., IV complications requiring treatment). Calling a code is appropriate care, and the others are routine."},{"id":112,"t":1,"s":2,"q":"A newly hired UAP is observed. Which action indicates correct client care?","o":["Donning sterile gloves to empty a urinary drainage device.","Removing protective eyewear before removing gloves.","Wearing a gown, mask, and gloves when entering the room of a client with influenza.","Placing dedicated equipment in the room of a client with cellulitis of the left leg."],"a":2,"r":"Influenza requires droplet precautions (mask) plus standard precautions (gloves, gown if splash anticipated), so this is the only acceptable choice. Gloves come off first, and drainage emptying uses clean gloves."},{"id":113,"t":1,"s":2,"q":"In an ambulatory center, which client should be removed from the waiting room immediately?","o":["Reports burning and pruritus with a linear rash of vesicles around the right rib cage.","A 1 mm flat, red area at a Mantoux site given 48 hours ago.","Congestive heart failure and a 3 lb gain in 3 days.","Fever of 99.6° F, generalized pain, and borderline personality disorder."],"a":0,"r":"Herpes zoster (shingles) lesions are infectious to anyone without varicella immunity; place the client in an exam room under contact precautions."},{"id":114,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"Which prescription should the nurse follow up on?","o":["Filgrastim for a client with WBC 2,500/mm³.","Preoperative atropine for a client with glaucoma.","Fluid bolus of 500 mL over 1 hour for a child in sickle cell crisis.","Low-potassium diet for a child with acute glomerulonephritis."],"a":1,"r":"Anticholinergics like atropine dilate the pupil and raise intraocular pressure (contraindicated in narrow-angle glaucoma). Filgrastim treats neutropenia, hydration helps sickle cell crisis, and K⁺ is restricted in AGN."},{"id":115,"t":0,"s":2,"q":"A client postoperative from a colon resection arrives from the PACU, responds to commands, and reports moderate pain. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Confirm the client’s identity.","Assess the client’s surgical bandage.","Auscultate the client’s abdomen.","Evaluate the client’s vital signs."],"a":0,"r":"On arrival, verify identity with two identifiers before any assessment or care (patient-safety first); then assess ABCs/vitals, and so on. (Marked with a “?” on your copy.)"},{"id":116,"t":11,"s":2,"q":"The nurse is caring for a newborn with hyperbilirubinemia receiving phototherapy. Which intervention should be included?","o":["Cover the newborn with a light blanket.","Maintain the newborn on their back.","Remove the eye shields each shift or during feedings.","Provide a quiet, stimulus-free environment."],"a":2,"r":"Eye shields protect the retinas but are removed during feeding/each shift to assess the eyes and promote bonding. Expose maximum skin (no blankets) and reposition regularly."},{"id":117,"t":12,"s":2,"q":"Which finding indicates a developmental delay?","o":["1-month-old who does not have head control.","3-month-old who is unable to grasp objects.","9-month-old who is unsteady when standing.","14-month-old who is unable to drink from a cup."],"a":3,"r":"By about 12–15 months toddlers can drink from a cup. Lack of head control is expected at 1 month, voluntary grasp appears ~4–5 months, and standing unsteadily at 9 months is normal."},{"id":118,"t":11,"s":2,"q":"LMP began August 26. Using Naegele’s rule, what is the EDD?","o":["May 19","May 26","June 2","June 9"],"a":2,"r":"Add 7 days → September 2; subtract 3 months → June 2."},{"id":119,"t":15,"s":2,"q":"A client with breast cancer had a mastectomy and is receiving chemotherapy. Which statement indicates effective coping?","o":["“I am glad the nausea and vomiting are subsiding.”","“I do not need as much pain medication as prescribed.”","“I made an appointment to get fitted for a prosthesis.”","“I will begin the next round of chemotherapy next week.”"],"a":2,"r":"Taking active steps toward adapting to body-image change shows effective coping. The other statements are only observations of symptoms/treatment."},{"id":120,"t":13,"s":2,"q":"A client with paranoid schizophrenia has lost 15 lb in 3 weeks and accuses staff of trying to poison them. Which intervention is a priority?","o":["Ask the client what foods they would like to eat.","Allow the client to observe how the food is prepared.","Provide the client with prepackaged foods that they like.","Offer to sit with the client during meals."],"a":2,"r":"Individually packaged foods the client can open themselves reduce suspicion of tampering, so the client can eat safely and gain weight."},{"id":121,"t":13,"s":2,"q":"A newly admitted client has major depressive disorder after the death of her newborn 2 weeks ago. Which goal is the priority?","o":["Signing a no-harm contract.","Exhibiting expected grieving behaviors.","Identifying positive qualities about herself.","Assuming an active role in the care planning process."],"a":0,"r":"Safety is the priority in depression with a major loss. Address suicide risk first; grieving, self-esteem, and participation come after."},{"id":122,"t":7,"s":2,"q":"A home health nurse is talking to the spouse of a client receiving intermittent PEG tube feedings. Which statement indicates correct understanding?","o":["“I check placement by checking gastric pH before each feeding.”","“I make sure my spouse sits up for at least an hour after the feeding.”","“I clean the reusable feeding system with soap and hot water every 48 hours.”","“I withhold the feeding if the gastric residual is 50 mL.”"],"a":1,"r":"Remain upright for 30–60 minutes after feeding to prevent aspiration. Home PEG placement isn’t checked with pH, systems are washed after each use, and residual thresholds are higher (typically hold at ≥250 mL in adults)."},{"id":123,"t":3,"s":2,"q":"A client on bed rest. Which action should the nurse take to help prevent venous thromboembolism?","o":["Place the client on an alternating pressure relieving mattress.","Maintain the HOB at 60 degrees or higher.","Instruct the client to cough and deep breathe every hour.","Ensure the client maintains adequate hydration."],"a":3,"r":"Hydration prevents hemoconcentration and venous stasis. Mattresses prevent pressure injuries, and coughing/deep breathing prevents atelectasis. (Also use SCDs, leg exercises and early ambulation.)"},{"id":124,"t":8,"s":2,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client in skeletal traction. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Maintain the bed in a reverse-Trendelenburg position.","Provide pin site care as prescribed.","Assess pedal push strength every 4 hours.","Turn the client to a 30-degree angle every 2 hours."],"a":1,"r":"Skeletal traction uses pins through bone, with a high risk of osteomyelitis, so pin care is essential. The pulleys, ropes, and weights must hang freely."},{"id":125,"t":5,"s":2,"q":"A client is 12 hours post TURP. Which should be in the plan of care?","o":["Monitor hemoglobin and hematocrit levels.","Restrict oral fluids while receiving continuous bladder irrigation.","Notify the PHCP if the client has blood-tinged urine.","Perform intermittent irrigation of the catheter with sterile saline every hour."],"a":0,"r":"Hemorrhage is the main complication after TURP. Pink-tinged urine is expected, fluids are encouraged to flush the bladder, and CBI is continuous."},{"id":126,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"A young adult female is newly prescribed carbamazepine for seizures. Which statement indicates correct understanding?","o":["“I will weigh myself every week to check for weight gain.”","“If I want children, I should not delay starting a family.”","“I will use condoms as a method of birth control.”","“A low-sodium diet will need to be followed while on this medication.”"],"a":2,"r":"Carbamazepine induces liver enzymes and reduces hormonal contraceptive effectiveness, and it’s teratogenic. Also teach to report sore throat/bruising (agranulocytosis)."},{"id":127,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"A client receiving epoetin alfa. Which finding indicates a therapeutic response?","o":["WBC increased from 3,200 to 6,000/mm³.","Platelets increased from 115,000 to 145,000/mm³.","Hemoglobin increased from 10 to 16 g/dL.","PT increased from 11 to 22 seconds."],"a":2,"r":"Epoetin stimulates RBC production, so Hgb/Hct rise. (Target Hgb is usually ≤11–12 g/dL; excessive rise increases clot/stroke risk.) WBC suggests filgrastim, platelets suggest oprelvekin."},{"id":128,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"IV morphine 1 mg every 2 hours is prescribed. Available: prefilled injectable 2 mg/mL. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Use a 1 mL syringe to remove 1 mg for later use.","Administer 1 mg and lock up the remaining 1 mg.","Have another nurse witness the waste of 1 mg from the prefilled injectable prior to administration.","Administer 1 mg and then have another nurse witness the waste of the remaining medication."],"a":2,"r":"Controlled substances must be wasted with a witness and documented; never save leftover opioid for later. Follow facility policy on the timing of the waste. (Your copy’s marked answer is C.)"},{"id":129,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"A client with heart failure taking digoxin refuses breakfast and reports nausea, weakness, and blurred vision. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Obtain current vital sign readings.","Obtain a prescription for a digoxin level.","Review the client’s current list of medications.","Request a prescription for an antiemetic."],"a":0,"r":"These are signs of digoxin toxicity (also visual halos, bradycardia, arrhythmias). Assess first, especially the apical pulse (hold if <60), then obtain a level and notify the provider."},{"id":130,"t":6,"s":2,"q":"Which client should the nurse follow up with the PHCP?","o":["Cushing’s disease prescribed fludrocortisone.","Potassium 6.2 mEq/L prescribed sodium polystyrene.","Aspirin sensitivity prescribed acetaminophen.","Penicillin rash prescribed an MAOI."],"a":0,"r":"Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid that retains sodium and water, which worsens the hypertension and fluid retention of Cushing’s disease. It is used in Addison’s disease."},{"id":131,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"The nurse is preparing to teach a client with anemia and a new prescription for liquid ferrous sulfate. Which instruction is appropriate?","o":["“Take the medication after dinner to avoid daytime drowsiness.”","“The medication can be taken with milk to prevent GI irritation.”","“Do not mix the medication with any other medications, foods, or liquids.”","“Eat plenty of iron-rich foods such as beans and green leafy vegetables.”"],"a":3,"r":"Dietary iron supports therapy. Iron isn’t sedating, milk decreases absorption, and liquid iron is typically diluted and taken through a straw."},{"id":132,"t":10,"s":2,"q":"A client receiving a continuous heparin infusion for PE. Which finding indicates an adverse effect?","o":["PT 11.6 seconds","PTT 60 seconds","INR 3","Platelet count 90,000/mm³"],"a":3,"r":"Thrombocytopenia signals heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT): stop heparin and notify the provider. PTT 60 s is in the therapeutic range, and PT/INR monitor warfarin."},{"id":133,"t":0,"s":2,"q":"A client who had abdominal surgery has these assessment data (PACU 1400 → floor 1415 → reassessment 1430): intraoperative blood loss 600 mL; dressing scant serous drainage; JP drain 5 mL; urine 60→50→40 mL/hr, BP 126/82→120/78→116/68, P 70→82→92, R 16→14→20, T 97.2° F, LR at 150 mL/hr. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Obtain a prescription for a bolus of IV fluids.","Irrigate the indwelling urinary catheter.","Continue to monitor the client.","Place the client on a warming blanket."],"a":2,"r":"Urine output stays above 30 mL/hr, vitals stay within normal limits, drainage is scant, and temperature is normal, so no intervention is needed. Continue monitoring the trends. (Your copy’s marked answer is C.)"},{"id":134,"t":5,"s":2,"q":"ABG: pH 7.52, PaCO₂ 38 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 30 mEq/L. Which imbalance?","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"a":1,"r":"High pH with high bicarbonate and normal CO₂ = metabolic alkalosis (normal pH 7.35–7.45, CO₂ 35–45, HCO₃ 22–26)."},{"id":135,"t":6,"s":2,"q":"A client with type 1 diabetes is taught about exercise. Which statement shows understanding?","o":["“I will start my exercise near the time my insulin peaks.”","“I will eat a carbohydrate snack after exercising if it will be more than 1 hour before my next meal.”","“I will carry a complex sugar when exercising.”","“I will avoid exercising if my blood glucose is less than 100.”"],"a":1,"r":"Exercise lowers glucose for hours; eat a carbohydrate snack to prevent delayed hypoglycemia. Avoid exercising at insulin peak and carry fast-acting simple sugar."},{"id":136,"t":16,"s":2,"q":"Which statement shows correct understanding of collecting a midstream urine specimen (female client)?","o":["“Since I’m right-handed, I’ll use my left hand to spread the labia apart.”","“I will use the towelette to cleanse using a back-and-forth motion.”","“I will urinate in the container and then finish urinating in the toilet.”","“I will hold the specimen container against my skin while collecting.”"],"a":0,"r":"Spread the labia with the non-dominant hand and keep the dominant hand free for the container. Cleanse front to back, start the stream in the toilet, then collect midstream without touching the container to the skin."},{"id":137,"t":15,"s":2,"q":"A client with lung cancer receives external radiation. Which statement shows a need for further teaching?","o":["“I will use a washcloth with antibacterial soap and water to cleanse the radiation area every day.”","“I will prevent direct sun exposure during treatment and for at least 1 year.”","“I should avoid moisturizers, creams, lotions, or powders on the radiation area unless prescribed.”","“It will be safe for me to be around my pregnant daughter and grandchildren.”"],"a":0,"r":"Use only mild soap and lukewarm water with the hand (no washcloth, no friction), pat dry, and do not scrub off the markings. External beam radiation does not make the client radioactive."},{"id":138,"t":9,"s":2,"q":"A client in the ED is bleeding heavily from a deep forearm laceration. After applying direct pressure, which action should the nurse take next?","o":["Perform a comprehensive head-to-toe assessment.","Insert a large-bore IV catheter.","Cleanse the wound with normal saline.","Prepare the client for suturing."],"a":1,"r":"Circulation: control the bleeding, then establish IV access for fluid/blood replacement. Cleaning and suturing happen after hemodynamic stability."},{"id":139,"t":7,"s":2,"q":"Which manifestations should the nurse monitor for peritonitis in a client receiving peritoneal dialysis?","o":["Hyperactive bowel sounds.","Nausea and vomiting.","Increased amylase level.","Burgundy-colored urine."],"a":1,"r":"Peritonitis: abdominal pain, cloudy outflow, fever, N/V, and decreased bowel sounds. Hyperactive bowel sounds and amylase are not typical."},{"id":140,"t":6,"s":2,"q":"A client has type 1 diabetes, blood glucose >600, and HHS. Which finding should the nurse expect?","o":["Positive urine ketones.","Fluid volume deficit.","Serum pH less than 7.35.","Kussmaul respirations."],"a":1,"r":"HHS causes profound osmotic diuresis → severe dehydration. Ketosis/acidosis/Kussmaul respirations are DKA findings (HHS has enough insulin to prevent ketones)."},{"id":141,"t":9,"s":2,"q":"A visitor in the hospital cafeteria begins to cough and signals that they are choking. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Begin CPR.","Call for assistance.","Prepare to deliver abdominal thrusts.","Open the airway and attempt to ventilate."],"a":2,"r":"A conscious choking adult with an obstructed airway needs abdominal thrusts immediately. CPR is for an unresponsive victim."},{"id":142,"t":11,"s":2,"q":"A client in labor has persistent late decelerations with 50% or more of contractions. The nurse interprets this as","o":["uteroplacental insufficiency.","maternal bradycardia.","umbilical cord compression.","fetal head compression."],"a":0,"r":"Late decels = uteroplacental insufficiency (VELCRO: Variable–cord compression, Early–head compression, Late–placental insufficiency). Turn left, give O₂, stop oxytocin, and notify."},{"id":143,"t":2,"s":3,"q":"The preceptor is teaching legal principles. Which situation is an example of battery?","o":["A nurse finds absent pulses in a casted extremity in the morning and reports it to the PHCP in the afternoon.","A competent client refuses an antidepressant; the nurse tells them they will be discharged if they do not take it.","An alert client leaves AMA; the nurse grabs the client’s arm and says they can’t leave.","A client on a low-sodium diet eats chips; the nurse tells the client wrist restraints will be applied."],"a":2,"r":"Battery = unwanted physical contact. Grabbing a competent client who has the right to leave is battery (and false imprisonment). A is negligence, B is coercion, and D is assault (threat)."},{"id":144,"t":2,"s":3,"q":"The nurse should recommend an interdisciplinary conference for the pediatric client who","o":["was diagnosed with cystic fibrosis and has a distended abdomen.","is 10 hours postoperative and just urinated 30 mL of clear yellow urine.","is receiving hemodialysis with a potassium of 5 mEq/L.","has norovirus and acute diarrhea."],"a":0,"r":"Cystic fibrosis is a chronic multisystem disease needing respiratory, nutrition, GI, and PT collaboration. A distended abdomen suggests GI obstruction/malabsorption complications. Other findings are acceptable or short-term."},{"id":145,"t":16,"s":3,"q":"The preceptor identifies correct care when the newly hired nurse","o":["uses sterile gloves when discontinuing a client’s IV infusion.","empties a client’s colostomy pouch when it is two-thirds full.","uses gastric pH as an indicator for correct nasogastric tube placement.","opens the top flap of a sterile package toward the body."],"a":2,"r":"Aspirate pH ≤5 supports gastric placement (X-ray is the gold standard). Clean gloves are used to remove an IV, pouches are emptied when 1/3 full, and the outer flap of sterile packages is opened away from the body."},{"id":146,"t":2,"s":3,"q":"A maternity nurse is floated to an adult med-surg unit. It is appropriate to assign the float nurse the client who","o":["is scheduled to have an above-the-knee amputation that morning.","was admitted the night before with COPD.","is 24 hours postoperative from an appendectomy.","is scheduled for a cardiac catheterization after admission for chest pain."],"a":2,"r":"Assign the float nurse clients that match their skills with predictable needs. A stable postoperative client with a common surgery is the safest, and the others require specialty skills or have unstable/complex needs."},{"id":147,"t":12,"s":3,"q":"On a pediatric cardiac unit, which client should the nurse initially assess?","o":["15-year-old reading with a pulse of 60.","5-year-old watching TV with a pulse of 78.","2-year-old playing with toys with a pulse of 68.","1-month-old sleeping with a pulse of 140."],"a":2,"r":"A toddler’s normal HR is ~80–140, so 68 is bradycardia. The others are within normal ranges for their ages."},{"id":148,"t":9,"s":3,"q":"A male client has a full-thickness burn to 35% of the lower body. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has a","o":["pulse oximeter reading of 89%.","hematocrit of 60%.","temperature of 100.2° F.","urinary output of 100 mL for the past 2 hours."],"a":0,"r":"SpO₂ <90–92% indicates hypoxemia (inhalation injury, carbon monoxide) — airway/breathing priority. A high Hct from fluid shifts, a low-grade fever, and 50 mL/hr output are expected."},{"id":149,"t":0,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is prioritizing clients in a mass casualty disaster using the triage tag system. Who should be transported first?","o":["Abdominal pain and facial lacerations.","Burns to 75% of the posterior trunk and lower body.","Confused with an open wound on the right lower leg.","Multiple compound fractures and reports chest pain."],"a":3,"r":"Chest pain suggests possible cardiac/pulmonary injury (fat embolus or contusion), so the client is critical but salvageable. 75% TBSA burns would be expectant in a mass casualty."},{"id":150,"t":1,"s":3,"q":"A client with dementia is agitated and having difficulty staying in the chair. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Place a seat alarm on the client’s chair.","Obtain a prescription to apply a vest restraint.","Transfer the client to bed and raise 2 side rails.","Place a table tray in front of the client and across their lap."],"a":0,"r":"Use the least restrictive alternative first. A seat alarm alerts staff without restricting movement; vests, trays across the lap, and raised rails are restraints."},{"id":151,"t":1,"s":3,"q":"Which action is appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Placing a client who had a stem cell transplant on contact precautions.","Having a client with pertussis wear an N95 during transport.","Initiating airborne precautions for a client with epiglottitis.","Wearing an N95 when caring for a client with disseminated varicella zoster."],"a":3,"r":"Disseminated zoster/varicella requires airborne (N95, negative pressure) plus contact precautions. Pertussis and epiglottitis are droplet, and transplant recipients need protective isolation."},{"id":152,"t":1,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is teaching car seat safety to the parents of a 10-month-old. Which statement shows correct understanding?","o":["“We will place the car seat rear-facing in the middle of the back seat.”","“We will be sure that a hand can fit between the infant and the harness.”","“Our car seat is a booster seat anchored with the car lap belt.”","“The harness straps should be below the level of the infant’s armpits.”"],"a":0,"r":"Infants ride rear-facing in the back seat (center is safest). The harness should be snug (1–2 fingers), and rear-facing harness straps are at or below the shoulders."},{"id":153,"t":10,"s":3,"q":"Which prescription should the nurse clarify?","o":["MI 3 hours ago with a troponin level to be drawn in 3 hours.","Ischemic stroke symptoms for 2 hours with IV alteplase.","Pacemaker placed 6 months ago with a chest x-ray scheduled.","Creatinine 2.8 mg/dL with cardiac catheterization scheduled in the morning."],"a":3,"r":"Contrast dye is nephrotoxic, and an elevated creatinine indicates impaired kidney function, so clarify/hydrate/hold. The other orders are appropriate (serial troponins; tPA within the window)."},{"id":154,"t":14,"s":3,"q":"Which action by the nurse is an example of PRIMARY prevention?","o":["Providing a community program on stress management.","Instructing women how to perform monthly breast self-examination.","Teaching foot care to a client with diabetes and a beginning foot ulcer.","Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group."],"a":0,"r":"Primary prevention: health promotion before disease (stress management, vaccines, nutrition). BSE is secondary (screening), and foot care and support groups are tertiary."},{"id":155,"t":14,"s":3,"q":"It is appropriate for the nurse to tell a group of men that they should","o":["begin annual prostate examinations at age 40.","begin sigmoidoscopy every 10 years at age 50.","perform monthly testicular self-examination immediately after a bath or shower.","be tested annually for STDs upon becoming sexually active."],"a":2,"r":"Warm water relaxes the scrotum, making abnormalities easier to feel. Prostate screening starts at ~50 (or 40–45 if high risk), and colonoscopy is every 10 years from 45–50."},{"id":156,"t":12,"s":3,"q":"Which statement by a newly hired nurse about infant/toddler nutrition indicates a need for further teaching?","o":["“Milk is introduced at 12 months to an infant who has been breastfed since birth.”","“Honey can be introduced to an infant at 9 months.”","“4 oz of fruit juice in a cup can be given to a 13-month-old per day.”","“Sliced banana is an appropriate snack for a toddler.”"],"a":1,"r":"Honey is avoided until after 12 months of age due to infant botulism. The other statements are acceptable."},{"id":157,"t":11,"s":3,"q":"A client 4 hours postpartum has saturated a perineal pad in 10 minutes. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Measure the blood pressure.","Assess for a distended bladder.","Massage the fundus.","Administer oxytocin."],"a":2,"r":"Saturating a pad in 15 minutes is postpartum hemorrhage; boggy uterine atony is the most common cause. Massage the fundus first (it is the first, non-invasive intervention), then empty the bladder, and give oxytocin per order."},{"id":158,"t":13,"s":3,"q":"A client has right-sided paralysis after a stroke. The son says, “None of this would have happened if I had been there.” What should the nurse say?","o":["“It sounds like you feel responsible for what has happened to your mother.”","“This must be difficult seeing your mother like this, but she will be fine.”","“Let’s talk about your mother’s therapy plan.”","“You could not have done anything to prevent this if you had been there.”"],"a":0,"r":"Reflecting the son’s feelings is therapeutic. Avoid false reassurance, changing the subject, or giving a definitive statement the nurse can’t prove."},{"id":159,"t":13,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is developing a plan of care for a client with histrionic personality disorder. Which intervention is appropriate?","o":["Assist the client with appropriate behavior during group therapy sessions.","Work with the client on ways to develop trusting relationships.","Communicate with the client using casual language.","Utilize compassionate behavior to keep the client in control."],"a":0,"r":"Cluster B clients need structure, firm limits, and coaching on appropriate behavior in groups. Use direct and concrete communication, not casual or vague language."},{"id":160,"t":12,"s":3,"q":"In the ED, a school-age child has left arm pain and several bruises on the arms and legs. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Separate the child from the parents until abuse is ruled out.","Obtain a detailed history of how the bruising occurred.","Report the bruising to Child Protective Services.","Request a psychiatric consultation."],"a":1,"r":"Assess first: obtain a detailed, objective history and document. Reporting follows if suspicion remains, and avoid accusing the parents."},{"id":161,"t":7,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is teaching the partner of a client who had a stroke and developed dysphagia. Which statement indicates the need for additional teaching?","o":["“Coughing while eating is a sign of dysphagia.”","“A change in my partner’s voice after swallowing may indicate dysphagia.”","“I will make sure my partner does not tilt the head forward while eating.”","“I should monitor for pocketing of food in the mouth.”"],"a":2,"r":"A chin-tuck (head forward) position closes the airway while swallowing and reduces aspiration. The other statements are correct."},{"id":162,"t":7,"s":3,"q":"Which statement shows understanding of GERD diet and lifestyle modification?","o":["“I will wear clothing with a spandex waistband to support my abdomen.”","“I will sleep with my head elevated on a wedge-style pillow.”","“I will sit upright for 30 minutes after meals.”","“I will have a snack 1 hour before bedtime.”"],"a":1,"r":"Elevate the head of the bed 6–8 inches (wedge pillow). Avoid tight clothing, sit upright ≥1–2 hours after meals, and avoid eating 2–3 hours before bed."},{"id":163,"t":16,"s":3,"q":"A client died of pneumonia and an autopsy is anticipated. Which action by the newly hired nurse requires intervention?","o":["Asking the family if they wish to participate in bathing the body.","Removing drainage tubes and IV lines from the body prior to autopsy.","Removing jewelry and placing it in a bag to return to the family.","Attaching 2 ID tags to the body and 1 on the outside of the shroud."],"a":1,"r":"When an autopsy is planned, tubes and lines are left in place. Document and secure valuables, identify the body with 3 tags, and offer family involvement."},{"id":164,"t":7,"s":3,"q":"A client with atherosclerosis and elevated cholesterol has been taught about diet. Which statement indicates a need for follow-up?","o":["“I flavor my fish with lemon juice.”","“I cook my food with canola oil.”","“I take an omega-3 supplement daily.”","“I eat two poached eggs for breakfast every morning.”"],"a":3,"r":"Egg yolks are high in cholesterol (~185 mg each). Limit them, and use lean protein, fish, and unsaturated oils."},{"id":165,"t":16,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is preparing to insert an IV catheter in an older adult. Which action is appropriate?","o":["Shaving the area surrounding the vein.","Slapping a vein on the back of the hand.","Placing a washcloth between the client’s skin and the tourniquet.","Using a gauze dressing with paper tape."],"a":2,"r":"Older adults have fragile skin and veins. Protect skin from the tourniquet, avoid slapping (hematoma), clip (not shave) hair, and use a transparent or cloth-friendly dressing/tape."},{"id":166,"t":10,"s":3,"q":"During a PRBC transfusion the client becomes flushed and reports low back pain. After stopping the transfusion, what should the nurse do next?","o":["Collect a urine specimen for the lab.","Notify the blood bank of a possible reaction.","Start 0.9% sodium chloride through new tubing.","Administer acetaminophen 500 mg PO."],"a":2,"r":"Suspected hemolytic reaction: stop the transfusion, keep the IV open with NS via NEW tubing (to avoid infusing residual blood), then notify the provider/blood bank, check vitals, and send samples."},{"id":167,"t":12,"s":3,"q":"A 4-year-old is to receive MMR, IPV, and DTaP. Which finding is a contraindication to administering the vaccines?","o":["WBC of 3,900/mm³.","Temperature of 98.8° F.","Injection-site swelling and redness with previous immunizations.","Runny nose since yesterday."],"a":0,"r":"Low WBC suggests possible immunosuppression — live vaccines (MMR) are contraindicated until evaluated. A mild illness, local reactions, and normal temp are not contraindications."},{"id":168,"t":10,"s":3,"q":"A client is prescribed warfarin. Which lab value indicates a therapeutic response?","o":["PT of 15 seconds.","Platelets increased from 100,000 to 150,000/mm³.","INR of 2.","aPTT increased from 30 to 60 seconds."],"a":2,"r":"Therapeutic INR on warfarin is 2–3. aPTT monitors heparin, and platelets are not a warfarin measure."},{"id":169,"t":16,"s":3,"q":"Which action is appropriate when preparing an IM injection for an adult?","o":["Limit the medication to 4 mL when using the deltoid muscle.","Hold the skin taut when using the Z-track method.","Use the deltoid site for a client who needs 3 mL.","Draw up an air bubble after the medication has been prepared."],"a":1,"r":"Z-track: pull the skin laterally (taut) to seal the medication in the muscle. The deltoid is limited to 1 mL (ventrogluteal up to 3 mL), and the air lock is optional per policy."},{"id":170,"t":10,"s":3,"q":"The nurse should follow up with the PHCP for a client who has","o":["constipation-predominant IBS and has been prescribed loperamide.","diabetes mellitus, a CT scan with IV contrast, and a prescription to withhold metformin.","a prosthetic mitral valve and INR 3.0 on warfarin.","MRSA infection, an allergy to sulfonamides, and has been prescribed vancomycin."],"a":0,"r":"Loperamide slows motility and is contraindicated in constipation. Withholding metformin around contrast is correct, INR 3 is therapeutic for mitral prosthetic valves, and vancomycin isn’t a sulfonamide."},{"id":171,"t":7,"s":3,"q":"A client with PUD in the ED has BP 92/58 mm Hg (history of PUD, vomiting). What is a priority?","o":["Initiate an IV nitroglycerin drip.","Administer an IV antiemetic.","Determine if the client has any stressors.","Type and cross for a PRBC transfusion."],"a":3,"r":"Hypotension in PUD suggests GI hemorrhage and hypovolemia, so prepare for transfusion (type and cross), start IV fluids, and notify the provider. Nitroglycerin would worsen hypotension."},{"id":172,"t":12,"s":3,"q":"A child is postoperative from a VP shunt placement and has become difficult to arouse. Which action should the nurse take immediately?","o":["Measure the head circumference.","Turn the client to the operative side.","Elevate the head of the bed to 30 degrees.","Prepare for endotracheal tube insertion."],"a":2,"r":"Decreased LOC suggests shunt malfunction with increased ICP. Use the least invasive intervention first (raise HOB 30°) and notify the provider. Do not lie on the shunt side."},{"id":173,"t":9,"s":3,"q":"A client in hypovolemic shock has a pulse of 116. IV 0.9% NaCl has been initiated. What should the nurse do next?","o":["Initiate oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula.","Obtain a prescription for a vasodilator.","Document and continue to monitor.","Insert an indwelling urethral catheter."],"a":0,"r":"Shock decreases oxygen delivery, so start oxygen, along with IV fluids. Vasodilators would worsen hypotension, and the client needs intervention, not just monitoring."},{"id":174,"t":5,"s":3,"q":"Which ABG results should the nurse expect in a client with DKA?","o":["pH 7.24, PaCO₂ 50, HCO₃ 24","pH 7.47, PaCO₂ 38, HCO₃ 32","pH 7.36, PaCO₂ 42, HCO₃ 26","pH 7.25, PaCO₂ 35, HCO₃ 14"],"a":3,"r":"DKA produces metabolic acidosis: low pH and low bicarbonate, with compensatory (low-normal) CO₂ from Kussmaul breathing."},{"id":175,"t":6,"s":3,"q":"A client with Addison disease is at risk for Addisonian crisis. Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Provide a low-carbohydrate diet.","Monitor blood pressure frequently.","Administer oral desmopressin.","Restrict fluid intake."],"a":1,"r":"Adrenal crisis causes severe hypotension, hypoglycemia, and hyponatremia. Monitor BP frequently, replace glucocorticoids/fluids, and prevent stress triggers."},{"id":176,"t":5,"s":3,"q":"A client who had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client has","o":["a fluid intake of 1,000 mL and urine output of 800 mL over 8 hours.","a weight increase from 199 to 201 lb over 72 hours.","a WBC decrease from 6,000 to 5,000/mm³.","a temperature increase from 99.5 to 100.3° F over 4 hours."],"a":3,"r":"Immunosuppressed clients may show only subtle signs of infection or rejection; a rising temperature must be reported. Other findings are within acceptable limits."},{"id":177,"t":4,"s":3,"q":"A client on mechanical ventilation has a high-pressure alarm. The nurse has checked for potential causes and cannot determine the problem. What should the nurse do next?","o":["Manually ventilate the client with a bag-mask-valve device.","Call respiratory therapy to check the ventilator settings.","Shut the alarm off and call a code.","Assess the client’s lung sounds and breathing pattern."],"a":0,"r":"If the cause cannot be found quickly, disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with 100% O₂ to ensure oxygenation while help is called. The assessment was already done."},{"id":178,"t":3,"s":3,"q":"A client is to receive a permanent pacemaker. Which statement should the nurse include in the teaching?","o":["“You will need to avoid using cellular phones after the pacemaker is placed.”","“Swelling and redness of the operative area are normal for the first 2 days.”","“Avoid lifting the arm above the head on the pacemaker side for the next 4 weeks.”","“Keep your incision covered for the first 4 days.”"],"a":2,"r":"Limit arm movement/raising above the shoulder until the leads are secured (about 4–6 weeks). Cell phones are fine at a distance of ~6 inches, and redness/swelling should be reported."},{"id":179,"t":1,"s":3,"q":"Which client should be placed in a private room when admitting several clients? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["A client with inhalation anthrax diagnosed 48 hours ago.","A client with a fever of unknown origin.","A client diagnosed with HIV 2 weeks ago.","A client with a WBC of 2,400/mm³."],"a":3,"r":"A WBC of 2,400 is leukopenia/neutropenia and needs protection from infection (private/protective room). Inhalation anthrax and HIV require standard precautions only."},{"id":180,"t":14,"s":3,"q":"Which action by the nurse is an example of SECONDARY prevention? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group.","Performing monthly self-breast examinations.","Providing a community program on stress reduction.","Teaching foot care to a client with diabetes."],"a":1,"r":"Secondary prevention = early detection/screening (BSE, mammograms, labs). Stress reduction is primary, and foot care/support groups prevent progression (tertiary)."},{"id":181,"t":11,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is caring for a pregnant client whose LMP began October 21. Using Naegele’s rule, what is the due date? (Exam 1 recording — options reconstructed)","o":["July 28","July 14","August 28","January 28"],"a":0,"r":"Add 7 days: Oct 21 → Oct 28, subtract 3 months → July 28."},{"id":182,"t":13,"s":3,"q":"A client has paranoid schizophrenia and a history of auditory hallucinations. The client is yelling and pacing in the dayroom, “I will do it.” What should the nurse do? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Obtain increased supervision for the client.","Provide the client with a diverting activity.","Send the client to their room."],"a":0,"r":"“I will do it” may be a command hallucination to harm self/others. Increase supervision for safety; you cannot divert a client actively responding to hallucinations."},{"id":183,"t":13,"s":3,"q":"A client in the manic phase of bipolar disorder says, “I am bored.” Which activity should the nurse suggest? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Walking with the nurse in the courtyard.","Basketball.","Participating in group."],"a":0,"r":"Manic clients need low-stimulation, non-competitive, solitary or one-on-one activity to burn off energy. Competitive games and groups increase stimulation."},{"id":184,"t":7,"s":3,"q":"Which statement by a client who recently had a colostomy placed indicates a correct understanding of diet teaching? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["“Eating yogurt can help decrease the amount of gas I have.”","“I should eliminate pasta from my diet so I don’t develop loose stools.”","“Drinking carbonated beverages can help control odor.”","“My largest meal of the day should be in the evening.”"],"a":0,"r":"Yogurt/buttermilk help decrease gas and odor. Carbonated drinks increase gas, large evening meals fill the pouch overnight, and pasta is not usually restricted."},{"id":185,"t":8,"s":3,"q":"A client who recently received hearing aids. Which statement indicates the need for ADDITIONAL teaching? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["“I will clean the hearing aids with alcohol wipes.”","“I expect the hearing aids to whistle when I cup my hand over them.”","“I will avoid using hair spray.”","“I will clean my hearing aids with soap and water.”"],"a":0,"r":"Alcohol damages the device. Clean with a dry cloth (or a slightly damp cloth with mild soap and water — keep the device dry). Whistling when cupped is expected feedback."},{"id":186,"t":4,"s":3,"q":"A client with asthma is prescribed beclomethasone. Which statement indicates a correct understanding? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["“I should rinse my mouth out after this medication.”","“I should use this medication to treat my symptoms during an asthma attack.”","“I need to check my pulse for 1 full minute.”","“I need to watch my caffeine intake.”"],"a":0,"r":"Beclomethasone is an inhaled corticosteroid: a controller medication, not a rescue inhaler. Rinse and spit to prevent oral thrush."},{"id":187,"t":10,"s":3,"q":"A client is receiving IV magnesium sulfate. Which finding indicates a side effect? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Respiratory rate of 10/min.","Elevated calcium level.","Urinary output of 125 mL/4 hours.","BP change from 122/74 to 136/84."],"a":0,"r":"Magnesium is a CNS depressant; toxicity causes respiratory depression, loss of deep tendon reflexes, and low urine output. The antidote is calcium gluconate. Note that 125 mL/4 hours (≈31 mL/hr) is borderline acceptable."},{"id":188,"t":5,"s":3,"q":"A client who had an open radical prostatectomy was discharged with a urinary catheter. Which statement requires follow-up? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["“I will not be able to take a shower for the next 2 weeks.”","“I will wash my catheter once a day with soap and water.”","“I will change my catheter drainage bag at least once a week or as needed.”","“I will use a leg bag with a catheter.”"],"a":0,"r":"Showering is allowed with a catheter; the bag stays below the bladder. Daily soap-and-water cleaning, weekly bag changes, and leg bags are appropriate."},{"id":189,"t":4,"s":3,"q":"The nurse is monitoring a client following a thoracentesis. It is a priority to notify the PHCP if the client develops (Exam 1 recording)","o":["a heart rate increase from 95 to 110 bpm.","an oxygen saturation decrease from 99% to 96%.","vesicular breath sounds in the peripheral lung field.","serosanguineous drainage from the puncture site."],"a":0,"r":"A rising heart rate may indicate pneumothorax or bleeding/tension. Sats of 96% are normal, vesicular breath sounds are expected, and slight serosanguineous drainage is expected."},{"id":190,"t":9,"s":3,"q":"A client in hypovolemic shock has a heart rate of 160 bpm and is receiving oxygen at 2 L via nasal cannula. Which action is the priority? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Obtain a prescription to increase the oxygen to 4 L/min.","Document the findings and continue to monitor.","Notify the PHCP.","Administer the prescribed normal saline at 150 mL/hr."],"a":3,"r":"Hypovolemic shock needs volume replacement. Give the prescribed isotonic IV fluid right away, then notify the provider."},{"id":191,"t":12,"s":3,"q":"A 3-year-old weighing 33 lb had 160 mL of urine in 8 hours. What action should the nurse take? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Notify the PHCP.","Provide oral fluids.","Perform a bladder scan at the bedside.","Continue to monitor."],"a":3,"r":"33 lb = 15 kg. 160 mL ÷ 8 hr = 20 mL/hr ≈ 1.3 mL/kg/hr, which is adequate (≥1 mL/kg/hr in children), so it is expected; keep monitoring."},{"id":192,"t":9,"s":3,"q":"A client has developed evisceration after abdominal surgery. After calling for assistance, which action should the nurse take next? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Place the client in a supine position with the knees drawn toward the abdomen.","Use a moist ABD pad to cover the abdomen."],"a":1,"r":"Evisceration: call for help, cover the viscera with a sterile saline-moistened dressing (keeps the organs from drying), position low Fowler’s with knees bent to reduce abdominal tension, and notify the surgeon."},{"id":193,"t":9,"s":3,"q":"The PACU nurse assesses a client who had a below-the-knee amputation. What should the nurse prioritize assessing for? (Exam 1 recording)","o":["Hypovolemic shock from bleeding.","Neurogenic shock."],"a":0,"r":"Major bleeding is the earliest, most life-threatening complication after amputation, so check the residual limb dressing and vital signs; keep a tourniquet at the bedside."},{"id":194,"t":8,"s":3,"q":"A client had lumbar spinal surgery 8 hours ago. It is a priority to notify the surgeon if the client (Exam 1 recording)","o":["independently log-rolls in bed.","has 125 mL serosanguineous fluid in the Hemovac.","has a moderate amount of clear drainage on the surgical dressing."],"a":2,"r":"Clear (halo-ring/glucose-positive) drainage suggests a cerebrospinal fluid leak, which risks meningitis and requires immediate reporting. Log-rolling and serosanguineous drainage are expected."},{"id":195,"t":3,"s":3,"q":"A client is on bed rest. Which action will help prevent pulmonary embolism? (Exam 1 recording — one option missing in source)","o":["Determine if the client is using the incentive spirometer.","Instruct the client to perform leg exercises.","Elevate the client’s legs on a pillow."],"a":1,"r":"Leg exercises/ROM prevent venous stasis and DVT (a PE source). Incentive spirometry prevents atelectasis/pneumonia, and pillows under the knees can impair venous return."},{"id":196,"t":1,"s":3,"q":"Which sequence correctly describes removal (doffing) of personal protective equipment? (Exam 1 recording — options reconstructed)","o":["Gloves, goggles, gown, mask","Mask, gown, goggles, gloves","Gown, gloves, mask, goggles","Goggles, mask, gloves, gown"],"a":0,"r":"Remove the most contaminated items first: gloves → goggles/face shield → gown → mask (mask last, outside the room), followed by hand hygiene."},{"id":197,"t":0,"s":4,"q":"Which of the following clients should the nurse delegate the taking of vital signs for to the unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP)?","o":["A client being prepared for elective facial surgery with a history of stable hypertension.","A client receiving a blood transfusion with a history of transfusion reactions.","A client recently started on a new antiarrhythmic agent.","A client who is admitted frequently with asthma attacks."],"a":0,"r":"Correct: “A client being prepared for elective facial surgery with a history of…” – The client is stable.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A client receiving a blood transfusion with a history of transfusion…” – This client is unstable.\n• “A client recently started on a new antiarrhythmic agent” – The UAP cannot take apical pulses.\n• “A client who is admitted frequently with asthma attacks” – This client is unstable and vital sign measurement should not be delegated.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":198,"t":1,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following medical asepsis actions should the nurse take to reduce and prevent the spread of microorganisms? Select all that apply.","o":["Practicing hand hygiene.","Reapplying a sterile dressing.","Sterilizing contaminating items.","Applying a sterile gown and gloves.","Routinely cleaning the hospital environment.","Wearing clean gloves to prevent direct contact with blood or body fluids."],"a":[0,4,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Practicing hand hygiene” – This is not a sterile procedure.\n• “Routinely cleaning the hospital environment” – This is not a sterile procedure.\n• “Wearing clean gloves to prevent direct contact with blood or body flu…” – This is not a sterile procedure.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Reapplying a sterile dressing” – This is a sterile procedure.\n• “Sterilizing contaminating items” – This is a sterile procedure.\n• “Applying a sterile gown and gloves” – This is a sterile procedure.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":199,"t":1,"s":5,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with a decubitus ulcer (stage 4). The wound culture result shows MRSA in the wound bed. Which type of transmission-based precautions should the nurse institute?","o":["Standard.","Droplet.","Contact.","Airborne."],"a":2,"r":"Correct: “Contact” – MRSA in a wound indicates contact isolation.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Standard” – Although they are used for all clients, standard precautions would not help decrease the spread of MRSA.\n• “Droplet” – MRSA in the nares would indicate droplet isolation.\n• “Airborne” – This isolation is indicated for very small molecules that linger for long periods in the air.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":200,"t":0,"s":5,"q":"The nurse is triaging clients involved in a mass causality disaster using the color-coded tagging system. Which clients should be tagged red? Select all that apply.","o":["Is deceased.","Has chest pain.","Has a leg sprain.","Has a chest wound.","Has multiple closed fractures.","Has full thickness burns over 30% of the body."],"a":[1,3,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Has chest pain” – This client would be in the red category.\n• “Has a chest wound” – This client would be in the red category.\n• “Has full thickness burns over 30% of the body” – This client would be red since they have less then 65% burns on their body.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Is deceased” – This client would be in the black category.\n• “Has a leg sprain” – This client would be in the green category.\n• “Has multiple closed fractures” – This client would be in the yellow category.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":201,"t":1,"s":5,"q":"The charge nurse observes a newly hired nurse caring for a client with a wrist restraint. Which of the following observations by the charge nurse requires follow up with the newly hired nurse?","o":["A safety knot was used to secure the restraint.","The call light was placed within reach of the client.","The restraint was applied tightly around the client's wrist.","The clients record indicates that the restraint was released every 2 hours."],"a":2,"r":"Correct: “The restraint was applied tightly around the client's wrist” – The client's restraints should not be tight.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A safety knot was used to secure the restraint” – This is the correct type of knot to use.\n• “The call light was placed within reach of the client” – This is correct for safety.\n• “The clients record indicates that the restraint was released every 2…” – This is the correct amount of time that restraints should be released for ROM.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":202,"t":1,"s":5,"q":"The nurse is discussing accident prevention with the family of an older client who is being discharged following hip surgery. Which of the following physical factors place the client at risk for injury in the home? Select all that apply.","o":["A night light in the bathroom.","Elevated toilet seat with armrests.","Cooking equipment (ex. stove).","Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors.","A low thermostat setting on the water heater.","Common household objects such as a doormat or rug."],"a":[2,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Cooking equipment (ex. stove)” – The client could burn themselves if they fall.\n• “Common household objects such as a doormat or rug” – The client could fall.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A night light in the bathroom” – This is safe.\n• “Elevated toilet seat with armrests” – This is safe.\n• “Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors” – This is safe.\n• “A low thermostat setting on the water heater” – This is safe.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":203,"t":2,"s":5,"q":"The nurse preceptor is describing critical pathways and variance analysis to a new graduate. The preceptor explains that a variance analysis is preformed on all clients with respect to which time frame?","o":["Continuously.","Daily.","Every third day.","Every other day."],"a":0,"r":"Correct: “Continuously” – It is an ongoing process.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Daily”\n• “Every third day”\n• “Every other day”\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":204,"t":3,"s":5,"q":"The nurse is teaching a client with cardiomyopathy about safety measures when at home. Which of the following instructions is most important?","o":["Reporting pain.","Taking a vasodilator.","Avoid over the counter medications.","Moving slowly from a sitting to a standing position."],"a":3,"r":"Correct: “Moving slowly from a sitting to a standing position” – Moving fast could result in orthostatic hypotension and the client would fall.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Reporting pain” – This is important to educate but does not relate directly to safety.\n• “Taking a vasodilator” – This is important to educate but does not relate directly to safety.\n• “Avoid over the counter medications” – This is important to educate but does not relate directly to safety.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":205,"t":0,"s":5,"q":"The nurse is assigning clients to a LPN/LVN and UAP. Which clients should the nurse assign to the LPN/LVN? Select all that apply","o":["A client needing frequent suctioning.","A client requiring a dressing change to the foot.","A client requiring passive range of motion exercises twice daily.","A client requiring reinforcement of teaching about a diabetic diet.","A client requiring vital signs every 8 hours."],"a":[0,1,3],"r":"Correct:\n• “A client needing frequent suctioning.” – Yes, this is under the LPN scope of practice.\n• “A client requiring a dressing change to the foot.” – Yes, this is under the LPN scope of practice.\n• “A client requiring reinforcement of teaching about a diabetic diet.” – Yes, this is under the LPN scope of practice.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A client requiring passive range of motion exercises twice daily.” – No, this can be done by the UAP.\n• “A client requiring vital signs every 8 hours.” – No, this can be done by the UAP.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn safety infection pre, slide 32.)"},{"id":206,"t":9,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following actions should the nurse take when caring for a client with accidental poisoning? Select all that apply.","o":["Save vomitus for laboratory analysis.","Place the client in a supine position.","Induce vomiting if a household cleaner was ingested.","Assess for airway patency, breathing, and circulation.","Determine the type and amount of substance ingested.","Remove any visible materials from the nose and mouth."],"a":[0,3,4,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Save vomitus for laboratory analysis” – This is correct.\n• “Assess for airway patency, breathing, and circulation” – This is correct.\n• “Determine the type and amount of substance ingested” – This is correct.\n• “Remove any visible materials from the nose and mouth” – This is correct.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Place the client in a supine position” – This could result in aspiration pneumonia.\n• “Induce vomiting if a household cleaner was ingested” – This would cause the esophagus to suffer burns twice.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":207,"t":10,"s":6,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with massive hemorrhage. The primary healthcare provider (PHCP) prescribes 1 unit of whole blood to be administered immediately. Which of the following can the nurse safety administer to this client?","o":["A negative.","B negative.","AB negative.","O negative."],"a":3,"r":"Correct: “O negative” – This type of blood is the universal donor.\n\nNot correct:\n• “A negative” – May cause an adverse reaction.\n• “B negative” – May cause an adverse reaction.\n• “AB negative” – May cause an adverse reaction.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":208,"t":10,"s":6,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client receiving packed red blood cells(PRBCs). Which of the following manifestations should alert the nurse to a hemolytic transfusion reaction? Select all that apply.","o":["Headache.","Tachycardia.","Hypertension.","Apprehension.","Distended neck veins.","Sense of impending doom."],"a":[0,1,3,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Headache”\n• “Tachycardia”\n• “Apprehension”\n• “Sense of impending doom”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hypertension” – The client would have hypotension.\n• “Distended neck veins” – This is circulatory overload reaction.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":209,"t":10,"s":6,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client prescribed a blood transfusion. The client asks if any disease can be transmitted from the donor. Which of the following diseases can be transmitted from a donor? Select all that apply.","o":["Epstein-Barr.","HIV.","CMV.","Hepatitis A.","Malaria."],"a":[0,1,2],"r":"Correct:\n• “Epstein-Barr” – Transmitted by blood route.\n• “HIV” – Transmitted by blood route.\n• “CMV” – Transmitted by blood route.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hepatitis A” – Transmitted by oral-fecal route.\n• “Malaria” – Transmitted by mosquitoes.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":210,"t":10,"s":6,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client prescribed 2 units of packed red blood cells. Which of the following actions should the nurse implement when caring for the client. Select all that apply.","o":["Verify that the ABO and Rh of the 2 units are the same.","Infuse a unit of PRBCs in less than 4 hours.","Stop the transfusion if a reaction occurs but keep the line open.","Take the vital signs every 15 minutes while the unit is transfusing.","Inspect the blood bag for leaks, abnormal color, and clots.","Use a 22-gauge catheter for optimal flow of a blood transfusion."],"a":[1,2,4],"r":"Correct:\n• “Infuse a unit of PRBCs in less than 4 hours” – A unit should infuse in 4 hours or less.\n• “Stop the transfusion if a reaction occurs but keep the line open” – If a reaction occurs you should stop the infusion and KVO.\n• “Inspect the blood bag for leaks, abnormal color, and clots” – Blood should be inspected for these prior to administration.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Verify that the ABO and Rh of the 2 units are the same” – You need to verify units with the client's identifier information not the bags.\n• “Take the vital signs every 15 minutes while the unit is transfusing” – Vitals should be taken prior to administration, every 15 minutes for the first hour, then every hour until complete. Not every 15 minutes for the entire transfusion.\n• “Use a 22-gauge catheter for optimal flow of a blood transfusion” – A 19 gauge or larger catheter should be used so RBCs are not destroyed by the lumen.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":211,"t":0,"s":7,"q":"Which of the following activities should the nurse delegate to the unlicensed assistant personnel(UAP)? Select all that apply.","o":["Take vital signs on a new admission.","Assist with the narcotic count.","Check the crash cart for necessary supplies.","Administer a tap water enema.","Obtain client finger sticks on diabetic clients.","Demonstrate how to use an incentive spirometry."],"a":[3,4],"r":"Correct:\n• “Administer a tap water enema” – There are no medications or electrolytes in the enema.\n• “Obtain client finger sticks on diabetic clients” – This is in their scope of practice.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Take vital signs on a new admission” – Only RNs assess new admissions.\n• “Assist with the narcotic count” – Only nurse can do this.\n• “Check the crash cart for necessary supplies” – Only nurse can do this.\n• “Demonstrate how to use an incentive spirometry” – They can not teach.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":212,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"Which of the following clinical situations should be viewed as an assault?","o":["The nurse threatens to apply restraints to a client who is exhibiting aggressive behavior.","The client requests a medical discharge, but the nurse physically forces the client to stay.","The charge nurse sends an email to a staff member which includes a poor performance evaluation about another person.","The nurse overhears the primary health care provider making derogatory remarks to the client about the nurse's level of competency."],"a":0,"r":"Correct: “The nurse threatens to apply restraints to a client who is exhibiting…” – Assault is a threat to harm.\n\nNot correct:\n• “The client requests a medical discharge, but the nurse physically for…” – This would be battery.\n• “The charge nurse sends an email to a staff member which includes a po…” – This would be inappropriate and violate the staff members rights.\n• “The nurse overhears the primary health care provider making derogator…” – This would be defamation.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":213,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"Which of the following actions by the nurse demonstrate ethical practice? Select all that apply","o":["Notifies PHCP about a client refusing dialysis and wants to stop.","Educates client about use and benefits of incentive spirometry when refusing to use device.","Refuses to stay overnight on a mandated shift due to fatigue, leaves at the end of current scheduled shift.","Confronts a coworker who took narcotics out of a client's medication bin for personal use and takes no further action.","Asks a client to “take a walk down the hall so their roommate can speak with the PHCP privately about their biopsy results.”","Tells a client they will find out an answer for them."],"a":[0,1,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Notifies PHCP about a client refusing dialysis and wants to stop”\n• “Educates client about use and benefits of incentive spirometry when r…”\n• “Tells a client they will find out an answer for them”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Refuses to stay overnight on a mandated shift due to fatigue, leaves…”\n• “Confronts a coworker who took narcotics out of a client's medication…”\n• “Asks a client to “take a walk down the hall so their roommate can spe…”\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":214,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"The nurse is caring for a multiple gunshot victim. Which of the following actions should the nurse take to properly secure and handle legal evidence? Select all that apply.","o":["Place bags on the hands and feet.","Give clothing and wallet to the family.","Cut clothing along the seams, avoiding bullet holes.","Collect all personal items, including items from clothing pockets.","Place wet clothing and personal belongings in a labeled, sealed plastic bag.","Do not allow family members, significant others, or friends to be alone with the client."],"a":[0,2,3,5],"r":"Correct:\n• Place paper bags on the hands and feet. Yes, need to preserve evidence.\n• “Cut clothing along the seams, avoiding bullet holes.” – Yes, forensics will need to test the bullet hole areas.\n• “Collect all personal items, including items from clothing pockets.” – Yes, it will all need listed for potential evidence.\n• “Do not allow family members, significant others, or friends to be alone with the client.” – Yes, they could tamper with evidence.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Give clothing and wallet to the family.” – No, this might be needed for evidence in future.\n• “Place wet clothing and personal belongings in a labeled, sealed plastic bag.” – No, this could harm the evidence if placed with wet items. They should be placed separately, labeled, and sealed.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn management of care s, slide 30.)"},{"id":215,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"Which of the following are behaviors of active listening? Select all that apply.","o":["Maintaining an open posture.","Writing down what the client says so that details are not forgotten.","Establishing and maintaining eye contact.","Nodding in agreement with the client throughout the conversation.","Responding positively when giving feedback."],"a":[0,2,4],"r":"Correct:\n• “Maintaining an open posture”\n• “Establishing and maintaining eye contact”\n• “Responding positively when giving feedback”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Writing down what the client says so that details are not forgotten” – The nurse should give the client undivided attention.\n• “Nodding in agreement with the client throughout the conversation” – The nurse should give quality verbal feedback.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":216,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"Which of the following clients is considered the most at risk for the onset of stress related problems?","o":["Client whose father died 3 months ago and lost their job.","Client graduating from college and getting married in a month.","Client whose new business is growing slowly financially.","Client passed over for promotion, who then quit and started their own business."],"a":0,"r":"Correct: “Client whose father died 3 months ago and lost their job” – They have suffered 2 significant loses.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Client graduating from college and getting married in a month” – These are positive events.\n• “Client whose new business is growing slowly financially” – This is considered a positive event.\n• “Client passed over for promotion, who then quit and started their own…” – Only 1 significant loss.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":217,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"Hospital-based admission has become more oriented to crisis intervention. Which of the following clients have priority for admission to an acute care facility? Select all that apply.","o":["Client who lives alone.","Client who is acutely psychotic.","Client who is depressed.","Client who is not sleeping and has a lack of appetite.","Client who is dangerous to self and others.","Client who is not complying with medication regimens."],"a":[1,4],"r":"Correct:\n• “Client who is acutely psychotic.” – Yes, safety issues is the admission criteria for mental health clients.\n• “Client who is dangerous to self and others.” – Yes, safety issues is the admission criteria for mental health clients.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Client who lives alone.” – No, this client is not at a safety risk.\n• Client who is acutely depressed No, this client is not at a safety risk.\n• “Client who is not sleeping and has a lack of appetite.” – No, this client is not at a safety risk.\n• Clients who is not complying with medication regimens No, this client is not at a safety risk.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn psychosocial integri, slide 16.)"},{"id":218,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"Which of the following foods should the nurse eliminate from the diet of a client experiencing alcohol withdrawal?","o":["Milk.","Regular coffee.","Orange juice.","Eggs."],"a":1,"r":"Correct: “Regular coffee” – Coffee contains caffeine that increases tremors and agitation.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Milk” – Milk is an appropriate choice.\n• “Orange juice” – Orange juice is an appropriate choice.\n• “Eggs” – Eggs are an appropriate choice.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":219,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"Which of the following clients are at highest risk for suicide completion?","o":["85-year-old Caucasian male who lives alone after his wife's death last month.","34-year-old single Hispanic female who recently was diagnosed with cancer.","15-year-old African American female whose boyfriend broke up with her several days ago.","52-year-old Asian male who was terminated from his job because of downsizing."],"a":0,"r":"Correct: “85-year-old Caucasian male who lives alone after his wife's death las…” – Over age 65, male and Caucasian are associated with high suicidal rates.\n\nNot correct:\n• “34-year-old single Hispanic female who recently was diagnosed with ca…” – Client is female and Hispanic. Both are associated with low suicidal rates.\n• “15-year-old African American female whose boyfriend broke up with her…” – Client is female and African American. Both are associated with low suicidal rates.\n• “52-year-old Asian male who was terminated from his job because of dow…” – Although client is male the  Asian culture does not have a high suicidal rate.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":220,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with a lithium level of 1.8 mEq/L. The client is due for the next scheduled dose of lithium. Which of the following actions should the nurse perform?","o":["Administer the dose of lithium.","Hold the dose of lithium.","Notify the clients family.","Obtain a set of vital signs."],"a":1,"r":"Correct:\n• Hold the 5PM dose of lithium. Yes, the level is above 1.5.\n\nNot correct:\n• Administer the 5PM dose of lithium. No, the lithium level is above 1.5 so should be held.\n• “Notify the clients family.” – No, the lithium level is above 1.5 so should be held.\n• “Obtain a set of vital signs.” – No, the lithium level is above 1.5 so should be held.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn psychosocial integri, slide 35.)"},{"id":221,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"The primary health care provider prescribes risperidone for a client with Alzheimer's disease. The nurse anticipates administering this medication to help decrease which of the following behaviors?","o":["Sleep disturbances","Concomitant depression","Agitation and assault potential","Confusion and withdrawal"],"a":2,"r":"Correct:\n• “Agitation and assault potential” – Yes, antipsychotics are most effective with agitation and assault potential.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Sleep disturbances” – No, the medication is an antipsychotic.\n• “Concomitant depression” – No, the medication is an antipsychotic.\n• “Confusion and withdrawal” – No, the medication is an antipsychotic.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn psychosocial integri, slide 40.)"},{"id":222,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with borderline personality disorder. The client tells the nurse “you are the best nurse here; the others just want a paycheck.” Which of the following responses by the nurse is appropriate?","o":["“Thank you, you are a nice person.”","“All of the nurses here provide good care.”","“Other clients have told me that also.”","“Have you had problems with other nurses?”"],"a":1,"r":"Correct: ““All of the nurses here provide good care.”” – This stops the client from ‘splitting’ the nurses.\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Thank you, you are a nice person.”” – The nurse needs to understand the client is using splitting behavior and respond with a neutral comment.\n• ““Other clients have told me that also.”” – The nurse needs to understand the client is using splitting behavior and respond with a neutral comment.\n• ““Have you had problems with other nurses?”” – The nurse needs to understand the client is using splitting behavior and respond with a neutral comment.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":223,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"Which of the following medication amounts is appropriate for a client who is being treated with imipramine on an outpatient basis for recurring depression and suicidal ideation to have prescribed?","o":["30-day supply","21-day supply","14-day supply","7-day supply"],"a":3,"r":"Correct: “7-day supply” – This medication amount would not allow the client to overdose if taken in its entirety.\n\nNot correct:\n• “30-day supply” – This medication is lethal in overdose and the client has suicidal ideation. A 30-day supply would allow them access to potentially overdose.\n• “21-day supply” – This medication is lethal in overdose and the client has suicidal ideation. A 21-day supply would allow them access to potentially overdose.\n• “14-day supply” – This medication is lethal in overdose and the client has suicidal ideation. A 14-day supply would allow them access to potentially overdose.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":224,"t":13,"s":8,"q":"A paranoid client reports that men in blue clothing keep looking in the window and whispering. Which of the following responses by the nurse is most appropriate?","o":["“Those men are groundskeepers. They are talking about their work, not you.”","“Do not take things personally, not everyone who is whispering is whispering about you.”","“Do not pay attention to the men. Shall we play cards instead.”","“I will close the drapes so you can't see the men.”"],"a":0,"r":"Correct: ““Those men are groundskeepers. They are talking about their work, not…” – The nurse does not want to agree with the client. They should be truthful and matter of fact.\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Do not take things personally, not everyone who is whispering is whi…” – This disregards the clients thought and appears condescending.\n• ““Do not pay attention to the men. Shall we play cards instead.”” – This is distraction and the client will still have the same thoughts.\n• ““I will close the drapes so you can't see the men.”” – This does not address the false thoughts of the client.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":225,"t":5,"s":9,"q":"A client has a total serum calcium level of 7.6mg/dL. Which clinical manifestations should the nurse expect to note on assessment of the client? Select all that apply.","o":["Constipation.","Muscle twitching.","Hypoactive bowel sounds.","Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes.","Positive Trousseau and Chvostek sign.","Prolonged ST interval and QT interval on EKG."],"a":[1,3,4,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Muscle twitching” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n• “Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n• “Positive Trousseau and Chvostek sign” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n• “Prolonged ST interval and QT interval on EKG” – A sign of hypocalcemia.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Constipation” – Client would have diarrhea.\n• “Hypoactive bowel sounds” – Bowel sounds would be hyperactive.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":226,"t":11,"s":10,"q":"A female client tells the nurse that she is quite sure she is pregnant. Which of the following is a positive sign of pregnancy?","o":["Amenorrhea.","Uterine enlargement.","HCG detected in the urine.","Fetal heartbeat."],"a":3,"r":"Correct: “Fetal heartbeat” – This is a positive sign.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Amenorrhea” – This is a presumptive sign.\n• “Uterine enlargement” – This is a probable sign.\n• “HCG detected in the urine” – This is a probable sign.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":227,"t":11,"s":10,"q":"The nurse is assessing a pregnant client with a diagnosis of abruptio placentae. Which of the following should the nurse expect to assess on the client? Select all that apply.","o":["Uterine irritability.","Uterine tenderness.","Bright red vaginal bleeding.","Abdominal and lower back pain.","Strong and frequent contractions.","Nonreassuring fetal heart patterns."],"a":[0,1,3,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “Uterine irritability”\n• “Uterine tenderness”\n• “Abdominal and lower back pain”\n• “Nonreassuring fetal heart patterns”\n\nNot correct:\n• “Bright red vaginal bleeding” – Dark red bleeding.\n• “Strong and frequent contractions” – Frequent low intensity contractions.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":228,"t":14,"s":11,"q":"The nurse is performing an assessment on an older adult client. Which manifestations are considered normal age-related changes in the eye? Select all that apply.","o":["Clear Lens.","Blurred vision.","Protruding cornea.","Increased tear production.","Diminished pupillary adaptation to darkness.","Increased ability to discriminate among colors."],"a":[1,4],"r":"Correct:\n• “Blurred vision” – Vision becomes blurry with age progression.\n• “Diminished pupillary adaptation to darkness” – This is a normal age-related change.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Clear Lens” – The lens becomes cloudy.\n• “Protruding cornea” – This is not a normal age-related change.\n• “Increased tear production” – They have a decreased tear production.\n• “Increased ability to discriminate among colors” – They have a decreased ability to discriminate among colors.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":229,"t":14,"s":11,"q":"The nurse is working at an osteoporosis screening clinic. Which of the following female clients is at greatest risk for developing osteoporosis? Select all that apply.","o":["An Asian female.","A large framed, dark-skinned female.","A female who started menopause early.","A female with a family history of osteoporosis.","A female who has a physically active lifestyle.","A female with an inadequate intake of calcium and vitamin D."],"a":[0,2,3,5],"r":"Correct:\n• “An Asian female.” – Yes, Asian and Caucasian females are prone to osteoporosis.\n• “A female who started menopause early.” – Yes, menopause predisposes females to osteoporosis.\n• “A female with a family history of osteoporosis.” – Yes, genetics predisposes females to osteoporosis.\n• “A female with an inadequate intake of calcium and vitamin D.” – Yes, calcium and vitamin D deficiency are associated with osteoporosis.\n\nNot correct:\n• A large frame, dark skinned female. No, its common in small framed females.\n• “A female who has a physically active lifestyle.” – No, those with a sedentary lifestyle are prone to osteoporosis.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn health promotion mai, slide 10.)"},{"id":230,"t":7,"s":11,"q":"The nurse provides menu options for a client prescribed a low-fat diet. Which of the following menu options should the nurse recommend to the client?","o":["Shrimp, avocado, and tomato salad.","Liver, potato salad, and sherbet.","Lean steak, mashed potatoes, and gravy.","Turkey breast, boiled rice, and strawberries."],"a":3,"r":"Correct: “Turkey breast, boiled rice, and strawberries” – Low fat options.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Shrimp, avocado, and tomato salad” – Avocado is high in fat.\n• “Liver, potato salad, and sherbet” – High in fat.\n• “Lean steak, mashed potatoes, and gravy” – High in fat.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":231,"t":1,"s":11,"q":"The charge nurse is re-assigning client rooms to accommodate new admissions. Which of the following clients should the nurse assign to the same room? Select all that apply.","o":["32-year-old female that is  2-day post op cholecystectomy and a 40-year-old female with pain due to pancreatitis.","36-year-old female with diarrhea/vomiting of unknown origin and a 31-year-old female receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer.","82-year-old male with pneumonia/candidiasis of the groin and a 76-year-old male with upper respiratory infection/thrush.","46-year-old male with MRSA infection and a 50-year-old male with C.difficile  infection.","18-year-old female that is 1-day post op appendectomy and an 84-year-old female who is agitated and confused."],"a":[0,2],"r":"Correct:\n• “32-year-old female that is  2-day post op cholecystectomy and a 40-ye…” – Similar age, both post-op and nothing contagious.\n• “82-year-old male with pneumonia/candidiasis of the groin and a 76-yea…” – Similar age and both have respiratory infections and yeast.\n\nNot correct:\n• “36-year-old female with diarrhea/vomiting of unknown origin and a 31-…” – One client is immunocompromised and should be placed in a private room.\n• “46-year-old male with MRSA infection and a 50-year-old male with C.di…” – It is not good practice to place clients with different MDRO in the same room.\n• “18-year-old female that is 1-day post op appendectomy and an 84-year-…” – Different ages and safety risk for anyone roomed with an agitated and confused client.\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":232,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who asks the nurse to witness an advance directive. Which of the following actions should the nurse to implement?","o":["Decline the client’s request politely.","Agree to sign the document as a witness.","Notify the PHCP of the client's request.","Help the client locate an unrelated third party."],"a":3,"r":"Correct:\n• “Help the client locate an unrelated third party.” – Correct. Two unrelated people witness the signature and sign the document, nurse cannot sign or family member. It's a conflict of interest.\n\nNot correct:\n• Decline the clients request politely. Does not help the client.\n• “Agree to sign the document as a witness.” – Illegal.\n• “Notify the PHCP of the client's request.” – Not the best intervention.\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn management of care s, slide 4.)"},{"id":233,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"The nurse needs to witness the signature on informed consent forms for multiple clients. Which of the following clients are legally able to sign a consent form for surgery? Select all that apply.","o":["16-year-old married client scheduled for a C-Section.","59-year-old client who speaks Spanish only.","30-year-old client who just received amoxicillin.","88-year-old client who is blind but alert and oriented.","17-year-old client who needs emergency appendectomy."],"a":[0,2,3],"r":"Correct:\n• 16-year-old married client – an emancipated minor (married under 18) can consent.\n• 30-year-old who received amoxicillin – an antibiotic does not impair the ability to give consent.\n• 88-year-old who is blind but alert and oriented – a disability that does not affect comprehension does not remove the right to sign.\n\nNot correct:\n• Spanish-only speaker – needs an interpreter before consent is informed.\n• 17-year-old needing an appendectomy – a minor who is not emancipated needs a parent or guardian (emergencies may proceed under implied consent).\n\n(From your class slides.)\n\n(From your class slides.)"},{"id":234,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"The nurse is discussing living wills with a client. The client asks the nurse if a living will, prepared 3 years ago, remains in effect. Which response should the nurse provide to the client?","o":["“Yes, a living will never expire.”","“You need to speak to an attorney.”","“I will call someone to answer your question.”","It is recommended to review them yearly and update with your health care provider.”"],"a":3,"r":"Correct:\n• “It is recommended to review them yearly and update with your health care provider.” – Yes, it is recommended to review them yearly and update with the health care provider.\n\nNot correct:\n• “Yes, a living will never expire.” – Not the best answer.\n• “You need to speak to an attorney.” – An attorney does not have to complete an advance directive.\n• “I will call someone to answer your question.” – This does not provide the client with help. “\n\n(Copied from the class slide answer page: live review rn management of care s, slide 32.)"},{"id":235,"t":2,"s":7,"q":"The nurse is teaching nursing staff about computerized documentation. Which of the following information should the nurse include ?","o":["Document pain findings within the hour of assessment.","Promote care continuity by using standardized care plans.","Omit narrative progress notes for clinical condition changes.","Use predefined statements for abnormal findings while charting by exception."],"a":1,"r":"Electronic records support continuity of care through standardized care plans that every discipline can see. Charting by exception uses predefined statements for NORMAL findings, and abnormal findings and condition changes still require narrative notes, so the other options are wrong.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":236,"t":7,"s":12,"q":"The nurse in the outpatient clinic is talking with a 22-year-old client who is seeking treatment for constipation and given a prescription and discharge instructions for bisacodyl therapy. Which of the following statements by the client would require follow-up? Select all that apply.","o":["“I should drink 2 to 4 glasses of water per day.”","“I will take the medication on an empty stomach to obtain quick relief from constipation.”","“I will notify my health care provider if I should experience abdominal pain without any relief.”","“I should make sure that I do not take the medication with an antacid even to prevent gastric upset.”","“I should plan on taking the medication for the rest of my life to prevent any future episodes of discomfort.”"],"a":[0,4],"r":"Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for short-term use. Follow-up is needed for: drinking only 2–4 glasses of water (the client should take in about 8–10 glasses a day to help the laxative work and prevent dehydration) and planning to use it for life (long-term use causes dependence and electrolyte loss). Taking it on an empty stomach, reporting unrelieved abdominal pain, and avoiding antacids (they dissolve the enteric coating early) are all correct.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":237,"t":0,"s":8,"q":"The nurse and unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) are caring for an agitated client who had physical restraints applied 5 minutes ago. Which of the following interventions would be appropriate for the nurse to assign to the UAP?","o":["Assessing the client’s behavior.","Monitoring the client’s orientation level.","Providing range-of-motion exercises for the client.","Deciding whether or not to remove the client’s restraints."],"a":2,"r":"UAPs may perform routine, stable tasks such as range of motion and basic care. Assessment, monitoring mental status, and the decision to remove restraints require the RN’s judgment and cannot be delegated.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":238,"t":0,"s":13,"q":"The nurse is caring for a group of assigned clients on the medical surgical unit. Which of the following clients should the nurse see first?","o":["Client with a spinal cord injury who is flushed, diaphoretic.","Client with diabetes mellitus (type 2) who has a finger stick glucose level of 242 mg/dL.","Client with hyperparathyroidism with a calcium level of 11.5 mg/dL.","Client with angina complaining of a headache after receiving prescribed nitroglycerin."],"a":0,"r":"A flushed, diaphoretic client with a spinal cord injury suggests autonomic dysreflexia, a life-threatening emergency (severe hypertension, risk of stroke). The glucose of 242, calcium of 11.5, and a nitroglycerin headache are expected or only mildly abnormal and can wait.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":239,"t":0,"s":13,"q":"The nurse is caring for a group of assigned clients on the medical surgical unit. The UAP reports that a client is complaining of chest pain. Which of the following actions should the nurse perform first?","o":["Ask the UAP to get a complete set of vital signs on the client.","Call the rapid response team.","Administer prescribed nitroglycerin sublingual tab.","Assess the client."],"a":3,"r":"Assessment comes first in the nursing process. The nurse must evaluate the chest pain before acting, then treat or escalate based on the findings.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":240,"t":10,"s":13,"q":"The nurse in the outpatient clinic is talking with a 42-year-old client who has hypertension and is receiving a loop diuretic. Which of the following statements by the client would require follow-up? Select all that apply.","o":["“I should still follow my low-sodium diet.”","“I will stand slowly from a seated position.”","“I will increase my fluid intake each day to 5L.”","“I should weight myself once a month.”","“I will take the medication at the same time of the day, every day.”"],"a":[2,3],"r":"Requires follow-up: drinking 5 L a day (fluid should not be pushed excessively and should be guided by the provider) and weighing once a month (diuretic clients should weigh daily to track fluid loss or gain). Low-sodium diet, standing slowly to prevent orthostatic hypotension, and a consistent daily dosing time are correct.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":242,"t":10,"s":13,"q":"The nurse in the outpatient clinic is caring for a 40-year-old client that is taking an ACE inhibitor. For each action listed, click to specify if the action is indicated or not indicated for the client. [In this app, select every action that is indicated.]","o":["Teach the client to report if a dry cough occurs","Teach the client that the medication should be taken without food.","Advise the client to increase potassium intake.","Female clients who become pregnant should immediately notify the provider."],"a":[0,3],"r":"ACE inhibitors commonly cause a dry cough (report it, the drug may need changing) and are teratogenic, so pregnancy must be reported at once. They cause potassium retention, so clients should NOT increase potassium intake, and most can be taken with or without food.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":246,"t":11,"s":10,"q":"A 26-year-old primigravida client at 4 weeks gestation arrives at the outpatient clinic for an initial prenatal visit. Which of the following findings would be consistent with the client’s history? Select all that apply.","o":["Fatigue.","Nausea.","Quickening.","Breast sensitivity.","Braxton Hicks contractions."],"a":[0,1,3],"r":"Early pregnancy (presumptive signs) includes fatigue, nausea, and breast tenderness. Quickening is first felt around 16–20 weeks (earlier in multiparas), and Braxton Hicks contractions appear in the second and third trimesters.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":247,"t":12,"s":14,"q":"The nurse in the pediatric clinic is caring for a 6-year-old client with recurrent group A streptococcal pharyngitis and inflammation of the conjunctivae. The eyelids are edematous. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Moisten a clean compress with warm water.","Remind the client to avoid rubbing their eyes.","Instill newly prescribed antibiotic drops in both eyes.","Wipe each client eye beginning at the inner canthus."],"a":0,"r":"Crusted drainage must be softened and removed before medication can reach the eye, so the nurse first prepares a warm, moist compress. Then wipe from the inner to the outer canthus with a clean area of cloth for each eye, instill drops, and teach not to rub the eyes.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":248,"t":14,"s":11,"q":"The community health nurse is preparing to host a seminar about preventive care at the local church. Which of the following information should the nurse include for the seminar?","o":["Family planning and well-baby care.","Adult screening for blood pressure and cholesterol.","Family focused medical home and ambulatory care.","Rehabilitative programs such as sports medicine and orthopedic."],"a":0,"r":"Preventive care is the primary level of health care, which focuses on health promotion and protection such as family planning and well-baby care. Screening is secondary, and rehabilitation is tertiary.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":249,"t":14,"s":11,"q":"The charge nurse in the long-term-care facility is preparing to deliver an in-service about the secondary level of health care for staff nurses. Which of the following information should the nurse include in the seminar?","o":["Family planning and well-baby care.","Adult screening for blood pressure and cholesterol.","Family focused medical home and ambulatory care.","Rehabilitative programs such as sports medicine and orthopedic."],"a":1,"r":"Secondary prevention is screening and early diagnosis and treatment, such as adult blood pressure and cholesterol screening. Family planning and well-baby care are primary, and rehabilitation is tertiary.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":250,"t":1,"s":5,"q":"A nurse is working with an emergency response team in caring for a group of people who may have been exposed to anthrax while doing farm work but are not exhibiting manifestation of illness. Select three prescriptions that the nurse would anticipate receiving from the primary health care provider (PHCP).","o":["Ciprofloxacin.","Propranolol.","Hydroxychloroquine.","Furosemide.","Doxycycline.","Clindamycin."],"a":[0,4,5],"r":"Antibiotics are used for anthrax exposure. Ciprofloxacin and doxycycline are the first-line choices and clindamycin is also used in anthrax regimens. Propranolol, hydroxychloroquine, and furosemide do not treat anthrax. Confirm the exact regimen against your class notes.\n\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":251,"t":10,"s":15,"q":"The home health nurse is caring for a 26-year-old client who has pulmonary tuberculosis and is receiving newly prescribed streptomycin 1 gram intramuscular (IM) once daily. Which of the following actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.","o":["Remind the client to monitor for hearing changes.","Tell the client to contact the primary health care provider if the stool is black.","Advise the client to take over-the-counter acetaminophen for fever and headache.","Inform the client that there is no need for a follow-up visit once the medication is completed.","Advise the client to continue streptomycin therapy before any procedures that may require anesthesia."],"a":[0,2],"r":"Correct:\n• “Remind the client to monitor for hearing changes.” – Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside and can damage the 8th cranial nerve (hearing and balance).\n• “Advise the client to take over-the-counter acetaminophen…” – Acetaminophen is the safer choice for fever and headache; NSAIDs add kidney risk to an aminoglycoside.\n\nNot correct:\n• Black stool is not an expected effect of streptomycin.\n• Follow-up visits are still needed after treatment (TB therapy is long and monitored).\n• Streptomycin can increase the effect of anesthesia and muscle relaxants, so the client should tell the provider rather than simply continue it before procedures.\n\n(From your class slides: Respiratory, slide 27.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The acetaminophen option is the least certain part of this key."},{"id":252,"t":7,"s":16,"q":"The nurse is preparing to teach an in-service for nursing staff about post operative care for the client following abdominal surgery. Which content should the nurse speak about in the in-service? Select all that apply.","o":["“Therapeutic touch is an effective way to decrease postoperative abdominal pain.”","“Total body relaxation is an effective adjunct for postoperative pain management.”","“The nursing process is linear throughout the postoperative care of clients who have had abdominal surgery.”","“The nurse’s priority is to check on the client who had abdominal surgery and returned to the unit just now and then check on the client who is requesting pain medication and returned from abdominal surgery 12 hours ago.”"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":"Correct:\n• Therapeutic touch and total body relaxation are accepted non-drug adjuncts for post-operative pain.\n• The newly returned post-operative client is seen first (airway, vital signs, bleeding) before the client at 12 hours who wants pain medication.\n\nNot correct: The nursing process is not linear; it is continuous and cyclical, so assessment and evaluation keep repeating.\n\n(From your class slides: GI, slide 32.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The therapeutic touch option is the least certain part of this key."},{"id":253,"t":7,"s":16,"q":"The nurse is preparing to teach an in-service for nursing staff and students about total parenteral nutrition. Which information should the nurse include in the in-service? Select all that apply.","o":["“Clients with paralytic ileus or severe diarrhea are candidates for parenteral nutrition.”","“Total parenteral nutrition is delivered for the client through a gastrostomy or nasogastric tube.”","“Protein, electrolyte, and glucose nutritional imbalances may be supplemented with total parenteral nutrition.”","“Clients who need long-term nutritional supplementation will receive total parenteral nutrition via IV solutions comprised of 5% dextrose.”"],"a":[0,2],"r":"Correct: TPN is for clients who cannot use the GI tract (for example paralytic ileus or severe diarrhea) and can supplement protein, electrolytes and glucose.\n\nNot correct:\n• Tube feeding through a gastrostomy or nasogastric tube is enteral nutrition; TPN is given by IV.\n• Long-term TPN is given through a central line in concentrated dextrose solutions; 5% dextrose supplies too few calories.\n\n(From your class slides: GI, slide 33.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":254,"t":3,"s":17,"q":"The nurse has taught a client who has hypertension and a body mass index of 28. Which of the following statements by the client would indicate a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I should limit my sodium intake to 3300 mg per day.”","“I will participate in a maximum of 1 hour of exercise per week.”","“I can drink the maximum of 3 beverages that contain alcohol per day.”","“I am going to eat less calories per day by following the Mediterranean diet.”"],"a":3,"r":"Correct: A lower-calorie heart-healthy eating pattern such as the Mediterranean diet supports weight loss, which lowers blood pressure.\n\nNot correct:\n• Sodium should be limited to about 2 g a day, not 3300 mg (class slide 5).\n• Aerobic exercise should be increased; 1 hour a week is too little.\n• Alcohol should be limited; 3 drinks a day is too many.\n\n(From your class slides: Cardiac, slide 46.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":255,"t":16,"s":18,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has severe anemia and requires a transfusion of packed red blood cells (PRBCs). Which of the following four actions should the nurse plan to take?","o":["Check vital signs before transfusion.","Insert an 18-gauge intravenous (IV) line.","Prime the blood tubing with 0.9% sodium chloride.","Transfuse the PRBCs within 6 hours.","Check the expiration date of the PRBCs with a second nurse."],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":"Correct: Take baseline vital signs, use a large-bore (18-gauge) IV, prime the tubing with 0.9% sodium chloride only, and verify the blood product and expiration with a second licensed nurse.\n\nNot correct: A unit of PRBCs should be completed within 4 hours of leaving the blood bank; 6 hours is too long.\n\n(From your class slides: Hematology, slide 14.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":256,"t":6,"s":19,"q":"A client has Diabetes Insipidus. Which nursing intervention is essential for monitoring this condition?","o":["Inserting a Foley catheter","Measuring intake and output","Analyzing blood glucose levels","Sending a urine specimen to the lab"],"a":1,"r":"Correct: Diabetes insipidus causes very large volumes of dilute urine (2–20 L a day), so measuring intake and output is essential, along with daily weights.\n\nNot correct: A Foley is not routinely needed, and blood glucose is not the problem; diabetes insipidus is unrelated to diabetes mellitus.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 8.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":257,"t":6,"s":19,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH).  Which of the following assessment findings would indicate that the client is developing a complication?","o":["A weight loss of 3 pounds in 4 days","Observance of neck vein distention","A decrease in blood pressure","An increase in urine output"],"a":1,"r":"Correct: In SIADH the body retains water, so neck vein distention is a sign of fluid overload.\n\nNot correct: Weight loss, lower blood pressure and increased urine output are not expected with water retention (SIADH gives low urine output, weight gain and low serum sodium).\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 9.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":258,"t":6,"s":19,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client who has possible Cushing’s syndrome.  The nurse expects to find:","o":["hypotension.","thick, coarse skin.","truncal obesity.","weight gain in arms and legs."],"a":2,"r":"Correct: Cushing's syndrome causes truncal obesity (with thin arms and legs), a rounded face, hypertension, muscle wasting and carbohydrate intolerance.\n\nNot correct: Hypotension is seen in Addison's disease; thick, coarse skin in hypothyroidism; and the extremities are thin, not heavy, in Cushing's.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 17.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":259,"t":6,"s":19,"q":"A nurse is educating on blood sugar control to the client who has uncontrolled diabetes that their vision may be impaired from:","o":["Prolonged periods of hyperglycemia","Frequent injections of regular insulin","Lens opacity","Corneal dryness"],"a":0,"r":"Correct: Prolonged hyperglycemia damages the small vessels of the retina (diabetic retinopathy) and can change the lens, impairing vision. Good glucose control protects the eyes.\n\nNot correct: Insulin injections do not cause vision problems; lens opacity (cataract) and corneal dryness are not the effect of high blood glucose being taught here.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 22.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":260,"t":6,"s":19,"q":"Hemoglobin A1C provides information on glucose control over the past:","o":["20 to 40 days","50 to 65 days","60 to 85 days","90-120 days"],"a":3,"r":"Correct: The hemoglobin A1C reflects average glucose control over about 90–120 days (roughly 3 months), the life span of red blood cells.\n\nNot correct: The shorter time frames are too short.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 23.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":261,"t":6,"s":19,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has pheochromocytoma.  Which of the following is the nurse most likely to assess?","o":["Constricted pupils","Blood glucose level of 130 mg/dl","Bradycardia","Blood pressure of 176/88 mm Hg"],"a":3,"r":"Correct: A pheochromocytoma releases excess catecholamines, causing hypertension (176/88 mm Hg), tachycardia, headache, sweating and anxiety.\n\nNot correct: Constricted pupils and bradycardia are the opposite effects, and a glucose of 130 mg/dL is not a key finding.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 25.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":262,"t":10,"s":19,"q":"The nurse is teaching a client who has diabetes mellitus (type 2) and a prescription for extended-release metformin. Select 2 statements the nurse should make.","o":["“You may chew the tablet if you experience any difficulty swallowing.”","“You need to carry emergency identification and a glucagon kit at all times.”","“You should contact the physician if you notice the metformin tablet in the stool.”","“You will need to discontinue the medication for any procedure that requires a contrast agent.”"],"a":[1,3],"r":"Correct:\n• “You will need to discontinue the medication for any procedure that requires a contrast agent.” – Metformin is held around contrast because of kidney injury and lactic acidosis risk.\n• “You need to carry emergency identification and a glucagon kit…” – the likely intended second answer for this select-two item.\n\nNot correct:\n• Extended-release tablets must be swallowed whole, never chewed.\n• The empty tablet shell can appear in the stool and is harmless; there is no need to call.\n\n(From your class slides: Endocrine, slide 26.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The glucagon-kit option is the least certain part of this key (metformin alone rarely causes low blood sugar)."},{"id":263,"t":8,"s":20,"q":"The nurse in the community clinic is caring for a 70-year-old client who has been wearing new bilateral hearing aids for 2 weeks. Which of the following should the nurse include in the client’s care plan?","o":["Check the client’s dexterity with hearing aid use.","Communicate with the client above the client’s eye level.","Speak with the client using loud, high-pitched tones.","Remind the client to take off the glasses while wearing hearing aids."],"a":0,"r":"Correct: A new hearing-aid user needs the nurse to check that the client can handle and adjust the devices (dexterity) and has realistic expectations.\n\nNot correct:\n• Speak at eye level, face to face, not above it.\n• Use a normal, lower-pitched tone; loud, high-pitched speech is harder to understand and can be distorted.\n• Glasses can be worn with hearing aids, and the client does not need to remove them.\n\n(From your class slides: Neurological, slide 80.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.)"},{"id":264,"t":8,"s":21,"q":"The nurse has taught a client who has sustained a right tibial fracture and had an external fixation device applied 36 hours ago. Which of the following statements by the client would indicate a correct understanding of the teaching? Select all that apply.","o":["“I will use chlorhexidine to clean around the pins.”","“I should limit my daily consumption of foods high in vitamin D.”","“I should expect to see some redness around the external pins.”","“I know that the metal rods help steady my leg fracture as it is healing.”","“I will notify my doctor if I experience increased pressure to the point of pain around my heel.”"],"a":[0,3,4],"r":"Correct:\n• Pin sites are cleaned with an antiseptic such as chlorhexidine, using aseptic technique.\n• The metal frame holds the fracture steady while it heals.\n• Increasing pressure and pain, for example around the heel, may signal compartment syndrome and must be reported.\n\nNot correct:\n• Vitamin D (and calcium) helps bone healing, so it should not be limited.\n• Redness at the pin sites should be reported as a possible infection rather than accepted as normal.\n\n(From your class slides: Musculoskeletal, slide 22.)\n(Answer key not on the slide; worked out with NCLEX reasoning, so check it against your class notes.) The pin-site redness item is the least certain part of this key."},{"id":265,"t":14,"s":22,"q":"A 75-year-old client arrived at the outpatient clinic for an annual visit. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Listen to the client reminisce about the stages of their life and critique accordingly.","Discourage the client from repetition while reminiscing about their lifespan.","Use open-ended questions to encourage the client to reminisce.","Teach the client to reminisce in an orderly fashion."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Use open-ended questions to encourage the client to reminisce.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Use open-ended questions to encourage the client to reminisce.” – Open-ended prompts encourage recall\n\nNot correct:\n• “Listen to the client reminisce about the stages of their life and critique accordingly.” – Criticism inhibits sharing\n• “Discourage the client from repetition while reminiscing about their lifespan.” – Repetition can reinforce recall\n• “Teach the client to reminisce in an orderly fashion.” – Recall need not follow a strict order\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":266,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"When a nurse is providing care for a client who has been diagnosed with rubella, the nurse should implement which precaution method?","o":["Airborne","Contact","Droplet"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Droplet\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Droplet” – Respiratory droplets transmit rubella\n\nNot correct:\n• “Airborne” – Used for measles or TB\n• “Contact” – Not the main transmission route\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 1 - Safety & Infection Prevention & Control, slide 10: “Rubella”"},{"id":267,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"The nurse is providing care for a client who has scabies.  Which of the following is appropriate when providing care to this client?","o":["Wear gloves when entering the room","Wear a mask when within 3 feet of this client","Place a mask on the client when taking them to X-Ray","Permit no pregnant visitors"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Wear gloves when entering the room\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Wear gloves when entering the room” – Contact precautions require gloves\n\nNot correct:\n• “Wear a mask when within 3 feet of this client” – Routine mask does not prevent skin transmission\n• “Place a mask on the client when taking them to X-Ray” – Transport mask is not needed for scabies\n• “Permit no pregnant visitors” – Pregnancy alone is not a reason to exclude visitors\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 1 - Safety & Infection Prevention & Control, slide 10: “Scabies”\n• Unit 1 - Safety & Infection Prevention & Control, slide 9: “Gloves”"},{"id":268,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"The nursing instructor is teaching a group of nursing students about how to prevent the spread of influenza. The nursing instructor identifies further teaching is necessary when a student states,","o":["“I will limit the amount of supplies that I bring into the client’s room.”","“The client should wear a mask if he/she must leave the room.”","“Hand hygiene must consist of soap and water in order to kill the influenza microorganism.”","“I will wear gloves, gown, and mask when I enter the room to perform a physical assessment on the client.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Hand hygiene must consist of soap and water in order to kill the influenza microorganism.”\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Hand hygiene must consist of soap and water in order to kill the influenza microorgani…” – Alcohol rub is also effective if hands are not visibly dirty\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I will limit the amount of supplies that I bring into the client’s room.”” – Limits contaminated supplies\n• ““The client should wear a mask if he/she must leave the room.”” – Reduces droplet transmission during transport\n• ““I will wear gloves, gown, and mask when I enter the room to perform a physical assessm…” – Appropriate protection for anticipated exposure\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":269,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client diagnosed with Clostridium difficile (C. diff). To prevent the transmission of C. diff, the nurse should:","o":["perform hand hygiene with soap and water rather than alcohol based hand sanitizer.","assume that only surfaces that the client has touched are actually contaminated.","sterilize all of the equipment and supplies that leave the client’s room.","wear a mask when in the room and for the client to wear a mask when in public."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• perform hand hygiene with soap and water rather than alcohol based hand sanitizer.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “perform hand hygiene with soap and water rather than alcohol based hand sanitizer.” – Washing physically removes spores\n\nNot correct:\n• “assume that only surfaces that the client has touched are actually contaminated.” – Untouched surfaces may also be contaminated\n• “sterilize all of the equipment and supplies that leave the client’s room.” – Appropriate sporicidal disinfection is needed, not universal sterilization\n• “wear a mask when in the room and for the client to wear a mask when in public.” – Contact rather than droplet precautions\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":270,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"The nurse is teaching a new group of unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) about the importance of hand hygiene. Which of the following statements should the nurse include?","o":["“If you don’t have an infection, your hands won’t infect others.”","“Use an alcohol rub when your hands are visibly soiled.”","“Rub all surfaces of your hands with an alcohol rub for 20 to 30 seconds.”","“If you wear gloves, you do not have to wash your hands.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Rub all surfaces of your hands with an alcohol rub for 20 to 30 seconds.”\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Rub all surfaces of your hands with an alcohol rub for 20 to 30 seconds.”” – Adequate rubbing covers all surfaces\n\nNot correct:\n• ““If you don’t have an infection, your hands won’t infect others.”” – Asymptomatic hands can transfer pathogens\n• ““Use an alcohol rub when your hands are visibly soiled.”” – Visible soil requires soap and water\n• ““If you wear gloves, you do not have to wash your hands.”” – Gloves do not replace hand hygiene\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":271,"t":16,"s":22,"q":"A nurse is observing an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) change the linens of a client's bed.  Which of the following observations requires follow-up?","o":["The UAP raises the bed to waist level","The UAP reaches across the bed to straighten the fitted sheet.","The UAP lowers the side rail on the side closest to them.","The UAP rolls the client back and forth from one side of the bed to the other to make access easier"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The UAP reaches across the bed to straighten the fitted sheet.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The UAP reaches across the bed to straighten the fitted sheet.” – Overreaching strains the back\n\nNot correct:\n• “The UAP raises the bed to waist level” – Promotes safe body mechanics\n• “The UAP lowers the side rail on the side closest to them.” – Allows access while working at bedside\n• “The UAP rolls the client back and forth from one side of the bed to the other to make a…” – Turning the client allows linen placement if done safely\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":272,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a new nurse wash their hands.  Which of the following, if observed, would require the preceptor to intervene?","o":["The nurse scrubs in circular motions using lots of friction","The nurse washes their hands for 15-20 seconds and sings Happy Birthday to themselves twice to ensure adequate time.","The nurse washes their hands as opposed to using hand gel when they notice blood on their palms","The nurse allows the water to run off their elbows when washing"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The nurse allows the water to run off their elbows when washing\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The nurse allows the water to run off their elbows when washing” – Routine washing should drain toward fingertips, not elbows\n\nNot correct:\n• “The nurse scrubs in circular motions using lots of friction” – Friction helps remove organisms\n• “The nurse washes their hands for 15-20 seconds and sings Happy Birthday to themselves t…” – Adequate washing time is intended\n• “The nurse washes their hands as opposed to using hand gel when they notice blood on the…” – Visible blood requires soap and water\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":273,"t":1,"s":22,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has a Clostridium difficile infection. Which of the following cleansing agents should the nurse use for hand hygiene?","o":["Nonantimicrobial soap","Alcohol-based hand rub","Povidone-iodine","Chlorhexidine"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Nonantimicrobial soap\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Nonantimicrobial soap” – Soap and water physically remove spores\n\nNot correct:\n• “Alcohol-based hand rub” – Alcohol does not reliably kill spores\n• “Povidone-iodine” – Not required for routine spore removal\n• “Chlorhexidine” – Not reliably sporicidal\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":274,"t":16,"s":22,"q":"A client who needs maximum support is ambulating with a cane for the first time. After educating the client on proper ambulation with a cane, you assess how well the client understood the instructions. Which findings below demonstrate the proper technique? (Select all that apply.)","o":["The top of the cane should be level with the hip joint.","The client moves the weak leg past the cane while walking.","The arm that is holding the cane is flexed at 40-degrees.","The cane should hold the cane 6 inches in front and 6 inches to the side of the stronger leg."],"a":[0,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• The top of the cane should be level with the hip joint.\n• The cane should hold the cane 6 inches in front and 6 inches to the side of the stronger leg.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The top of the cane should be level with the hip joint.” – Proper cane height supports posture\n• “The cane should hold the cane 6 inches in front and 6 inches to the side of the stronge…” – Forward and lateral placement provides support\n\nNot correct:\n• “The client moves the weak leg past the cane while walking.” – Weak leg advances with cane, not beyond it\n• “The arm that is holding the cane is flexed at 40-degrees.” – Elbow should usually flex 15–30 degrees\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":275,"t":16,"s":22,"q":"A client who is ambulating with crutches moves both crutches forward along with the injured leg and then moves the non-injured forward. What type of gait did the client use while ambulating with crutches?","o":["Three-point gait","Swing-to-gait","Four-point gait","Two-point gait"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Three-point gait\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Three-point gait” – Both crutches and affected leg move together\n\nNot correct:\n• “Swing-to-gait” – Both legs swing to crutches\n• “Four-point gait” – Four separate points advance in sequence\n• “Two-point gait” – Opposite crutch and foot advance together\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":276,"t":2,"s":22,"q":"Which of the following qualities does the nurse need to provide caring?","o":["Ability to create a trusting environment","Desire to have a stable career and income","Sensitivity to the needs of other nurses","Longing to help others live a healthy life"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Ability to create a trusting environment\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Ability to create a trusting environment” – Trust enables therapeutic caring\n\nNot correct:\n• “Desire to have a stable career and income” – Personal financial goals are not patient-centered\n• “Sensitivity to the needs of other nurses” – Collegial sensitivity alone does not establish patient trust\n• “Longing to help others live a healthy life” – Desire to help alone does not establish trust\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":277,"t":2,"s":22,"q":"Which of the following describes the nurse’s role for an older adult client with a chronic illness?","o":["Provide caring to help the client live at the optimal level of health and wellness.","Suggest that the client accept eventual death to reduce the burdens on the client’s family.","Implement an individualized therapeutic regimen that brings about a cure.","Encourage the client to minimize the use of services to control costs."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Provide caring to help the client live at the optimal level of health and wellness.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Provide caring to help the client live at the optimal level of health and wellness.” – Supports optimal function and wellness\n\nNot correct:\n• “Suggest that the client accept eventual death to reduce the burdens on the client’s fam…” – Undermines dignity and autonomy\n• “Implement an individualized therapeutic regimen that brings about a cure.” – Chronic illness may not be curable\n• “Encourage the client to minimize the use of services to control costs.” – Cost reduction is not the primary goal\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":278,"t":13,"s":22,"q":"A 76-year-old is indifferent and responds to others only when they initiate an interaction. What form of group therapy would be most useful to promote resocialization?","o":["Psychotherapy","Orientation","Reminiscence","Activity group"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Reminiscence\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Reminiscence” – Shared past experiences encourage interaction\n\nNot correct:\n• “Psychotherapy” – Focuses on psychological treatment rather than shared recall\n• “Orientation” – Targets orientation to person, time and place\n• “Activity group” – May engage clients but is less targeted to life review\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":279,"t":2,"s":22,"q":"An advance directive gives legally binding direction for health care interventions when a patient","o":["has a new diagnosis of cancer.","is diagnosed with Parkinson’s disease.","is unable to make decisions for self because of illness.","diagnosed with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is unable to speak."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• is unable to make decisions for self because of illness.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “is unable to make decisions for self because of illness.” – Directives guide decisions when capacity is lost\n\nNot correct:\n• “has a new diagnosis of cancer.” – Diagnosis alone does not establish incapacity\n• “is diagnosed with Parkinson’s disease.” – Diagnosis alone does not establish incapacity\n• “diagnosed with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is unable to speak.” – Inability to speak does not imply incapacity\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Management of Care, slide 5: “Legal document  that expresses the client's medical decisions should they become unable to do so.”"},{"id":280,"t":10,"s":22,"q":"A nurse is caring for a client who has Parkinson's disease and is taking diphenhydramine 25 mg PO TID. Which of the following therapeutic outcomes should the nurse expect to see?","o":["Improved bladder function","Delay in disease progression","Decreased tremors","Relief of depression"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Decreased tremors\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Decreased tremors” – Anticholinergic action may reduce tremor\n\nNot correct:\n• “Improved bladder function” – Anticholinergic effects can cause retention\n• “Delay in disease progression” – Does not slow progression\n• “Relief of depression” – Not an antidepressant\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":281,"t":8,"s":22,"q":"A nurse is providing teaching to the family of a client who has Parkinson's disease. Which of the following information should the nurse include in the teaching?","o":["Provide client supervision.","Speak loudly to the client.","Limit client physical activity.","Leave the television on continuously."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Provide client supervision.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Provide client supervision.” – Reduces falls while allowing activity\n\nNot correct:\n• “Speak loudly to the client.” – Parkinson disease does not imply hearing loss\n• “Limit client physical activity.” – Activity helps preserve mobility\n• “Leave the television on continuously.” – Continuous noise may be distracting\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 10 - Neurological, slide 49: “Daily program of exercise”\nThe slide's daily exercise program rules out “Limit client physical activity”; supervision is not stated directly."},{"id":282,"t":10,"s":22,"q":"A patient has been admitted after overdosing on acetaminophen. The nurse plans to monitor this patient for development of which complication related to the overdose?","o":["Decreased urinary output","Kidney stones","Acute hepatic necrosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Acute hepatic necrosis\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Acute hepatic necrosis” – Toxic metabolites cause liver injury\n\nNot correct:\n• “Decreased urinary output” – Renal injury may occur but is not the hallmark\n• “Kidney stones” – Stones are not typical\n• “Metabolic alkalosis” – Acidosis may occur, not typical alkalosis\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":283,"t":10,"s":22,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer an injection of morphine to a patient and notes a respiratory rate of 10 breaths/min. What is the nurse’s best action?","o":["Hold the drug, record the assessment, and recheck in 1 hour.","Administer a smaller dose and record the findings.","Notify the health care provider and delay drug administration.","Administer the prescribed dose and notify the health care provider."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Notify the health care provider and delay drug administration.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Notify the health care provider and delay drug administration.” – Hold opioid and report respiratory depression\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hold the drug, record the assessment, and recheck in 1 hour.” – Delays necessary provider notification\n• “Administer a smaller dose and record the findings.” – Dose adjustment needs authorization\n• “Administer the prescribed dose and notify the health care provider.” – Further opioid can worsen breathing\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":284,"t":12,"s":22,"q":"A nurse is making a home visit on a new mother with an infant. What action by the mother requires the nurse to intervene?","o":["Shows the nurse the water heater setting that is on 110° F (43.3° C)","Keeps hand on infant while reaching for supplies on changing table","Places baby to sleep in crib with no blankets, toys, or other objects","Cooks while holding and cuddling infant to provide comfort"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Cooks while holding and cuddling infant to provide comfort\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Cooks while holding and cuddling infant to provide comfort” – Hot food and surfaces threaten burns\n\nNot correct:\n• “Shows the nurse the water heater setting that is on 110° F (43.3° C)” – Low setting reduces scald risk\n• “Keeps hand on infant while reaching for supplies on changing table” – Hand contact prevents falls\n• “Places baby to sleep in crib with no blankets, toys, or other objects” – Empty crib supports safe sleep\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":285,"t":12,"s":22,"q":"The nurse plans a teaching session with a toddler’s parents on car safety. Which will the nurse teach? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Place the car safety seat in the front passenger seat equipped with an airbag.","Place the car safety seat in the rear seat, behind the driver’s seat.","Secure in a rear-facing, upright car safety seat.","After the age of 2 years, toddlers can be placed in a forward-facing car seat.","Harness safety straps should fit snugly."],"a":[1,2,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Place the car safety seat in the rear seat, behind the driver’s seat.\n• Secure in a rear-facing, upright car safety seat.\n• After the age of 2 years, toddlers can be placed in a forward-facing car seat.\n• Harness safety straps should fit snugly.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Place the car safety seat in the rear seat, behind the driver’s seat.” – Rear seat is safer\n• “Secure in a rear-facing, upright car safety seat.” – Rear-face until seat height or weight limit\n• “After the age of 2 years, toddlers can be placed in a forward-facing car seat.” – Source expects this; only transition after rear-facing limits\n• “Harness safety straps should fit snugly.” – Snug harness reduces movement in collision\n\nNot correct:\n• “Place the car safety seat in the front passenger seat equipped with an airbag.” – Airbags can seriously injure children\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":286,"t":3,"s":23,"q":"The nurse in the telemetry unit is caring for a 60-year-old client who has a history of several episodes of ventricular tachycardia in the past 48 hours. A normal QT interval was observed earlier today. A left ventricular assist device was inserted 2 weeks ago.\nElectrocardiogram:\n[image.png](https://classroom.galencollege.edu/courses/8273805/files/359448245/preview)\nSelect the best option to complete the following sentence.\nThe nurse should first ___________.","o":["check the client’s deep tendon reflexes","asses (collect data about/lpn) the client’s Chvostek and Trousseau signs","hold the client’s next dose of amiodarone","administer magnesium sulfate, 2 g, IV over 2 minutes for the client"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• administer magnesium sulfate, 2 g, IV over 2 minutes for the client\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “administer magnesium sulfate, 2 g, IV over 2 minutes for the client” – Source identifies torsades; magnesium treats it\n\nNot correct:\n• “check the client’s deep tendon reflexes” – Reflex assessment delays rhythm treatment\n• “asses (collect data about/lpn) the client’s Chvostek and Trousseau signs” – Calcium signs are secondary\n• “hold the client’s next dose of amiodarone” – Holding a later dose does not treat current rhythm\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":287,"t":10,"s":23,"q":"The nurse is caring for a patient who is receiving a high dose of intravenous azithromycin to treat an infection. The patient is also taking acetaminophen for pain. The nurse should expect to review which lab values when monitoring for this drug’s side effects?","o":["Electrolytes","Liver enzymes","Complete blood counts","Urinalysis"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Liver enzymes\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Liver enzymes” – Both agents can injure liver\n\nNot correct:\n• “Electrolytes” – Not the main shared toxicity\n• “Complete blood counts” – Not primary monitoring for hepatic injury\n• “Urinalysis” – Does not detect liver toxicity directly\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":288,"t":10,"s":23,"q":"The nurse provides home-care instructions for a patient who will take a high dose of azithromycin after discharge from the hospital. Which statement by the patient indicates an understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I should avoid dairy products while taking this drug.”","“I should expect diarrhea to be a common, mild side effect.”","“I may take antacids 2 hours before taking this drug.”","“I should take acetaminophen for fever or mild pain.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I may take antacids 2 hours before taking this drug.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I may take antacids 2 hours before taking this drug.”” – Separating relevant antacids avoids absorption interaction\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I should avoid dairy products while taking this drug.”” – Dairy avoidance is not routinely required\n• ““I should expect diarrhea to be a common, mild side effect.”” – Diarrhea can occur, but significant or persistent diarrhea needs evaluation\n• ““I should take acetaminophen for fever or mild pain.”” – Additional acetaminophen may increase hepatic risk\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":289,"t":14,"s":23,"q":"A nurse is conducting a physical assessment of a 90-year-old client. The nurse understands that special considerations when working with older adults include which of the following?","o":["Older adults do not require a “head to toe assessment,” so an abbreviated assessment should be done.","The nurse needs to leave the assessment that is most painful until the end.","It is important to complete the entire physical assessment at one time, conducting a “head to toe assessment.”","The nurse needs to first direct the assessment to that which is most likely associated with the presenting problem."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The nurse needs to first direct the assessment to that which is most likely associated with the presenting problem.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The nurse needs to first direct the assessment to that which is most likely associated …” – Prioritize presenting problem\n\nNot correct:\n• “Older adults do not require a “head to toe assessment,” so an abbreviated assessment sh…” – Older adults still need comprehensive assessment\n• “The nurse needs to leave the assessment that is most painful until the end.” – Painful assessment need not invariably be last\n• “It is important to complete the entire physical assessment at one time, conducting a “h…” – Fatigue may require breaks\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":290,"t":2,"s":23,"q":"Using the Resident Assessment Instrument (RAI), the nurse identifies a trigger for a male nursing home resident who requires an indwelling urinary catheter from the Minimum Data Set (MDS). Which should the nurse do next?","o":["Refer to the appropriate quality measures.","Assign suitable nursing interventions.","Institute agency-approved catheter care.","Develop an individualized care plan."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Refer to the appropriate quality measures.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Refer to the appropriate quality measures.” – Investigate identified trigger before planning\n\nNot correct:\n• “Assign suitable nursing interventions.” – Interventions follow further assessment\n• “Institute agency-approved catheter care.” – Routine catheter care does not investigate trigger\n• “Develop an individualized care plan.” – Individual plan follows assessment\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":291,"t":2,"s":23,"q":"A nurse hears a colleague tell a nursing student that they never touch a patient unless they are performing a procedure or doing an assessment. From a caring perspective, the nurse tells the student","o":["touch is only used when a patient is in pain.","touch forms a connection between nurse and patient.","touch is a type of verbal communication.","they do not touch the patients either."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• touch forms a connection between nurse and patient.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “touch forms a connection between nurse and patient.” – Appropriate touch strengthens connection\n\nNot correct:\n• “touch is only used when a patient is in pain.” – Comforting touch is not limited to pain\n• “touch is a type of verbal communication.” – Touch is nonverbal\n• “they do not touch the patients either.” – Reinforces an unnecessarily restrictive approach\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":292,"t":2,"s":23,"q":"A nurse completing a hospital discharge to home understands that which of the following interventions is most important before discharge?","o":["Medication reconciliation","Contacting a durable medical equipment facility","Providing a list of community resources","Educating the client on appropriate range-of-motion exercises"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Medication reconciliation\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Medication reconciliation” – Prevents omissions and medication discrepancies\n\nNot correct:\n• “Contacting a durable medical equipment facility” – Important only when equipment needed\n• “Providing a list of community resources” – Useful but less immediately safety-critical\n• “Educating the client on appropriate range-of-motion exercises” – Specific to mobility needs\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":293,"t":14,"s":23,"q":"Which of the following statements are true about long-term care? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Most long-term care is paid for by Medicare.","The number of older adults needing long-term care services is expected to increase 233%.","Costs of long-term care have outpaced inflation since 2003.","Long-term care is the most cost-effective manner in which to care for older adults.","Most individuals in the United States have purchased long-term care insurance."],"a":[1,2],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• The number of older adults needing long-term care services is expected to increase 233%.\n• Costs of long-term care have outpaced inflation since 2003.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The number of older adults needing long-term care services is expected to increase 233%.” – Source projects increased need\n• “Costs of long-term care have outpaced inflation since 2003.” – Source reports historical cost growth\n\nNot correct:\n• “Most long-term care is paid for by Medicare.” – Medicare generally does not cover custodial long-term care\n• “Long-term care is the most cost-effective manner in which to care for older adults.” – Cost-effectiveness depends on setting and need\n• “Most individuals in the United States have purchased long-term care insurance.” – Most people lack dedicated long-term care insurance\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":294,"t":1,"s":23,"q":"The nurse is concerned that a break in sterile procedure has occured.  Which of the following actions would caused a break in sterile field?","o":["Grasping the edge of the outermost flap and opening it away from oneself","Keeping objects on the field 1 inch from the edge","Keeping the sterile field in eyesight","Transferring a sterile object to a sterile field with a clean gloved hand"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Transferring a sterile object to a sterile field with a clean gloved hand\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Transferring a sterile object to a sterile field with a clean gloved hand” – Clean gloves contaminate sterile objects\n\nNot correct:\n• “Grasping the edge of the outermost flap and opening it away from oneself” – Opening away avoids reaching over field\n• “Keeping objects on the field 1 inch from the edge” – Keep items outside the contaminated border\n• “Keeping the sterile field in eyesight” – Visibility preserves monitoring\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 1 - Safety & Infection Prevention & Control, slide 5: “You MUST use sterile gloves, instruments, devices and  mask”\nYour slide says sterile technique needs sterile gloves, which supports the answer; the exact clean-glove wording is not on your slides."},{"id":295,"t":1,"s":23,"q":"A nurse is performing a sterile dressing change for a client.  Which of the following actions by the nurse would compromise the sterility of the procedure? (Select all that apply.)","o":["The nurse leans across the sterile field.","The nurse turning their back on the sterile field.","Opening a sterile package and then dropping the item onto a non-sterile field.","The nurse opens a sterile package and drops a sterile item in the one inch border around the field."],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• The nurse leans across the sterile field.\n• The nurse turning their back on the sterile field.\n• Opening a sterile package and then dropping the item onto a non-sterile field.\n• The nurse opens a sterile package and drops a sterile item in the one inch border around the field.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The nurse leans across the sterile field.” – Reaching over field risks contamination\n• “The nurse turning their back on the sterile field.” – Unobserved field is considered contaminated\n• “Opening a sterile package and then dropping the item onto a non-sterile field.” – Nonsterile surface contaminates item\n• “The nurse opens a sterile package and drops a sterile item in the one inch border aroun…” – Outer inch is contaminated\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":296,"t":10,"s":23,"q":"A patient who takes a diuretic and a b-blocker to control blood pressure is scheduled for breast reconstruction surgery. Which patient information is most important to communicate to the health care provider before surgery?","o":["Hematocrit 36%","Pulse rate 58 beats/minute","Blood pressure 144/82","Serum potassium 3.2 mEq/L"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Serum potassium 3.2 mEq/L\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Serum potassium 3.2 mEq/L” – Low potassium raises perioperative arrhythmia risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hematocrit 36%” – Mildly low or near-normal value\n• “Pulse rate 58 beats/minute” – Beta blockers may explain mild bradycardia\n• “Blood pressure 144/82” – Mild elevation is less urgent\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 7: “Hypokalemia”\nThiazide diuretics cause hypokalemia; the surgical-risk reasoning itself is not in your materials."},{"id":297,"t":5,"s":23,"q":"A client with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) is scheduled for hemodialysis in one hour.  The nurse should notify the primary health care provider that the client has a","o":["Sodium 145 mEq/L","Creatinine 3.5 mg/dl","Potassium 6.8 mEq/L","BUN of 60 mg/dl"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Potassium 6.8 mEq/L\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Potassium 6.8 mEq/L” – Severe hyperkalemia threatens cardiac rhythm\n\nNot correct:\n• “Sodium 145 mEq/L” – At upper end of normal\n• “Creatinine 3.5 mg/dl” – Expected elevation in ESRD\n• “BUN of 60 mg/dl” – Expected elevation in ESRD\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 7 - Renal, slide 7: “Hyperkalemia”\nThe slide lists hyperkalemia for acute kidney injury; ESRD-specific lab values are not covered."},{"id":298,"t":7,"s":23,"q":"As the nurse you are providing care for a client who is 2 days post-op and has still not had a bowel movement nor have they passed flatus.  On assessment, you note the abdomen is distended and no bowel sounds are noted in the four quadrants. You notify the MD. What non-invasive nursing interventions can you perform without a MD order?","o":["Encourage at least 3000 mL of fluids per day","Encourage ambulation, maintain NPO status, and monitor intake and output","Administer IV fluids","Insert a nasogastric attached to intermittent suction"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Encourage ambulation, maintain NPO status, and monitor intake and output\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Encourage ambulation, maintain NPO status, and monitor intake and output” – NPO, mobility and I&O are supportive measures\n\nNot correct:\n• “Encourage at least 3000 mL of fluids per day” – Oral fluids inappropriate with suspected ileus\n• “Administer IV fluids” – IV fluids require an order\n• “Insert a nasogastric attached to intermittent suction” – NG decompression requires an order\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":299,"t":4,"s":23,"q":"A nurse is developing a care plan for a patient who is at risk for developing pneumonia after surgery. Which of the following are appropriate nursing interventions? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Encourage early ambulation and patient to eat meals in beside chair","Encourage patient intake of 3000 ml/day of fluids if not contraindicated","Encourage patient to use the incentive spirometer device 10 times every 1-2 hours while awake","Repositioning every 3-4 hours"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Encourage early ambulation and patient to eat meals in beside chair\n• Encourage patient intake of 3000 ml/day of fluids if not contraindicated\n• Encourage patient to use the incentive spirometer device 10 times every 1-2 hours while awake\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Encourage early ambulation and patient to eat meals in beside chair” – Mobilization improves ventilation\n• “Encourage patient intake of 3000 ml/day of fluids if not contraindicated” – Hydration helps clear secretions if allowed\n• “Encourage patient to use the incentive spirometer device 10 times every 1-2 hours while…” – Incentive spirometry encourages lung expansion\n\nNot correct:\n• “Repositioning every 3-4 hours” – Reposition more frequently as appropriate\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 4: “Increase fluids 2500-3000 ml/day”\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 4: “Encourage client to turn, cough, and deep breath, splint chest wall, use incentive spirometer”"},{"id":300,"t":4,"s":23,"q":"A patient who will begin oxygen therapy has a history of sinus disorders. This patient would benefit most from which oxygen setup?","o":["High oxygen flow rate","A humidifier","A nasal cannula","A Venturi mask"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A humidifier\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A humidifier” – Adds moisture to inspired gas\n\nNot correct:\n• “High oxygen flow rate” – May increase mucosal drying\n• “A nasal cannula” – Cannula alone does not humidify\n• “A Venturi mask” – Precise oxygen delivery does not address dryness\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":301,"t":4,"s":23,"q":"The nurse loosens mucus plugs by using percussion on a patient over the area of the","o":["thorax","spine between the scapulae","sternum","midaxillary line on the rib cage"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• thorax\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “thorax” – Percussion targets lung fields\n\nNot correct:\n• “spine between the scapulae” – Avoid direct percussion over spine\n• “sternum” – Avoid sternum\n• “midaxillary line on the rib cage” – Specific location depends on affected lung segment\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 16: “Percussion – clapping the chest wall over the lung segment to be drained to force secretions loose from the bronchioles”"},{"id":302,"t":6,"s":23,"q":"Which of the following nursing diagnosis would the nurse expect to assign for a client with diabetes insipidus ?","o":["excess fluid volume related to intake greater than output.","risk for impaired skin integrity related to generalized edema.","sleep pattern disturbance related to frequent waking to void.","impaired gas exchange related to fluid retention in lungs."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• sleep pattern disturbance related to frequent waking to void.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “sleep pattern disturbance related to frequent waking to void.” – Nocturia interrupts sleep\n\nNot correct:\n• “excess fluid volume related to intake greater than output.” – DI causes water loss, not retention\n• “risk for impaired skin integrity related to generalized edema.” – Generalized edema is not typical\n• “impaired gas exchange related to fluid retention in lungs.” – Pulmonary fluid retention is not characteristic\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 9 - Endocrine, slide 6: “Polyuria, polydipsia, weakness,    anorectic, loses weight”"},{"id":303,"t":6,"s":23,"q":"Which assessment finding for a client admitted with Graves’ disease requires the most rapid intervention by the nurse?","o":["Heart rate 126 beats/minute","Blood pressure 166/98 mm Hg","Abdominal pain with nausea","Temperature 103.8° F (40.4° C)"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Temperature 103.8° F (40.4° C)\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Temperature 103.8° F (40.4° C)” – High fever suggests thyroid storm\n\nNot correct:\n• “Heart rate 126 beats/minute” – Tachycardia can occur in hyperthyroidism\n• “Blood pressure 166/98 mm Hg” – Hypertension can occur in hyperthyroidism\n• “Abdominal pain with nausea” – GI symptoms may occur but fever is more alarming\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":304,"t":5,"s":23,"q":"The nurse is caring for a 63-year-old with Cushing’s who is scheduled for a computed tomography (CT) scan with contrast. Which information about the client is most important to discuss with the health care provider before the test?","o":["Recent history of paralytic ilieus","History of renal insufficiency","Complains of chronic headache","Blood glucose level of 224 mg/dL"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• History of renal insufficiency\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “History of renal insufficiency” – Renal insufficiency increases contrast-related risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “Recent history of paralytic ilieus” – Less directly related to contrast safety\n• “Complains of chronic headache” – Not the main contrast risk\n• “Blood glucose level of 224 mg/dL” – Hyperglycemia needs monitoring but renal status is more relevant\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":305,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"The nurse in the inpatient psychiatric unit is caring for a 26-year-old client who has bipolar I disorder and is receiving prescribed carbamazepine. The nurse should first _____.","o":["question the client about suicidal thoughts","review the client’s serum carbamazepine level","weigh the client","check the client for urinary retention"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• question the client about suicidal thoughts\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “question the client about suicidal thoughts” – Suicide assessment addresses immediate safety\n\nNot correct:\n• “review the client’s serum carbamazepine level” – Drug levels are important but secondary to safety\n• “weigh the client” – Weight is routine monitoring\n• “check the client for urinary retention” – Retention assessment is not the priority\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":306,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"An emergency code was called after a patient pulled a knife from a pocket and threatened, “I will kill anyone who tries to get near me.” The patient was safely disarmed and placed in seclusion. Justification for use of seclusion was that the patient","o":["was threatening to others.","presented a clear and present danger to others.","presented an undeniable escape risk.","was experiencing psychosis."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• presented a clear and present danger to others.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “presented a clear and present danger to others.” – Immediate danger supports last-resort seclusion\n\nNot correct:\n• “was threatening to others.” – Threats alone do not always justify seclusion\n• “presented an undeniable escape risk.” – Escape risk alone is insufficient\n• “was experiencing psychosis.” – Psychosis alone is insufficient\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":307,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"An emergency department nurse realizes that the spouse of a patient is becoming increasingly irritable while waiting. Which intervention should the nurse use to prevent further escalation of the spouse’s anger?","o":["Offer the waiting spouse a cup of coffee.","Periodically provide an update and progress report on the patient.","Explain that the patient’s condition is not life threatening.","Suggest that the spouse return home until the patient’s treatment is complete."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Periodically provide an update and progress report on the patient.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Periodically provide an update and progress report on the patient.” – Updates reduce uncertainty and frustration\n\nNot correct:\n• “Offer the waiting spouse a cup of coffee.” – Refreshments do not address uncertainty\n• “Explain that the patient’s condition is not life threatening.” – Can minimize the spouse’s distress\n• “Suggest that the spouse return home until the patient’s treatment is complete.” – May increase separation anxiety\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":308,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient undergoing diagnostic tests says, “Nothing is wrong with me except a stubborn chest cold.” The spouse reports the patient smokes, coughs daily, lost 15 pounds, and is easily fatigued. Which defense mechanism is the patient using?","o":["Projection","Denial","Displacement","Regression"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Denial\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Denial” – Minimizes threatening evidence\n\nNot correct:\n• “Projection” – Projection attributes own feelings to others\n• “Displacement” – Displacement redirects emotions\n• “Regression” – Regression returns to earlier behavior\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":309,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A nurse assesses a patient with a tentative diagnosis of generalized anxiety disorder. Which question would be most appropriate for the nurse to ask?","o":["“Do you find it difficult to control your worrying?”","“Do you feel especially uncomfortable in social situations involving people?”","“Have you been a victim of a crime or seen someone badly injured or killed?”","“Do you repeatedly do certain things over and over again?”"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Do you find it difficult to control your worrying?”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Do you find it difficult to control your worrying?”” – GAD involves difficult-to-control worry\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Do you feel especially uncomfortable in social situations involving people?”” – Screens for social anxiety\n• ““Have you been a victim of a crime or seen someone badly injured or killed?”” – Screens for trauma exposure\n• ““Do you repeatedly do certain things over and over again?”” – Screens for compulsions\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":310,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A nurse assesses a patient who takes lithium. Which findings demonstrate evidence of complications?","o":["Ascites, dyspnea, and edema","Pharyngitis, mydriasis, and dystonia","Alopecia, purpura, and drowsiness","Diaphoresis, weakness, and nausea"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Diaphoresis, weakness, and nausea\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Diaphoresis, weakness, and nausea” – GI symptoms and weakness suggest toxicity\n\nNot correct:\n• “Ascites, dyspnea, and edema” – More typical of fluid overload\n• “Pharyngitis, mydriasis, and dystonia” – Not the typical lithium toxicity pattern\n• “Alopecia, purpura, and drowsiness” – Not the classic toxicity cluster\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":311,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A person was online continuously for over 24 hours, posting rhymes on official government websites and inviting politicians to join social networks. The person has not slept or eaten for 3 days. What features of mania are evident?","o":["Increased muscle tension and anxiety","Poor judgment and hyperactivity","Cognitive deficits and paranoia","Vegetative signs and poor grooming"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Poor judgment and hyperactivity\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Poor judgment and hyperactivity” – Prolonged activity and risky posting indicate mania\n\nNot correct:\n• “Increased muscle tension and anxiety” – Does not capture impulsive activity\n• “Cognitive deficits and paranoia” – Paranoia is not established by the stem\n• “Vegetative signs and poor grooming” – More suggestive of depressive symptoms\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":312,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"What feelings are most commonly experienced by nurses working with abusive families?","o":["Unconcern for the victim and dislike for the abuser","Outrage toward the victim and discouragement regarding the abuser","Vulnerability for self and empathy with the abuser","Helplessness regarding the victim and anger toward the abuser"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Helplessness regarding the victim and anger toward the abuser\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Helplessness regarding the victim and anger toward the abuser” – Source identifies helplessness and anger\n\nNot correct:\n• “Unconcern for the victim and dislike for the abuser” – Victims usually elicit concern\n• “Outrage toward the victim and discouragement regarding the abuser” – Anger toward victims is not the expected response\n• “Vulnerability for self and empathy with the abuser” – Not the common pattern identified by source\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":313,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"An adult tells the nurse, “My partner abuses me when I make mistakes, but I always get an apology and a gift afterward. I’ve considered leaving but haven’t been able to bring myself to actually do it.” Which phase in the cycle of violence prevents this adult from leaving?","o":["Tension-building","Honeymoon","Acute battering","Stabilization"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Honeymoon\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Honeymoon” – Apologies and gifts encourage hope\n\nNot correct:\n• “Tension-building” – Tension precedes violence\n• “Acute battering” – Acute phase involves actual violence\n• “Stabilization” – Not the named phase in this model\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":314,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient is seen in the clinic for superficial cuts on both wrists. Initially the patient paces and sobs but after a few minutes, the patient is calmer. The nurse attempts to determine the patient’s perception of the precipitating event by asking:","o":["“What was happening when you started feeling this way?”","“Tell me why you were crying.”","“How can I help you feel more comfortable?”","“How did your wrists get injured?”"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “What was happening when you started feeling this way?”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““What was happening when you started feeling this way?”” – Identifies the trigger without judgment\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Tell me why you were crying.”” – Why questions may sound accusatory\n• ““How can I help you feel more comfortable?”” – Focuses on comfort rather than the trigger\n• ““How did your wrists get injured?”” – Focuses narrowly on injury mechanism\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":315,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"After celebrating the fortieth birthday, an individual becomes concerned with the loss of youthful appearance. What type of crisis has occurred?","o":["Situational","Reactive","Maturational","Body image"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Maturational\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Maturational” – Age transition creates developmental stress\n\nNot correct:\n• “Situational” – Usually results from an unexpected external event\n• “Reactive” – Not the relevant crisis category\n• “Body image” – Describes concern but not the crisis type\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":316,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient being treated for depression has taken sertraline daily for a year. The patient calls the clinic nurse and says, “I stopped taking my antidepressant 2 days ago. Now I am having nausea, nervous feelings, and I can’t sleep.” The nurse will advise the patient to:","o":["“Take a dose of your antidepressant now and come to the clinic to see the health care provider.”","“Resume taking your antidepressants for 2 more weeks and then discontinue them again.”","“Do not to be alarmed. Take two aspirin and drink plenty of fluids.”","“Go to the nearest emergency department immediately.”"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Take a dose of your antidepressant now and come to the clinic to see the health care provider.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Take a dose of your antidepressant now and come to the clinic to see the health care p…” – Source expects resumption and clinical review; confirm with prescriber\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Resume taking your antidepressants for 2 more weeks and then discontinue them again.”” – Repeats abrupt discontinuation without a plan\n• ““Do not to be alarmed. Take two aspirin and drink plenty of fluids.”” – Minimizes discontinuation symptoms\n• ““Go to the nearest emergency department immediately.”” – Not necessarily an emergency absent severe symptoms\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":317,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient diagnosed with depressive disorder begins selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) antidepressant therapy. The nurse should provide information to the patient and family about","o":["restricting sodium intake to 1 gram daily.","reporting increased suicidal thoughts.","minimizing exposure to bright sunlight.","maintaining a tyramine-free diet."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• reporting increased suicidal thoughts.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “reporting increased suicidal thoughts.” – Early treatment requires monitoring suicide risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “restricting sodium intake to 1 gram daily.” – Not a routine SSRI restriction\n• “minimizing exposure to bright sunlight.” – Not the central SSRI teaching\n• “maintaining a tyramine-free diet.” – Tyramine restriction applies to MAOIs\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":318,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"Which medication should the nurse be concerned about for a client taking fluoxetine?","o":["Multivitamins","Diuretics","Anticoagulants","Acetaminophen"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Anticoagulants\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Anticoagulants” – Combined therapy increases bleeding risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “Multivitamins” – No comparable routine major interaction\n• “Diuretics” – Can increase hyponatremia risk, but source prioritizes bleeding\n• “Acetaminophen” – Does not have the same antiplatelet interaction\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":319,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A widower tells friends, “I am taking my neighbor out for dinner. It’s time for me to be more sociable again.” Considering the stages of grief described by Kübler-Ross, which stage is evident?","o":["Acceptance","Denial","Anger","Depression"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Acceptance\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Acceptance” – Reengages with life after loss\n\nNot correct:\n• “Denial” – Does not deny the death\n• “Anger” – No anger expressed\n• “Depression” – Shows renewed interest rather than withdrawal\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 3 - Psychosocial Integrity, slide 19: “Acceptance – The client accepts what is happening and plans for the future.”"},{"id":320,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A wife received news that her husband died of heart failure and called her family to come to the hospital. She angrily tells the nurse who cared for him, “He would still be alive if you had given him your undivided attention.” Select the nurse’s most therapeutic action.","o":["Hold the spouse’s hand in silence until the family arrives.","Say, “Your husband’s heart was so severely damaged that it could no longer pump.”","Say, “I understand you are feeling upset. I will stay with you until your family comes.”","Say, “I will call my supervisor to discuss this matter with you.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Say, “I understand you are feeling upset. I will stay with you until your family comes.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Say, “I understand you are feeling upset. I will stay with you until your family comes.”” – Validates emotion and offers presence\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hold the spouse’s hand in silence until the family arrives.” – Silent touch may be supportive but does not explicitly acknowledge feelings\n• “Say, “Your husband’s heart was so severely damaged that it could no longer pump.”” – Defensive explanation can minimize distress\n• “Say, “I will call my supervisor to discuss this matter with you.”” – Shifts interaction away from emotional support\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":321,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"Which of the following would be assessed as a negative symptom of schizophrenia?","o":["Anhedonia","Agitation","Hostility","Hallucinations"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Anhedonia\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Anhedonia” – Loss of pleasure is diminished normal function\n\nNot correct:\n• “Agitation” – Behavioral activation, not a negative symptom\n• “Hostility” – Not a core negative symptom\n• “Hallucinations” – Perceptual experience is a positive symptom\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":322,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A client diagnosed with paranoid schizophrenia tells the nurse, “I have to get away. The volmers are coming to execute me.” The term “volmers” can be assessed as what?","o":["a neologism.","clang association.","a delusion.","blocking."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• a neologism.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “a neologism.” – Invented word\n\nNot correct:\n• “clang association.” – Rhyming or sound-based word choice\n• “a delusion.” – Fixed false belief, not the coined word itself\n• “blocking.” – Sudden interruption of thought\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":323,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient who had been experiencing significant stress learned to use progressive muscle relaxation and deep breathing exercises. When the patient returns to the clinic 2 weeks later, which finding most clearly shows the patient is coping more effectively with stress?","o":["The patient reports, “I feel better, and that things are not bothering me as much.”","The patient reports, “I spend more time napping or sitting quietly at home.”","The patient’s systolic blood pressure has changed from the 140s to the 120s mm Hg.","The patient’s weight decreased by 3 pounds."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The patient’s systolic blood pressure has changed from the 140s to the 120s mm Hg.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The patient’s systolic blood pressure has changed from the 140s to the 120s mm Hg.” – Lower BP is objective evidence of reduced physiologic stress\n\nNot correct:\n• “The patient reports, “I feel better, and that things are not bothering me as much.”” – Subjective improvement is useful but less objective\n• “The patient reports, “I spend more time napping or sitting quietly at home.”” – Napping may reflect avoidance\n• “The patient’s weight decreased by 3 pounds.” – Weight loss is nonspecific\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":324,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"The nurse wishes to use guided imagery to help a patient relax. Which comments would be appropriate to include in the guided imagery script? (Select all that apply.)","o":["“You will feel better as work calms down, as your boss becomes more understanding …”","“You have grown calm, your mind is still, there is nothing to disturb your well-being …”","“You are alone on a beach, the sun is warm, and you hear only the sound of the surf …”","“You have taken control, nothing can hurt you now. Everything is going your way …”","“With each breath, you feel calmer, more relaxed, almost as if you are floating …”","“Imagine others treating you the way they should, the way you want to be treated …”"],"a":[1,2,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• “You have grown calm, your mind is still, there is nothing to disturb your well-being …”\n• “You are alone on a beach, the sun is warm, and you hear only the sound of the surf …”\n• “With each breath, you feel calmer, more relaxed, almost as if you are floating …”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““You have grown calm, your mind is still, there is nothing to disturb your well-being …”” – Promotes calm mental focus\n• ““You are alone on a beach, the sun is warm, and you hear only the sound of the surf …”” – Engages peaceful sensory imagination\n• ““With each breath, you feel calmer, more relaxed, almost as if you are floating …”” – Links breathing to relaxation\n\nNot correct:\n• ““You will feel better as work calms down, as your boss becomes more understanding …”” – Depends on other people changing\n• ““You have taken control, nothing can hurt you now. Everything is going your way …”” – Promises unrealistic immunity from harm\n• ““Imagine others treating you the way they should, the way you want to be treated …”” – Depends on others behaving differently\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":325,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"The nurse is caring for a patient on valproic acid for seizures.  Which statement made by the client requires follow-up from the nurse?","o":["“I have a cold and will stop by the drugstore on my way home for something to help.”","“I am still using birth control.”","“I take my medicine at the same time every day.”","“I do not drink alcohol anymore.”"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I have a cold and will stop by the drugstore on my way home for something to help.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I have a cold and will stop by the drugstore on my way home for something to help.”” – OTC products can interact or increase sedation\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I am still using birth control.”” – Contraception is important given fetal risk\n• ““I take my medicine at the same time every day.”” – Consistent dosing supports seizure control\n• ““I do not drink alcohol anymore.”” – Avoiding alcohol reduces adverse effects\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":326,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient has smoked two packs of cigarettes daily for many years. When the patient tries to reduce smoking, anxiety, craving, poor concentration, and headache occur. This scenario describes","o":["cross-tolerance.","substance intoxication.","substance abuse.","substance addiction."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• substance addiction.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “substance addiction.” – Withdrawal indicates dependence; source calls this addiction\n\nNot correct:\n• “cross-tolerance.” – Tolerance across similar substances\n• “substance intoxication.” – Symptoms occur after reduction, not intoxication\n• “substance abuse.” – Does not specifically describe physiologic dependence\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":327,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"During the third week of treatment, the spouse of a patient in a rehabilitation program for substance abuse says, “After this treatment program, I think everything will be all right.” Which remark by the nurse will be most helpful to the spouse?","o":["“It is good that you are supportive of your spouse’s sobriety and want to help maintain it.”","“Addiction is a lifelong disease of self-destruction. You will need to observe your spouse’s behavior carefully.”","“It will be important for you to structure life to avoid as much stress as you can and provide social protection.”","“While sobriety solves some problems, new ones may emerge as one adjusts to living without drugs and alcohol.”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “While sobriety solves some problems, new ones may emerge as one adjusts to living without drugs and alcohol.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““While sobriety solves some problems, new ones may emerge as one adjusts to living with…” – Prepares spouse for ongoing adjustment\n\nNot correct:\n• ““It is good that you are supportive of your spouse’s sobriety and want to help maintain…” – Affirms support without correcting expectations\n• ““Addiction is a lifelong disease of self-destruction. You will need to observe your spo…” – Stigmatizing and promotes surveillance\n• ““It will be important for you to structure life to avoid as much stress as you can and …” – Avoiding all stress is unrealistic\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":328,"t":13,"s":24,"q":"A patient has been taking aripiprazole for 2 weeks. The client reports drowsiness and headache. What will the nurse do?","o":["Counsel the patient to request changing to something else.","Suggest that these may be signs of agranulocytosis.","Explain to the patient that these are common side effects of the medication.","Suggest that the patient have serum glucose testing."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Explain to the patient that these are common side effects of the medication.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Explain to the patient that these are common side effects of the medication.” – Drowsiness and headache are recognized effects\n\nNot correct:\n• “Counsel the patient to request changing to something else.” – Common symptoms do not automatically require changing drugs\n• “Suggest that these may be signs of agranulocytosis.” – Agranulocytosis typically manifests with infection signs\n• “Suggest that the patient have serum glucose testing.” – Glucose monitoring is appropriate but does not explain these common symptoms alone\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":329,"t":10,"s":24,"q":"A nurse is taking care of a client prescribed antipsychotic medication. Which nursing intervention is appropriate for the nurse to do?","o":["Teach the patient to get up slowly and dangle on the side of the bed before standing.","Leave the patient on the bedside table so the patient can take when ready.","Do not dilute liquid preparations of the drug.","Advise the patient to take on an empty stomach."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Teach the patient to get up slowly and dangle on the side of the bed before standing.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Teach the patient to get up slowly and dangle on the side of the bed before standing.” – Reduces orthostatic hypotension and fall risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “Leave the patient on the bedside table so the patient can take when ready.” – Medication administration should be observed as appropriate\n• “Do not dilute liquid preparations of the drug.” – Dilution depends on the formulation\n• “Advise the patient to take on an empty stomach.” – Food instructions depend on the drug\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":330,"t":14,"s":25,"q":"A 45-year-old female client arrived at the outpatient clinic for an annual physical examination. Which of the following would be most advisable for the nurse to continue to monitor?","o":["Nutrition status.","Peripheral vision.","Bone composition.","Urinary incontinence."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Peripheral vision.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Peripheral vision.” – Age-related visual changes warrant assessment\n\nNot correct:\n• “Nutrition status.” – General screening, not the source priority\n• “Bone composition.” – Bone loss matters but is not the keyed focus\n• “Urinary incontinence.” – Not an expected universal finding\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":331,"t":14,"s":25,"q":"A 25-year-old client arrived at the outpatient clinic for an annual physical examination. Which of the following findings is consistent with this client’s age?","o":["Mastering Erickson’s integrity vs. despair.","Reviewing many regrets over the lifespan.","Searching for meaning in life.","Forming close relationships."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Forming close relationships.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Forming close relationships.” – Intimacy versus isolation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Mastering Erickson’s integrity vs. despair.” – Older-adult developmental task\n• “Reviewing many regrets over the lifespan.” – More typical of late-life review\n• “Searching for meaning in life.” – Not the defining young-adult task\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":332,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"A nurse is educating a client on the effects of immobility. Which of the following, if stated by the patient, indicates they needs additional teaching?","o":["I may experience more frequent UTI’s","I may experience pooling of respiratory secretions","I may develop diarrhea which can lead to skin breakdown","I may experience stiff joints or contractures"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• I may develop diarrhea which can lead to skin breakdown\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “I may develop diarrhea which can lead to skin breakdown” – Constipation is more typical\n\nNot correct:\n• “I may experience more frequent UTI’s” – Urinary stasis increases infection risk\n• “I may experience pooling of respiratory secretions” – Reduced mobility impairs secretion clearance\n• “I may experience stiff joints or contractures” – Disuse promotes contractures\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 4 - Basic Care & Comfort, slide 42: “Slow peristalsis”\nSlow peristalsis (constipation), not diarrhea, is listed as an effect of immobility."},{"id":333,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"A nurse is providing nail care for a non-diabetic client. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Clean under the nail with an orange stick","File the nails in a rounded shape.","Push the cuticles back with a metal nail file.","Trim the nails at the lateral corners."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Clean under the nail with an orange stick\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Clean under the nail with an orange stick” – Gently removes debris\n\nNot correct:\n• “File the nails in a rounded shape.” – Rounded toenail edges risk ingrowth\n• “Push the cuticles back with a metal nail file.” – Can injure cuticles\n• “Trim the nails at the lateral corners.” – Cutting corners risks ingrowth\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":334,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"A nurse is changing a client's bed linens. Which action, if observed, requires follow up by the preceptor?","o":["The nurse puts on gloves and a gown before removing bed sheets.","The nurse makes sure the call-light is in reach before leaving the room.","The nurse puts all 4 side rails up prior to leaving the room.","The nurse places the draw under the client’s hips for transfer."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The nurse puts all 4 side rails up prior to leaving the room.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The nurse puts all 4 side rails up prior to leaving the room.” – May constitute a restraint\n\nNot correct:\n• “The nurse puts on gloves and a gown before removing bed sheets.” – PPE protects against anticipated contamination\n• “The nurse makes sure the call-light is in reach before leaving the room.” – Maintains access to assistance\n• “The nurse places the draw under the client’s hips for transfer.” – Draw sheet aids repositioning\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 4 - Basic Care & Comfort, slide 62: “Raise 2 side rails up”\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":335,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"A nurse is assessing a group of clients for those at risk for falls. Which of the following clients is most at risk?","o":["The 30 y.o. male client receiving IV narcotics every 2hrs.","The 80 y.o. client who had a stroke with left sided weakness and urinary frequency.","The 25 y.o. client who had a spinal fusion who is completely reliant on staff to turn and reposition.","The 72 y.o. client taking a beta blocker that is causing orthostatic hypotension."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The 80 y.o. client who had a stroke with left sided weakness and urinary frequency.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The 80 y.o. client who had a stroke with left sided weakness and urinary frequency.” – Weakness, age and urgency compound risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “The 30 y.o. male client receiving IV narcotics every 2hrs.” – Sedation increases risk, but fewer listed factors\n• “The 25 y.o. client who had a spinal fusion who is completely reliant on staff to turn a…” – Dependent mobility limits independent attempts\n• “The 72 y.o. client taking a beta blocker that is causing orthostatic hypotension.” – Orthostasis is a risk, but fewer listed factors\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":336,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"After instruction, which actions by a patient who can bear weight on both feet but has left-side weakness indicates an understand of the proper use of a walker?","o":["The patient steps forward with the right leg and then moves the walker forward.","The patient moves the walker and the left leg forward at the same time, then takes a step with the right leg forward.","The patient steps forward with the left leg and then moves the walker forward.","The patient moves the walker and the right leg forward at the same time, then takes a step with the left leg forward."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The patient moves the walker and the left leg forward at the same time, then takes a step with the right leg forward.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The patient moves the walker and the left leg forward at the same time, then takes a st…” – Supports weaker leg before strong leg\n\nNot correct:\n• “The patient steps forward with the right leg and then moves the walker forward.” – Walker should lead\n• “The patient steps forward with the left leg and then moves the walker forward.” – Weak leg advances without support\n• “The patient moves the walker and the right leg forward at the same time, then takes a s…” – Incorrect leg sequence\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":337,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"While using crutches the client moves both crutches forward and then moves both legs forward past the placement of the crutches. This is known as the","o":["Two-point gait","Swing-through-gait","Three-point gait","Swing-to-gait"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Swing-through-gait\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Swing-through-gait” – Both legs pass crutches\n\nNot correct:\n• “Two-point gait” – Opposite crutch and foot alternate\n• “Three-point gait” – Affected leg and crutches advance then strong leg\n• “Swing-to-gait” – Legs advance only to crutches\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":338,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"There is an order to administer a cleansing enema to an adult patient before bowel surgery. The nurse will fill the enema bag with how many milliliters of fluid for this procedure?","o":["500 to 1000 mL","50 to 150 mL","200 to 300 mL","300 to 500 mL"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• 500 to 1000 mL\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “500 to 1000 mL” – Standard adult large-volume enema\n\nNot correct:\n• “50 to 150 mL” – Small-volume preparation\n• “200 to 300 mL” – Below typical adult large-volume range\n• “300 to 500 mL” – Below usual adult range except upper endpoint\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":339,"t":7,"s":25,"q":"The nurse has performed a health assessment of a client who has had a bowel movement which is green in color.  The nurse is aware that a green bowel movement is often an indication of","o":["gallbladder infection.","liver infection.","pancreatic infection.","intestinal infection."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• intestinal infection.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “intestinal infection.” – Rapid transit may cause green stool; nonspecific\n\nNot correct:\n• “gallbladder infection.” – More often associated with impaired bile flow\n• “liver infection.” – May cause pale stools with cholestasis\n• “pancreatic infection.” – Malabsorption may cause fatty stools\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":340,"t":3,"s":25,"q":"The nurse is caring for a patient who will begin taking atenolol. What information will the nurse include when teaching the patient about taking this medication?","o":["The patient must rise slowly from a chair or bed.","Use NSAIDs as needed for mild to moderate pain.","The medication is safe to take during pregnancy.","The drug must be taken twice daily."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The patient must rise slowly from a chair or bed.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The patient must rise slowly from a chair or bed.” – Reduces orthostatic symptoms\n\nNot correct:\n• “Use NSAIDs as needed for mild to moderate pain.” – NSAIDs may blunt BP control\n• “The medication is safe to take during pregnancy.” – Potential fetal risk; requires review\n• “The drug must be taken twice daily.” – Frequency depends on prescription\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 5: “Dangle from supine to standing”\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":341,"t":10,"s":25,"q":"The nurse administers subcutaneous epinephrine to a patient who is experiencing an anaphylactic reaction. The nurse should expect to monitor the patient for which symptom?","o":["Bradycardia","Decreased urinary output","Nausea and vomiting","Hypotension"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Decreased urinary output\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Decreased urinary output” – Vasoconstriction may decrease renal perfusion\n\nNot correct:\n• “Bradycardia” – Epinephrine usually increases heart rate\n• “Nausea and vomiting” – Possible but not the keyed perfusion concern\n• “Hypotension” – Usually raises blood pressure\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":342,"t":2,"s":25,"q":"The nursing student learns that which of the following is the guideline for nursing documentation?","o":["HIPAA","Nursing process","Ethical standards","Code of Ethics"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Nursing process\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Nursing process” – Assessment through evaluation guides charting\n\nNot correct:\n• “HIPAA” – Governs privacy\n• “Ethical standards” – General professional principles\n• “Code of Ethics” – Ethical duties rather than charting framework\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":343,"t":2,"s":25,"q":"The impact of computerized provider order entry (CPOE) on the medical record has been which of the following?","o":["Increased legibility of orders","A reduction of duplicate orders","The need for more unit secretaries","Better billing practices."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Increased legibility of orders\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Increased legibility of orders” – Eliminates handwritten illegibility\n\nNot correct:\n• “A reduction of duplicate orders” – Possible benefit but not guaranteed\n• “The need for more unit secretaries” – Not an inherent benefit\n• “Better billing practices.” – Not the main clinical safety benefit\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Management of Care, slide 24: “Eliminates handwritten orders”"},{"id":344,"t":14,"s":25,"q":"Using the concept of “Maslow’s Hierarchy”, the nurse knows to address which clients' needs first?","o":["Comfort, sleep, and warmth","Issues with elimination","Inadequate nutrition","Oxygen, Cardiac Function"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Oxygen, Cardiac Function\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Oxygen, Cardiac Function” – Breathing and circulation sustain life\n\nNot correct:\n• “Comfort, sleep, and warmth” – Lower priority than ABCs\n• “Issues with elimination” – Address after immediate ABC threats\n• “Inadequate nutrition” – Address after immediate ABC threats\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":345,"t":14,"s":25,"q":"The nurse is taking care of 4 clients on a medical-surgical unit. Which of the following Maslow’s “areas of need” will the nurse address first?","o":["The client who is hyperventilating about having had an incontinent episode.","The client with concerns and fears about their procedure in the morning.","The client who is hungry and requesting a second tray.","The client that is being discharged this morning who has medication questions."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The client who is hyperventilating about having had an incontinent episode.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The client who is hyperventilating about having had an incontinent episode.” – Assess acute breathing change first\n\nNot correct:\n• “The client with concerns and fears about their procedure in the morning.” – Anxiety teaching can follow\n• “The client who is hungry and requesting a second tray.” – Food request can wait\n• “The client that is being discharged this morning who has medication questions.” – Discharge questions can wait\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":346,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"Maintaining the wound suction of a drain improves the healing process by draining excess exudate that might otherwise interfere with the formation of granulation tissue.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• True\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “True” – Removes excess wound fluid\n\nNot correct:\n• “False” – Denies the drainage function\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Physiological Adaptation, slide 45: “Promotes healing from inside wound”\nYour slide says drains promote healing from inside the wound; the exudate and granulation wording is not stated."},{"id":347,"t":10,"s":25,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer bethanechol to a patient who is experiencing urinary retention. The nurse notes that the patient has a blood pressure of 90/60 mm Hg and a heart rate of 98 beats per minute. The nurse will perform which action?","o":["Hold the medication and notify the provider of the decreased blood pressure.","Administer the drug and monitor urine output.","Give the medication and notify the provider of the increased heart rate.","Administer the medication and monitor vital signs frequently."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Hold the medication and notify the provider of the decreased blood pressure.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Hold the medication and notify the provider of the decreased blood pressure.” – Low BP may worsen with bethanechol\n\nNot correct:\n• “Administer the drug and monitor urine output.” – Unsafe to give with hypotension\n• “Give the medication and notify the provider of the increased heart rate.” – Increased pulse does not justify giving\n• “Administer the medication and monitor vital signs frequently.” – Monitoring does not prevent worsening hypotension\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":348,"t":10,"s":25,"q":"The nurse is caring for a postoperative patient and notes that the patient received atropine sulfate preoperatively. Which assessment finding would prompt the nurse to notify the provider?","o":["Dry mouth","Heart rate of 78 beats per minute","Absent bowel sounds","Drowsiness"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Absent bowel sounds\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Absent bowel sounds” – Possible paralytic ileus\n\nNot correct:\n• “Dry mouth” – Common anticholinergic effect\n• “Heart rate of 78 beats per minute” – Within normal adult range\n• “Drowsiness” – Common CNS adverse effect\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 4 - Basic Care & Comfort, slide 32: “Monitor for S&S of Atropine overdose: flushing, dry mouth, tachycardia, urinary retention, blurred vision”\nYour slide lists dry mouth as an expected atropine effect, which rules out “Dry mouth”; absent bowel sounds is not listed there.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":349,"t":16,"s":25,"q":"The nurse is caring for a patient in the post-anesthesia care unit who has received a spinal anesthetic. Which action will the nurse perform?","o":["Have the patient lay flat for 6 to 8 hours after the surgery.","Elevate the head of the bed to a semi-Fowler’s position.","Ambulate the patient as soon as consciousness returns.","Turn the patient from side to side every 15 minutes."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Have the patient lay flat for 6 to 8 hours after the surgery.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Have the patient lay flat for 6 to 8 hours after the surgery.” – Older exam expectation, not routine current practice\n\nNot correct:\n• “Elevate the head of the bed to a semi-Fowler’s position.” – Position depends on clinical status\n• “Ambulate the patient as soon as consciousness returns.” – Wait for motor and sensory recovery\n• “Turn the patient from side to side every 15 minutes.” – No routine need for 15-minute turns\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 10."},{"id":350,"t":10,"s":25,"q":"A patient who has been taking a benzodiazepine as a sleep aid for several months wishes to stop taking the medication. The nurse will suggest that the patient taper the dose gradually to avoid which effect?","o":["Hypnotic rebound","Hangover","Withdrawal","Depression"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Withdrawal\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Withdrawal” – Taper prevents withdrawal\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hypnotic rebound” – Rebound insomnia is possible but narrower\n• “Hangover” – Residual sedation, not abrupt cessation syndrome\n• “Depression” – Not the primary rationale for taper\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 4 (B)."},{"id":351,"t":16,"s":26,"q":"The nurse needs to obtain a sputum specimen from a client. What should the nurse have the client do?","o":["Apply sterile gloves.","Rinse the mouth with mouthwash prior to the collection.","Cough to bring up secretions.","Clear the throat."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Cough to bring up secretions.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Cough to bring up secretions.” – Deep cough brings lower-airway sputum\n\nNot correct:\n• “Apply sterile gloves.” – Clean technique generally sufficient\n• “Rinse the mouth with mouthwash prior to the collection.” – May interfere with specimen quality\n• “Clear the throat.” – Collects saliva or upper-airway secretions\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 8: “Take several deep breaths and deep cough”\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 8: “Early am, do not brush teeth, rinse mouth w/ water only, no mouthwash or toothpaste”"},{"id":352,"t":1,"s":26,"q":"A nurse notes that a client’s bed has a frayed cord that plugs into the outlet. Which of the following is the best response?","o":["Plug the bed in to see if it’s still functional","Put a sign on the bed that says “do not use”, call engineering, and find a different bed","Ask engineering for electrical tape to cover the areas that are frayed, and then check to see if the bed is still functional.","Be sure it’s plugged into a grounded outlet only"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Put a sign on the bed that says “do not use”, call engineering, and find a different bed\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Put a sign on the bed that says “do not use”, call engineering, and find a different bed” – Isolate and report defective equipment\n\nNot correct:\n• “Plug the bed in to see if it’s still functional” – Electrical shock risk\n• “Ask engineering for electrical tape to cover the areas that are frayed, and then check …” – Improvised repairs are unsafe\n• “Be sure it’s plugged into a grounded outlet only” – Grounding does not fix damaged wiring\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":353,"t":16,"s":26,"q":"The nurse is selecting dressings for a clean abdominal incision that will be allowed to heal by secondary intention. What principles should the nurse use in choosing this dressing?","o":["Dressings should be simple as they will be changed at least every 4 hours.","The dressing should allow good air circulation through the wound.","Materials used in dressing this wound should keep the wound bed moist.","Absorbent material to wick exudates away and support drying should be used."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Materials used in dressing this wound should keep the wound bed moist.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Materials used in dressing this wound should keep the wound bed moist.” – Moisture supports granulation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Dressings should be simple as they will be changed at least every 4 hours.” – Frequency depends on wound and dressing\n• “The dressing should allow good air circulation through the wound.” – Open drying impairs healing\n• “Absorbent material to wick exudates away and support drying should be used.” – Excess drying damages healing tissue\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":354,"t":16,"s":26,"q":"Which action by the client below demonstrates that the client knows how to properly ambulate with a cane?","o":["The client holds the cane on the weak side and moves the cane and weak side forward together, and then moves the strong side.","The client holds the cane on the weak side and moves the cane forward, then moves the weak side, and then moves the strong side.","The patient should hold the cane on his or her stronger side and move the cane forward first, followed by the weaker leg and then the stronger leg.","The patient should hold the cane on his or her stronger side and move the cane and weaker leg forward at the same time."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The patient should hold the cane on his or her stronger side and move the cane forward first, followed by the weaker leg and then the stronger leg.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The patient should hold the cane on his or her stronger side and move the cane forward …” – Correct strong-side placement and sequence\n\nNot correct:\n• “The client holds the cane on the weak side and moves the cane and weak side forward tog…” – Cane belongs on stronger side\n• “The client holds the cane on the weak side and moves the cane forward, then moves the w…” – Cane belongs on stronger side\n• “The patient should hold the cane on his or her stronger side and move the cane and weak…” – Can also be valid; source item ambiguous\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":355,"t":7,"s":26,"q":"A nurse is monitoring bowel elimination of a patient who has a history of constipation. The nurse implements measures to assist with bowel elimination if the patient has not had a bowel movement within how many days?","o":["5","2","1","3"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• 3\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “3” – Common exam threshold; assess individual baseline\n\nNot correct:\n• “5” – Longer than customary test threshold\n• “2” – May be normal depending on baseline\n• “1” – May be normal depending on baseline\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":356,"t":10,"s":26,"q":"The nurse caring for a patient who is taking an adrenergic agent will expect which side effects? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Increase gastrointestinal motility","Bronchospasm","Dilated pupils","Vasodilation","Increased heart rate"],"a":[2,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Dilated pupils\n• Increased heart rate\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Dilated pupils” – Sympathetic pupillary dilation\n• “Increased heart rate” – Beta1 stimulation increases rate\n\nNot correct:\n• “Increase gastrointestinal motility” – Sympathetic activity reduces motility\n• “Bronchospasm” – Beta2 stimulation causes bronchodilation\n• “Vasodilation” – Alpha stimulation causes vasoconstriction; some beds dilate\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 18: “Side Effects:  tachycardia, palpitations, HTN, dyspnea, HA, dilated pupils, anxiety, nervousness, GI upset”"},{"id":357,"t":2,"s":26,"q":"Legal issues related to medical records include which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Medical record entries can be altered or erased to increase accuracy.","The nurse cannot make corrections at all to the record.","The medical record is the legal documentation of care provided to a patient.","In the event of litigation, the medical record is often the only available evidence.","Medical record documentation should be based strictly on facts, not opinions."],"a":[2,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• The medical record is the legal documentation of care provided to a patient.\n• In the event of litigation, the medical record is often the only available evidence.\n• Medical record documentation should be based strictly on facts, not opinions.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The medical record is the legal documentation of care provided to a patient.” – Legal record of care\n• “In the event of litigation, the medical record is often the only available evidence.” – Important evidence in litigation\n• “Medical record documentation should be based strictly on facts, not opinions.” – Objective documentation is essential\n\nNot correct:\n• “Medical record entries can be altered or erased to increase accuracy.” – Never erase original entries\n• “The nurse cannot make corrections at all to the record.” – Corrections with audit trail are permitted\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":358,"t":14,"s":26,"q":"The nurse is prioritizing a client’s problems based on Maslow’s hierarchy of needs. The nurse would address which problem first?","o":["Social isolation","Poor understanding","Fear of the unknown","Lack of sleep"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Lack of sleep\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Lack of sleep” – Sleep loss worsens physical coping\n\nNot correct:\n• “Social isolation” – Psychosocial factor rather than physical sleep deprivation\n• “Poor understanding” – Cognitive factor\n• “Fear of the unknown” – Psychological factor\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":359,"t":16,"s":26,"q":"A hemovac drain is also known as a wound drainage reservoir.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• True\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “True” – Hemovac is a closed suction wound reservoir\n\nNot correct:\n• “False” – Hemovac is indeed a wound reservoir\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":360,"t":10,"s":26,"q":"The nurse administers bethanechol to a patient to treat urinary retention. After 30 minutes, the patient voids 800 mL of urine and reports having a loose stool but no cramping or gastrointestinal pain. The patient’s blood pressure is 110/70 mm Hg. The nurse will perform which action?","o":["Notify the provider of bethanechol adverse effects.","Record the urine output and the blood pressure and continue to monitor.","Suggest another dose of bethanechol to the provider.","Request an order for intravenous atropine sulfate."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Record the urine output and the blood pressure and continue to monitor.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Record the urine output and the blood pressure and continue to monitor.” – Document therapeutic response and monitor\n\nNot correct:\n• “Notify the provider of bethanechol adverse effects.” – No concerning adverse reaction established\n• “Suggest another dose of bethanechol to the provider.” – Additional dose not indicated\n• “Request an order for intravenous atropine sulfate.” – Reserved for severe cholinergic toxicity\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":361,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"A 26-year-old primigravida client at 4 weeks gestation arrives at the outpatient clinic for an initial prenatal visit.\nWhich of the following should the nurse address first?","o":["Encourage the client to schedule a tour of the birth facility.","Describe the expected weight gain pattern for the client.","Acknowledge the client’s feelings about pregnancy.","Review the signs of imminent labor with the client."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Acknowledge the client’s feelings about pregnancy.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Acknowledge the client’s feelings about pregnancy.” – Assess early pregnancy adjustment\n\nNot correct:\n• “Encourage the client to schedule a tour of the birth facility.” – Can be addressed later\n• “Describe the expected weight gain pattern for the client.” – Nutrition teaching follows initial concerns\n• “Review the signs of imminent labor with the client.” – Labor teaching is premature\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (B), Unit 5 (C)."},{"id":362,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"The nurse is caring for a newborn infant who was born 1 hour ago and is resting quietly in the mother’s arms. Maintains head position in direction of mother’s voice. Vital signs: 97.7°F (36.5°C), apical P 158, RR 52. Occasional nasal flaring observed, coarse lung sounds per auscultation, and acrocyanosis to the feet. Mucus membranes and nailbeds are pink.\nComplete the following sentence with one of the following options.\nThe nurse should first attend to __________.","o":["Feeding tolerance.","Coarse lung sounds.","Apical pulse.","Feet discoloration."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Feeding tolerance.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Feeding tolerance.” – Source prioritizes coordinated sucking and breathing; assess persistent distress\n\nNot correct:\n• “Coarse lung sounds.” – May reflect transitional fluid; persistent abnormal sounds need assessment\n• “Apical pulse.” – 158 is within newborn range\n• “Feet discoloration.” – Acrocyanosis may be normal initially\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (B), Unit 5 (C)."},{"id":363,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"A nurse is caring for a hospitalized child who is receiving a continuous infusion of intravenous (IV) potassium for the treatment of dehydration. The nurse monitors the child closely and notifies the physician if which of the following is noted?","o":["Blood pressure (BP) unchanged from baseline","A decrease in urine output to 0.5 mL/kg/hr","Temperature of 100.8º F rectally","Weight increase of 0.5 kg"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A decrease in urine output to 0.5 mL/kg/hr\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A decrease in urine output to 0.5 mL/kg/hr” – Low output risks potassium retention\n\nNot correct:\n• “Blood pressure (BP) unchanged from baseline” – No new BP change\n• “Temperature of 100.8º F rectally” – Requires assessment but not potassium-specific\n• “Weight increase of 0.5 kg” – Assess trend; output is more directly relevant\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":364,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"What do parents of preschool children need to understand about discipline?","o":["The method of discipline should be consistent with that of the child’s peers.","Discipline should include positive reinforcement of desired behaviors.","Discipline should involve some physical restriction.","Both parents and the child should agree on the method of discipline."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Discipline should include positive reinforcement of desired behaviors.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Discipline should include positive reinforcement of desired behaviors.” – Reinforces desirable behavior\n\nNot correct:\n• “The method of discipline should be consistent with that of the child’s peers.” – Tailor discipline to child and family\n• “Discipline should involve some physical restriction.” – Physical restriction is not routine discipline\n• “Both parents and the child should agree on the method of discipline.” – Parents set consistent limits\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":365,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"The nurse teaches parents signs that a child might be being bullied or otherwise victimized. What signs does the nurse include in this teaching? (Select all that apply.)","o":["The child wants to be driven to school.","School performance improves.","Belongings frequently go missing or are damaged.","The child freely talks about his day.","Spends an inordinate amount of time in the nurse’s office"],"a":[0,2,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• The child wants to be driven to school.\n• Belongings frequently go missing or are damaged.\n• Spends an inordinate amount of time in the nurse’s office\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The child wants to be driven to school.” – Avoiding the trip may signal bullying\n• “Belongings frequently go missing or are damaged.” – Lost or damaged items can signal bullying\n• “Spends an inordinate amount of time in the nurse’s office” – Frequent somatic complaints may reflect bullying\n\nNot correct:\n• “School performance improves.” – Improvement is not a typical warning sign\n• “The child freely talks about his day.” – Open communication is reassuring\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":366,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"An immunocompromised child is in the clinic for immunizations. Which vaccine prescriptions should the nurse question? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Varicella","DTaP","IPV","HepA","MMR"],"a":[0,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Varicella\n• MMR\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Varicella” – Live vaccine generally contraindicated\n• “MMR” – Live vaccine generally contraindicated\n\nNot correct:\n• “DTaP” – Inactivated vaccine\n• “IPV” – Inactivated vaccine\n• “HepA” – Inactivated vaccine\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":367,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"A patient is being admitted to prenatal care by the nurse. The patient states her last menstrual period was October 30, 2018. The nurse correctly calculates this mother’s due date as (select an answer from the following options):","o":["July 30, 2019","August 6, 2019","July 23, 2019","August 13, 2019"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• August 6, 2019\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “August 6, 2019” – Naegele rule: October 30 plus 7 days minus 3 months plus 1 year\n\nNot correct:\n• “July 30, 2019” – Omits seven-day addition\n• “July 23, 2019” – Subtracts rather than adds days\n• “August 13, 2019” – Adds an extra week\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":368,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"A pregnant client calls the nurse at the physician’s office and reports that she has noticed a thin, colorless, vaginal drainage. Which of the following information would be most appropriate for the nurse to provide to the client?","o":["Use tampons if the discharge is bothersome but be sure to change the tampons every 2 hours.","Report to the emergency department at the maternity center immediately.","Come to the clinic immediately.","The vaginal discharge may be bothersome but is a normal occurrence."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The vaginal discharge may be bothersome but is a normal occurrence.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The vaginal discharge may be bothersome but is a normal occurrence.” – Normal discharge is possible; watery leakage warrants assessment\n\nNot correct:\n• “Use tampons if the discharge is bothersome but be sure to change the tampons every 2 ho…” – Tampons are not recommended\n• “Report to the emergency department at the maternity center immediately.” – Not routine for benign leukorrhea; suspected fluid leak needs evaluation\n• “Come to the clinic immediately.” – Urgency depends on possible membrane rupture\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":369,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"A nurse is counseling a newly pregnant woman with borderline high blood pressure readings about symptoms to contact the clinic during the pregnancy related to preeclampsia. The nurse correctly tells the patient to call immediately if (select an answer from the following options):","o":["The patient has ankle edema at the end of the day.","Her weight increases by 2 lbs. in a week or 5 lbs. in four weeks.","There is any wrist pain with numbness on the sides of the fingers.","She experiences any significant headaches, visual changes or epigastric pain."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• She experiences any significant headaches, visual changes or epigastric pain.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “She experiences any significant headaches, visual changes or epigastric pain.” – Severe preeclampsia warning symptoms\n\nNot correct:\n• “The patient has ankle edema at the end of the day.” – Dependent edema can be normal\n• “Her weight increases by 2 lbs. in a week or 5 lbs. in four weeks.” – Not the listed severe-feature cluster\n• “There is any wrist pain with numbness on the sides of the fingers.” – Suggests carpal tunnel\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":370,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"The nurse is evaluating a patient who states her last period was approximately 8 weeks ago. The patient relates symptoms of morning nausea, breast tenderness, and a positive pregnancy test. The patient further states all symptoms have subsided in the last week with scant pink vaginal discharge. The nurse correctly suspects that this patient (select an answer from the following options):","o":["was not really pregnant","has a possible eating disorder","is experiencing a spontaneous abortion","is presenting with a hormonal imbalance"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• is experiencing a spontaneous abortion\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “is experiencing a spontaneous abortion” – Possible pregnancy loss; evaluate urgently as appropriate\n\nNot correct:\n• “was not really pregnant” – Positive test supports pregnancy\n• “has a possible eating disorder” – Does not explain bleeding and symptom loss\n• “is presenting with a hormonal imbalance” – Less specific than pregnancy loss\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 23: “Abortion”"},{"id":371,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"A patient presents to the clinic for her first prenatal appointment. She states that she has had a set of twins who were born at 34 weeks, a son who was born at 40 weeks, and she has had two miscarriages.","o":["5-1-2-2-3","5-1-2-2-3","5-1-1-2-3","6-1-1-2-3"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• 5-1-1-2-3\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “5-1-1-2-3” – Five pregnancies, one term, one preterm, two losses, three living\n\nNot correct:\n• “5-1-2-2-3” – Twins count as one preterm pregnancy\n• “5-1-2-2-3” – Same incorrect preterm count\n• “6-1-1-2-3” – Overcounts pregnancies\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 8: “Para: births after 20 weeks gestation”\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 8: “Abortions: birth that occurs <20 weeks gestation”\nWorked out with your slide definitions: gravida 5 (with this pregnancy), term 1, preterm 1 (twins at 34 weeks), abortions 2, living 3."},{"id":372,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"A patient presents to the clinic for her first prenatal appointment. She states that she has one child living who was born at 35 weeks gestation. Which of the following would be her correct GPTPtAL?","o":["1-1-0-1-0-1","2-1-1-0-0-1","1-1-1-0-0-1","2-1-0-1-0-1"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• 2-1-0-1-0-1\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “2-1-0-1-0-1” – Two pregnancies, one para, zero term, one preterm, zero losses, one living\n\nNot correct:\n• “1-1-0-1-0-1” – Omits current pregnancy\n• “2-1-1-0-0-1” – Incorrectly counts prior preterm birth as term\n• “1-1-1-0-0-1” – Omits current pregnancy and misclassifies birth\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 8: “Para: births after 20 weeks gestation”\nWorked out with your slide definitions: gravida 2 (with this pregnancy), term 0, preterm 1 (35 weeks), abortions 0, living 1.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (B), Unit 5 (C)."},{"id":373,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"The nurse has developed a plan of care for a client experiencing dystocia and includes several nursing interventions in the plan. The nurse prioritizes the plan and selects which of the following nursing interventions as the highest priority?","o":["Keeping the significant other informed of the progress of the labor","Changing the client’s position frequently","Providing comfort measures","Monitoring fetal status"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Monitoring fetal status\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Monitoring fetal status” – Dystocia risks fetal compromise\n\nNot correct:\n• “Keeping the significant other informed of the progress of the labor” – Supportive but not immediate fetal priority\n• “Changing the client’s position frequently” – May help labor but fetal assessment first\n• “Providing comfort measures” – Comfort secondary to fetal safety\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (C)."},{"id":374,"t":11,"s":27,"q":"The nurse caring for a woman in active labor notes late decelerations in the fetal heart tones. The first action of the nurse would be to:","o":["Reposition the woman.","Readjust the external monitor.","Call the primary caregiver.","Start oxygen by mask."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Reposition the woman.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Reposition the woman.” – Improves uteroplacental perfusion\n\nNot correct:\n• “Readjust the external monitor.” – Does not correct uteroplacental insufficiency\n• “Call the primary caregiver.” – Notify after immediate corrective measures as appropriate\n• “Start oxygen by mask.” – Not routine if maternal saturation normal\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 78: “Position mother on left side”\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 78: “Provide O2 to mother”\nYour slide lists repositioning and oxygen among the interventions; it does not rank which comes first."},{"id":375,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"Which CHD results in increased pulmonary blood flow?","o":["Ventricular septal defect","Pulmonary stenosis","Tetralogy of Fallot","Coarctation of the aorta"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Ventricular septal defect\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Ventricular septal defect” – Left-to-right shunt increases pulmonary flow\n\nNot correct:\n• “Pulmonary stenosis” – Obstructs pulmonary outflow\n• “Tetralogy of Fallot” – Typically decreases pulmonary flow\n• “Coarctation of the aorta” – Obstructs systemic outflow\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - Pediatrics, slide 27: “Disorders with increased pulmonary blood flow (Acyanotic)”"},{"id":376,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"A nurse is conducting discharge teaching to parents about the care of their infant after cardiac surgery. The nurse instructs the parents to notify the physician if which conditions occur? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Respiratory rate of 36 at rest","Temperature above 37.7° C (100° F)","New, frequent coughing","Turning blue or bluer than normal","Appetite slowly increasing"],"a":[1,2,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Temperature above 37.7° C (100° F)\n• New, frequent coughing\n• Turning blue or bluer than normal\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Temperature above 37.7° C (100° F)” – Fever may indicate infection\n• “New, frequent coughing” – New cough may indicate respiratory compromise\n• “Turning blue or bluer than normal” – Worsening cyanosis indicates poor oxygenation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Respiratory rate of 36 at rest” – May be normal for infant at rest\n• “Appetite slowly increasing” – Improving appetite is reassuring\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (B)."},{"id":377,"t":6,"s":27,"q":"Exophthalmos (protruding eyeballs) may occur in children with which condition?","o":["Hypoparathyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Hyperparathyroidism","Hyperthyroidism"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Hyperthyroidism\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Hyperthyroidism” – Excess thyroid hormone increases metabolism\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hypoparathyroidism” – Usually causes low calcium\n• “Hypothyroidism” – Slows metabolism\n• “Hyperparathyroidism” – Usually causes high calcium\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (B), Unit 9."},{"id":378,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"A nurse is assessing lab results on four patients in the general pediatric unit. What child should the nurse go see first?","o":["Urine ketones: positive in large amounts","Serum creatinine 0.7 mg/dL","Serum BUN 21 mg/dL","Urine specific gravity: 1.025"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Urine ketones: positive in large amounts\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Urine ketones: positive in large amounts” – Large ketones suggest ketosis and possible DKA\n\nNot correct:\n• “Serum creatinine 0.7 mg/dL” – Generally normal creatinine\n• “Serum BUN 21 mg/dL” – Mild elevation is less specific\n• “Urine specific gravity: 1.025” – Concentration is less specific than ketonuria\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":379,"t":7,"s":27,"q":"Which nursing diagnosis has the highest priority for the toddler with celiac disease?","o":["Risk for Disproportionate Growth related to obesity","Excess Fluid Volume related to celiac crisis","Disturbed Body Image related to chronic constipation","Imbalanced Nutrition: Less than Body Requirements related to malabsorption"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Imbalanced Nutrition: Less than Body Requirements related to malabsorption\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Imbalanced Nutrition: Less than Body Requirements related to malabsorption” – Poor absorption causes inadequate nutrient uptake\n\nNot correct:\n• “Risk for Disproportionate Growth related to obesity” – Malabsorption generally impairs growth\n• “Excess Fluid Volume related to celiac crisis” – Dehydration can occur in crisis\n• “Disturbed Body Image related to chronic constipation” – Does not address primary nutrition issue\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":380,"t":7,"s":27,"q":"A school-age child with diarrhea has been rehydrated. The nurse is discussing the child’s diet with the family. Which statement by the parent indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I will keep my child on a clear liquid diet for the next 24 hours.”","“I will offer my child bananas, rice, applesauce, and toast for the next 48 hours.”","“I should have my child eat a normal diet with easily digested foods for the next 48 hours.”","“I should encourage my child to drink carbonated drinks but avoid food for the next 24 hours.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I should have my child eat a normal diet with easily digested foods for the next 48 hours.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I should have my child eat a normal diet with easily digested foods for the next 48 ho…” – Resume age-appropriate foods after rehydration\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I will keep my child on a clear liquid diet for the next 24 hours.”” – Prolonged restriction delays nutrition\n• ““I will offer my child bananas, rice, applesauce, and toast for the next 48 hours.”” – BRAT diet lacks needed nutrients\n• ““I should encourage my child to drink carbonated drinks but avoid food for the next 24 …” – Carbonation and fasting are not recommended\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 7."},{"id":381,"t":8,"s":27,"q":"In caring for a child with an open fracture, the nurse should carefully assess for","o":["infection.","osteoarthritis.","epiphyseal disruption.","periosteum thickening."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• infection.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “infection.” – Open fracture allows microorganisms to enter\n\nNot correct:\n• “osteoarthritis.” – Degenerative joint disease is not the immediate open-fracture concern\n• “epiphyseal disruption.” – Growth plate injury is possible but infection is the immediate open-fracture concern\n• “periosteum thickening.” – Not the primary complication of an open fracture\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":382,"t":12,"s":27,"q":"When infants are seen for fractures, which nursing intervention is a priority?","o":["Assess for child abuse. Fractures in infants are often nonaccidental.","Assess for genetic factors.","No intervention is necessary. It is not uncommon for infants to fracture bones.","Assess the family’s safety practices. Fractures in infants usually result from falls."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Assess for child abuse. Fractures in infants are often nonaccidental.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Assess for child abuse. Fractures in infants are often nonaccidental.” – Unexplained infant fracture requires safeguarding evaluation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Assess for genetic factors.” – Consider bone disease but do not bypass abuse evaluation\n• “No intervention is necessary. It is not uncommon for infants to fracture bones.” – Fractures are not routine in infants\n• “Assess the family’s safety practices. Fractures in infants usually result from falls.” – Falls do not explain all infant fractures\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":383,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"Which statement, made by a nursing student to the father of a 4-year-old child, warrants correction by the nurse?","o":["“Encourage your child to practice brushing his teeth after you have thoroughly cleaned them.”","“Fluoride supplements are needed if you do not have fluoridated water.”","“Your child’s ‘permanent teeth’ will begin to come in around 6 years of age.”","“Because the ‘baby teeth’ are not permanent, they are not important to the child.”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Because the ‘baby teeth’ are not permanent, they are not important to the child.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Because the ‘baby teeth’ are not permanent, they are not important to the child.”” – Primary teeth support nutrition, speech and spacing\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Encourage your child to practice brushing his teeth after you have thoroughly cleaned …” – Practice builds skills with supervision\n• ““Fluoride supplements are needed if you do not have fluoridated water.”” – Supplementation may be indicated after assessing fluoride exposure\n• ““Your child’s ‘permanent teeth’ will begin to come in around 6 years of age.”” – Permanent teeth generally emerge around age six\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":384,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"Which strategy is the best approach when initiating the physical examination of a 9-month-old male infant?","o":["Have the parent hold the child on his or her lap.","Undress the infant and do a head-to-toe examination.","Ask the parent to leave because the infant will be upset.","Put the infant on the examination table and begin assessments at the head."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Have the parent hold the child on his or her lap.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Have the parent hold the child on his or her lap.” – Parent provides security\n\nNot correct:\n• “Undress the infant and do a head-to-toe examination.” – May provoke distress\n• “Ask the parent to leave because the infant will be upset.” – Separation increases anxiety\n• “Put the infant on the examination table and begin assessments at the head.” – May be frightening before rapport\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":385,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"Which action is appropriate when the nurse is assessing breath sounds of an 18-month-old crying child?","o":["Let the child play with the stethoscope for distraction.","Ask the parent to quiet the child so the nurse can listen.","Auscultate breath sounds and chart that the child was crying.","Document that data are not available because of crying."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Let the child play with the stethoscope for distraction.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Let the child play with the stethoscope for distraction.” – Familiarization reduces fear\n\nNot correct:\n• “Ask the parent to quiet the child so the nurse can listen.” – May not reliably settle child\n• “Auscultate breath sounds and chart that the child was crying.” – Crying limits accurate breath assessment\n• “Document that data are not available because of crying.” – Attempt to obtain accurate data first\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":386,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"A nurse is planning to teach about injury prevention to a group of parents. What action by the nurse would best ensure a successful event?","o":["Have handouts listing community resources.","Present the material in an interactive way.","Group parents by child’s developmental stage.","Provide free safety gear like bike helmets."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Group parents by child’s developmental stage.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Group parents by child’s developmental stage.” – Age-specific injuries and prevention differ\n\nNot correct:\n• “Have handouts listing community resources.” – Useful supplement\n• “Present the material in an interactive way.” – Useful delivery method\n• “Provide free safety gear like bike helmets.” – Helpful but not the primary planning approach\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":387,"t":11,"s":28,"q":"The nursing instructor asks a nursing student to describe the process of quickening. Which of the following statements, if made by the student, indicates an understanding of this term?","o":["“It is irregular painless contractions that occur throughout pregnancy.”","“It is the soft blowing sound that can be heard when the uterus is auscultated.”","“It is the thinning of the lower uterine segment.”","“It is the fetal movement that is felt by the mother.”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “It is the fetal movement that is felt by the mother.”\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““It is the fetal movement that is felt by the mother.”” – Maternal perception of fetal movement\n\nNot correct:\n• ““It is irregular painless contractions that occur throughout pregnancy.”” – Describes Braxton Hicks\n• ““It is the soft blowing sound that can be heard when the uterus is auscultated.”” – Describes uterine souffle\n• ““It is the thinning of the lower uterine segment.”” – Describes effacement\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":388,"t":10,"s":28,"q":"A pregnant patient has prenatal blood work. The Hg. is 9.3 and Hct. 23.1. The patient is diagnosed with anemia. Which of the following breakfast menus would provide the most absorbable iron?","o":["Eggs with cheese, buttered toast, coffee","Sausage with cheese biscuit, decaffeinated tea","Oatmeal, brown sugar, apple, glass of milk","Eggs, sausage, toast, raisins, orange juice"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Eggs, sausage, toast, raisins, orange juice\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Eggs, sausage, toast, raisins, orange juice” – Iron sources plus vitamin C\n\nNot correct:\n• “Eggs with cheese, buttered toast, coffee” – Less iron and vitamin C\n• “Sausage with cheese biscuit, decaffeinated tea” – Less vitamin C to aid absorption\n• “Oatmeal, brown sugar, apple, glass of milk” – Milk calcium may inhibit iron absorption\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Hematology, slide 8: “Vit. C enhances absorption of iron; antacids interfere”\n• Unit 8 - Hematology, slide 8: “Dietary sources; organ meats, beans, leafy greens, raisins, molasses”"},{"id":389,"t":11,"s":28,"q":"The nurse is preparing to care for a client who is being admitted to the hospital with a possible diagnosis of ectopic pregnancy. The nurse develops a plan of care for the client and determines that which of the following is the priority nursing action?","o":["Monitoring the apical pulse","Assessing for edema","Monitoring daily weight","Monitoring the temperature"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Monitoring the apical pulse\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Monitoring the apical pulse” – Tachycardia may signal hemorrhage\n\nNot correct:\n• “Assessing for edema” – Not a primary hemorrhage indicator\n• “Monitoring daily weight” – Less immediate measure of bleeding\n• “Monitoring the temperature” – Less sensitive to acute hemorrhage\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":390,"t":10,"s":28,"q":"A pregnant client reports to the health care clinic complaining of loss of appetite, weight loss, and fatigue. Following assessment, tuberculosis is suspected. A sputum culture is obtained, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis is identified in the sputum. The nurse provides instructions to the client regarding therapeutic management of tuberculosis. Which of the following instructions does the nurse provide to the client?","o":["The need for therapeutic abortion is required.","Isoniazid (INH) plus rifampin (Rifadin) will be required for a total of 9 months.","Medication will not be started until after delivery of the fetus.","The newborn must receive medication therapy immediately following birth."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Isoniazid (INH) plus rifampin (Rifadin) will be required for a total of 9 months.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Isoniazid (INH) plus rifampin (Rifadin) will be required for a total of 9 months.” – Source regimen; active TB treatment is individualized\n\nNot correct:\n• “The need for therapeutic abortion is required.” – TB alone does not require abortion\n• “Medication will not be started until after delivery of the fetus.” – Active TB should not be delayed\n• “The newborn must receive medication therapy immediately following birth.” – Newborn management depends on exposure and evaluation\n\n⚠ One detail differs from your PowerPoints.\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 9: “Isoniazid (INH):  most potent TB drug. Bactericidal-given to prevent active TB in converters”\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 9: “Long course of treatment 12-36 months”\nThe isoniazid-plus-rifampin answer is still the best of the four, but the quiz says 9 months while your slide says TB treatment is a long course of 12-36 months. Check the duration against your class notes.\n\nAlso appears in the weekly quiz for: Unit 5 (C)."},{"id":391,"t":11,"s":28,"q":"A patient presents to the clinic for her first prenatal appointment. She states that she has had a set of twins who were born at 34 weeks, a son who was born at 40 weeks, and she has had two miscarriages.","o":["5-2-1-1-2-3","6-3-1-1-2-3","5-3-1-2-2-3","5-2-1-2-2-3"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• 5-2-1-1-2-3\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “5-2-1-1-2-3” – Five pregnancies, two births, one term and one preterm, two losses, three living\n\nNot correct:\n• “6-3-1-1-2-3” – Overcounts pregnancies and parity\n• “5-3-1-2-2-3” – Overcounts parity and preterm births\n• “5-2-1-2-2-3” – Twins count as one preterm birth\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 8: “Para: births after 20 weeks gestation”\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 8: “Abortions: birth that occurs <20 weeks gestation”\nWorked out with your slide definitions: gravida 5 (with this pregnancy), 2 births (twins count as one), term 1, preterm 1, abortions 2, living 3."},{"id":392,"t":11,"s":28,"q":"The nurse is preparing to care for a client in labor. The physician has prescribed an intravenous (IV) infusion of oxytocin (Pitocin). The nurse ensures that which of the following is implemented prior to the beginning of the infusion?","o":["Continuous electronic fetal monitoring","Placing the client on complete bed rest","Placing a code cart at the client’s bedside","An IV infusion of antibiotics"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Continuous electronic fetal monitoring\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Continuous electronic fetal monitoring” – Monitors fetal tolerance of labor\n\nNot correct:\n• “Placing the client on complete bed rest” – Not routinely indicated\n• “Placing a code cart at the client’s bedside” – Not the primary intervention\n• “An IV infusion of antibiotics” – Antibiotics only for specific indications\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 71: “EFM 15-20 mins.  prior to beginning Pitocin and throughout infusion”"},{"id":393,"t":11,"s":28,"q":"A nurse admits a laboring woman to the triage area. A vaginal exam reveals a breech presentation of the fetus. The nurse should take which of the following actions?","o":["Prepare for a caesarean section.","Apply internal monitors.","Turn the mother to her side.","Place the mother in knee-chest position."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Prepare for a caesarean section.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Prepare for a caesarean section.” – Breech presentation may require cesarean delivery; confirm circumstances\n\nNot correct:\n• “Apply internal monitors.” – Internal monitoring does not correct breech presentation\n• “Turn the mother to her side.” – Side-lying does not correct breech presentation\n• “Place the mother in knee-chest position.” – Knee-chest is used for cord prolapse, not routine breech\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":394,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"The nurse is assigned to care for an infant with tetralogy of Fallot. The mother of the infant calls the nurse to the room because the infant suddenly seems to be having difficulty breathing. The nurse enters the room and notes that the infant is experiencing a hypercyanotic episode. The initial nursing action is to:","o":["Contact the respiratory therapy department.","Place the infant in a knee-chest position.","Place the infant in a prone position.","Call a code."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Place the infant in a knee-chest position.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Place the infant in a knee-chest position.” – Increases systemic resistance and reduces right-to-left shunting\n\nNot correct:\n• “Contact the respiratory therapy department.” – Delays immediate intervention\n• “Place the infant in a prone position.” – Does not reliably correct shunting\n• “Call a code.” – Escalate if severe or unresponsive; position first as appropriate\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":395,"t":6,"s":28,"q":"What is the best nursing action when a child with type 1 diabetes mellitus is sweating, trembling, and pale?","o":["Give the child 5 units of regular insulin subcutaneously.","Offer the child a glass of water.","Give the child a glass of orange juice.","Give the child glucagon subcutaneously."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Give the child a glass of orange juice.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Give the child a glass of orange juice.” – Fast-acting carbohydrate if able to swallow\n\nNot correct:\n• “Give the child 5 units of regular insulin subcutaneously.” – Worsens hypoglycemia\n• “Offer the child a glass of water.” – No glucose replacement\n• “Give the child glucagon subcutaneously.” – Reserved for severe hypoglycemia or inability to swallow\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":396,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"The mother of a child with an umbilical hernia calls the clinic and reports to the nurse that the child has been vomiting and is complaining of pain in the abdominal area. The nurse would most appropriately instruct the mother to:","o":["Administer acetaminophen (Tylenol) suppositories to the child.","Contact the physician.","Apply an ice pack to the abdomen.","Keep the child on clear liquids."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Contact the physician.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Contact the physician.” – Pain and vomiting suggest incarceration or obstruction; urgent evaluation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Administer acetaminophen (Tylenol) suppositories to the child.” – Analgesia alone does not address possible incarceration\n• “Apply an ice pack to the abdomen.” – Ice does not address possible strangulation\n• “Keep the child on clear liquids.” – Diet change does not address possible obstruction\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":397,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"A baby is scheduled for abdominal surgery for hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and has an NG tube to intermittent suction. When the family asks why the child has the tube, what response by the nurse is best?","o":["“The nasogastric tube makes the baby more comfortable after surgery.”","“We can keep a more accurate measure of intake and output with the tube.”","“The tube is used to decrease postoperative diarrhea.”","“The nasogastric tube decompresses the abdomen and decreases vomiting.”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “The nasogastric tube decompresses the abdomen and decreases vomiting.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““The nasogastric tube decompresses the abdomen and decreases vomiting.”” – Removes gastric contents and reduces distention\n\nNot correct:\n• ““The nasogastric tube makes the baby more comfortable after surgery.”” – Not primary purpose\n• ““We can keep a more accurate measure of intake and output with the tube.”” – Output monitoring is secondary\n• ““The tube is used to decrease postoperative diarrhea.”” – Does not treat diarrhea\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":398,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"During a well-child visit, the nurse identifies that an 18-month-old infant is bowlegged. What action by the nurse is most appropriate?","o":["Document the finding in the chart.","Facilitate a referral to an orthopedist.","Perform further assessment of the musculoskeletal system.","Assess the infant’s diet history."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Document the finding in the chart.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Document the finding in the chart.” – Physiologic bowlegs are common at 18 months\n\nNot correct:\n• “Facilitate a referral to an orthopedist.” – No referral solely for expected symmetric bowlegs\n• “Perform further assessment of the musculoskeletal system.” – Further assessment if asymmetric, severe or persistent\n• “Assess the infant’s diet history.” – Diet history may matter if rickets suspected, but typical bowlegs are expected\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":399,"t":12,"s":28,"q":"A priority nursing intervention when caring for a child in a Pavlik harness is","o":["skin care.","bowel function.","respiratory function.","feeding patterns."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• skin care.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “skin care.” – Harness straps may cause pressure injury\n\nNot correct:\n• “bowel function.” – Not the primary harness-related complication\n• “respiratory function.” – Not the primary harness-related complication\n• “feeding patterns.” – Not the primary harness-related complication\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":400,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"In providing anticipatory guidance to parents whose child will soon be entering kindergarten, which is a critical factor to include in this teaching?","o":["The child needs to be able to sit still.","The child should attend a preschool program first.","The child should be able to count to 25.","The parent should have interaction and be responsive to the child."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The parent should have interaction and be responsive to the child.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The parent should have interaction and be responsive to the child.” – Responsive interaction supports development\n\nNot correct:\n• “The child needs to be able to sit still.” – Not sufficient alone\n• “The child should attend a preschool program first.” – Preschool attendance not mandatory\n• “The child should be able to count to 25.” – Counting is not the sole readiness indicator\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":401,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"The parents of a newborn infant state, “We will probably not have our baby immunized because we are concerned about the risks.” What is the nurse’s best response?","o":["“You should probably think about this decision.”","“This has to be reported to the health department.”","“It is your decision to immunize your child or not.”","“It is far riskier to not immunize your baby.”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “It is far riskier to not immunize your baby.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““It is far riskier to not immunize your baby.”” – Explains risk; use respectful shared decision-making\n\nNot correct:\n• ““You should probably think about this decision.”” – Vague; does not explain risk\n• ““This has to be reported to the health department.”” – Reporting not automatically required\n• ““It is your decision to immunize your child or not.”” – Respects autonomy but lacks risk education\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":402,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"A student nurse asks the faculty why peer relationships become more important during adolescence. Which of the following is the nurse’s best response?","o":["Adolescents dislike their parents.","They provide adolescents with a feeling of belonging.","They promote a sense of individuality in adolescents.","Adolescents no longer need parental control."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• They provide adolescents with a feeling of belonging.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “They provide adolescents with a feeling of belonging.” – Peer groups support belonging\n\nNot correct:\n• “Adolescents dislike their parents.” – Not an inherent adolescent trait\n• “They promote a sense of individuality in adolescents.” – Peers often reinforce group norms\n• “Adolescents no longer need parental control.” – Parental guidance remains important\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":403,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"A student nurse is preparing to administer an Hib vaccination to an infant. What action by the student requires the registered nurse to intervene?","o":["Assesses the family’s beliefs and values about vaccinations","Wipes the dorsal gluteal area with alcohol prior to injection","Gives the vaccine information statement prior to administering the vaccine","Obtains written informed consent before giving the vaccine"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Wipes the dorsal gluteal area with alcohol prior to injection\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Wipes the dorsal gluteal area with alcohol prior to injection” – Dorsogluteal site is inappropriate for routine vaccination\n\nNot correct:\n• “Assesses the family’s beliefs and values about vaccinations” – Supports individualized counseling\n• “Gives the vaccine information statement prior to administering the vaccine” – VIS should precede vaccine\n• “Obtains written informed consent before giving the vaccine” – Consent requirements depend on policy and law\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":404,"t":11,"s":29,"q":"The nurse is admitting a patient into the maternity clinic. Which of the following would the nurse document as a probable sign of pregnancy?","o":["The patient has a positive pregnancy test.","The nurse obtains fetal heart rate with a doppler.","The patient complains of increased hunger, anxiety, and shakiness.","The patient states she has missed her last two menstrual periods."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The patient has a positive pregnancy test.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The patient has a positive pregnancy test.” – Probable sign, not definitive\n\nNot correct:\n• “The nurse obtains fetal heart rate with a doppler.” – Positive sign of pregnancy\n• “The patient complains of increased hunger, anxiety, and shakiness.” – Nonspecific symptoms\n• “The patient states she has missed her last two menstrual periods.” – Presumptive sign\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 2: “Pregnancy test +”\nYour slide also lists a missed period (amenorrhea) as presumptive and the fetal heartbeat as a positive sign, which rules out the other options."},{"id":405,"t":11,"s":29,"q":"The nurse is teaching a pregnant client about the physiological effects and hormonal changes that occur during pregnancy. The client asks the nurse about the purpose of estrogen. The nurse bases the response on which of the following purposes of estrogen?","o":["It prevents the involution of the corpus luteum and maintains the production of progesterone until the placenta is formed.","It stimulates metabolism of glucose and converts the glucose to fat.","It stimulates uterine development to provide an environment for the fetus and stimulates the breasts to prepare for lactation.","It maintains the uterine lining for implantation."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• It stimulates uterine development to provide an environment for the fetus and stimulates the breasts to prepare for lactation.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “It stimulates uterine development to provide an environment for the fetus and stimulate…” – Estrogen supports uterine and breast growth\n\nNot correct:\n• “It prevents the involution of the corpus luteum and maintains the production of progest…” – Describes hCG\n• “It stimulates metabolism of glucose and converts the glucose to fat.” – Not primary estrogen role\n• “It maintains the uterine lining for implantation.” – Describes progesterone\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":406,"t":11,"s":29,"q":"The nurse has assisted in performing a nonstress test on a pregnant client and is reviewing the documentation related to the results of the test. The nurse notes that the physician has documented the test results as reactive. The nurse interprets that this result indicates (select an answer from the following options):","o":["That the findings on the monitor were difficult to interpret","Abnormal findings","Normal findings","The need for further evaluation"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Normal findings\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Normal findings” – Reactive NST indicates reassuring accelerations\n\nNot correct:\n• “That the findings on the monitor were difficult to interpret” – Reactive is an interpretable reassuring result\n• “Abnormal findings” – Reactive is generally reassuring\n• “The need for further evaluation” – Reactive result alone does not require additional evaluation\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":407,"t":11,"s":29,"q":"Pregnancies-5\nBirths - 2 at 39 weeks,\nMiscarriages - 2\nLiving Children – 1","o":["G-6, T-3, P-1, A-1, L-1","G-5, T-2, P-0, A-2, L-1","G-5, T-2, P-1, A- , L-3","G-4, T-3, P-0, A-1, L-2"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• G-5, T-2, P-0, A-2, L-1\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “G-5, T-2, P-0, A-2, L-1” – Five pregnancies, two term, no preterm, two losses, one living\n\nNot correct:\n• “G-6, T-3, P-1, A-1, L-1” – Overcounts pregnancies and preterm births\n• “G-5, T-2, P-1, A- , L-3” – Incorrect preterm and living counts\n• “G-4, T-3, P-0, A-1, L-2” – Incorrect total and outcomes\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 8: “Para: births after 20 weeks gestation”\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 9: “Living children”\nWorked out with your slide definitions: 5 pregnancies, 2 term births, 0 preterm, 2 abortions, 1 living child."},{"id":408,"t":11,"s":29,"q":"The nurse is assisting in caring for a client in the active stage of labor. The nurse is told that the fetal patterns show a late deceleration on the monitor strip. Based on this finding, the nurse prepares for which most appropriate nursing actions?","o":["Documenting the findings and continuing to monitor the fetal patterns","Increasing the rate of the intravenous (IV) oxytocin infusion","Administering oxygen via face mask","Placing the mother in a supine position"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Administering oxygen via face mask\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Administering oxygen via face mask” – Source choice; oxygen is not routine if mother is normoxic\n\nNot correct:\n• “Documenting the findings and continuing to monitor the fetal patterns” – Does not address nonreassuring pattern\n• “Increasing the rate of the intravenous (IV) oxytocin infusion” – May worsen uterine tachysystole or fetal compromise\n• “Placing the mother in a supine position” – May worsen vena cava compression\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 78: “Provide O2 to mother”\n• Unit 5 - OB and Newborn Care, slide 78: “Position mother on left side”"},{"id":409,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"Which defect results in increased pulmonary blood flow?","o":["Tricuspid atresia","Transposition of the great arteries","Atrial septal defect","Pulmonic stenosis"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Atrial septal defect\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Atrial septal defect” – Left-to-right shunt increases pulmonary flow\n\nNot correct:\n• “Tricuspid atresia” – Cyanotic lesion restricts pulmonary flow\n• “Transposition of the great arteries” – Parallel circulations cause cyanosis\n• “Pulmonic stenosis” – Obstructs pulmonary flow\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - Pediatrics, slide 27: “Atrial and Ventricular septal defect”"},{"id":410,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"For what reason might a newborn infant with a cardiac defect, such as coarctation of the aorta, that results in a right-to-left shunt receive prostaglandin E1?","o":["To decrease inflammation","To improve oxygenation","To control pain","To decrease respirations"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• To improve oxygenation\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “To improve oxygenation” – Increases available oxygen\n\nNot correct:\n• “To decrease inflammation” – Oxygen does not directly reduce inflammation\n• “To control pain” – Not an analgesic\n• “To decrease respirations” – Not intended to suppress breathing\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":411,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"A common clinical manifestation of congenital hypothyroidism is","o":["insomnia.","diarrhea.","jitteriness.","hoarse cry."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• hoarse cry.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “hoarse cry.” – Hypothyroidism can cause hoarse cry\n\nNot correct:\n• “insomnia.” – More typical of hyperthyroidism\n• “diarrhea.” – More typical of hyperthyroidism\n• “jitteriness.” – More typical of hyperthyroidism\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":412,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"A parent asks the nurse why self-monitoring of blood glucose is being recommended for her child with diabetes. The nurse should base the explanation on the knowledge that","o":["children are better able to manage the diabetes.","it is not as accurate as laboratory testing.","the parents are better able to manage the disease.","it is a less expensive method of testing."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• children are better able to manage the diabetes.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “children are better able to manage the diabetes.” – Supports age-appropriate self-management\n\nNot correct:\n• “it is not as accurate as laboratory testing.” – Home monitoring is useful despite measurement limits\n• “the parents are better able to manage the disease.” – Parent support helps but child autonomy is focus\n• “it is a less expensive method of testing.” – Cost is not primary advantage\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":413,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"The nurse is reviewing the physician’s documentation in the record of a child admitted with a diagnosis of intussusception. The nurse expects to note that the physician has documented the presence of:","o":["Currant jelly–type stools","Scleral jaundice","Pale-colored and hard stools","Projectile vomiting"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Currant jelly–type stools\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Currant jelly–type stools” – Blood and mucus suggest intussusception\n\nNot correct:\n• “Scleral jaundice” – Suggests bilirubin accumulation\n• “Pale-colored and hard stools” – Not classic intussusception stool\n• “Projectile vomiting” – More typical of pyloric stenosis\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":414,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"Therapeutic management of most children with Hirschsprung disease is primarily","o":["permanent colostomy.","low-fiber diet.","surgical removal of the affected section of the bowel.","daily enemas."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• surgical removal of the affected section of the bowel.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “surgical removal of the affected section of the bowel.” – Removes nonfunctioning aganglionic segment\n\nNot correct:\n• “permanent colostomy.” – Usually not permanently required\n• “low-fiber diet.” – Does not correct aganglionosis\n• “daily enemas.” – Does not definitively treat condition\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":415,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"What is the major concern guiding treatment for the child with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","o":["Encourage new hobbies.","Minimize pain.","Maintain normal activities.","Avoid permanent deformity."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Avoid permanent deformity.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Avoid permanent deformity.” – Preserve joint function and prevent deformity\n\nNot correct:\n• “Encourage new hobbies.” – May support coping but not primary outcome\n• “Minimize pain.” – Important but not long-term deformity prevention\n• “Maintain normal activities.” – Supports development but not the primary orthopedic goal\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":416,"t":12,"s":29,"q":"A 6-year-old patient who has been placed in skeletal traction has pain, edema, and fever. The nurse should assess which of the following?","o":["Warmth at site of pain","Blood pressure","Range of motion of all extremities","Neurologic status"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Warmth at site of pain\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Warmth at site of pain” – Pain, edema and fever plus localized warmth may suggest pin-site infection\n\nNot correct:\n• “Blood pressure” – Systemic measure less specific to local infection\n• “Range of motion of all extremities” – Moving a tractioned limb may be inappropriate; does not identify infection\n• “Neurologic status” – Less specific for suspected pin-site infection\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":417,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"The nurse in the emergency department (ED) is caring for a 9-year-old client with asthma. Parent at bedside. Client reports chest tightness with cough especially at night for the past 2 evenings. Expiratory wheeze auscultated throughout the chest. Vital signs: T 98.6 °F (37°C), P 110, RR 28, pulse oximetry reading 89% on room air. Sitting in tripod position with arms draped over bedside table. Humidified supplemental oxygen applied at 2 L/min via nasal cannula.\nComplete the following sentence by choosing from the list of options.\nThe nurse should first _____.","o":["Ask parent about allergens in the home.","Check the client’s peak flow with a peak expiratory flow meter.","Administer albuterol to the client.","Remind the client how to use metered dose inhaler."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Administer albuterol to the client.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Administer albuterol to the client.” – Rapid bronchodilation relieves acute obstruction\n\nNot correct:\n• “Ask parent about allergens in the home.” – History can wait until acute symptoms treated\n• “Check the client’s peak flow with a peak expiratory flow meter.” – Peak flow can follow rescue therapy when appropriate\n• “Remind the client how to use metered dose inhaler.” – Teaching follows stabilization\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 26: “Beta2-adrenergic agonists (albuterol)”\n• Unit 1 - NCLEX Test Taking Strategies, slide 19: “apply humidified supplemental oxygen at 2L/min via nasal cannula”\nYour Unit 1 slide 19 uses this same scenario (\"The nurse should first ...\") with humidified oxygen as one of its options and no answer shown; oxygen is not offered in this quiz version, and your Respiratory slide lists albuterol as a quick-relief medication."},{"id":418,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"The nurse uses a diagram to show that when the diaphragm moves","o":["up, the decreased negative pressure allows air to enter the lungs.","down, the negative pressure in the thoracic space pulls air into the lungs.","up, the increased negative pressure in the thoracic space forces air into the lungs.","down, the intercostal muscles retract, forcing air out of the lungs."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• down, the negative pressure in the thoracic space pulls air into the lungs.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “down, the negative pressure in the thoracic space pulls air into the lungs.” – Downward contraction increases thoracic volume and draws air in\n\nNot correct:\n• “up, the decreased negative pressure allows air to enter the lungs.” – Upward motion reduces thoracic volume\n• “up, the increased negative pressure in the thoracic space forces air into the lungs.” – Upward motion promotes expiration\n• “down, the intercostal muscles retract, forcing air out of the lungs.” – Downward movement promotes inspiration\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":419,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"A patient requires a precise concentration of 40% oxygen. Which of the following devices would best allow for this?","o":["A non-rebreather mask","A simple face mask","A partial rebreathing mask","A Venturi mask"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A Venturi mask\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A Venturi mask” – Fixed precise oxygen concentration\n\nNot correct:\n• “A non-rebreather mask” – High oxygen concentration but variable FiO2\n• “A simple face mask” – Variable FiO2\n• “A partial rebreathing mask” – Variable FiO2\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":420,"t":5,"s":30,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with the following ABG result.\npH 7.49\nPaCO2 41mmHg\nHCO3 38mEq/L\nWhich of the following is an appropriate interpretation of these results?","o":["metabolic acidosis","respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","respiratory acidosis"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Metabolic alkalosis\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Metabolic alkalosis” – High pH and high bicarbonate\n\nNot correct:\n• “metabolic acidosis” – pH is elevated, not low\n• “respiratory alkalosis” – CO2 is not low\n• “respiratory acidosis” – CO2 is not elevated\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “pH greater than 7.4 = Alkalinity”\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “HCO3: 22-28 mEq/L (Metabolic)”\nWorked out with the normal ranges on your slide: high pH with normal CO2 and high HCO3 is metabolic alkalosis."},{"id":421,"t":5,"s":30,"q":"The nurse is providing care for a client with prolonged gastric suctioning.  Which arterial blood gas result should the nurse anticipate?","o":["pH: 7.31, PaCO2: 60 mmHg, HCO3: 29 mEq/L","pH: 7.28, PaCO2: 40 mmHg, HCO3: 16 mEq/L","pH: 7.48, PaCO2: 45 mmHg, HCO3: 38 mEq/L","pH: 7.49, PaCO2: 30 mmHg, HCO3: 22 mEq/L"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• pH: 7.48, PaCO2: 45 mmHg, HCO3: 38 mEq/L\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “pH: 7.48, PaCO2: 45 mmHg, HCO3: 38 mEq/L” – Elevated bicarbonate with alkalemia\n\nNot correct:\n• “pH: 7.31, PaCO2: 60 mmHg, HCO3: 29 mEq/L” – Elevated CO2 with acidemia\n• “pH: 7.28, PaCO2: 40 mmHg, HCO3: 16 mEq/L” – Low bicarbonate with acidemia\n• “pH: 7.49, PaCO2: 30 mmHg, HCO3: 22 mEq/L” – Low CO2 with alkalemia\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “pH greater than 7.4 = Alkalinity”\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “HCO3: 22-28 mEq/L (Metabolic)”\nWorked out with the normal ranges on your slide (the link to gastric suctioning itself is not on the slide)."},{"id":422,"t":5,"s":30,"q":"You are caring for a client with the following ABG results.\npH 7.30\nCO2 44\nHCO-3 20\nWhich of the following is an appropriate interpretation?","o":["Metabolic Alkalosis","Respiratory Acidosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Metabolic Acidosis"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Metabolic Acidosis\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Metabolic Acidosis” – Low bicarbonate with acidemia\n\nNot correct:\n• “Metabolic Alkalosis” – Would show elevated bicarbonate\n• “Respiratory Acidosis” – Would show elevated CO2\n• “Respiratory Alkalosis” – Would show low CO2 with alkalemia\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “pH less than 7.35 = Acidity”\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “HCO3: 22-28 mEq/L (Metabolic)”\nWorked out with the normal ranges on your slide: low pH with normal CO2 and low HCO3 is metabolic acidosis."},{"id":423,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"The nurse is caring for an adult client who has an ABG return with the following values:\npH 7.30\nCO2 49\nHCO-3 23\nK+ 4.9\nBased on the ABG, which of the following interventions is appropriate?","o":["Position the client in a low-Fowlers position.","Encourage the client to use the incentive spirometer.","Have the client breath into a paper bag.","Inform the client to slow their respirations down."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Encourage the client to use the incentive spirometer.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Encourage the client to use the incentive spirometer.” – Promotes lung expansion and ventilation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Position the client in a low-Fowlers position.” – Low position limits lung expansion\n• “Have the client breath into a paper bag.” – Unsafe without confirming cause of hyperventilation\n• “Inform the client to slow their respirations down.” – May worsen hypoventilation\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 30: “PacCO2: 35-45mm Hg (Respiratory)”\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 4: “Encourage client to turn, cough, and deep breath, splint chest wall, use incentive spirometer”\nWorked out with the ranges on your slide: low pH with high CO2 is respiratory acidosis; incentive spirometry is on your pneumonia slide."},{"id":424,"t":10,"s":30,"q":"When educating a patient recently placed on inhaled corticosteroids, the nurse will discuss which potential adverse effects?","o":["Anxiety and palpitations","Headache and rapid heart rate","Oral candidiasis and dry mouth","Fatigue and depression"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Oral candidiasis and dry mouth\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Oral candidiasis and dry mouth” – Inhaled steroids can cause thrush and oral irritation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Anxiety and palpitations” – More typical of beta agonists\n• “Headache and rapid heart rate” – More typical of beta agonists\n• “Fatigue and depression” – Not the typical local adverse effects\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 18: “Side Effects:  tachycardia, palpitations, HTN, dyspnea, HA, dilated pupils, anxiety, nervousness, GI upset”\nThis shows anxiety, headache and rapid heart rate belong to adrenergic bronchodilators, which rules out the first two choices; oral candidiasis with inhaled steroids is not stated on your slides."},{"id":425,"t":10,"s":30,"q":"A patient will begin taking albuterol to treat asthma. When teaching the patient about this drug, the nurse will make which recommendation?","o":["Take a calcium supplement.","Report rapid or irregular heart rate.","Drink 8 to 16 extra ounces of fluid each day.","Monitor serum glucose daily."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Report rapid or irregular heart rate.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Report rapid or irregular heart rate.” – Tachyarrhythmia may indicate adverse effect\n\nNot correct:\n• “Take a calcium supplement.” – Not the primary medication safety concern\n• “Drink 8 to 16 extra ounces of fluid each day.” – Extra fluid not a primary drug precaution\n• “Monitor serum glucose daily.” – Not routinely required for this medication\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 18: “Side Effects:  tachycardia, palpitations, HTN, dyspnea, HA, dilated pupils, anxiety, nervousness, GI upset”"},{"id":426,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"The nurse is assessing voice sounds during a respiratory assessment. Which of these findings indicates a normal assessment? (Select all that apply).","o":["When the patient speaks in a normal voice, the examiner can hear a sound but cannot exactly distinguish what is being said.","As the patient repeatedly says “ninety-nine,” the examiner clearly hears the words “ninety-nine.”","Voice sounds are faint, muffled, and almost inaudible when the patient whispers “one, two, three” in a very soft voice.","As the patient says a long “ee-ee-ee” sound, the examiner hears a long “aaaaaa” sound.","As the patient says a long “ee-ee-ee” sound, the examiner also hears a long “ee-ee-ee” sound."],"a":[0,2,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• When the patient speaks in a normal voice, the examiner can hear a sound but cannot exactly distinguish what is being said.\n• Voice sounds are faint, muffled, and almost inaudible when the patient whispers “one, two, three” in a very soft voice.\n• As the patient says a long “ee-ee-ee” sound, the examiner also hears a long “ee-ee-ee” sound.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “When the patient speaks in a normal voice, the examiner can hear a sound but cannot exa…” – Normal speech is muffled\n• “Voice sounds are faint, muffled, and almost inaudible when the patient whispers “one, t…” – Whispered sounds are normally faint\n• “As the patient says a long “ee-ee-ee” sound, the examiner also hears a long “ee-ee-ee” …” – Normal E sound remains E\n\nNot correct:\n• “As the patient repeatedly says “ninety-nine,” the examiner clearly hears the words “nin…” – Clearly transmitted words suggest bronchophony\n• “As the patient says a long “ee-ee-ee” sound, the examiner hears a long “aaaaaa” sound.” – E-to-A change is egophony\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":427,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"During palpation of the anterior chest wall, the nurse notices a coarse, crackling sensation over the skin surface. What does this finding indicate?","o":["Crepitus","Adventitious sounds","Tactile fremitus","Friction rub"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Crepitus\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Crepitus” – Air under skin produces crackling on palpation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Adventitious sounds” – Abnormal auscultated lung sounds\n• “Tactile fremitus” – Vibrations transmitted through chest wall\n• “Friction rub” – Grating sound on auscultation\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":428,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"The client is prescribed to have 80% oxygen administered continuously in a noninvasive manner. Which type of delivery system should you select to meet these criteria?","o":["Venturi mask","Partial rebreather mask","Transtracheal oxygen","Nasal cannula"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Partial rebreather mask\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Partial rebreather mask” – Reservoir supplies higher oxygen concentration\n\nNot correct:\n• “Venturi mask” – Precise moderate FiO2\n• “Transtracheal oxygen” – Specialized long-term oxygen delivery\n• “Nasal cannula” – Lower flow and variable concentration\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":429,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"A patient is prescribed a decongestant nasal spray that contains oxymetazoline. What will the nurse teach the patient?","o":["Directly spray the medication away from the nasal septum and gently sniff.","This drug may be used in maintenance treatment for asthma.","Take this drug at bedtime because it may cause drowsiness.","Limit use of the drug to 5 to 7 days to prevent rebound nasal congestion"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Limit use of the drug to 5 to 7 days to prevent rebound nasal congestion\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Limit use of the drug to 5 to 7 days to prevent rebound nasal congestion” – Prolonged topical use causes rebound; many products limit to 3 days\n\nNot correct:\n• “Directly spray the medication away from the nasal septum and gently sniff.” – Can be valid spray technique but does not address duration risk\n• “This drug may be used in maintenance treatment for asthma.” – Not asthma maintenance therapy\n• “Take this drug at bedtime because it may cause drowsiness.” – Not primarily sedating\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":430,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"A patient is taking guaifenesin as part of treatment for a sinus infection. Which instruction will the nurse include during patient teaching?","o":["Report symptoms that last longer than 2 days.","Report clear-colored sputum to the prescriber.","Avoid driving a car or operating heavy machinery because of the sedating effects.","Force fluids to help loosen and liquefy secretions."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Force fluids to help loosen and liquefy secretions.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Force fluids to help loosen and liquefy secretions.” – Adequate fluids help thin secretions; avoid excess if restricted\n\nNot correct:\n• “Report symptoms that last longer than 2 days.” – Two-day threshold is not routine\n• “Report clear-colored sputum to the prescriber.” – Clear sputum alone is not alarming\n• “Avoid driving a car or operating heavy machinery because of the sedating effects.” – Not a typical sedating drug effect\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":431,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"A patient with a pleural effusion is scheduled for a thoracentesis. Which action should the nurse take to prepare the patient for the procedure?","o":["Obtain a large collection device to hold 2 to 3 liters of pleural fluid at one time.","Start a peripheral IV line to administer the necessary sedative drugs.","Position the patient sitting upright on the edge of the bed and leaning forward.","Remove the water pitcher and remind the patient not to eat or drink anything for 6 hours."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Position the patient sitting upright on the edge of the bed and leaning forward.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Position the patient sitting upright on the edge of the bed and leaning forward.” – Upright forward position exposes intercostal space\n\nNot correct:\n• “Obtain a large collection device to hold 2 to 3 liters of pleural fluid at one time.” – Large removal may cause complications\n• “Start a peripheral IV line to administer the necessary sedative drugs.” – Routine sedation not required\n• “Remove the water pitcher and remind the patient not to eat or drink anything for 6 hours.” – Routine fasting not necessary\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":432,"t":6,"s":30,"q":"After receiving change-of-shift report about the following four clients, which client should the nurse assess first?","o":["A 22-year-old male admitted with syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) who has a serum sodium level of 122 mEq/L","A 31-year-old female with Cushing syndrome and a blood glucose level of 244 mg/dL","A 70-year-old female taking levothyroxine who has an irregular pulse of 134","A 53-year-old male who has Addison’s disease and is due for a scheduled dose of hydrocortisone"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A 70-year-old female taking levothyroxine who has an irregular pulse of 134\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A 70-year-old female taking levothyroxine who has an irregular pulse of 134” – Rapid irregular pulse suggests cardiac instability\n\nNot correct:\n• “A 22-year-old male admitted with syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH)…” – Severe hyponatremia requires prompt assessment, but no acute symptoms listed\n• “A 31-year-old female with Cushing syndrome and a blood glucose level of 244 mg/dL” – Hyperglycemia is concerning but less immediately unstable\n• “A 53-year-old male who has Addison’s disease and is due for a scheduled dose of hydroco…” – Scheduled replacement may be given after acute priority\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":433,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"A patient has been taking antitubercular therapy for 3 months. The nurse will assess for what findings that indicate a therapeutic response to the drug therapy?","o":["There is increased tolerance to the medication therapy, and there are fewer reports of adverse effects.","The chronic cough is gone.","There are two consecutive negative purified protein derivative (PPD) results over 2 months.","There is a decrease in symptoms of tuberculosis along with improved chest x-rays and sputum cultures."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• There is a decrease in symptoms of tuberculosis along with improved chest x-rays and sputum cultures.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “There is a decrease in symptoms of tuberculosis along with improved chest x-rays and sp…” – Clinical and microbiologic improvement indicate response\n\nNot correct:\n• “There is increased tolerance to the medication therapy, and there are fewer reports of …” – Tolerance does not demonstrate bacterial clearance\n• “The chronic cough is gone.” – Symptom improvement alone is insufficient\n• “There are two consecutive negative purified protein derivative (PPD) results over 2 mon…” – PPD can remain positive after treatment\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":434,"t":12,"s":30,"q":"Which statement made by parents of a child with cystic fibrosis indicates that they understood the nurse’s teaching on pancreatic enzyme replacement?","o":["“Enzymes will improve my child’s breathing.”","“Enzymes should be given with meals and snacks.”","“The enzymes are stopped if my child begins wheezing.”","“I should give the enzymes 1 hour after meals.”"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Enzymes should be given with meals and snacks.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Enzymes should be given with meals and snacks.”” – Needed with meals and snacks for absorption\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Enzymes will improve my child’s breathing.”” – Enzymes improve digestion, not airway clearance\n• ““The enzymes are stopped if my child begins wheezing.”” – Wheezing does not justify stopping enzymes\n• ““I should give the enzymes 1 hour after meals.”” – Take with food rather than an hour afterward\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":435,"t":12,"s":30,"q":"What intervention can be taught to the parents of a 3-year-old child with pneumonia who is not hospitalized?","o":["Offer the child favorite warm liquid drinks.","Use a warm mist humidifier.","Offer the child only cool liquids.","Report a respiratory rate less than 28 breaths/min."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Offer the child favorite warm liquid drinks.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Offer the child favorite warm liquid drinks.” – Warm fluids soothe and support hydration\n\nNot correct:\n• “Use a warm mist humidifier.” – Warm mist poses burn risk\n• “Offer the child only cool liquids.” – No need to restrict to cool fluids\n• “Report a respiratory rate less than 28 breaths/min.” – Low rate alone is not the specified warning sign; assess age and breathing\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":436,"t":4,"s":30,"q":"Which classification of drugs is used to relieve an acute asthma episode?","o":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Leukotriene blockers","Short-acting beta2-adrenergic agonist","Long-acting bronchodilators"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Short-acting beta2-adrenergic agonist\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Short-acting beta2-adrenergic agonist” – Rapid bronchodilation for acute symptoms\n\nNot correct:\n• “Inhaled corticosteroids” – Controller, not immediate rescue\n• “Leukotriene blockers” – Controller, not immediate rescue\n• “Long-acting bronchodilators” – Not routine standalone rescue\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 26: “Quick-relief medications”\n• Unit 6 - Respiratory, slide 26: “Beta2-adrenergic agonists (albuterol)”"},{"id":437,"t":7,"s":31,"q":"The medical-surgical unit nurse is admitting a client who has cirrhosis with a history of chronic hepatitis C. Reports poor appetite. Disoriented to time. Dull abdominal pain 5/10 numeric rating scale. Peripheral edema with 2+ depression of the feet. Client is receiving diuretic therapy.  Select 3 nursing interventions the nurse should include in the client’s care plan.","o":["Initiate intake and output including stool consistency.","Assess the client’s neurological status every 4 hours.","Anticipate discontinuation of diuretic therapy.","Monitor the client for purpura and petechiae.","Ask the client about their sleep cycle.","Remind the client to limit intake of alcohol containing beverages."],"a":[0,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Initiate intake and output including stool consistency.\n• Monitor the client for purpura and petechiae.\n• Ask the client about their sleep cycle.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Initiate intake and output including stool consistency.” – Tracks fluid balance and stool changes\n• “Monitor the client for purpura and petechiae.” – Detects bleeding from impaired clotting\n• “Ask the client about their sleep cycle.” – Sleep reversal suggests encephalopathy\n\nNot correct:\n• “Assess the client’s neurological status every 4 hours.” – Source recommends more frequent neuro checks\n• “Anticipate discontinuation of diuretic therapy.” – Edema supports continued diuresis if tolerated\n• “Remind the client to limit intake of alcohol containing beverages.” – Alcohol should be avoided, not merely limited\n\n⚠ Your PowerPoints disagree with this quiz key.\n• The quiz key is shown above (source-backed).\n• Your PowerPoints also support: “Remind the client to limit intake of alcohol containing beverages.”\n• Unit 7 - GI, slide 31: “Limit or decrease ETOH”\nThe quiz key leaves out “Remind the client to limit intake of alcohol containing beverages.” and its note says alcohol should be avoided entirely. Your GI slide says to limit or decrease ETOH, which supports that option. Because the question says to select 3, check with your instructor which three they want."},{"id":438,"t":13,"s":31,"q":"The nurse in the emergency department is caring for a 52-year-old client with abdominal distention and a history of opioid and alcohol abuse. Disoriented to time. Intermittent, slow response to questions during interview. Abdominal striae observed. Asterixis to hands. The client cannot recall the time of last void. T 99.2° F (37.3° C), P 96, RR 18 deep and slow, BP 138/88. Serum potassium level is 3.6 mEq/L. Serum ammonia level is 82 mcg/dL. Which of the following findings would require immediate follow-up?","o":["Respiratory rate and characteristics","Asterixis to hands","Serum potassium level","Serum ammonia level"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Asterixis to hands\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Asterixis to hands” – Asterixis indicates neurologic deterioration\n\nNot correct:\n• “Respiratory rate and characteristics” – Rate is not clearly abnormal in the stem\n• “Serum potassium level” – Potassium is within usual range\n• “Serum ammonia level” – Elevated ammonia supports concern but clinical findings take priority\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":439,"t":9,"s":31,"q":"The registered nurse in the emergency department is caring for a 40-year-old client who has appendicitis and is experiencing localized right upper quadrant pain rated 9/10 on the numerical rating scale. The nurse is preparing to insert an 18-gauge IV.  Which of the following should the nurse include in the client’s care plan? Select all that apply.","o":["Ensure catheter hub is covered with a moist skin barrier.","Review accuracy of IV solution order.","Assess the client’s body weight.","Monitor IV patency every 2 hours.","Evaluate IV insertion site every shift.","Remove catheter within 30 minutes if edema is observed around insertion site."],"a":[1,2,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Review accuracy of IV solution order.\n• Assess the client’s body weight.\n• Monitor IV patency every 2 hours.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Review accuracy of IV solution order.” – Checks prescription accuracy\n• “Assess the client’s body weight.” – Establishes baseline fluid status\n• “Monitor IV patency every 2 hours.” – Checks ongoing IV function\n\nNot correct:\n• “Ensure catheter hub is covered with a moist skin barrier.” – Skin barrier should dry before securement\n• “Evaluate IV insertion site every shift.” – Site needs more frequent assessment\n• “Remove catheter within 30 minutes if edema is observed around insertion site.” – Remove infiltrated peripheral IV promptly\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":440,"t":5,"s":31,"q":"The outpatient clinic nurse is planning to teach a client with a urinary tract infection who has a new prescription for ciprofloxacin-XR tablets, 500 mg, p.o., daily x 7 days.  Which of the following statements should the nurse make?","o":["“Crush the tablet and mix with applesauce if the tablet is too large to swallow.”","“Swallow the medication with a full glass of milk.”","\"Complete the entire prescription even if you are feeling better.”","“Take an over-the-counter antacid 1 hour prior to taking the ciprofloxacin dose.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• \"Complete the entire prescription even if you are feeling better.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “\"Complete the entire prescription even if you are feeling better.”” – Completing course treats infection\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Crush the tablet and mix with applesauce if the tablet is too large to swallow.”” – Crushing disrupts extended release\n• ““Swallow the medication with a full glass of milk.”” – Milk alone can reduce absorption\n• ““Take an over-the-counter antacid 1 hour prior to taking the ciprofloxacin dose.”” – Antacids bind drug; separate as instructed\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":441,"t":7,"s":31,"q":"A patient who takes nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) for arthritis asks a nurse what can be done to prevent ulcers. The nurse will recommend asking the provider about using which medication?","o":["Antibiotics","Histamine-2 receptor antagonists","Proton pump inhibitors","Mucosal protectants"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Proton pump inhibitors\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Proton pump inhibitors” – Preferred acid suppression for NSAID ulcer prevention\n\nNot correct:\n• “Antibiotics” – Treat H. pylori, not NSAID injury alone\n• “Histamine-2 receptor antagonists” – Generally less effective at standard doses\n• “Mucosal protectants” – Misoprostol can prevent ulcers, but source prefers PPIs\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":442,"t":7,"s":31,"q":"Which non-pharmaceutical intervention can be used to reduce symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux disease? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Stopping smoking","Losing weight","Drinking soft drinks","Avoiding late-night meals","Consuming a glass of wine with meals"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Stopping smoking\n• Losing weight\n• Avoiding late-night meals\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Stopping smoking” – Reduces reflux triggers\n• “Losing weight” – Weight reduction can reduce abdominal pressure\n• “Avoiding late-night meals” – Reduces recumbent reflux\n\nNot correct:\n• “Drinking soft drinks” – Carbonation may worsen reflux\n• “Consuming a glass of wine with meals” – Alcohol can worsen reflux\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 7 - GI, slide 8: “Avoid food & fluids 2 hours before HS”\n“Avoiding late-night meals” is on your GERD slide; stopping smoking and losing weight are not listed there."},{"id":443,"t":7,"s":31,"q":"A patient has hypoactive bowel sounds. What is a possible cause of this finding?","o":["Laxative use","Peritonitis","Diarrhea","Gastroenteritis"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Peritonitis\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Peritonitis” – Inflammation may cause ileus\n\nNot correct:\n• “Laxative use” – Generally increases motility\n• “Diarrhea” – Usually increased bowel activity\n• “Gastroenteritis” – Usually increased bowel activity\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":444,"t":4,"s":31,"q":"A nurse is caring for a client who just arrived to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU). The client is mechanically ventilated and the rate of respiration on the ventilator is set at 22. The nurse understands that this client is at risk for which acid-base derangement?","o":["Respiratory Acidosis","Metabolic Acidosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Metabolic Alkalosis"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Respiratory Alkalosis\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Respiratory Alkalosis” – Excess ventilation can lower CO2\n\nNot correct:\n• “Respiratory Acidosis” – CO2 retention reflects inadequate ventilation\n• “Metabolic Acidosis” – Primary bicarbonate loss or acid accumulation\n• “Metabolic Alkalosis” – Primary bicarbonate excess or acid loss\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":445,"t":11,"s":31,"q":"What clinical manifestation should a nurse be alert for when suspecting a diagnosis of esophageal atresia?","o":["It is visually identified at the time of delivery.","A nasogastric tube fails to pass at birth.","The infant has a low birth weight.","A radiograph in the prenatal period indicates abnormal development."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A nasogastric tube fails to pass at birth.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A nasogastric tube fails to pass at birth.” – Tube cannot traverse blind-ending esophagus\n\nNot correct:\n• “It is visually identified at the time of delivery.” – Internal defect is not visible externally\n• “The infant has a low birth weight.” – Nonspecific finding\n• “A radiograph in the prenatal period indicates abnormal development.” – Prenatal ultrasound may suggest it, but this is not the classic sign\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":446,"t":4,"s":31,"q":"The client has been hospitalized with pancreatitis and is being discharged with home health services. The client is severely weakened after this illness. Which nursing intervention is the highest priority in conserving the client's strength?","o":["Arranging for the client to have a nutritional consult to assess the client's diet","Limiting the client's activities to one floor of the home","Instructing the client to take a whenever necessary (PRN) sleeping medication on a nightly basis","Asking the physician for a request for PRN nasal oxygen"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Limiting the client's activities to one floor of the home\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Limiting the client's activities to one floor of the home” – Avoids unnecessary stair climbing\n\nNot correct:\n• “Arranging for the client to have a nutritional consult to assess the client's diet” – Nutrition supports recovery but does not immediately reduce exertion\n• “Instructing the client to take a whenever necessary (PRN) sleeping medication on a nigh…” – Routine sedatives may increase falls and fatigue\n• “Asking the physician for a request for PRN nasal oxygen” – Oxygen requires an indication\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":447,"t":13,"s":31,"q":"The nurse finds a client vomiting coffee ground-type material. On assessment, the client has blood pressure of 100/74 mm Hg, is acutely confused, and has a weak and thready pulse. Which intervention will be the nurse's first priority?","o":["Administering an H2 antagonist","Administering intravenous (IV) fluids","Administering antianxiety medication","Initiating enteral nutrition"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Administering intravenous (IV) fluids\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Administering intravenous (IV) fluids” – Supports circulation during suspected hemorrhage\n\nNot correct:\n• “Administering an H2 antagonist” – Acid suppression does not immediately restore perfusion\n• “Administering antianxiety medication” – Sedation may worsen instability or obscure assessment\n• “Initiating enteral nutrition” – Feeding inappropriate during acute bleeding\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":448,"t":7,"s":31,"q":"When caring for the client 24 hours after a nephrectomy, the nurse notes that the client's abdomen is distended. Which assessment should be made next?","o":["Check the vital signs.","Notify the surgeon.","Continue to monitor.","Insert a nasogastric (NG) tube."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Check the vital signs.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Check the vital signs.” – Assesses instability from possible complication\n\nNot correct:\n• “Notify the surgeon.” – Report after rapid assessment unless emergency demands simultaneous action\n• “Continue to monitor.” – New distention warrants assessment\n• “Insert a nasogastric (NG) tube.” – Tube insertion requires indication and order\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":449,"t":7,"s":31,"q":"Which statement by the client who is prescribed pancreatic enzyme replacements indicates a need for further teaching by the nurse?","o":["“I need to take the enzymes at every meal and with snacks.”","“I should chew each capsule carefully so that it works in my stomach.”","\"I should wipe my mouth in case any of the enzymes got on my lips.”"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I should chew each capsule carefully so that it works in my stomach.”\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I should chew each capsule carefully so that it works in my stomach.”” – Chewing can damage enteric coating and irritate mucosa\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I need to take the enzymes at every meal and with snacks.”” – Enzymes aid digestion with food\n• “\"I should wipe my mouth in case any of the enzymes got on my lips.”” – Cleaning lips prevents local irritation\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - Pediatrics, slide 25: “Pancreatic enzymes with each meal”\nThis supports taking the enzymes at every meal and with snacks being correct; chewing the capsules is not addressed on your slides."},{"id":450,"t":5,"s":31,"q":"The nurse is providing care for a client who has serum potassium level of 6.0mEq/L.  The nurse should assess for which of the following manifestations?  (Select all that apply.)","o":["seizures","hypoactive bowel sounds","dysrhythmias (premature ventricular contractions)","confusion"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• dysrhythmias (premature ventricular contractions)\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “dysrhythmias (premature ventricular contractions)” – Elevated potassium disrupts cardiac conduction\n\nNot correct:\n• “seizures” – Not a classic isolated hyperkalemia manifestation\n• “hypoactive bowel sounds” – Uncertain: usually hypokalemia causes reduced motility; source lacks key\n• “confusion” – Nonspecific; not classic isolated hyperkalemia\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 7 - Renal, slide 7: “Monitor with EKG”\nYour slide lists hyperkalemia with an EKG for acute kidney injury; the specific dysrhythmia is not named."},{"id":451,"t":5,"s":31,"q":"The nurse is providing care for a client who has a diagnosis of hypercalcemia.  Which of the following would be an expected finding? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Trousseau's sign","weakness, lethargy","increased deep tendon reflexes","muscle spasm","kidney stones","dcreased reflexes","bone pain"],"a":[1,4,5,6],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• weakness, lethargy\n• kidney stones\n• dcreased reflexes\n• bone pain\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “weakness, lethargy” – High calcium depresses neuromuscular activity\n• “kidney stones” – Hypercalciuria can promote stones\n• “dcreased reflexes” – Neuromuscular excitability decreases\n• “bone pain” – May reflect underlying bone resorption\n\nNot correct:\n• “Trousseau's sign” – Suggests hypocalcemia\n• “increased deep tendon reflexes” – Suggests hypocalcemia\n• “muscle spasm” – Tetany more typical of low calcium\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 2 - Physiological Adaptation, slide 12: “Muscle weakness”\n• Unit 2 - Physiological Adaptation, slide 12: “Deep bone pain”\n• Unit 2 - Physiological Adaptation, slide 12: “Calcium stones”\n• Unit 2 - Reduction of Risk Potential, slide 29: “Muscle twitching. Yes, a sign of hypocalcemia.”\nTrousseau sign, muscle spasm and hyperactive reflexes are hypocalcemia signs on your Unit 2 slide, which rules those options out; decreased reflexes are not named on your slides."},{"id":452,"t":5,"s":31,"q":"A nurse cares for a client who has kidney stones from gout ricemia. Which medication does the nurse anticipate administering?A nurse cares for a client who has kidney stones from gout ricemia. Which medication does the nurse anticipate administering?","o":["Allopurinol","Tolterodine","Phenazopyridine","Doxycyline"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Allopurinol\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Allopurinol” – Reduces uric acid production\n\nNot correct:\n• “Tolterodine” – Treats overactive bladder\n• “Phenazopyridine” – Relieves urinary discomfort only\n• “Doxycyline” – Antibiotic does not lower uric acid\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":453,"t":5,"s":31,"q":"A nurse teaches a client with functional urinary incontinence. Which statement would the nurse include in this client’s teaching?","o":["“You might want to get pants with elastic waistbands.”","“You might want to get pants with a zipper and button waistbands.”","“You will need to be on your drug therapy for life.”","“You must clean around your catheter daily with soap and water.”"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “You might want to get pants with elastic waistbands.”\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““You might want to get pants with elastic waistbands.”” – Makes clothing easier to remove\n\nNot correct:\n• ““You might want to get pants with a zipper and button waistbands.”” – Fasteners can delay toileting\n• ““You will need to be on your drug therapy for life.”” – Functional barriers do not require lifelong drugs\n• ““You must clean around your catheter daily with soap and water.”” – Catheter is not routinely indicated\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":454,"t":12,"s":31,"q":"Which finding indicates that a child receiving prednisone for primary nephrotic syndrome is in remission?","o":["Urine is negative for casts for 5 days.","Urine is positive for glucose for 1 week.","Urine has 1+ protein for 3 to 7 consecutive days.","Urine is up to a trace for blood for 1 week."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Urine has 1+ protein for 3 to 7 consecutive days.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Urine has 1+ protein for 3 to 7 consecutive days.” – Source criterion; modern complete remission generally negative or trace\n\nNot correct:\n• “Urine is negative for casts for 5 days.” – Casts do not establish remission\n• “Urine is positive for glucose for 1 week.” – Glucose is not a proteinuria measure\n• “Urine is up to a trace for blood for 1 week.” – Blood does not establish remission\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":455,"t":12,"s":31,"q":"The most appropriate nursing diagnosis for the child with acute glomerulonephritis is","o":["Risk for Imbalanced Fluid Volume related to a decrease in plasma filtration.","Excess Fluid Volume related to fluid accumulation in tissues and third spaces.","Deficient Fluid Volume related to excessive losses.","Risk for Injury related to malignant process and treatment."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Excess Fluid Volume related to fluid accumulation in tissues and third spaces.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Excess Fluid Volume related to fluid accumulation in tissues and third spaces.” – Reduced filtration promotes water and sodium retention\n\nNot correct:\n• “Risk for Imbalanced Fluid Volume related to a decrease in plasma filtration.” – Actual fluid excess is more specific in this disease\n• “Deficient Fluid Volume related to excessive losses.” – Fluid retention predominates\n• “Risk for Injury related to malignant process and treatment.” – Not a malignant disorder\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":456,"t":3,"s":31,"q":"A client with a history of COPD and heart failure comes to the emergency department stating he feels palpitations in his heart. The nurse places the client on a heart monitor and obtains the following electrocardiographic (ECG) tracing.\nWhich action should the nurse take next?\n[Practice EKG Strips (class 7 review) | Ekg strips, School health ...](https://i.pinimg.com/originals/12/d9/a4/12d9a4898513e236a30242d310882658.png)","o":["Obtain the client’s vital signs including oxygen saturation.","Prepare to administer Adenosine IV","Obtain stat blood work, including cardiac enzymes","Prepare to the client for a cardiac catherization."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Obtain the client’s vital signs including oxygen saturation.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Obtain the client’s vital signs including oxygen saturation.” – Determine hemodynamic stability first\n\nNot correct:\n• “Prepare to administer Adenosine IV” – Only appropriate for selected rhythms\n• “Obtain stat blood work, including cardiac enzymes” – Labs do not replace immediate assessment\n• “Prepare to the client for a cardiac catherization.” – Requires separate indication; tracing unverified\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":457,"t":3,"s":31,"q":"A client admitted with acute dyspnea is newly diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy. Which information will the nurse plan to teach the client about managing this disorder?","o":["Notify the doctor about any symptoms of heart failure such as shortness of breath.","Careful compliance with diet and medications will prevent heart failure.","Elevating the legs above the heart will help relieve dyspnea.","A heart transplant should be scheduled as soon as possible."],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Notify the doctor about any symptoms of heart failure such as shortness of breath.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Notify the doctor about any symptoms of heart failure such as shortness of breath.” – Early reporting may prevent worsening decompensation\n\nNot correct:\n• “Careful compliance with diet and medications will prevent heart failure.” – Adherence reduces risk but cannot guarantee prevention\n• “Elevating the legs above the heart will help relieve dyspnea.” – Leg elevation may increase venous return and worsen dyspnea\n• “A heart transplant should be scheduled as soon as possible.” – Transplant reserved for selected advanced disease\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":458,"t":5,"s":31,"q":"Which statement made by the client with chronic kidney disease (CKD) regarding discharge instructions requires immediate follow up by the nurse? Select all that apply.","o":["I will measure and log my blood pressure daily.","“I need to take erythropoietin to boost my immune system and help prevent infection.”","“I need to get most of my protein from low-fat dairy products.”","“I will increase my intake of fruits and vegetables to 5 per day.”"],"a":[1,2,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• “I need to take erythropoietin to boost my immune system and help prevent infection.”\n• “I need to get most of my protein from low-fat dairy products.”\n• “I will increase my intake of fruits and vegetables to 5 per day.”\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I need to take erythropoietin to boost my immune system and help prevent infection.”” – Erythropoietin stimulates red cell production\n• ““I need to get most of my protein from low-fat dairy products.”” – Dairy phosphorus intake needs individualized advice\n• ““I will increase my intake of fruits and vegetables to 5 per day.”” – Produce intake depends on potassium and renal diet needs\n\nNot correct:\n• “I will measure and log my blood pressure daily.” – Daily BP monitoring is appropriate\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 7 - Renal, slide 11: “Low protein”\n• Unit 7 - Renal, slide 3: “Stimulation of red blood cell (RBC) production”\nYour slide gives a low-protein, low-sodium, low-potassium, low-phosphate renal diet, and lists red blood cell production (not immunity) as the kidney function erythropoietin supports."},{"id":459,"t":3,"s":31,"q":"The nurse has received change-of-shift report about the following clients on the progressive care unit. Which client should the nurse see first?","o":["A post aortic valvular repair client receiving warfarin and INR of 3.4","A client whose implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) fired two times today who has a dose of amiodarone due","A client with new onset atrial fibrillation, rate 88, who has a first dose of warfarin due","A client with pericarditis oral temperature is 101.2 F."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A client whose implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) fired two times today who has a dose of amiodarone due\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A client whose implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) fired two times today who h…” – Recurrent ICD shocks suggest important ventricular arrhythmia\n\nNot correct:\n• “A post aortic valvular repair client receiving warfarin and INR of 3.4” – INR may be appropriate depending on valve and target\n• “A client with new onset atrial fibrillation, rate 88, who has a first dose of warfarin due” – Controlled rate makes this less urgent\n• “A client with pericarditis oral temperature is 101.2 F.” – Fever needs evaluation but repeated shocks take priority\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":460,"t":3,"s":31,"q":"The nurse is caring for a patient who develops marked edema and a low urine output as a result of heart failure. Which medication will the nurse expect the provider to order for this patient?","o":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Digoxin","Spironolactone","Furosemide"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Furosemide\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Furosemide” – Loop diuretic promotes brisk fluid removal\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hydrochlorothiazide” – Less potent for marked congestion\n• “Digoxin” – Improves contractility/rate but is not a diuretic\n• “Spironolactone” – Weak diuretic relative to loop agents\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 8: “Used when thiazides fail or need rapid diuresis”"},{"id":461,"t":3,"s":31,"q":"A patient has been taking spironolactone to treat heart failure. The nurse will monitor for","o":["hypocalcemia.","hypoglycemia.","hypermagnesemia","hyperkalemia."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• hyperkalemia.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “hyperkalemia.” – Potassium-sparing action can raise potassium\n\nNot correct:\n• “hypocalcemia.” – Not hallmark adverse effect\n• “hypoglycemia.” – Not hallmark adverse effect\n• “hypermagnesemia” – Not the primary electrolyte risk\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 8: “Monitor for hyperkalemia”"},{"id":462,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse in the community clinic is caring for a 72-year-old client who has heart failure and is taking prescribed spironolactone.\nWhich of the following should the nurse include in the client’s care plan?","o":["Encourage the client to prepare foods using potassium salt substitutes.","Reinforce teaching for the client about the low sodium diet.","Remind the client to complete all activities of daily living within 1 hour of awakening.","Tell the client to walk their dog for no more than 5 minutes per day as the primary exercise modality."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Reinforce teaching for the client about the low sodium diet.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Reinforce teaching for the client about the low sodium diet.” – Less sodium reduces fluid retention\n\nNot correct:\n• “Encourage the client to prepare foods using potassium salt substitutes.” – Potassium-sparing drug raises hyperkalemia risk\n• “Remind the client to complete all activities of daily living within 1 hour of awakening.” – Clusters activity and may increase fatigue\n• “Tell the client to walk their dog for no more than 5 minutes per day as the primary exe…” – Exercise should be individualized, not arbitrarily limited\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 39: “Client may be placed on a low sodium diet”\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 8: “Monitor for hyperkalemia”"},{"id":463,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"There are 4 chambers of the heart.  Which of the following is accurate regarding the left atrium?  (Select all that apply.)","o":["This chamber sends blood to the left ventricle through the aorta (aortic) valve","This chamber receives blood from the lungs via the pulmonary arteries","This chamber receives blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins","This chamber pumps blood to the left ventricle","This chamber sends blood through the mitral valve","This chamber sends blood to the left ventricle through the tricuspid valve"],"a":[2,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• This chamber receives blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins\n• This chamber pumps blood to the left ventricle\n• This chamber sends blood through the mitral valve\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “This chamber receives blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins” – Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood\n• “This chamber pumps blood to the left ventricle” – Left atrium empties into LV\n• “This chamber sends blood through the mitral valve” – Mitral valve separates LA and LV\n\nNot correct:\n• “This chamber sends blood to the left ventricle through the aorta (aortic) valve” – Aortic valve connects LV to aorta\n• “This chamber receives blood from the lungs via the pulmonary arteries” – Pulmonary arteries carry blood away from right ventricle\n• “This chamber sends blood to the left ventricle through the tricuspid valve” – Tricuspid valve is right-sided\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":464,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse is caring for a patient who is having issues with their tricuspid valve. The nurse is explaining to the patient about the tricuspid valve. Which of the following is accurate? (Select all that apply.)","o":["The tricuspid valve is also known as the right atrioventricular valve because it sits between the ventricle and the aorta","This valve is also known as the tricuspid valve because it has 3 flaps","This valve is open when the heart pumps blood out to the body","The tricuspid valve is also known as the right atrioventricular valve because it sits between the right atrium and the right ventricle","This valve is closed when the heart pumps blood out to the body"],"a":[1,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• This valve is also known as the tricuspid valve because it has 3 flaps\n• The tricuspid valve is also known as the right atrioventricular valve because it sits between the right atrium and the right ventricle\n• This valve is closed when the heart pumps blood out to the body\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “This valve is also known as the tricuspid valve because it has 3 flaps” – Three leaflets\n• “The tricuspid valve is also known as the right atrioventricular valve because it sits b…” – Correct right AV valve location\n• “This valve is closed when the heart pumps blood out to the body” – Closes to prevent backflow during systole\n\nNot correct:\n• “The tricuspid valve is also known as the right atrioventricular valve because it sits b…” – Valve is between right atrium and ventricle\n• “This valve is open when the heart pumps blood out to the body” – Closes during ventricular systole\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":465,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse is assessing the client's aorta. Which area of the aorta is the longest?","o":["abdominal aorta","thoracic aorta","ascending aorta","aortic aorta"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• thoracic aorta\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “thoracic aorta” – Longest in the source classification\n\nNot correct:\n• “abdominal aorta” – Shorter than descending thoracic segment in the source framing\n• “ascending aorta” – Relatively short proximal segment\n• “aortic aorta” – Not an anatomical term\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":466,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer an ACE inhibitor to a patient who has hypertension. The nurse notes peripheral edema and swelling of the patient’s lips. The patient has a blood pressure of 160/80 mm Hg and a heart rate of 76 beats per minute. What is the nurse’s next action?","o":["Administer the dose and observe carefully for hypotension.","Notify the provider and request an order for a diuretic medication.","Request an order for serum electrolytes and renal function tests.","Hold the dose and notify the provider of a hypersensitivity reaction."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Hold the dose and notify the provider of a hypersensitivity reaction.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Hold the dose and notify the provider of a hypersensitivity reaction.” – Hold ACE inhibitor; evaluate airway urgently\n\nNot correct:\n• “Administer the dose and observe carefully for hypotension.” – Giving another dose risks airway swelling\n• “Notify the provider and request an order for a diuretic medication.” – Does not treat angioedema\n• “Request an order for serum electrolytes and renal function tests.” – Labs do not address immediate airway risk\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":467,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse is administering a beta-blocker to a patient. Which is the most important assessment to perform before administration?","o":["Serum level of medication","Potassium level","Urine output","Apical pulse"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Apical pulse\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Apical pulse” – Assess for bradycardia before beta blockade\n\nNot correct:\n• “Serum level of medication” – Routine serum drug levels not required\n• “Potassium level” – Not the most direct immediate assessment\n• “Urine output” – Not primary before dose\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 10: “Check apical pulse and B/P before administering”"},{"id":468,"t":16,"s":32,"q":"During an assessment of a healthy adult, where would the nurse expect to palpate the apical impulse?","o":["Third left intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Fourth left intercostal space at the anterior axillary line","Fourth left intercostal space at the sternal border","Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line” – Usual adult PMI location\n\nNot correct:\n• “Third left intercostal space at the midclavicular line” – Too superior\n• “Fourth left intercostal space at the anterior axillary line” – Too lateral and superior\n• “Fourth left intercostal space at the sternal border” – Too medial and superior\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - Pediatrics, slide 26: “Left midclavicular line at the fifth intercostal space in children ages 7 and over”\nYour slide gives this for children age 7 and over; the adult placement is not stated separately."},{"id":469,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"What should the nurse do when assessing the carotid arteries of an older patient with cardiovascular disease?","o":["Simultaneously palpate both arteries to compare amplitude.","Instruct the patient to take slow deep breaths during auscultation.","Palpate the artery in the upper one-third of the neck.","Listen with the bell of the stethoscope to assess for bruits."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Listen with the bell of the stethoscope to assess for bruits.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Listen with the bell of the stethoscope to assess for bruits.” – Bell detects low-pitched vascular bruit\n\nNot correct:\n• “Simultaneously palpate both arteries to compare amplitude.” – Bilateral pressure can reduce cerebral perfusion\n• “Instruct the patient to take slow deep breaths during auscultation.” – Breath sounds interfere with bruit assessment\n• “Palpate the artery in the upper one-third of the neck.” – Avoid carotid sinus pressure\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":470,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"A patient is ordered to receive a nitrate to relieve stable angina. What side effect(s) will the nurse anticipate in a patient receiving this medication?","o":["Increased blood pressure","Pounding headache","Nausea and vomiting","Pruritus and skin rash"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Pounding headache\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Pounding headache” – Vasodilation commonly causes headache\n\nNot correct:\n• “Increased blood pressure” – Nitrates usually lower BP\n• “Nausea and vomiting” – Possible but less typical\n• “Pruritus and skin rash” – Not characteristic nitrate effect\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":471,"t":10,"s":32,"q":"A patient has congestive HF and has been taking digoxin for 9 years. The patient is admitted with signs and symptoms of digoxin toxicity. Which signs and symptoms are associated with digoxin toxicity? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Tachycardia","Insomnia","Dysuria","Vomiting","Diarrhea","Yellow haloes in the visual field"],"a":[3,4,5],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Vomiting\n• Diarrhea\n• Yellow haloes in the visual field\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Vomiting” – GI toxicity symptom\n• “Diarrhea” – GI toxicity symptom\n• “Yellow haloes in the visual field” – Characteristic visual disturbance\n\nNot correct:\n• “Tachycardia” – Bradycardia or other arrhythmias are more typical, though tachyarrhythmias can occur\n• “Insomnia” – Not characteristic\n• “Dysuria” – Not characteristic\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 36: “GI disturbances”\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 36: “Visual changes”"},{"id":472,"t":13,"s":32,"q":"The client who has been admitted for the third time this year for cardiac failure says, \"This isn't worth it anymore. I just want it all to end.\" What is the nurse's best response?","o":["Considers further assessment for depression","Calls the family to lift the client's spirits","Tells the client that things will get better","Sedates the client to decrease myocardial oxygen demand"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Considers further assessment for depression\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Considers further assessment for depression” – Assess depression and suicidal intent directly\n\nNot correct:\n• “Calls the family to lift the client's spirits” – Does not assess safety\n• “Tells the client that things will get better” – False reassurance\n• “Sedates the client to decrease myocardial oxygen demand” – Sedation does not address suicidal thoughts\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":473,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA). Which finding in the history does the nurse recognize as a risk factor for aneurysm formation?","o":["Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)","Osteoarthritis","Marfan syndrome","Peptic ulcer disease"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Marfan syndrome\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Marfan syndrome” – Connective tissue weakness predisposes to aneurysm\n\nNot correct:\n• “Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)” – Venous thrombosis is not a typical cause\n• “Osteoarthritis” – Degenerative joint disease is not a risk factor\n• “Peptic ulcer disease” – Not a typical risk factor\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":474,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"Which client has pain most consistent with myocardial infarction (MI) requiring notification of the health care provider?","o":["A client with dyspnea on exertion (DOE) and inability to sleep flat who sleeps on four pillows","A client with claudication and fatigue","A client with pressure in the mid abdomen and profound diaphoresis","A client with abdominal pain and belching"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• A client with pressure in the mid abdomen and profound diaphoresis\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “A client with pressure in the mid abdomen and profound diaphoresis” – Epigastric pressure with diaphoresis can indicate MI\n\nNot correct:\n• “A client with dyspnea on exertion (DOE) and inability to sleep flat who sleeps on four …” – Suggests chronic heart failure\n• “A client with claudication and fatigue” – Suggests peripheral arterial disease\n• “A client with abdominal pain and belching” – May be GI-related; less typical without other cues\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":475,"t":13,"s":32,"q":"The nurse working on a step-down unit has just received report on the following patients.  The nurse should first assess the patent with","o":["stable VS who returned 40 min ago after a PTCA.","angina whose chest pain was relieved by two nitroglycerin tablets taken two hours ago.","angina currently, nausea and increased anxiety.","complaints of a headache who was transferred from CCU earlier after recovering from a MI."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• angina currently, nausea and increased anxiety.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “angina currently, nausea and increased anxiety.” – Current angina with nausea may indicate acute ischemia\n\nNot correct:\n• “stable VS who returned 40 min ago after a PTCA.” – Recent PCI requires monitoring but stable vital signs listed\n• “angina whose chest pain was relieved by two nitroglycerin tablets taken two hours ago.” – Pain resolved earlier\n• “complaints of a headache who was transferred from CCU earlier after recovering from a MI.” – Assess headache after acute ischemic concern\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":476,"t":10,"s":32,"q":"A patient has been prescribed cilostazol. Which statement by the client indicates the need for further teaching?","o":["“This medication is used to improve blood flow.”","“I can continue to take ginkgo biloba to improve my memory.”","“I can take the medication with food if it causes nausea.”","“I should be cautious when standing.”"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I can continue to take ginkgo biloba to improve my memory.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I can continue to take ginkgo biloba to improve my memory.”” – Ginkgo may increase bleeding risk\n\nNot correct:\n• ““This medication is used to improve blood flow.”” – Increases walking distance via vasodilation and antiplatelet effects\n• ““I can take the medication with food if it causes nausea.”” – Food timing actually should follow label; take on empty stomach\n• ““I should be cautious when standing.”” – Drug can cause dizziness\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":477,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"The nurse is assessing a patient who takes warfarin. The nurse notes a heart rate of 92 beats per minute and a blood pressure of 88/78 mm Hg. To evaluate the reason for these vital signs, the nurse will assess the patient’s (select an answer from the following options):","o":["skin turgor and oral mucous membranes.","gums, nose, and skin.","lung sounds and respiratory effort.","urine output and level of consciousness."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• gums, nose, and skin.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “gums, nose, and skin.” – Look for evidence of anticoagulant-associated bleeding\n\nNot correct:\n• “skin turgor and oral mucous membranes.” – Evaluates dehydration but not specifically bleeding\n• “lung sounds and respiratory effort.” – Not the most targeted initial bleeding check\n• “urine output and level of consciousness.” – Helpful for perfusion but less direct bleeding assessment\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":478,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"After receiving change-of-shift report about the following four clients, which client should the nurse assess first?","o":["59-year-old with unstable angina who has just returned to the unit after having a percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)","56-year-old with Atrial Fibrillation who is to receive a dose of warfarin po.","54 year old with coronary artery disease complains of shortness of breath when ambulating.","65-year-old who had a myocardial infarction (MI) 4 days ago and is anxious about the planned discharge"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• 59-year-old with unstable angina who has just returned to the unit after having a percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “59-year-old with unstable angina who has just returned to the unit after having a percu…” – Immediate post-PCI assessment for bleeding and perfusion\n\nNot correct:\n• “56-year-old with Atrial Fibrillation who is to receive a dose of warfarin po.” – Scheduled anticoagulant less urgent if stable\n• “54 year old with coronary artery disease complains of shortness of breath when ambulating.” – Exertional symptom less urgent than immediate postprocedure\n• “65-year-old who had a myocardial infarction (MI) 4 days ago and is anxious about the pl…” – Discharge anxiety less urgent\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":479,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"A client who was admitted with a myocardial infarction experiences a 15-second episode of ventricular tachycardia, then converts to sinus rhythm with a heart rate of 98 beats/minute. Which of the following actions should the nurse take next?","o":["Document the rhythm and continue to monitor the client.","Prepare to start IV Heparin protocol.","Obtain stat cardiac enzymes","Prepare to give IV amiodarone"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Document the rhythm and continue to monitor the client.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Document the rhythm and continue to monitor the client.” – Source expects continued monitoring after self-termination; assess and notify as appropriate\n\nNot correct:\n• “Prepare to start IV Heparin protocol.” – Heparin does not specifically treat VT\n• “Obtain stat cardiac enzymes” – Enzymes do not immediately address arrhythmia\n• “Prepare to give IV amiodarone” – Antiarrhythmic depends on recurrence and stability\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":480,"t":12,"s":32,"q":"A child has beta-thalassemia and is receiving deferoxamine. The parent asks what the purpose of this medication is. Which response by the nurse is best?","o":["“To prepare your child for a bone marrow transplant”","“To improve the anemia”","“To decrease liver and spleen swelling”","“To eliminate excessive iron being stored in the organs”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “To eliminate excessive iron being stored in the organs”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““To eliminate excessive iron being stored in the organs”” – Chelates excess iron from transfusions\n\nNot correct:\n• ““To prepare your child for a bone marrow transplant”” – Not the specific indication\n• ““To improve the anemia”” – Does not directly improve anemia\n• ““To decrease liver and spleen swelling”” – Not direct purpose\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":481,"t":3,"s":32,"q":"Blood type B can donate to: (select all that apply)","o":["O","B","AB","A"],"a":[1,2],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• B\n• AB\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “B” – B recipients can receive B RBCs\n• “AB” – AB recipients can receive B RBCs\n\nNot correct:\n• “O” – Type O recipients cannot receive B RBCs\n• “A” – A recipients have anti-B antibodies\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":482,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"A 55-year-old client arrived to the outpatient clinic for an annual visit and the nurse is planning to teach about enhancing the quality of life. Which of the following actions should the nurse implement first?","o":["Adapt teaching modalities to the client’s sensory limitations.","Encourage the client to set goals.","Promote the client to learn independently about work-life-balance.","Shorten the client’s teaching sessions."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Encourage the client to set goals.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Encourage the client to set goals.” – Collaborative goal-setting guides teaching\n\nNot correct:\n• “Adapt teaching modalities to the client’s sensory limitations.” – No sensory limitation stated\n• “Promote the client to learn independently about work-life-balance.” – Independent learning follows goal-setting\n• “Shorten the client’s teaching sessions.” – No need to shorten sessions absent limitations\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":483,"t":8,"s":33,"q":"A 32-year-old female client arrived at the outpatient clinic for an annual physical examination. Which of the following would be most advisable for the nurse to continue to monitor?","o":["Fall hazard.","Cognitive aptitude.","Intimate partner violence.","Bone density."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Intimate partner violence.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Intimate partner violence.” – Screen for intimate partner violence\n\nNot correct:\n• “Fall hazard.” – More typical priority in older adults\n• “Cognitive aptitude.” – No cognitive concern stated\n• “Bone density.” – More typical focus in older or at-risk adults\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":484,"t":13,"s":33,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client with diabetes experiences breakdown of fats for conversion to glucose. The nurse determines that this response is occurring if the client has elevated levels of which of the following substances?","o":["Glucose","Glucagon","Ketones","Lactic dehydrogenase"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Ketones\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Ketones” – Ketones form from fatty acid breakdown\n\nNot correct:\n• “Glucose” – Hyperglycemia is not specific to fat breakdown\n• “Glucagon” – Promotes mobilization but not the direct marker\n• “Lactic dehydrogenase” – Nonspecific enzyme marker\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":485,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"The nurse understands that which of the following hormones is directly responsible for maintaining the free or unbound portion of serum calcium within normal limits?","o":["Follicle-stimulating hormone","Thyroid hormone","Adrenocorticotropic hormone","Parathyroid hormone"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Parathyroid hormone\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Parathyroid hormone” – Regulates circulating calcium\n\nNot correct:\n• “Follicle-stimulating hormone” – Regulates gonadal function\n• “Thyroid hormone” – Controls metabolism\n• “Adrenocorticotropic hormone” – Stimulates adrenal cortex\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 9 - Endocrine, slide 14: “Hormone (PTH) regulates calcium & phosphorus levels”"},{"id":486,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"A patient with Grave's disease exhibits tachycardia, heat intolerance, and exophthalmos. Prior to surgery, which drug is used to alter thyroid hormone levels?","o":["Propylthiouracil","Thyroid","Propranolol","Liotrix"],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Propylthiouracil\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Propylthiouracil” – Inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis and peripheral T4 conversion\n\nNot correct:\n• “Thyroid” – Would replace thyroid hormone\n• “Propranolol” – Controls adrenergic symptoms rather than synthesis\n• “Liotrix” – Thyroid replacement hormone\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 9 - Endocrine, slide 11: “Antithyroid medication methimazole (Tapazole) blocks the production of thyroid hormones”\nPropylthiouracil is an antithyroid drug like the methimazole on your slide (it is not named there); your slide also says iodine is given before surgery."},{"id":487,"t":5,"s":33,"q":"A client who takes high-dose aspirin to treat arthritis will need to take prednisone to treat an acute flare of symptoms. What action will the nurse perform?","o":["Monitor closely for increased urine output.","Observe the patient for hypotension.","Observe the patient for hypoglycemia.","Request an order for enteric-coated aspirin."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Request an order for enteric-coated aspirin.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Request an order for enteric-coated aspirin.” – Source expects GI protection; enteric coating does not eliminate bleeding risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “Monitor closely for increased urine output.” – Not primary interaction concern\n• “Observe the patient for hypotension.” – Not primary interaction concern\n• “Observe the patient for hypoglycemia.” – Prednisone more often raises glucose\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":488,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"The nurse is teaching a client about home administration of insulin. The client will receive regular and NPH insulin at 0700 every day. What is important to teach this client?","o":["“Draw up the NPH insulin first.”","“Draw up the medications after mixing them in a vial.”","“Draw up the regular insulin first.”","“Draw up the medications in separate syringes.”"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “Draw up the regular insulin first.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““Draw up the regular insulin first.”” – Clear regular before cloudy NPH\n\nNot correct:\n• ““Draw up the NPH insulin first.”” – Cloudy insulin can contaminate regular vial\n• ““Draw up the medications after mixing them in a vial.”” – Do not mix inside storage vials\n• ““Draw up the medications in separate syringes.”” – Compatible insulins can be mixed when prescribed\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":489,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"The nurse receives the following order for insulin: IV NPH (Humulin NPH) 10 units. The nurse will perform which action?","o":["Give the drug subcutaneously.","Question the insulin dose.","Clarify the insulin type and route.","Administer the dose as ordered."],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Clarify the insulin type and route.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Clarify the insulin type and route.” – NPH is not for IV use; clarify\n\nNot correct:\n• “Give the drug subcutaneously.” – Do not independently change prescribed route\n• “Question the insulin dose.” – Dose alone is not the central issue\n• “Administer the dose as ordered.” – Unsafe IV administration of NPH\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":490,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"The client recently admitted with hyperparathyroidism has a very high urine output. Of the following actions, what does the nurse do next?","o":["Performs an immediate cardiac assessment","Monitors intake and output","Calls the health care provider","Slows the rate of IV fluids"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Monitors intake and output\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Monitors intake and output” – Quantifies ongoing volume loss\n\nNot correct:\n• “Performs an immediate cardiac assessment” – Relevant with cardiac symptoms but polyuria needs fluid assessment\n• “Calls the health care provider” – Gather assessment data unless urgent instability\n• “Slows the rate of IV fluids” – Could worsen dehydration\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":491,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"The client with Cushing's disease begins to laugh loudly and inappropriately, causing the family in the room to be uncomfortable. What is the nurse's best response?","o":["\"Don't mind this. The disease is causing this.\"","\"The disease can sometimes affect emotional responses.\"","\"Medication is available to help with this.\"","\"I need to check the client's cortisol level.\""],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• \"The disease can sometimes affect emotional responses.\"\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “\"The disease can sometimes affect emotional responses.\"” – Explains cortisol-related mood changes\n\nNot correct:\n• “\"Don't mind this. The disease is causing this.\"” – Dismisses family concern\n• “\"Medication is available to help with this.\"” – Prematurely proposes medication\n• “\"I need to check the client's cortisol level.\"” – Does not answer emotional concern\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":492,"t":3,"s":33,"q":"A student nurse asks an RN on the medical-surgical floor what effect can starting a dose of levothyroxine sodium (Synthroid) too high or increasing a dose too rapidly have on a client. What effect does the RN tell the student nurse?","o":["Hypotension and shock","Hypertension and heart failure","Decreased respiratory rate","Bradycardia and decreased level of consciousness"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Hypertension and heart failure\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Hypertension and heart failure” – Excess replacement can strain heart and raise BP\n\nNot correct:\n• “Hypotension and shock” – More consistent with severe hormone deficiency\n• “Decreased respiratory rate” – Not typical of excess thyroid hormone\n• “Bradycardia and decreased level of consciousness” – More consistent with hypothyroidism\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":493,"t":10,"s":33,"q":"The nurse carefully observes for toxicity of drugs excreted through the kidney. Which of these represents a sign or symptom of digoxin toxicity?","o":["Polyphagia","Serum digoxin level of 1.2 ng/mL","Anorexia","Serum potassium of 5.0 mEq/L"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Anorexia\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Anorexia” – Classic GI toxicity sign\n\nNot correct:\n• “Polyphagia” – Toxicity commonly reduces appetite\n• “Serum digoxin level of 1.2 ng/mL” – May be within range; symptoms and target matter\n• “Serum potassium of 5.0 mEq/L” – Upper normal range is not diagnostic alone\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 36: “GI disturbances”\n• Unit 8 - Cardiac, slide 36: “Normal digoxin level – 0.5-2 ng/ml”"},{"id":494,"t":1,"s":33,"q":"The client is being discharged with hypothyroidism. Which environmental change may the client experience in the home?","o":["Strict infection control measures","Frequent home care","Increased thermostat setting","Handrails in the bath"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Increased thermostat setting\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Increased thermostat setting” – Cold intolerance may require more warmth\n\nNot correct:\n• “Strict infection control measures” – No special infection measures solely for hypothyroidism\n• “Frequent home care” – Not universally required\n• “Handrails in the bath” – Not specific to hypothyroidism\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":495,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"The client admitted with hyperthyroidism is fidgeting with the bedcovers and talking extremely fast. What does the nurse do next?","o":["Calls the provider","Encourages the client to rest","Immediately assesses cardiac status","Tells the client to slow down"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Immediately assesses cardiac status\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Immediately assesses cardiac status” – Tachyarrhythmia may accompany severe thyrotoxicosis\n\nNot correct:\n• “Calls the provider” – Assess current physiologic stability first\n• “Encourages the client to rest” – Rest alone does not evaluate cardiac risk\n• “Tells the client to slow down” – Dismissive and does not assess danger\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":496,"t":4,"s":33,"q":"The client has undergone a trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy. Which intervention does the nurse implement to avoid increasing intracranial pressure (ICP) in the client?","o":["Encourages the client to cough and deep-breathe","Places the client in Trendelenburg position","Instructs the client not to strain during a bowel movement","Instructs the client to blow his nose for postnasal drip"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Instructs the client not to strain during a bowel movement\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Instructs the client not to strain during a bowel movement” – Avoid Valsalva and straining\n\nNot correct:\n• “Encourages the client to cough and deep-breathe” – Forceful coughing increases ICP\n• “Places the client in Trendelenburg position” – Head-down positioning increases venous pressure\n• “Instructs the client to blow his nose for postnasal drip” – Nose blowing may disrupt surgical site\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":497,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"Which statement by the client with diabetic nephropathy indicates a need for further education about the disease?","o":["\"I need less insulin, so I am getting better.\"","\"I may need to reduce my insulin.\"","\"Diabetes is the leading cause of kidney failure.\"","\"I must call my provider if the urine dipstick shows protein.\""],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• \"I need less insulin, so I am getting better.\"\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “\"I need less insulin, so I am getting better.\"” – Reduced kidney clearance may lower insulin needs despite worsening disease\n\nNot correct:\n• “\"I may need to reduce my insulin.\"” – Renal impairment can reduce insulin clearance\n• “\"Diabetes is the leading cause of kidney failure.\"” – Diabetes is a major cause of kidney failure\n• “\"I must call my provider if the urine dipstick shows protein.\"” – Proteinuria warrants provider follow-up\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":498,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"Which of the following statements is appropriate regarding glucose? (select all that apply.)","o":["It is the main component of starch and glycogen","No answer text provided.","It is the main component of start and glycogen","No answer text provided.","it is also called blood sugar","it is the most common disaccharide in our diet.","it is the most important carbohydrate fuel for the human body"],"a":[0,2,4,6],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• It is the main component of starch and glycogen\n• It is the main component of start and glycogen\n• it is also called blood sugar\n• it is the most important carbohydrate fuel for the human body\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “It is the main component of starch and glycogen” – Glucose units form starch and glycogen\n• “It is the main component of start and glycogen” – Apparent duplicate with typo\n• “it is also called blood sugar” – Glucose is blood sugar\n• “it is the most important carbohydrate fuel for the human body” – Primary carbohydrate energy source\n\nNot correct:\n• “No answer text provided.” – Blank in source; cannot assess\n• “No answer text provided.” – Blank in source; cannot assess\n• “it is the most common disaccharide in our diet.” – Glucose is a monosaccharide\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":499,"t":7,"s":33,"q":"Starch and dietary fiber are classified as","o":["refined sugars","complex carbohydrates","monosaccharides","simple sugars"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• complex carbohydrates\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “complex carbohydrates” – Polysaccharides include starch and fiber\n\nNot correct:\n• “refined sugars” – Refined sugar is generally simple carbohydrate\n• “monosaccharides” – Single sugar units\n• “simple sugars” – Starch and fiber are not simple sugars\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":500,"t":6,"s":33,"q":"Plant foods generally are excellent sources of (Select all that apply.)","o":["phytochemicals","fiber","unsaturated fats"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• phytochemicals\n• fiber\n• unsaturated fats\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “phytochemicals” – Plants contain bioactive compounds\n• “fiber” – Plant cell walls provide dietary fiber\n• “unsaturated fats” – Many nuts and seeds provide unsaturated fats\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":501,"t":12,"s":33,"q":"Which comment by a 12-year-old child with type 1 diabetes indicates deficient knowledge?","o":["“I rotate my insulin injection sites every time I give myself an injection.”","“I keep Lifesavers in my school bag in case I have a low-sugar reaction.”","“I keep records of my glucose levels and insulin sites and amounts.”","“I’ll be glad when I can take a pill for my diabetes like my uncle does.”"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I’ll be glad when I can take a pill for my diabetes like my uncle does.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I’ll be glad when I can take a pill for my diabetes like my uncle does.”” – Type 1 diabetes requires insulin replacement\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I rotate my insulin injection sites every time I give myself an injection.”” – Rotation helps prevent lipodystrophy\n• ““I keep Lifesavers in my school bag in case I have a low-sugar reaction.”” – Fast sugar helps treat hypoglycemia\n• ““I keep records of my glucose levels and insulin sites and amounts.”” – Logs support dosing and monitoring\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":502,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who sustained a cervical spinal cord injury and had halo traction therapy applied 1 day ago.  Which of the following should the nurse include in the care plan?","o":["Cleanse the halo traction pin sites with 100% hydrogen peroxide.","Maintain the client’s neck in a flexed position.","Expect drainage around the halo traction pins for the first 72 hours.","Change the client’s position via logroll."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Change the client’s position via logroll.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Change the client’s position via logroll.” – Logroll preserves spinal alignment\n\nNot correct:\n• “Cleanse the halo traction pin sites with 100% hydrogen peroxide.” – Full-strength peroxide damages tissue; follow pin-care protocol\n• “Maintain the client’s neck in a flexed position.” – Maintain neutral alignment\n• “Expect drainage around the halo traction pins for the first 72 hours.” – Drainage may indicate infection; assess rather than assume normal\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":503,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"A nurse is caring for the client who has Ménière's disease and asks if he is allowed to ambulate independently. Which of the following responses should the nurse make?","o":["\"Please ring for assistance when you wish to get out of bed.\"","\"We will have to get a prescription from your provider.\"","\"No, you are on strict bedrest and must not be up.\"","\"Yes, you are free to move around as you wish.\""],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• \"Please ring for assistance when you wish to get out of bed.\"\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “\"Please ring for assistance when you wish to get out of bed.\"” – Vertigo increases fall risk\n\nNot correct:\n• “\"We will have to get a prescription from your provider.\"” – Nursing safety assistance does not require a prescription\n• “\"No, you are on strict bedrest and must not be up.\"” – Strict bedrest not routinely required\n• “\"Yes, you are free to move around as you wish.\"” – Unassisted mobility risks falls\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":504,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"The nurse has given medication instructions to a patient receiving phenytoin. Which statement by the patient indicates that the patient has an adequate understanding?","o":["“I can skip doses if the side effects bother me.”","“I will need to take extra care of my teeth and gums while on this medication.”","“I can go out for a beer while on this medication.”","“I will be able to stop taking this drug once the seizures stop.”"],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• “I will need to take extra care of my teeth and gums while on this medication.”\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• ““I will need to take extra care of my teeth and gums while on this medication.”” – Phenytoin may cause gingival overgrowth\n\nNot correct:\n• ““I can skip doses if the side effects bother me.”” – Abrupt interruption risks seizures\n• ““I can go out for a beer while on this medication.”” – Alcohol may interact or impair safety\n• ““I will be able to stop taking this drug once the seizures stop.”” – Do not stop without provider-directed taper\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":505,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"During the taking of the health history of a 78-year-old man, his wife states that he occasionally has problems with short-term memory loss and confusion: “He can’t even remember how to button his shirt.” When assessing his sensory system, which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","o":["The nurse would perform the tests, knowing that mental status does not affect sensory ability.","Before testing, the nurse would assess the patient’s mental status and ability to follow directions.","The nurse would not test the sensory system as part of the examination because the results would not be valid.","The nurse would proceed with an explanation of each test, making certain that the wife understands."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Before testing, the nurse would assess the patient’s mental status and ability to follow directions.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Before testing, the nurse would assess the patient’s mental status and ability to follo…” – Check ability to understand and follow commands\n\nNot correct:\n• “The nurse would perform the tests, knowing that mental status does not affect sensory a…” – Cognitive status affects test reliability\n• “The nurse would not test the sensory system as part of the examination because the resu…” – Do not automatically omit testing\n• “The nurse would proceed with an explanation of each test, making certain that the wife …” – Patient participation matters more than wife understanding\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":506,"t":9,"s":34,"q":"A 21-year-old patient has a head injury resulting from trauma and is unconscious. There are no other injuries. During the assessment what would the nurse expect to find when testing the patient’s deep tendon reflexes?","o":["Reflexes cannot be elicited.","All reflexes will be diminished but present.","Some reflexes will be present, depending on the area of injury.","Reflexes will be normal."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Reflexes will be normal.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Reflexes will be normal.” – Source assumption of intact reflex arcs; actual findings vary\n\nNot correct:\n• “Reflexes cannot be elicited.” – Unconsciousness alone does not abolish reflexes\n• “All reflexes will be diminished but present.” – Reduced reflexes are not inevitable\n• “Some reflexes will be present, depending on the area of injury.” – Clinically possible depending on injury; source expects D\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":507,"t":7,"s":34,"q":"The client has Guillain-Barré syndrome. Which interdisciplinary health care team members will the nurse plan to collaborate with to help prevent pressure ulcers related to immobility in the client with Guillain-Barré syndrome? Select all that apply.","o":["Social worker","Occupational therapist (OT)","Family","Dietitian","Certified hospital chaplain"],"a":[1,2,3],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Occupational therapist (OT)\n• Family\n• Dietitian\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Occupational therapist (OT)” – Supports positioning and adaptive equipment\n• “Family” – Can assist with turning and skin inspection\n• “Dietitian” – Supports adequate nutrition for skin integrity\n\nNot correct:\n• “Social worker” – Addresses resources, not direct pressure prevention in this item\n• “Certified hospital chaplain” – Spiritual care not direct pressure prevention\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":508,"t":3,"s":34,"q":"An 80-year-old client is bedridden after having a cerebrovascular accident (CVA). Which nursing intervention is used to help prevent skin breakdown?","o":["Applying moist packs to the skin every shift","Turning and repositioning at least every 4 hours","Assuring the client's skin remains dry and clean.","Decreasing calories consumed; avoiding weight gain"],"a":2,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Assuring the client's skin remains dry and clean.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Assuring the client's skin remains dry and clean.” – Clean dry skin reduces moisture injury\n\nNot correct:\n• “Applying moist packs to the skin every shift” – Excess moisture promotes skin breakdown\n• “Turning and repositioning at least every 4 hours” – Four hours may be too long for some patients\n• “Decreasing calories consumed; avoiding weight gain” – Adequate calories and protein support skin integrity\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":509,"t":7,"s":34,"q":"A client has been hospitalized with a non-life-threatening C-spine neck injury. The interdisciplinary rehabilitation team has worked with the quadriplegic client for 4 months. Which outcome indicates that the team's efforts are effective?","o":["Mobility requires multiple assistive devices.","Skin is intact, with no evidence of skin impairment.","Personal care is performed with help from the family.","Constipation now occurs only 3 days a week."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Skin is intact, with no evidence of skin impairment.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Skin is intact, with no evidence of skin impairment.” – Intact skin shows effective pressure injury prevention\n\nNot correct:\n• “Mobility requires multiple assistive devices.” – Device use alone does not show complication prevention\n• “Personal care is performed with help from the family.” – Family assistance may be appropriate but not the keyed outcome\n• “Constipation now occurs only 3 days a week.” – Persistent constipation indicates ongoing issue\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":510,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"The nurse is obtaining a focused health history for a client with suspected bacterial meningitis. Which of the following questions should the nurse ask?","o":["\"Do you live in a crowded residence?\"","\"Have you had any viral infections recently?\"","\"Have you traveled out of the country in the last month?\"","\"When was your last tetanus vaccination?\""],"a":0,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• \"Do you live in a crowded residence?\"\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “\"Do you live in a crowded residence?\"” – Crowding increases meningococcal transmission\n\nNot correct:\n• “\"Have you had any viral infections recently?\"” – Less specific for bacterial meningitis\n• “\"Have you traveled out of the country in the last month?\"” – Travel may matter but less specific than crowding\n• “\"When was your last tetanus vaccination?\"” – Tetanus status not a primary meningitis risk\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":511,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"A nurse is planning care for a client who has meningitis and is at risk for increased intracranial pressure (ICP). Which of the following actions should the nurse plan to take?  (Select all that apply.)","o":["Administer morphine for the report of neck and generalized pain.","Encourage the client to cough frequently","Turn off room lights and television.","Implement seizure precautions.","Monitor for impaired extraocular movements.","Perform neurological checks four times a day."],"a":[2,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Turn off room lights and television.\n• Implement seizure precautions.\n• Monitor for impaired extraocular movements.\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Turn off room lights and television.” – Reduces stimulation and photophobia\n• “Implement seizure precautions.” – Meningitis and increased ICP raise seizure risk\n• “Monitor for impaired extraocular movements.” – Cranial nerve changes may signal worsening ICP\n\nNot correct:\n• “Administer morphine for the report of neck and generalized pain.” – Opioids may obscure neurologic changes; use cautiously\n• “Encourage the client to cough frequently” – Frequent forceful coughing can raise ICP\n• “Perform neurological checks four times a day.” – Four daily checks may be too infrequent\n\n✔ Checked against your PowerPoints:\n• Unit 5 - Pediatrics, slide 55: “Administer antibiotics, isolate 24 hours, keep area dark and quiet, seizure precautions”\n• Unit 10 - Neurological, slide 58: “Continuous neuro assessment”\nYour slides support turning off the lights and seizure precautions, and rule out checking neuro status only four times a day; extraocular movements are not named."},{"id":512,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client recovering from a head injury. The nurse determines that the client needs further education to prevent elevation in ICP if the nurse observes the client doing which of the following? Select all that apply.","o":["Bending over to tie the shoes","Blowing the nose","Exhaling during repositioning","Coughing vigorously","Isometric exercises"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Bending over to tie the shoes\n• Blowing the nose\n• Coughing vigorously\n• Isometric exercises\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Bending over to tie the shoes” – Bending can raise venous and intracranial pressure\n• “Blowing the nose” – Nose blowing increases pressure\n• “Coughing vigorously” – Vigorous coughing increases ICP\n• “Isometric exercises” – Straining during isometrics can raise ICP\n\nNot correct:\n• “Exhaling during repositioning” – Exhaling avoids breath-holding and Valsalva\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":513,"t":12,"s":34,"q":"What is the goal of therapeutic management for a child diagnosed with ADHD?","o":["Correct nutritional imbalances.","Assess the child for other psychosocial disorders.","Administer stimulant medications.","Reduce the frequency and intensity of unsocialized behaviors."],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Reduce the frequency and intensity of unsocialized behaviors.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Reduce the frequency and intensity of unsocialized behaviors.” – Improve functioning and reduce disruptive behaviors\n\nNot correct:\n• “Correct nutritional imbalances.” – Nutrition is a consideration but not core behavioral goal\n• “Assess the child for other psychosocial disorders.” – Assessment is a means, not therapeutic outcome\n• “Administer stimulant medications.” – Medication is a treatment, not goal\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":514,"t":12,"s":34,"q":"A nurse is giving a parent information about autism. Which statement made by the parent indicates understanding of the teaching?","o":["Autism can be treated effectively with medication.","The onset of autism usually occurs before 3 years of age.","Autism is characterized by periods of remission and exacerbation.","Children with autism have imitation and gesturing skills."],"a":1,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• The onset of autism usually occurs before 3 years of age.\n(This answer is keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “The onset of autism usually occurs before 3 years of age.” – Signs typically emerge early in development\n\nNot correct:\n• “Autism can be treated effectively with medication.” – Medications address associated symptoms, not core autism\n• “Autism is characterized by periods of remission and exacerbation.” – Persistent neurodevelopmental condition, not episodic\n• “Children with autism have imitation and gesturing skills.” – Imitation and gesturing may be impaired\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":515,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"Which assessment finding in a patient taking NSAIDs requires immediate intervention?","o":["Headache","Nonproductive cough","Pain in their joints","Black, tarry stools"],"a":3,"r":"Weekly quiz key (one answer):\n• Black, tarry stools\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Black, tarry stools” – Melena suggests GI bleeding and requires urgent assessment\n\nNot correct:\n• “Headache” – Nonspecific adverse symptom\n• “Nonproductive cough” – Not typical major NSAID complication\n• “Pain in their joints” – Not evidence of acute GI hemorrhage\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":516,"t":8,"s":34,"q":"Which are characteristic signs of inflammation? (Select all that apply.)","o":["Numbness","Heat","Edema","Paresthesia","Erythema","Pallor"],"a":[1,2,4],"r":"Weekly quiz key (select all):\n• Heat\n• Edema\n• Erythema\n(This answer was inferred when the master guide was put together; it is not keyed in the original quiz source.)\n\nStudy notes from your master guide (not official Galen rationales):\n• “Heat” – Increased local blood flow produces heat\n• “Edema” – Increased permeability causes swelling\n• “Erythema” – Vasodilation causes redness\n\nNot correct:\n• “Numbness” – Sensory change is not a cardinal sign\n• “Paresthesia” – Tingling is not a cardinal sign\n• “Pallor” – Inflammation typically produces redness rather than pallor\n\nNot covered in your class PowerPoints, so this answer comes from the quiz file only."},{"id":517,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a 75-year-old client who has just been diagnosed with cancer. The client’s spouse has requested that the client not be told of the diagnosis because it would be devastating to the client. The client asks the nurse if the diagnosis is cancer. Which of the responses by the nurse reflects the ethical principle of veracity?","o":["“Let me contact your health care provider (HCP) to talk to you about your diagnosis.”","“Any serious health care issues will be discussed with you and your family before you are discharged.”","“At this time, I do not know your diagnosis because the tests were inconclusive.”","“Your doctor is the only one who can discuss health care issues with you.”"],"a":0,"r":"The nurse should practice veracity by ensuring the client receives truthful information and arranging for the provider to discuss the diagnosis.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":518,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse has attended a conference on informed consent. It indicates a correct understanding of the conference if the nurse states that informed consent:","o":["Is signed by the nurse to indicate that the client understands the procedure.","May not be withdrawn by the client once they have confirmed understanding and signed the consent.","Can be signed by a client who has dementia if they are oriented at the time of signing the form.","Can be waived, per facility policy, for urgent medical or surgical treatment if the client is unconscious."],"a":3,"r":"Informed consent may be waived for urgent treatment if the patient is unconscious and immediate intervention is needed.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":519,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is prioritizing client care after receiving the hand-off report. The nurse should first plan to see the client who:","o":["Has cirrhosis, reports several new areas of ecchymosis, and has a partial thromboplastin time (PTT) of 76 seconds.","Has pancreatitis and is reporting mid-epigastric discomfort.","Had a transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE) 2 hours ago, has a blood pressure (BP) of 106/61 mm Hg, and reports a sore throat.","Had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy 18 hours ago and is reporting bilateral shoulder pain."],"a":0,"r":"A new onset of ecchymosis and elevated PTT suggests risk for bleeding and requires immediate assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":520,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for the following assigned clients who are 24 hours postoperative. The nurse performed a change-of-shift assessment 4 hours ago. Which of the following changes in client condition should the nurse identify as a priority?","o":["The client whose blood pressure (BP) was 138/86 and is now 136/84 mm Hg.","The client whose wound drainage was sanguineous and is now serosanguineous.","The client who reports an increased pain level of 8 on a scale of 0 to 10 after receiving opioid analgesic.","The client who received NPH insulin and has a current capillary blood glucose of 110 mg/dL."],"a":2,"r":"An increase in pain despite analgesic administration indicates a possible complication and requires priority assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":521,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse preceptor is teaching a newly hired nurse about legal principles when providing client care. Which of the following situations should the nurse preceptor use as an example of malpractice?","o":["A client who is alert and oriented makes an informed decision to leave the hospital against medical advice (AMA). The nurse applies restraints to the client to prevent the client from leaving.","A client who is competent refuses an antidepressant medication. The nurse dissolves the medication in food and administers it to the client without the client’s knowledge.","A nurse is plugging in an IV pump that is alarming with a low battery. The nurse notices the outlet cover in a client’s room is loose and proceeds with plugging in the IV pump.","A nurse finds a client who is on a low-sodium diet eating salted potato chips. The nurse tells the client that wrist restraints will be applied if the client does not stop eating the chips."],"a":1,"r":"Administering medication without the client’s knowledge violates patient autonomy and is an example of malpractice.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":522,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for assigned clients. It is a priority for the nurse to follow up with a client who:","o":["Is receiving hydrocortisone and has a blood glucose level of 135 mg/dL.","Has multiple sclerosis (MS), reports feeling tired, and is experiencing diplopia.","Is receiving pyridostigmine and reports nausea and congested cough.","Has active pulmonary tuberculosis (TB), hemoptysis, and weight loss."],"a":3,"r":"Hemoptysis and weight loss in TB are urgent concerns that could indicate worsening disease and respiratory compromise.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":523,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse working on a pediatric cardiac unit is reviewing the telemetry monitors for assigned clients. Which of the following clients should the nurse initially plan to assess?","o":["A 15-year-old adolescent who is reading and has a pulse (P) of 60","A 5-year-old child who is watching television and has a pulse (P) of 78.","A 2-year-old who is playing with toys and has a pulse (P) of 100.","A 1-month-old infant who is sleeping and has a pulse (P) of 180."],"a":3,"r":"A pulse of 180 in a sleeping infant is tachycardia and needs prompt assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":524,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse working on a pediatric unit is caring for the following children who have been admitted within the past 12 hours. It is a priority for the nurse to initiate an interdisciplinary conference for the child who is:","o":["4 years old, was recently diagnosed with Kawasaki disease, and recently starting on immunoglobulin therapy.","5 years old, was diagnosed with cystic fibrosis (CF) at birth, and has been hospitalized 4 times in the past 2 months.","8 years old, was admitted with multiple compound fractures, and recently placed in Buck’s traction","3 years old, was admitted with epiglottis, and is currently having difficulty breathing."],"a":3,"r":"DiGiculty breathing in a child with epiglottitis is a medical emergency and requires immediate interdisciplinary management.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":525,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is reviewing the plan of care on a client who is diagnosed with pericarditis and has been in the hospital for 24 hours. The nurse notes that the client’s current white blood cell (WBC) count is 13,000 mm3 and they have a fever of 100.8 F orally. Which of the following actions is most appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Change the client’s goal to reflect their current status.","Update the nursing interventions as needed.","Notify the charge nurse that the plan of care is outdated.","Obtain an order for an infectious disease referral."],"a":1,"r":"The nurse should update interventions to reflect the client’s current status of infection (elevated WBC and fever).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":526,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The charge nurse is reviewing clients’ electronic medical records (EMRs). Which of the following notes by a staff nurse indicates correct documentation?","o":["“Dressing change performed to coccyx wound with no adverse effects noted by the client. Client transferred to chair.”","“IV catheter site in left antecubital space is red and warm to touch. IV catheter removed intact. 20-gauge catheter placed in right forearm.”","“Reports of pain in abdomen with vomiting noted. Medications administered.”","“Found client sitting on floor. All 4 side rails were in upright position. Client reports no pain. No abrasions or bleeding noted. PHCP notified. Incident report completed.”"],"a":1,"r":"Accurate documentation includes describing the IV site status and actions taken.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":527,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has brought in their own continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) machine for use while hospitalized. Which of the following actions is most important for the nurse to take?","o":["Review the instruction booklet on how to operate the machine.","Have the machine inspected by the facility’s engineering department.","Explain to the client that the hospital has to supply the CPAP machine.","Ask the client when they last cleaned the CPAP machine."],"a":1,"r":"Any personal medical equipment must be inspected by hospital engineering to ensure safety and compliance.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":528,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse working on a medical-surgical unit is caring for the following assigned clients when the facility’s emergency response plan is activated. Which client should the nurse recommend for discharge?","o":["The client who has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), is receiving IV steroid therapy, and has an oxygen saturation (SAO2) level of 86% on room air.","The client who has a deep vein thrombosis (DVT), is receiving warfarin, and has a prothrombin time (PT) of 28 seconds.","The client who has suspected appendicitis, intense pain at McBurney’s point, and now reports cessation of pain.","The client who is positive for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), has a nonproductive cough, and a 24-hour Mantoux test reading of 6 mm induration."],"a":3,"r":"The client with HIV and minor PPD reaction is stable and can be discharged to free up hospital resources during an emergency.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":529,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is observing a newly hired unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) care for assigned clients. It indicates correct client care if the newly hired UAP is observed:","o":["Placing dedicated equipment in the room of a client who has herpes zoster.","Wearing a gown when entering the room of a client who has influenza.","Donning sterile gloves to empty a urinary drainage device.","Removing protective eyewear before removing gloves."],"a":0,"r":"Dedicated equipment prevents transmission of infection in clients with herpes zoster (shingles).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":530,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is teaching a class on child car passenger safety to a group of parents. Which of the following statements by a parent indicates a need for further teaching?","o":["“The care seat harness chest clip should be at the level of the infant’s armpits.”","“When using a booster seat, the lap belt should be across the child’s upper thighs.”","“Car seats should be rear-facing in the back seat around ages 2 to 4 years.”","“One hand should fit between the infant and the car seat harness.”"],"a":3,"r":"The car seat harness should be snug, allowing no more than one finger width, not one hand.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":531,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse working on a pediatric unit is reviewing newly written orders for assigned clients. It requires follow-up by the nurse if the primary health care provider (PHCP) orders:","o":["Chelation therapy for an 8-year-old child who has lead poisoning.","Discontinuation of hydrocortisone IV for a 10-year-old who has adrenocortical insufficiency.","A fluid bolus of 500 mL over 1 hour for a 12-year-old child who has sickle cell crisis.","A low-sodium client for a 14-year-old child who has acute glomerulonephritis."],"a":2,"r":"A fluid bolus of 500 mL over 1 hour is too large and rapid for a child with sickle cell crisis and risks worsening complications.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":532,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is reviewing orders for the following assigned clients. The nurse should clarify the order for the client who:","o":["Presented with clinical manifestations of an ischemic stroke that started 2 hours ago and is to receive IV alteplase.","Had a permanent pacemaker inserted 6 months ago and is scheduled for a radiograph (x-ray) of the chest.","Is diagnosed with acute kidney injury (AKI) and is to receive a low-sodium, low-potassium diet.","Has a diagnosis of polycythemia vera and is to receive 1 unit of packed red blood cells (RBCs)."],"a":3,"r":"Patients with polycythemia vera have increased blood volume and viscosity; giving packed RBCs is inappropriate and should be clarified.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":533,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client whose labor is being supplemented with oxytocin IV. The nurse should discontinue the oxytocin if the client develops which of the following assessment findings?","o":["Dark, red streaks in her bloody show.","Intermittent back pain with each contraction.","Uterine relaxation between contractions.","Fetal heart rate (FHR) of 68 for 90 seconds."],"a":3,"r":"A prolonged fetal heart rate of 68 bpm indicates severe fetal distress and requires immediate discontinuation of oxytocin.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":534,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a newborn immediately after birth and has ensured the newborn has a patent airway. Which of the following actions should the nurse take next?","o":["Administer eye prophylaxes.","Weigh the newborn and document the weight.","Administer vitamin K.","Dry the newborn’s skin and cover with a warm blanket."],"a":3,"r":"After ensuring a patent airway, the priority is thermoregulation by drying the newborn and covering them with a warm blanket. 19-D # Rationale Chronic alcohol use leads to poor nutritional intake and absorption, especially\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":535,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a group of clients. The nurse recognizes the client at greatest risk for developing vitamin B6 deficiency is the client who:","o":["Has chronic alcohol use disorder.","Has osteoporosis.","Takes gabapentin to treat a seizure disorder.","Takes digoxin to treat atrial fibrillation (A-fib)."],"a":0,"r":"of vitamin B6. Using Naegele’s Rule: LMP (Nov 19) + 7 days = Nov 26, then +9 months\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":536,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a pregnant client whose last menstrual period (LMP) began on November 19. Using Naegele’s rule, which of the following is the estimated date of delivery (EDD)?","o":["July 26","August 12","August 26","July 12"],"a":2,"r":"= August 26. It is important to assess underlying causes of ineffective coping before\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":537,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who had a left below-the-knee amputation (BKA) caused by poor circulation secondary to diabetes mellitus (type 1). The client is demonstrating ineffective coping with use of defense mechanisms. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Tell the client their behavior is not acceptable.","Determine what is causing the client to respond in such a way.","Obtain an order for a psychotic consultation.","Explain to the client that their reactions are unproductive to getting better."],"a":1,"r":"intervening. Immediate priority is to limit absorption of the drug through gastric lavage\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":538,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has presented to the emergency department (ED) due to an overdose of the tricyclic antidepressant, amitriptyline. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Determine the events leading up to the overdose.","Administer oxygen via nasal cannula.","Initiate gastric lavage with activated charcoal.","Document the number of pills ingested."],"a":2,"r":"and activated charcoal. Suicidal ideations are always the highest priority due to potential for\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":539,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse working on a mental health unit has become aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first assess the client who has:","o":["Suicidal ideations and is reluctantly discussing a suicide contract with a staff member.","Bulimia nervosa and has gone to the restroom 15 minutes after eating a meal.","A major depressive disorder and is stating, “I wish I could stop crying all the time.”","Paranoid schizophrenia and has not bathed or brushed their teeth in 3 days."],"a":0,"r":"immediate harm.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":540,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is preparing client assignments for the staff on the psychiatric unit. Which of the following clients should the nurse recognize as being at highest risk for suicide?","o":["39-year-old female who was just diagnosed with breast cancer and is seeking a second opinion about the diagnosis.","52-year-old male who lost his job 3 months ago and feels he will never find another job.","70-year-old male whose wife recently died and has been going to weekly bingo with friends.","15-year-old female who just broke up with her boyfriend and is spending more time with friends."],"a":1,"r":"Middle-aged males recently unemployed are at a very high risk for suicide. Open irrigation sets must be replaced every 24 hours; using one for 48 hours\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":541,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for a client who is receiving continuous tube feedings via a gastrostomy tube (g-tube). Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse requires immediate intervention by the nurse preceptor?","o":["Using an irrigation set that was opened 48 hours ago.","Returning the residual obtained from the g-tube.","Adding no more than 4 hours’ worth of feeding to the bag.","Maintaining the head of the bed (HOB) at a 30- to 45-degree angle."],"a":0,"r":"risks infection.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":542,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has urinary incontinence and is at risk for skin breakdown. Which of the following actions should the nurse implement as a preventative measure?","o":["Request an order for the insertion of an indwelling urethral catheter.","Apply a moisture barrier ointment to the skin.","Check the skin every 8 hours for signs of breakdown.","Clean the perineum with hot water after each episode of incontinence."],"a":1,"r":"A moisture barrier protects skin from breakdown due to incontinence. # Rationale Skin traction patients must have frequent neurovascular checks, including\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":543,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is in skin traction (Buck’s traction). Which of the following actions should the nurse take when caring for this client?","o":["Provide pin care site.","Keep the foot of the bed elevated.","Assess pedal pulse strength every 4 hours.","Turn the client to a 30-degree angle every 2 hours."],"a":2,"r":"pedal pulse strength.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":544,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is assessing an older adult client for urinary incontinence. Which of the following questions is important for the nurse to ask the client?","o":["“Do you ever use pads or tissues in your underwear to catch urine?”","“Do you have a history of kidney stones?”","“Do you have a family history of polycystic kidney disease?”","“Do you take the herbal preparation gingko biloba?”"],"a":0,"r":"Asking about pad/tissue use identifies coping methods for incontinence. Ciprofloxacin can cause dizziness; patients should change positions slowly to\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":545,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client who has a urinary tract infection (UTI) about the prescribed drug, ciprofloxacin. Which of the following instructions should the nurse give the client?","o":["“This medication is prescribed because it provides an analgesic effect and decreases painful urination.”","“You could experience dizziness and should change positions slowly.”","“You should not operate heavy machinery or drive an automobile while taking this medicine.”","“Antacids should be taken 30 minutes after this medication.”"],"a":1,"r":"prevent falls.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":546,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who was recently prescribed furosemide. Which of the following actions is necessary for the nurse to take?","o":["Inspect the client’s oral mucosa for ulcerations.","Limit the client’s intake of citrus fruit juices.","Monitor the client’s potassium level.","Encourage the client to increase intake of dietary sodium."],"a":2,"r":"Furosemide can cause hypokalemia; potassium must be closely monitored. Lithium does not change urine color; reddish-orange urine is linked to\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":547,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is teaching a 22-year-old female client who was recently prescribed lithium. Which of the following statements by the client indicates a need for further teaching?","o":["“I should anticipate that this medication will make my urine turn reddish-orange.”","“I should take this medication with food.”","“I should be sure to eat food that contains sodium.”","“I should avoid becoming dehydrated while taking this medication.”"],"a":0,"r":"rifampin or phenazopyridine. Vasopressin decreases urinary output, which is the expected outcome for\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":548,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has diabetes insipidus (DI) and is receiving vasopressin. It indicates the client is having an expected outcome to the medication of the client demonstrates a decrease in:","o":["Blood pressure (BP) from 146/82 to 116/64 mm Hg.","Body weight of 2 pounds in the past 24 hours.","Specific gravity from 1.025 to 1.05.","Urinary output from 500 to 125 mL/hr."],"a":3,"r":"diabetes insipidus treatment. Sodium polystyrene treats hyperkalemia; a potassium of 3.8 is normal and\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":549,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is reviewing newly written prescriptions for assigned clients. The nurse should follow up with the primary health care provider (PHCP) on the client who has:","o":["A sensitivity to acetylsalicylic acid and is prescribed acetaminophen.","A potassium level of 3.8 mEq/L and is prescribed sodium polystyrene.","A history of rash with penicillin and is prescribed a monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI).","A decreased phenytoin level and is prescribed seizure precautions."],"a":1,"r":"does not warrant treatment. Iron supplements may cause dark stools and constipation; clients should be\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":550,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is preparing to teach a client who has anemia and a new prescription for liquid ferrous sulfate. Which of the following instructions should the nurse include in the teaching?","o":["“Do not mix the medication with any other medications, foods, or liquids.”","“The medication can be taken with milk to prevent gastrointestinal irritation.”","“Avoid eating excessive amounts of beans and green leafy vegetables.”","“You will notice a change in your bowel habits and stool color.”"],"a":3,"r":"educated. Enoxaparin injections are given at a 90-degree angle without aspiration or\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":551,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse has a prescription to administer subcutaneous enoxaparin to an assigned client. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Aspirate for the blood prior to injecting the medication.","Check the most current d-Dimer.","Insert the needle in the skin at a 90-degree angle.","Gently massage the area after administration."],"a":2,"r":"massage. Medications for children are dosed by weight (kg); weight must be verified\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":552,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The primary health care provider (PHCP) has prescribed ampicillin 150 mg PO three times daily to a toddler who weighs 22 lb. The drug available is ampicillin suspension 250 mg/5 mL. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Administer 3 mL by mouth 3 times per day.","Check for an allergy to sulfa medications.","Determine the toddler’s weight in kilograms.","Contact the PHCP for clarification about the prescription."],"a":2,"r":"first. Pruritus after paracentesis could indicate internal bleeding or liver\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":553,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is monitoring a client following a paracentesis. It is a priority for the nurse to notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if theclient develops:","o":["Vesicular breath sounds.","Flatulence.","Pink-tinged urine.","Pruritus."],"a":3,"r":"dysfunction; needs urgent evaluation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":554,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client whose arterial blood gas (ABG) results show the following pH 7.52, PaCO2 26 mm Hg, HCO3 24 mEq/L. Which of the following acid-base imbalances should the nurse identify that the client is experiencing?","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis."],"a":3,"r":"A pH >7.45 and PaCO2 <35 indicate respiratory alkalosis. Hallucinations, diaphoresis, and nausea during withdrawal are dangerous;\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":555,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who was admitted for alcohol withdrawal. Based on the nursing notes below, which of the following actions is appropriate for the nurse to take? 0700 PT admission completed. PT is alert and oriented to person place time and situation. No complaints of pain at this time. Will continue to monitor. 1500 Client has peripheral IV catheter to left wrist with lactated Ringer’s solution infusing at 30 mL/hr, lungs clear bilaterally, profuse diaphoresis, hallucinations, nausea, and vomiting.","o":["Attempt to orient the client.","Notify the primary health care provider (PHCP).","Administer flumazenil.","Increase the rate of the IV infusion."],"a":1,"r":"notify the provider immediately. Abdominal distension and rigidity suggest bowel perforation or serious\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":556,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is administering a large-volume enema to a client. During administration, the client’s abdomen becomes distended and rigid. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Lower the enema fluid bag.","Reposition the client for better installation of the enema.","Discontinue the enema treatment.","Ask the client to rate their pain level."],"a":2,"r":"problem; the enema must be stopped.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":557,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse has instructed a female client on how to collect a midstream urine specimen. Which of the following client statements requires additional teaching by the nurse?","o":["“After collecting the specimen, I will remove the container before stopping the flow of my urine.”","“Since I am right-handed, I will use my left hand to spread the labia apart.”","“To collect the specimen, I will start to urinate in the toilet before I urinate in the container.”","“I will use the provided towelette to cleanse the area using a back-and-forth motion.”"],"a":3,"r":"Cleansing should be front to back, not back and forth, to prevent infection.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":558,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has Addison disease and is at risk for Addisonian crisis. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Provie a low-carbohydrate diet.","Restrict fluid intake.","Administer hydrocortisone.","Monitor the client’s antidiuretic hormone (ADH) levels."],"a":2,"r":"Hydrocortisone is the treatment of choice for Addisonian crisis. Persistent earache may signal a post-tonsillectomy complication like infection\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":559,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is providing discharge instructions to the parents of a child who had a tonsillectomy. Which of the findings should the nurse instruct the parents to report to the surgeon?","o":["Odor to the breath.","Persistent earache.","Temperature (T) of 99.4 F.","Mouth breathing."],"a":1,"r":"or abscess formation. 45.C Peritonitis symptoms include hypoactive bowel sounds, rebound tenderness, nausea/vomiting, and cloudy\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":560,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is talking with a newly hired nurse about peritoneal dialysis (PD) and how to recognize peritonitis. Which signs and symptoms from the box below should the nurse include in the discussion? 1. Hypoactive bowel sounds. 2. Rebound abdominal tenderness. 3. Amber-colored urine. 4. Nausea and vomiting. 5. Cloudy or opaque outflow. 6. Diarrhea. 7. Bradycardia.","o":["2, 5, 6, 7.","1, 4, 7.","4, 5, 6.","1, 2, 4, 5."],"a":3,"r":"Peritonitis symptoms include hypoactive bowel sounds, rebound tenderness, nausea/vomiting, and cloudy peritoneal outflow. Diarrhea and bradycardia are not hallmark symptoms.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":561,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client who is suspected of having a retinal detachment of the left eye. Which of the following client statements is consistent with this diagnosis?","o":["“I see floating dark spots in my left eye.”","“I feel as if a curtain has been pulled down over my right eye.”","“I am experiencing a sharp pain in my eye.”","“I have lost the central part of my vision.”"],"a":0,"r":"Retinal detachment typically presents with floaters and flashes of light, sometimes described as a curtain over vision, but floaters are the earliest clue.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":562,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse has an order to remove intermittent sutures from a client’s abdominal wound. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Cut the suture by lifting it with forceps and cutting as close as possible to the skin.","Apply adhesive strips to each suture area after each suture has been removed.","Place the removed sutures in a sterile cup and send to the laboratory for culture and sensitivity (C&S).","Remove each suture in order starting at the top and moving toward the bottom of the suture line."],"a":0,"r":"Cutting close to the skin avoids pulling contaminated suture material through the underlying tissue and reduces infection risk.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":563,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse is reviewing the morning laboratory values for a client who has a pulmonary embolism (PE) and is receiving heparin. Which of the following laboratory values requires immediate attention by the nurse?","o":["Hemoglobin (Hbg) of 10.8 g/dL.","Platelet count of 100,000 mm3.","Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) of 60 seconds.","Prothrombin time (PT) of 12 seconds."],"a":1,"r":"A platelet count this low suggests potential heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), a critical complication requiring immediate action.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":564,"t":17,"s":35,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) is caring for a client who sustained deep partial-thickness burns to the lower torso, both forearms, and thighs from a hot grease spill in the kitchen. Which of the following actions is a priority for the nurse to take?","o":["Maintain a warm environment.","Inserting an indwelling urethral catheter.","Preparing to intubate the client.","Inspecting for signs of inhalation injuries."],"a":3,"r":"Inhalation injury is a life-threatening complication with burns involving the torso or face and must be ruled out early. Airway compromise is always priority in burn care.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":565,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse has been made aware of the following client situations. It is necessary for the nurse to initially assess the client who has:","o":["Diabetes mellitus (type 1), is difficult to arouse, and has a blood glucose of 38 mg/dL.","A urinary tract infection (UTI), is receiving oral (PO) cephalexin, and reports urinary frequency.","Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP), has a white blood cell (WBC) count of 18,400/mm3, and is scheduled to receive IV vancomycin.","Appendicitis, reports pain rated 3 on a scale of 0 (no pain) to 10 (severe pain), and needs assistance ambulating to the bathroom."],"a":0,"r":"Blood glucose of 38 mg/dL with decreased responsiveness is life-threatening hypoglycemia and must be treated immediately.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":566,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse has received the hand-off report and is assigning tasks to unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP). Which of the following tasks should the nurse instruct the UAP to perform first?","o":["Obtain a sputum specimen from a client who has suspected pulmonary tuberculosis (TB).","Turn and reposition a client who was last turned 2 hours ago.","Deliver a lunch tray to a client who is returning from radiology and has been NPO since breakfast.","Obtain a capillary blood glucose on a client who had a hypoglycemia episode 30 minutes ago and received dextrose IV."],"a":3,"r":"After hypoglycemia, reassess blood sugar promptly to prevent recurrence of low blood glucose.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":567,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working on a pediatric unit has received the hand-off report and is reviewing client data and orders. Which of the following clients should the nurse plan to see first?","o":["The toddler who has bilateral arm casts and needs to be fed their breakfast.","The school-aged child who has diabetes mellitus (type 1) and requires blood glucose monitoring.","The infant who has a diagnosis of pertussis and is receiving oxygen via nasal cannula.","The adolescent who was admitted with sickle cell crisis and is being discharged home."],"a":2,"r":"An infant with pertussis and oxygen therapy needs urgent monitoring due to risk of airway obstruction.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":568,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse who normally works on the maternity unit has been floated to work on an adult medical surgical unit for the shift. It is appropriate to assign the float nurse the client who:","o":["Is being discharged home and needs teaching about dressing changes to a wound.","Is 24 hours postoperative from having an appendectomy.","Was admitted the night before with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).","Is scheduled to have an above-the-knee amputation (AKA) that morning."],"a":1,"r":"A client 24 hours postoperative after a simple procedure (appendectomy) is stable and appropriate for a float nurse.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":569,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse teach a client who has pelvic inflammatory disease about self-care. The client does not speak English. Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse requires intervention by the nurse preceptor?","o":["Providing written instructions to the client in their primary language.","Contacting the charge nurse to obtain assistance from a facility approved interpreter.","Having a family member who speaks both English and the client’s language interpret the instructions.","Utilizing a facility-approved telephone interpretation service to conduct the teaching."],"a":2,"r":"Family members should never be used for medical interpretation; a professional interpreter must be used.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":570,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in the labor and delivery (L&D) unit is caring for a client in labor whose membranes just spontaneously ruptured. Which of the following findings is a priority for the nurse to report to the primary health care provider (PHCP)?","o":["A maternal temperature (T) of 99.5 F.","New appearance of late decelerations.","4 contractions in a 10-minute period.","Contractions lasting 60 seconds."],"a":1,"r":"Late decelerations suggest uteroplacental insufficiency and fetal hypoxia, requiring immediate intervention.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":571,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for assigned clients. Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse indicates correct care?","o":["Using a 23-gauge, 1-inch needle when administering a medication in the deltoid muscle.","Placing a particular respirator (N95) mask on a client who has active pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) in preparation for transport to a long-term care (LTC) facility.","Administering oral pain medication to a client 20-minutes prior to hospital discharge.","Teaching a client who has a new ileostomy to empty the ostomy bag 30 minutes after eating a meal."],"a":0,"r":"Not from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":572,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in an outpatient care clinic is assessing clients who are waiting to be seen. Which of the following clients should the nurse have the primary health care provider (PHCP) see first?","o":["45-year-old who has pleurisy and is reporting worsening shortness of breath.","15-year-old who reports the development of a bull’s eye rash 2 days after being on a camping trip.","57-year-old who has Grave’s disease and has an intolerance to heat with diaphoresis.","37-year-old who has polycystic kidney disease (PKD) and lumbar pain when ambulating in the hallway."],"a":0,"r":"Shortness of breath worsening with pleurisy can indicate serious complications like pleural effusion or PE. Priority assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":573,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in the labor and delivery (L&D) unit has become aware of the following clients who are in the second stage of labor and are being monitored with external fetal monitors. The nurse identifies which client as a priority for follow-up?","o":["The client whose fetal heart rate (FHR) remains consistent at 132 beats per minute.","The client whose cervix has dilated to 10 cm with moderately intense contractions of 40-50 seconds.","The client whose contractions are lasting 60 seconds with a 3-minute interval.","The client whose bloody show is dark red with active pushing."],"a":3,"r":"Dark red bloody show and active pushing could indicate imminent birth. Monitor closely for rapid labor progression.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":574,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a male client who has full-thickness burns to 50% of the lower body. It is a priority for the nurse to notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if the client has a:","o":["Pulse oximeter (SpO2) reading 91%.","Hematocrit (Hct) level of 60%","Temperature (T) of 104.2 F.","Urinary output of 100 mL for the past 2 hours."],"a":2,"r":"A fever of 104.2°F after burn injury suggests sepsis and must be reported urgently.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":575,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) is assisting to triage clients following an explosion at a local factory. Which of the following clients should the nurse identify as the priority for treatment?","o":["A client who reports having diabetes mellitus (type 1) and is feeling shaky with clammy skin.","The client who has a large head wound with exposed grey matter and respirations (R) of 6.","The client who has second-degree burns to the chest and arms with respirations (R) greater than 30.","The client who has a closed tibia fracture to the left leg and minimal swelling to the ankle."],"a":2,"r":"Burns to chest + respirations >30/min indicate risk for airway compromise and shock: treat first.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":576,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has severe pre-eclampsia. Which of the following should the nurse include in the client’s plan of care?","o":["Administer IV calcium as a continuous drip.","Position the client on the right side while in bed.","Provide a quiet, darkened environment.","Report an increase in urine output."],"a":2,"r":"In severe preeclampsia, quiet dark environment prevents stimulation that could provoke seizures.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":577,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for assigned clients. Which of the following actions is appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Wearing a particulate respirator (N95) mask when caring for a client who has impetigo.","Having a client who has pertussis wear a surgical mask during transport.","Placing a client who had a stem cell transport in contact isolation.","Initiating airborne precautions for the client who is diagnosed with epiglottis."],"a":1,"r":"A client with pertussis (droplet precaution) must wear a surgical mask during transport.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":578,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in an ambulatory health center has assessed the following clients who are waiting to see the primary health care provider (PHCP). The nurse should immediately remove from the waiting room and place in an examining room the client who has:","o":["A 1 mm area that is flat and red at the site of a Mantoux test that was administered 48 hours ago.","A fever of 101.8 F and is reporting a headache, vomiting, and sensitivity to light.","Heart failure (HF) and reports gaining 3 lbs in the past 3 days.","Myasthenia gravis, ptosis of the left eye, and a nasal-sounding voice."],"a":1,"r":"Headache, vomiting, photophobia with fever = signs of meningitis, needs immediate isolation and evaluation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":579,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has a sealed radiation implant. Which of the following precautions should the nurse implement?","o":["Place bed linen in a biohazard receptacle outside the client’s room.","Assign a different nurse to care for the client each day.","Keep dedicated equipment in the client’s room until the implant is removed.","Limit each visitor to 1 hour per day."],"a":1,"r":"Not from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":580,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for assigned clients when a fire with smoke is noted in the nurses’ station. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Move clients close to a window with unit doors closed.","Extinguish the fire starting at the ceiling and move down to the floor.","Use a bag valve mask to move a client on a mechanical ventilator.","Transfer clients by elevator to another floor."],"a":2,"r":"Not from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":581,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is teaching a class on health promotion and illness prevention. Which of the following actions by the nurse is an example of primary prevention?","o":["Teaching foot care to a client who has diabetes mellitus.","Recommending the use of helmets when riding bicycles or motorcycles.","Instructing female clients how to perform monthly breast self-examinations (BSE).","Referring a client who recently lost a spouse to a support group."],"a":1,"r":"Primary prevention includes measures like helmets to prevent injury before it occurs.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":582,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is discussing health screening guidelines with a group of men. It is appropriate for the nurse to state that men should:","o":["Perform a monthly testicular self-examination (TSE).","Begin having an annual prostate examination beginning at age 40.","Begina having a sigmoidoscopy once every 10 years beginning at age 50.","Be tested annually for sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) upon becoming sexually active."],"a":0,"r":"Men should perform monthly TSE (testicular self-exam) for early detection of abnormalities.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":583,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is teaching the mother of a 9-year-old child about age-appropriate toys. Which of the following toys is appropriate for the nurse to recommend?","o":["Board games and reading books.","Large building blocks and balls.","Dolls and wooden puzzles.","Finger paints and bubbles."],"a":0,"r":"Board games and reading books are appropriate for a 9-year-old’s cognitive and social development.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":584,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is screening adult clients at a health fair. Which of the following clients is a priority for the nurse to recommend follow-up with a primary health care provider (PHCP)?","o":["45 years old, has a history of exercise-induced asthma, and has vesicular breath sounds in the peripheral lung fields.","50 years old, has a family history of ovarian cancer, and has not had the human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine.","55 years old, has a blood pressure of 164/96 mm Hg, and stopped smoking 2 months ago.","65 years old, has an elevated high-density lipoprotein (HDL) level, a decreased low-density lipoprotein (LDL) level, and does not follow a low-cholesterol diet."],"a":2,"r":"BP 164/96 mmHg needs urgent follow-up for hypertension to prevent complications like stroke or heart disease.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":585,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in a well-baby clinic has assessed several infants. It is most important for the nurse to suggest follow-up for the infant who is:","o":["6 months old and unable to roll over.","3 months old and unable to switch objects from hand to hand.","4 months old and the posterior fontanel is closed.","5 months old and sleeps up to 9 hours at night."],"a":0,"r":"A 6-month-old should be rolling over. Not meeting this milestone indicates a developmental delay needing follow-up.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":586,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a child who is receiving treatment for leukemia. Upon entering the child’s room, the nurse observes the parent sitting at the bedside, staring out the window, and crying. When approached by the nurse, the parent states, “I’m so worried about my child.” Which of the following statements is most therapeutic for the nurse to make?","o":["“Life must seem very scary right now.”","“I’ll stay here and set with you awhile.”","“Have you considered attending a support group?”","“Are you able to take good care of yourself?”"],"a":0,"r":"Responding empathically (“Life must seem very scary”) validates emotions and promotes therapeutic communication.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":587,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is developing a plan of care for a newly admitted client who has histrionic personality disorder. Which of the following interventions is appropriate for the nurse to implement?","o":["Utilize compassionate behavior to keep the client in control.","Have the client remain quiet and observe during group therapy sessions.","Work with the client on ways to develop trusting relationships.","Communicate with the client using concrete and descriptive language."],"a":2,"r":"Clients with histrionic personality disorder need help with developing trusting (not superficial) relationships.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":588,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working on a mental health unit has become aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first assess the client who has:","o":["Bipolar disorder who is manic, has not slept in 24 hours, and is pacing in the hallways.","A major depressive disorder who was recently overheard saying, “I can’t take it anymore.”","Suicidal ideations and is reluctantly discussing a suicide contract with a staff member.","Anorexia nervosa and has gone to the restroom 15 minutes after eating a meal."],"a":1,"r":"A client verbalizing \"I can't take it anymore\" signals potential suicidal intent — immediate priority.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":589,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is talking with a parent of a 3-year-old child who states, “I am concerned that my child is in constant motion and is unable to sit long enough to listen to a story. I am wondering if my child may be hyperactive?” Which of the following responses is most appropriate for the nurse to make?","o":["“I am not seeing any signs of hyperactivity.”","“It is normal for parents to worry about hyperactivity in their children.”","“At 3 years of age, it is hard to tell.”","“Describe when and where this occurs.”"],"a":3,"r":"Asking for a description (“Describe when and where...”) gathers more objective data rather than assuming.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":590,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is teaching the partner of a client who had a stroke. Which of the following statements by the client’s partner indicates the need for additional teaching?","o":["“A change in my partner’s voice after swallowing may indicate dysphagia.”","“I should have my partner alternate a bite of food with sips of water.”","“Hesitancy with wanting to eat could indicate difficulty swallowing.”","“I will make sure that my partner has a snack 1 to 2 hours before going to bed.”"],"a":3,"r":"Late-night snacks increase aspiration risk in dysphagia; needs correction.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":591,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse instructed a client who has gastroesophageal reflex disease (GERD) about dietary and lifestyle modifications. Which of the following client statements indicates the need for additional teaching?","o":["“I will avoid wearing tight clothing.”","“I will sit upright for 1 to 2 hours after meals when possible.”","“I will sleep with the head of my bed elevated 6 to 12 inches.”","“I will have a snack 1 hour before bedtime.”"],"a":3,"r":"Eating before bed worsens GERD symptoms. Patients should avoid eating 2-3 hours before bedtime.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":592,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The charge nurse is observing a newly hired nurse care for a client who was admitted 12 hours ago with pneumonia and has just died. Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse requires intervention by the charge nurse?","o":["Attaching 2 identification tags to the body and 1 on the outside of the shroud (body bag).","Asking the family if they wish to participate in bathing and caring for the body.","Leaving drainage tubes and IV lines in the body prior to autopsy.","Leaving any jewelry on the client to be transported with them to the funeral home."],"a":3,"r":"Jewelry must be removed before transporting a body unless otherwise specified (e.g., coroners' case).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":593,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client who has high blood pressure about a low-sodium diet. Which of the following statements by the client indicates a correct understating of the teaching?","o":["“For breakfast, I could have a low-fat bagel with organic strawberry jam.”","“For lunch, I could have a chicken breast with barbecue sauce.”","“For a snack, I could have 2% cottage cheese and fruit.”","“For dinner, I could have grilled salmon and broccoli.”"],"a":3,"r":"Grilled salmon and broccoli = low sodium, heart-healthy. Barbecue sauce, cottage cheese, and bagels are often high-sodium.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":594,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer a Mantoux test to a client. Which of the following actions by the nurse indicates correct technique?","o":["Injecting the medication in the subcutaneous tissue.","Drawing up 0.1 mL of purified protein derivative (PPD) tuberculin.","Inserting the needle at a 90-degree angle.","Using a 20-gauge needle."],"a":1,"r":"0.1 mL of purified protein derivative (PPD) is correct for a Mantoux TB test.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":595,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse has just received a hand-off report and is assessing a client who is receiving a transfusion of O positive packed red blood cells (PRBCs). The client’s blood type is B positive. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Document on an incident report.","Continue to assess the client.","Immediately stop the transfusion.","Administer acetaminophen and diphenhydramine PRN."],"a":1,"r":"Not from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":596,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for an older adult client who is being seen for a well check-up. The primary health care provider (PHCP) prescribes the following vaccinations for the client: Herpes zoster (Zostavax) Influenza (quadrivalent) Inactivated polio (IPV) series Tetanus, diphtheria, and pertussis (Tdap) booster Which vaccination should the nurse clarify with the PHCP?","o":["Inactivated polio (IPV) series.","Tdap booster.","Herpes zoster (Zostavax).","Influenza (quadrivalent)."],"a":0,"r":"IPV (polio vaccine) is not routinely recommended for healthy adults; clarify order.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":597,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is prescribed warfarin for a prosthetic heart valve. Which of the following findings indicates a therapeutic effect of the medication?","o":["Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) of 35 seconds.","International normalized ratio (INR) of 3 seconds.","Prothrombin time (PT) of 11.6 seconds.","Partial thromboplastin time (PTT) of 20 seconds."],"a":1,"r":"INR of 3 is therapeutic for mechanical heart valves (range 2.5–3.5).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":598,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is preparing to start an IV infusion of lactated Ringer’s solution for a client who sustained a burn injury. The client is prescribed 5,200 mL of fluid over the first 24 hours. How many mL/hr should the nurse set the pump to infuse for the first 8 hours? Round to the nearest whole number.","o":["325 mL/hr.","433 mL/hr.","217 mL/hr.","350 mL/hr."],"a":0,"r":"Not from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":599,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer an insulin dose to an adult client. Which of the following actions is appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Limit the dose to 1 mL when injecting into the deltoid muscle.","Administer the dose using the Z-track method.","Draw up the dose using a 25-gauge, 3/6-inch needle.","Insert the needle at a 15-degree angle."],"a":2,"r":"Subcutaneous insulin requires a 25–27 gauge, 3/8- to 5/8-inch needle.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":600,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving streptomycin for treatment of an infection. Which of the following findings indicates an adverse effect of the medication?","o":["Creatinine level of 2.2 mg/dL.","Increased liver enzymes.","Photosensitivity.","Staining of the teeth."],"a":0,"r":"Creatinine 2.2 suggests nephrotoxicity — a known adverse effect of streptomycin (aminoglycoside).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":601,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer prescribed medications to the following clients. It is most important for the nurse to follow-up with the primary health care provider (PHCP) for the client who has:","o":["A prosthetic mitral valve, an international normalized ratio (INR) of 3, and has been prescribed warfarin.","Irritable bowel disease (IBD) and has been prescribed psyllium.","A methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) wound infection, an allergy to sulfonamides, and has been prescribed vancomycin.","Diabetes mellitus just had a computed tomography (CT) scan with IV contrast, and has been prescribed metformin."],"a":3,"r":"IV contrast with metformin risks lactic acidosis. Hold metformin after contrast and monitor renal function.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":602,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) is caring for a newly admitted client who has a history of peptic ulcer disease (PUD). Initial assessment findings include: BP: 110/72 mm Hg P: 90 Yellow colored emesis Frequent belching Hgb: 9.2 g/dL It is a priority for the nurse to:","o":["Determine if the client has any stressors.","Prepare the client for surgery.","Administer an IV antiemetic.","Type and cross for a packed red blood cell (PRBC) transfusion."],"a":3,"r":"Hgb 9.2 + GI symptoms (yellow emesis) = GI bleeding. Prepare for transfusion (type and crossmatch).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":603,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is in labor and receiving fetal heart monitoring. The fetal heart rate (FHR) is 180. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Continue to monitor the FHR.","Turn the client to the left side.","Assist the client with guided imagery.","Prepare the client for a cesarean delivery."],"a":1,"r":"A fetal heart rate of 180 bpm suggests fetal distress (tachycardia). First action: reposition to improve oxygenation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":604,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a pediatric client who is postoperative following ventriculoperitoneal IVP) shunt placement. The nurse notes that the client has become difficult to arouse. Which of the following actions should the nurse take immediately?","o":["Elevate the client’s head of bed (HOB) to 30 degrees.","Measure the client’s head circumference.","Prepare the client for endotracheal (ET) tube insertion.","Turn the client to the operative side."],"a":0,"r":"Difficult arousal = possible increased ICP. Elevate HOB to 30 degrees to reduce pressure.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":605,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is 4-hours postoperative after receiving general anesthesia. The client has a temperature (T) of 98.4 F (36.88 C), pulse (P) of 58 with thready peripheral pulses, a blood pressure (BP) of 98/64 mm Hg, respirations shallow with a rate of 26, and pale, dry skin. The client currently has an indwelling urethral catheter with 400 mL of clear yellow urine. The client is receiving IV 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) at 125 mL/hr. Arterial blood gas (ABG) results: pH 7.20, PaCO2 54 mm Hg, HCO3 26 mEq/L. Which assessment findings from the box before are related to the ABG findings? 1. IV fluids 2. Blood pressure 3. Pulse 4. Temperature 5. Urine output 6. General anesthesia 7. Pale, dry skin 8. Shallow respirations","o":["2, 3, 6, 7, 8.","4, 5, 7, 8.","2, 5, 6, 8.","1, 2, 4, 5, 6."],"a":0,"r":"Respiratory acidosis (pH ↓, PaCO2 ↑) correlates with shallow respirations, poor perfusion, and hypoventilation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":606,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse working in a pediatric cardiac unit is reviewing the current vital signs (VS) of assigned clients. Which of the following children should the nurse assess first?","o":["16-year-old adolescent who is sleeping and has a pulse (P) of 55.","2-year-old toddler who is walking around in the playroom and has a pulse (P) of 150.","6-year-old child who is sitting at a table eating and has a pulse (P) of 130.","2-month-old infant who is crying and has a pulse (P) of 170."],"a":2,"r":"A 6-year-old eating calmly should have a normal pulse (~75–115). Pulse 130 = tachycardia needing assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":607,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client who has lung cancer and is receiving external radiation therapy. Which of the following statements by the client indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I will need to avoid direct skin exposure to the sun during the treatment and for at least 1 year afterward.”","“I should avoid close contact with anyone who is pregnant or children under the age of 12 while I am receiving my treatments.”","“I should apply a 50-SPF sunscreen to the radiation area before going out into the sun.”","“I will need to avoid eating raw fruits and vegetables while receiving radiation therapy.”"],"a":0,"r":"Skin treated with radiation must avoid sun exposure directly for at least 1 year to prevent skin injury.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":608,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who had a kidney transplant 1 week ago. It is a priority for the nurse to notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if the client has a/an:","o":["White blood cell (WBC) count that has decreased from 6,000 to 5,000/mm3.","Fluid intake of 1,000 mL and a urine output of 800 mL over the past 8 hours.","Increase in weight from 199 to 203 lb over the past 24 hours.","Increase in temperature (T) from 98.2 to 99.3 F over the past 4 hours."],"a":2,"r":"A weight gain of 4 lbs/24 hours post-transplant suggests fluid retention, possibly acute rejection. Report immediately. 45. C ---- ---------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":609,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who had gastric surgery 8 hours ago and has a nasogastric (NG) tube to low wall suction. Which of the interventions from the box below should the nurse include in the plan of care? 1. Turn down the suction when assessing for peristalsis. 2. Encourage the use of incentive spirometer (IS) every 4 hours while awake. 3. Maintain sequential compression device to bilateral lower extremities. 4. Irrigate the NG tube with warm tap water as needed. 5. Turn and reposition the client every 2 hours. 6. Place a sign at the head of the bed (HOB) that says, “Do not manipulate the NG tube.”","o":["1, 3, 5.","2, 4, 5.","1, 2, 3.","3, 5, 6."],"a":3,"r":"After gastric surgery, key interventions include sequential compression devices (SCDs) to prevent DVT (3), turning every 2 hours to promote lung expansion and circulation (5), and posting a sign not to manipulate the NG tube to prevent dislodging (6).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":610,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse has performed tracheostomy care for an assigned client. Which of the following findings requires intervention by the nurse before leaving the client’s room?","o":["There is space for 1 finger to be placed between the tie and the neck.","The client places a finger over the tube opening when speaking.","The tracheostomy cuff can be easily inflated and deflated.","The tracheostomy tube moves in and out of the stoma opening when the client coughs."],"a":3,"r":"A tracheostomy tube should not move in and out of the stoma when the client coughs. This suggests the tube is unstable and could dislodge, requiring immediate intervention.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":611,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving mechanical ventilation when the low-pressure alarm sounds. Which of the following actions should the nurse take next?","o":["Manually ventilate the client with a bag-mask-valve device.","Shut the alarm off and call a code.","Call respirate therapy (RT) to check the ventilator settings.","Assess the cuff of the client’s endotracheal (ET) tube."],"a":3,"r":"A low-pressure alarm often indicates a cuff leak or disconnection. The nurse should first assess the cuff on the endotracheal (ET) tube to find the source of the leak.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":612,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is providing discharge instructions to the parents of a child who was admitted with sickle cell crisis. Which of the following instructions should the nurse include in the teaching?","o":["“Restrict play activity to 1 hour per day.”","“Monitor your child’s temperature daily.”","“Avoid areas with low altitude.”","“Maintain a warm temperature in your home.”"],"a":1,"r":"After a sickle cell crisis, monitoring temperature daily is important because infection can trigger another crisis. Avoiding high altitudes (not low), promoting hydration, and maintaining warmth are also part of teaching.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":613,"t":17,"s":36,"q":"The nurse is caring for an older adult client who has dementia and often displays confabulation to cope with anxiety. The nurse should recognize this coping mechanism when the client:","o":["Displays ritualistic type behaviors.","Refuses to see family members.","Tells made-up stories.","Reminisces about the past. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------"],"a":2,"r":"Confabulation refers to fabricated or made-up stories the client tells to fill memory gaps, commonly seen in clients with dementia coping with cognitive deficits.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":614,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has attended a conference on legal and ethical issues in nursing practice. Which of the following statements by the nurse would indicate a correct understanding of the conference?","o":["“A durable power of attorney for health care’s (DPAHCs) authority goes into effect once a client can no longer make decisions.”","“Advanced directives are routinely suspended during treatments and surgical procedures.”","“A ‘do not resuscitate’ (DNR) order must be renewed every 48 hours by the client’s health care proxy.”","“A nurse should serve as a witness to the signature of a client who signs an advanced directive.”"],"a":0,"r":"A Durable Power of Attorney for Health Care (DPAHC) becomes effective when the client is unable to make their own medical decisions.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":615,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is triaging children in a pediatric asthma clinic. The nurse should ask the primary health care provider (PHCP) to first see the child who is","o":["14 years old, has moderate persistent asthma, and whose peak flow rates have dropped from 96% to 88% of the personal best.","12 years old, has mild persistent asthma, and has been using the prescribed rescue inhaler every two hours without relief.","7 years old, has exercise-induced asthma, and required the use of an albuterol inhaler while playing soccer two hours ago.","6 years old, has intermittent asthma, and awoke this morning with a fever and a productive cough."],"a":1,"r":"Using a rescue inhaler every two hours without relief indicates worsening symptoms and possible status asthmaticus.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":616,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has had a C7 spinal cord injury. The client’s blood pressure has changed from 136/84 mm Hg to 156/96 mm Hg and the client is reporting nasal stuffiness and a severe, throbbing headache. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Auscultate the client’s breath sounds.","Assess the client’s oxygen saturation level.","Elevate the head of the client’s bed.","Determine the client’s pulse pressure."],"a":2,"r":"This is likely autonomic dysreflexia. Elevating the head of the bed reduces blood pressure and prevents complications.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":617,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has cirrhosis of the liver with portal hypertension and esophageal varices. It would be necessary for the nurse to immediately notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if a client has","o":["a musty odor to the breath.","an elevated serum ammonia level.","passed a black, tarry stool.","palms that are warm and erythematous."],"a":2,"r":"Black, tarry stools suggest GI bleeding from esophageal varices, which is life-threatening.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":618,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has been made aware of the following client situations. It would be necessary for the nurse to immediately notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if a client has","o":["congestive heart failure (CHF), received digoxin two hours ago, and has a pulse rate that has increased from 72 to 83 over the past hour.","emphysema and has an oxygen saturation level that has decreased from 93% to 87% after ambulating in the hallway.","pericarditis and has jugular veins that are now visible when the head of the client’s bed is at 60 degrees.","has a suspected myocardial infarction (MI), is receiving a continuous heparin infusion, and has an international normalized ratio (INR) that is three times the control value."],"a":2,"r":"Jugular vein distention in pericarditis can suggest cardiac tamponade, which is an emergency.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":619,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse working in an emergency department (ED) is participating in an external disaster drill. The nurse should first assess the client who is","o":["8 months old, has a laceration over the left eye, a blood pressure of 84/50 mm Hg, and a pulse rate of 105.","28 years old, has a hematoma on the forehead, a Glasgow coma scale (GCS) score of 13, and is crying.","20 years old, has a traumatic left below the knee amputation (BKA), a blood pressure of 70/46 mm Hg, and a pulse rate of 124.","6 years old, has crush injuries to both legs, fixed and dilated pupils, and an absent pulse."],"a":2,"r":"The adult with BKA and hypotension is in shock and needs immediate assessment/intervention.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":620,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for assigned clients. The nurse should recommend to the primary health care provider (PHCP) that a referral be initiated to a mental health professional for which of the following clients?","o":["44-year-old who has multiple sclerosis (MS), and is reporting extreme fatigue and periods of forgetfulness.","39-year-old who has myasthenia gravis, cries frequently, and has developed a decreased appetite.","53-year-old who recently had a cerebral vascular accident (CVA), has labile emotions, and is uncharacteristically using profanity.","69-year-old who has Parkinson’s syndrome, a flat affect, and monotone speech."],"a":1,"r":"Crying, appetite loss, and myasthenia gravis may indicate depression requiring mental health support.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":621,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse working the emergency department (ED) is assessing a client involved in a motor vehicle accident (MVA) and notes a large hematoma on the client’s right flank. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Obtain a urine specimen from the client.","Determine the client’s vital signs.","Palpate the client’s bladder.","Auscultate the client’s bowel sounds."],"a":1,"r":"Vital signs give an immediate indication of internal bleeding or shock, especially with flank trauma.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":622,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is participating in the development of a staff education program about ethical responsibilities in nursing practice. Which of the following should the nurse include as an ethical dilemma that requires follow up by a nurse?","o":["A family member is talking with the client about the client’s diagnosis while the roommate is present.","A nurse is preparing to administer a unit of prescribed packed red blood cells (PRBCs) to a client who is a Jehovah’s Witness.","A primary health care provider (PHCP) is placing a percutaneous intravenous central catheter (PICC) line into a client who wishes to receive palliative care.","A client who is scheduled for surgery in one hour does not have a signed surgical consent form in the medical record."],"a":1,"r":"Administering PRBCs to a Jehovah’s Witness violates their religious beliefs and requires an ethical follow-up.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":623,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has provided discharge instructions to a mother who gave birth to a healthy, full-term male. Which of the following statements by the mother would indicate a correct understanding of the instructions?","o":["“I should expect that my baby’s umbilical cord will fall off within the first week of life.”","“I should place my baby on his back to sleep for the first year of life.”","“I should wash my baby’s penis with soap and water until the circumcision is healed.”","“I should make sure that the bumper pads in my baby’s crib are hypoallergenic.”"],"a":1,"r":"The safest sleeping position to prevent SIDS is on the back until 1 year of age.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":624,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has attended a staff development conference on providing safe care for clients who have had a stroke. It would indicate the need for additional instruction if the nurse states","o":["“I should assess the blink reflex of a client who has had a stroke.”","“I will instruct caregivers to approach a client with unilateral neglect on the unaffected side.”","“I will place the wheelchair on the client’s affected side when transferring the client from the bed to the wheelchair.”","“I should ensure that client’s who have a stroke wear their hearing aid and glasses while awake.”"],"a":2,"r":"The wheelchair should be placed on the unaffected side to promote safety during transfers.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":625,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is discussing the use of physical restraints with a licensed practical/vocational nurse (LPN/VN). It would require follow up by the nurse if the LPN/VN states","o":["“I will release the restraint and perform range-of-motion (ROM) exercises every two hours.”","“I will use hand mitts rather than wrist restraints whenever possible.”","“I will make sure that I can not fit my finger between the restraint and the client’s extremity.”","“I will check on the client once every 30 to 60 minutes.”"],"a":2,"r":"Restraints should allow room for fingers to slip underneath—tight restraints risk circulation issues.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":626,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) is triaging recently admitting clients. Which of the following actions would be appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Triaging a client who has a rectal temperature of 87.8^(○ )F and is bradycardic as urgent.","Placing a client who has suspected Haemophilus influenzae on droplet precautions.","Triaging a client who has multiple fractures as emergent.","Placing a client who has suspected inhalation anthrax on airborne precautions."],"a":1,"r":"Suspected Haemophilus influenzae requires droplet precautions.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":627,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nursing supervisor is participating in a disaster drill. In order to increase the availability of beds, which of the clients should the nursing supervisor recommend for discharge from the hospital?","o":["14-year-old with anorexia nervosa whose weight is within the fifth percentile and has a recent potassium level of 3.2 mEq/L.","2-year-old child who was recently diagnosed with cystic fibrosis (CF) who requires percussion and postural drainage twice a day.","10-year-old who was admitted with Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) two days ago and is receiving IV immunoglobulin (IVIG).","7-year-old child who was admitted with acute glomerulonephritis (AGN) and has a blood pressure that ranges from 140/82 to 154/86 mm Hg."],"a":0,"r":"Despite a low potassium level, this patient is the most stable and can be discharged to free up beds.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":628,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The charge nurse has been made aware of the following client situations. It would be necessary for an incident report to be completed if a","o":["client who has Parkinson’s syndrome falls while being transferred from the bed to the chair.","nurse who has not had the hepatitis B vaccine is caring for a client who has hepatitis B virus (HBV).","client who has methicillin resistant staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) refuses to take a prescribed antibiotic.","nurse documents the administration of a medication to a client one hour after it was given."],"a":3,"r":"Late documentation of med administration can create legal issues and should be reported.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":629,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has been made aware of the following client situations. The nurse should first plan to see the client who has","o":["cervical cancer, is receiving internal radiation therapy, and is attempting to ambulate to the bathroom.","thrombocytopenia and has a visitor who has brought fresh fruit and flowers into the client’s room.","bone marrow suppression who is receiving prescribed epoetin alfa and has a change in blood pressure from 140/94 mm Hg to 124/80 mm Hg over the past hour.","neutropenia and has a recent white blood cell (WBC) count of 3,000 mm³ and is eating homemade cookies brought in by a visitor."],"a":0,"r":"The client with internal radiation must stay on bedrest to prevent dislodging the radioactive source.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":630,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse in a community health center is screening clients for the risk of developing bladder cancer. Which of the following questions would be most important for the nurse to ask?","o":["“Do you have a history of cigarette smoking? ”","“Do you routinely take non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)?”","“Have you ever been diagnosed with kidney stones?”","“Have you ever had more than two urinary tract infections (UTIs) in one year?”"],"a":0,"r":"Smoking is the most significant modifiable risk factor for bladder cancer.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":631,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is talking with the following female clients at a health fair. It would be most important for the nurse to suggest follow up with the primary health care provider (PHCP) for the client who is","o":["36 years old, reports taking oral contraceptives for the past 10 years and has not had a baseline mammogram performed.","57 years old, reports a 40-year history of smoking cigarettes and has bronchovesicular breath sounds ausculated between the scapulae.","67 years old, has never had children, and is experiencing episodes of vaginal bleeding.","48 years old, has been treated for an infection of the cervix with human papillomavirus (HPV) and has a negative pap smear."],"a":2,"r":"Vaginal bleeding post-menopause is never normal and requires evaluation for possible endometrial cancer.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":632,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is providing discharge instructions to the parents of a newborn who was delivered vaginally without complications. Which of the following statements by a parent would indicate a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“We should expect our infant to have a wet diaper at least six times a day.”","“We will need to bathe our infant in tepid water once a day until the umbilical cord falls off.”","“We will need to bring our infant in for the first follow up appointment at 2 weeks of age.”","“We should swaddle our infant in a blanket before placing our infant on the abdomen to go to sleep.”"],"a":0,"r":"Infants should have at least six wet diapers/day to ensure adequate hydration.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":633,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client who was recently diagnosed with osteoporosis. Which of the following client statements would indicate a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I should walk for 30 minutes three to five times a week.”","“I need to swim for 30 minutes at least three times a week.”","“I will limit my caloric intake to 1500 calories per day.”","“I must avoid drinking grapefruit juice because I am taking calcium supplements.”"],"a":0,"r":"Weight-bearing exercise like walking strengthens bones and prevents progression of osteoporosis.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":634,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is preparing discharge plans for a client who requires treatment for infective endocarditis. The nurse should recognize that the factor that will have the greatest impact on discharge planning is the fact that the client","o":["does not have an automobile or a driver’s license.","immigrated to the country from Liberia six months ago.","is homeless and intermittently lives in a shelter.","stopped going to school after the ninth grade."],"a":2,"r":"Homelessness impacts discharge planning, medication adherence, and follow-up care for infective endocarditis.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":635,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving treatment for pancreatic cancer. Upon entering the client’s room, the nurse observes the client’s spouse sitting at the bedside, staring out the window, and crying. When approached by the nurse, the spouse states, “I’m so worried.” Which of the following statements would be therapeutic for the nurse to make?","o":["“Are you able to take good care of yourself?”","“Life must seem very unfair right now.”","“I’ll stay here and sit with you awhile.”","“Have you considered attending a support group?”"],"a":1,"r":"This response shows empathy and therapeutic communication.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":636,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is admitting a client diagnosed with major depression. Which of the following questions would be a priority for the nurse to ask the client?","o":["“Do you have a plan to hurt yourself?”","“How long have you been feeling this way?”","“Have you completed a no-harm contract?”","“Have you ever attempted to commit suicide in the past?”"],"a":0,"r":"Suicide assessment is always the priority when a client presents with depression.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":637,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has paranoid schizophrenia and a history of command hallucinations. The client is pacing and yelling in the day room, \"I will do it. I will do it!\" Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Determine if the client plans to act upon the hallucination.","Ignore the client's behavior until the client becomes calm.","Using a firm, loud voice, instruct the client to stop yelling.","Encourage the client to return to the room."],"a":0,"r":"Assess for suicidal or harmful intent if a client has command hallucinations.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":638,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client who has borderline personally disorder (BPD). Which of the following findings is consistent with this diagnosis?","o":["Ritualistic behavior to relieve stress.","Irresistible urge to cut self.","Binge eating.","Hearing voices."],"a":1,"r":"Borderline Personality Disorder is associated with self-harm behaviors like cutting.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":639,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client about how to care for a hearing aid. Which of the following client statements would require follow up by the nurse?","o":["“I will keep extra batteries readily available.”","“I will adjust the hearing aid volume to the highest setting that allows me to hear.”","“I will clean the ear mold with mild soap and water.”","“I will turn off the hearing aid and remove the disposable battery when it is not in use.”"],"a":1,"r":"Setting the hearing aid to the highest volume may cause feedback or hearing damage; gradual adjustment is safer.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":640,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is instructing a client who has full weight bearing ambulation prescribed how to properly use a walker. Which of the following instructions should the nurse give the client?","o":["“Use the chair arms for support when getting out of a chair to the walker.”","“Advance the walker 12 inches and then take a step forward.”","“Look down at your feet when moving them forward.”","“Hold the walker with the elbows locked.”"],"a":0,"r":"Support with chair arms prevents falls and promotes safety when standing.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":641,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly-hired nurse provide postmortem care for a client. Which of the following actions by the nurse would require follow up by the preceptor?","o":["Positioning the client with the head of bed (HOB) elevated at 60 degrees during family visitation.","Keeping the client’s dentures in place until the family has had an opportunity to visit.","Removing soiled dressings and replacing them with clean dressings.","Ensuring the death certificate has been signed by the primary health care provider (PHCP)."],"a":0,"r":"Elevating the head of bed that high postmortem may cause the client to appear unnatural and should be followed up.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":642,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has an ileal conduit. Which of the following actions should the nurse take when caring for the client?","o":["Applying a skin protectant to a 2-inch radius around the stoma when changing the stoma appliance.","Placing a surgical mask on the client when changing the stoma appliance.","Notifying the primary health care provider (PHCP) if mucus is noted in the urine.","Instructing the client to empty the drainage bag once in the morning and once in the evening."],"a":0,"r":"Applying protectant around the stoma helps prevent skin breakdown due to constant exposure to urine.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":643,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is providing instructions to a client who has a history of developing uric acid kidney stones. Which of the following information should the nurse give the client?","o":["“Drink at least 750 mL of water every two hours. “","“Decrease your intake of foods containing purine.”","“Limit your sodium intake to 1000 mg/day.”","“Drink at least 8 ounces of cranberry juice every day.”"],"a":1,"r":"Uric acid stones are linked to purine metabolism; dietary purine intake should be decreased.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":644,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has provided medication instruction to a client with osteoporosis who has just been prescribed the bisphosphonate alendronate. Which of the following instructions should the nurse provide the client?","o":["“You should take this medication at least one hour after you take your medication to treat gastric reflux.”","“You will need to stop taking this medication two weeks before having a dental implant.”","“You will need to sit upright for 30 to 60 minutes after taking this medication.”","“You should take this medication with foods containing calcium.”"],"a":2,"r":"Sitting upright prevents esophageal irritation and ulcers from alendronate.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":645,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) has received the following prescriptions for newly admitted clients. Which prescription should the nurse implement first?","o":["Administer dexamethasone 12 mg IV to a client admitted with acute adrenal insufficiency.","Give lorazepam 2 mg, IM to a client who has alcohol dependence and is slightly agitated.","Administer human lispro 10 units SQ to a client who has type 2 diabetes mellitus and has a blood glucose level of 290 mg/dL.","Add potassium chloride (KCL) 40 mEq/L to the intravenous infusion of a client admitted with a suspected myocardial infarction (MI) who has has a serum potassium level of 3.5 mEq/L."],"a":0,"r":"Acute adrenal insufficiency is life-threatening and should be treated immediately with corticosteroids.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":646,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has been prescribed dopamine at 6 mcg/kg/min. The nurse has on hand dopamine 400 mg/250 mL D5W. The client weighs 65 kg. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Infuse the dopamine drip via an infusion pump at 14.6 mL/hr.","Monitor and record the client’s vital signs once every four hours.","Withhold the dopamine drip if the client’s systolic blood pressure is < 100 mm Hg.","Contact the primary health care provider (PHCP) for an order clarification."],"a":0,"r":"6 mcg/kg/min for a 65 kg patient = 390 mcg/min. With a 1.6 mg/mL solution, 390/1600 = 0.24375 mL/min = ~14.6 mL/hr.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":647,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who was admitted 24 hours ago and is receiving a prescribed continuous infusion of heparin sodium. It would indicate that the client is having a therapeutic response to the heparin if the client has","o":["a decrease in the platelet count from 350,000 mm³ to 250,000 mm³","activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) that is 1.5 times the control value.","a decrease in the amount of petechiae over the chest and axilla.","an increase in the oxygen saturation from 88% to 94%."],"a":1,"r":"Therapeutic aPTT for heparin is 1.5–2.5× control; this suggests therapeutic response.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":648,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is in active labor and has received an epidural for pain relief 10 minutes ago. It would require immediate intervention by the nurse if the client","o":["reports that she does not yet feel pain relief.","does not seem aware of the intensity of her contractions","states she feels tingling in her legs and feet.","reports the development of dizziness and lightheadedness."],"a":3,"r":"Hypotension (dizziness, lightheadedness) is a complication of epidural and must be treated immediately.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":649,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse working in the surgical center is performing a preoperative assessment for a 53-year-old female client who is scheduled to have a total hip replacement in one week. It would be a priority for the nurse to notify the surgeon if the client reports","o":["the last menstrual period (LMP) was one year ago.","taking prescribed levothyroxine for the treatment of hypothyroidism.","taking the herbal preparations garlic, ginkgo biloba, and ginseng.","having a history of osteoarthritis."],"a":2,"r":"Herbal supplements like garlic, ginkgo, and ginseng increase bleeding risk and should be discontinued before surgery.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":650,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is taking a prescribed potassium-sparing diuretic. Which of the following medications, if ordered by the primary health care provider (PHCP), should the nurse question?","o":["an angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor.","a statin.","a calcium channel blocker.","an anticoagulant."],"a":0,"r":"Potassium-sparing diuretics + ACE inhibitors increase risk of hyperkalemia.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":651,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is teaching a client about insulin administration. Which of the following client statements would indicate the need for additional teaching?","o":["“I need to gently massage the site after I inject the insulin.”","“I must mix insulin by drawing up regular insulin first, before drawing up NPH insulin in the same syringe.”","“I should not recap my insulin syringe before I dispose of the syringe.”","“I can keep the bottle of regular insulin that I am using at room temperature for up to 28 days.”"],"a":0,"r":"Massaging after insulin injection may increase absorption rate and risk of hypoglycemia.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":652,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client who has a fractured left tibia and has a cast on the left leg. It would be a most important for the nurse to follow up if the client states","o":["“Both of my feet seem slightly cool to me.”","“My leg pain has increased since I received my pain medication.”","“My cast seems loose around my ankle.”","“Can you do something to stop the itching I have under my cast?”"],"a":1,"r":"Increasing pain after pain medication could suggest compartment syndrome, a medical emergency.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":653,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has a sudden onset of slurred speech, confusion and unilateral weakness. Which diagnostic test for this client should the nurse question?","o":["A 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG).","Lumbar puncture (LP).","Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI).","Computed tomography (CT) scan."],"a":1,"r":"LP should not be performed until hemorrhagic stroke has been ruled out (risk of brain herniation).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":654,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is reviewing the laboratory results of a male client who is scheduled to have a cardiac catheterization in one hour. Which of the following results should the nurse immediately report to the primary health care provider (PHCP)?","o":["Platelet count of 85,000 mm³.","Blood glucose level of 162 mg/dL.","Hematocrit (Hct) level of 45%.","Cholesterol level of 235 mg/dL."],"a":0,"r":"A platelet count <100,000 increases bleeding risk and should be reported prior to an invasive procedure.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":655,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse working in the post-anesthesia care unit (PACU) is caring for a client who had abdominal surgery under general anesthesia. The client is receiving oxygen at 40% facemask and the respirations are shallow at a rate of 7 breaths/minute. Which of the following arterial blood gas (ABG) results are consistent with the client’s current respiratory assessment?","o":["pH = 7.45; PaO₂ = 70 mm Hg; PaCO₂ = 38 mm Hg; HCO₃⁻ = 28 mEq/L; SaO₂ = 86%.","pH = 7.31; PaO₂ = 73 mm Hg; PaCO₂ = 51 mm Hg; HCO₃⁻ = 22 mEq/L; SaO₂ = 85%.","pH = 7.36; PaO₂ = 63 mm Hg; PaCO₂ = 42 mm Hg; HCO₃⁻ = 26 mEq/L; SaO₂ = 84%.","pH = 7.50; PaO₂ = 79 mm Hg; PaCO₂ = 35 mm Hg; HCO₃⁻ = 30 mEq/L; SaO₂ = 88%."],"a":1,"r":"These ABG values indicate respiratory acidosis due to hypoventilation under anesthesia.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":656,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is auscultating the mitral valve. It would be correct technique if the nurse placed the stethoscope on the","o":["fourth intercostal space, left sternal border.","fifth intercostal space, midclavicular line.","third intercostal space, left midclavicular line.","second intercostal space, right sternal border."],"a":1,"r":"The mitral valve is best auscultated at the fifth intercostal space, midclavicular line.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":657,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The home health nurse is observing a client provide self-tracheostomy care. It would require follow up if the client","o":["allows for two fingers to be placed between the tracheostomy ties and the neck.","cleans the stoma site with half-strength hydrogen peroxide followed by sterile saline","removes the old tracheostomy ties before applying the new ties.","rinses the inner cannula with sterile saline after washing it with half-strength hydrogen peroxide."],"a":2,"r":"The old trach ties should remain in place until the new ones are secured to avoid accidental decannulation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":658,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse has attended a staff development conference about osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Which of the following statements, if made by the nurse, would indicate a correct understanding of arthritis?","o":["“Osteoarthritis often occurs as a result of antibodies collecting in the joints from a previous infection.”","“RA presents with stiffness upon awakening which decreases as the day goes on.”","“RA primarily affects joints on one side of the body.”","“Osteoarthritis can be treated with immunosuppressive agents.”"],"a":1,"r":"RA stiffness decreases throughout the day; OA pain worsens with activity.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":659,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"A nurse has just received report on a client who was transferred from the post-anesthesia care unit (PACU) following femoral-popliteal bypass graft surgery. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Teach the client to utilize an incentive spirometer every hour to improve lung expansion.","Determine when the client received the last dose of pain medication.","Assess the client’s dorsalis pedis pulse in the operative leg.","Assess the client’s boney prominences for the presence of skin breakdown."],"a":2,"r":"Assessing perfusion of the operative leg is the top priority following bypass graft surgery.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":660,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is performing a preoperative assessment for a client who is scheduled to have surgery under general anesthesia. It would be a priority for the nurse to notify the surgeon if the client states","o":["“I sometimes become so claustrophobic that I feel like I should run.”","“I had my will finalized last week, just in case something happens during my surgery today.”","“I have a brother who died unexpectedly during surgery when his temperature became very high.”","“I have heard about surgeons removing an incorrect limb during surgery, does that happen here?”"],"a":2,"r":"This may indicate a family history of malignant hyperthermia, a surgical emergency.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":661,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who had a transsphenoidal hypophysectomy 12 hours ago. It would require immediate follow up by the nurse if the client","o":["has current vital signs that are T: 97.6 °F, P: 96, R: 20, and BP: 130/80 mm Hg.","has a 2 cm amount of dark-red drainage on the surgical dressing.","is alert to name and date but is unable to tell the nurse the name of the hospital.","has an intake of 1,000 mL and a urinary output of 2500 mL since surgery."],"a":3,"r":"Excessive urinary output may indicate diabetes insipidus, a complication of pituitary surgery.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":662,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is caring for a newly admitted client who has a prescription for a continuous intravenous (IV) infusion of D5.45 NaCl with 20 mEq of KCl/L. The client does not have an IV in place and is right-handed. Which of the following actions would be appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Prepare the insertion site with antibacterial soap and water.","Shave any hair that is located near the selected IV insertion site.","Insert the IV catheter in the right hand near the wrist.","Select a 20-gauge, 1 or 1-1/4-inch length peripheral catheter."],"a":3,"r":"A 20-gauge, 1 to 1¼ inch catheter is appropriate for general IV fluids and KCl.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":663,"t":17,"s":37,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client who has lung cancer and is at risk for developing superior vena cava syndrome (SVCS). Which of the following findings is consistent with this diagnosis?","o":["Evidence of swelling of the arms and face.","A diastolic blood pressure greater than 100 mm Hg.","The development of petechiae on the chest and back.","A report of tingling of the lower extremities."],"a":0,"r":"Swelling of the face and upper body is a classic sign of SVCS due to impaired venous return.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":664,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse insert an indwelling urethral catheter into a female client. Which of the following actions performed by the newly hired nurse requires follow up by the nurse preceptor?","o":["Selecting a 16F catheter with a 10ml balloon","Immediately obtaining a urine specimen from the drainage bag","Cleaning the meatus with antiseptic swab after cleaning both labial folds","Inflating the catheter balloon as soon as a return of urine is noted in the tubing (once you get return advance an additional 1-2inches)"],"a":1,"r":"Urine specimens should be obtained from the sampling port, not the drainage bag, because the bag may contain contaminated urine.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":665,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client and administers the wrong drug. The nurse performs an assessment and does not identify any adverse effects. Which of the following actions should the nurse take next?","o":["Notify the client’s primary health care provider (PHCP).","Complete an incident report.","Ask the client if they have taken the medication in the past.","Document client status in the medical record."],"a":0,"r":"After assessing the client, the nurse should immediately notify the provider regarding the medication error. Documentation and incident reports follow.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":666,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has received the change of shift report and is assigning task to an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP). Which of the following tasks should the nurse instruct the UAP to perform first?","o":["Recheck the vital signs of a client who returned from a liver biopsy 2 hours ago","Deliver a lunch tray to a client who is returning from radiology and has been NPO since breakfast.","Turn and reposition a client who was last turned 2 hours ago","Obtain a sputum specimen from a client who has suspected pulmonary tuberculosis (TB)"],"a":0,"r":"A client returning from a liver biopsy is at risk for hemorrhage; vital signs are the priority assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":667,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for assigned clients. Which of the following actions is appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Placing a client who had a stem cell transplant on contact precautions","Having a client with pertussis wear a particulate respirator (N95) mask during transport","Initiating airborne precautions for the client diagnosed with epiglottitis","Wearing a particulate respirator (N95) mask when caring for a client who has disseminated varicella zoster"],"a":3,"r":"Disseminated varicella requires airborne and contact precautions; an N95 respirator is required.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":668,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has been receiving prescribed warfarin for the past 2 weeks. Which of the following lab values indicates that the client is having a therapeutic response to the medication?","o":["A prothrombin time (PT) of 15 seconds","A platelet count that has increased from 100,000 to 150,000mm","An international normalized ratio (INR) of 2 seconds","An activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) that has increased from 30 to 60 seconds"],"a":2,"r":"Therapeutic INR for warfarin is generally 2–3.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":669,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is assessing a client who has diabetes mellitus (type 1), a blood glucose level of 725mg/dL, and a diagnosis of hyperglycemic-hyperosmolar state (HHS). Which of the following assessment findings should the nurse expect the client to have?","o":["Positive urine ketones","Fluid Volume deficit","A serum pH less than 7.35","Kussmaul respirations"],"a":1,"r":"HHS causes profound dehydration and fluid volume deficit. Ketosis and acidosis are usually absent.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":670,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working on the medical surgical unit has just received a hand off report on assigned clients. The nurse should first plan to assess the client who has","o":["Acute pancreatitis and is reporting radiation of pain to the left shoulder","Myasthenia gravis and is experiencing ptosis and diplopia","Atrial fibrillation (AF) and reports severe pain in the right lower extremity","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) who intermittently coughs up a moderate amount of secretions"],"a":2,"r":"Severe leg pain in a client with atrial fibrillation suggests arterial embolism and impaired perfusion requiring immediate intervention.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":671,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has Addison disease and is at risk for Addison crisis. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Provide a low carbohydrate diet","Monitor blood pressure frequently","Administer oral desmopressin","Restrict fluid intake"],"a":1,"r":"Addisonian crisis can cause severe hypotension; frequent BP monitoring is essential.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":672,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has a new prescription for knee high anti embolism stockings. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Fold the top of the stockings over if they are too long","Remove the stockings once each day for 1 hour","Make sure the stockings are tight around the clients toes","Measure the circumference and length of the clients calf"],"a":3,"r":"Proper fit requires measuring calf circumference and length before application.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":673,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) is precepting a newly hired nurse. It requires intervention by the preceptor if the newly hired nurse","o":["Gently massages the fingers of a client who has frostbite","Refuses to give prescribed lorazepam to a client who is anxious and needs to sign a consent form","Removes the pants of a client with chemical burns to the lower extremities by cutting them off","Places a client who has a temperature of 104.5 F on a cooling blanket"],"a":0,"r":"Frostbitten tissue should never be massaged because it increases tissue damage.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":674,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in a pediatric health clinic has assessed the following clients. Which client findings indicates to the nurse the presence of a developmental delay?","o":["A 1 month old who does not have head control","A 3 month old who does not roll over","A 9 month old who does not sit without support","A 10 month old who does not drink from a cup"],"a":2,"r":"A 9-month-old should sit independently. Failure to do so suggests developmental delay.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":675,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has an anxiety disorder and has begun having a panic attack. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Ask the client what led to the attack","Offer the client a prescribed PRN antianxiety medication","Tell the client the nurse will stay with them","Take the client outside for mild exercise"],"a":2,"r":"During a panic attack, the priority is to remain with the client and provide reassurance and safety.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":676,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is teaching the partner of a client who had a stroke and has developed dysphagia. Which of the following statements by the clients partner indicates the need for additional teaching?","o":["I will provide my partner with fruits that are juicier and softer to swallow","I should serve meals to my partner when they are feeling the strongest","I will make sure that my partner tilts the head forward while eating","I should monitor my partner for pocketing food in the mouth while eating"],"a":0,"r":"Juicy foods may increase aspiration risk in dysphagia clients.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":677,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is 12 hours postoperative from a transurethral resection of the prostate(TURP). Which of the following should the nurse include in the clients plan of care?","o":["Maintain the bladder irrigation flow rate to keep urine a light pink color","I will be able to take this medication while I am pregnant","I will notify my primary health care provider immediately if I develop a sore throat","It is normal to develop bruising while taking this medication"],"a":0,"r":"Following TURP with continuous bladder irrigation, urine should remain light pink.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":678,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has an infection and has been prescribed gentamicin intermittent IV bolus Q8hr. A peak and trough level is required with the new order. Which of the following actions is correct for the nurse to take?","o":["Draw a trough level immediately prior to administering the medication and a peak level 30 min after the dose","Draw a peak level 90 min prior to administering the medication and a peak level 30 min after the dose","Draw a peak level at 0900 hours and a trough level at 2100","Draw a trough level at 0900 hours and a peak level at 2100"],"a":0,"r":"Aminoglycoside trough levels are drawn immediately before administration; peak levels are drawn approximately 30 minutes after infusion.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":679,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is teaching individuals at a community health fair about guidelines for health related screenings, examinations, and immunizations. It is correct for the nurse to state that","o":["Children age 5 and older should receive the pneumococcal vaccine every year","Children who are between the ages of 10 to 14 should be screened annually for scolosis","Men age 40 and over who are at average risk for prostate cancer should have a prostate specific antigen (PS) test performed every 5 years.","Men and women age 50 and older should have a colonoscopy every 2 years"],"a":1,"r":"School-age children are commonly screened annually for scoliosis during adolescence.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":680,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has attended a conference on informed consent. It indicates a correct understanding of the teaching if the nurse states that informed consent","o":["Is an agreement to receive medical treatment that cant be revoked once signed","Forms must be signed when a client is competent to make an informed decision","Is signed by the nurse to indicate that the client understands the procedure","Is the nurses responsibility to explain the risks, benefits, and alternative treatments"],"a":1,"r":"Clients must be competent when signing informed consent.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":681,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has been receiving prescribed warfarin for the past 2 weeks. Which of the following lab values indicates that the client is having a therapeutic response to the medication?","o":["A prothrombin time (PT) of 15 seconds","A platelet count that has increased from 100,000 to 150,000 mm","An international normalized ratio (INR) of 2 seconds","An activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) that has increased from 30 to 60 seconds"],"a":2,"r":"Therapeutic INR for warfarin is approximately 2–3.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":682,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is preparing a client who has pneumonia for percussion, vibration, and postural drainage. Which of the following actions is most important for the nurse to take prior to performing this treatment?","o":["Review the clients most recent arterial blood gas (ABG) result","Instruct the client how to slowly inhale using pursed lips","Assess the character and consistency of the clients sputum","Auscultate the clients breath sounds"],"a":3,"r":"Breath sounds should be assessed before chest physiotherapy to establish a baseline.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":683,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has terminal cancer and is receiving intravenous (IV) morphine sulfate 1 mg every 2 hours for pain. The medication is dispensed as a prefilled injectable of 2mg/ml. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Use a 1ml syringe to remove 1 mg of the medication from the prefilled injectable for later use","Administer 1mg of the medication from the prefilled injectable and lock up the remaining 1mg","Have another nurse witness the waste of 1mg of the medication from the prefilled injectable prior to administration","Admin 1 mg of the medication from the prefilled injectable and then have another nurse witness the waste of the remaining medication"],"a":2,"r":"Controlled substances require a witness when wasting unused medication.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":684,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is preparing to teach a client who has anemia and a new prescription for liquid ferrous sulfate. Which of the following instructions should the nurse include in the teaching?","o":["Take the medication after dinner to avoid daytime drowsiness","The medication can be taken with milk to prevent gastrointestional irritation","Do not mix the medication with any other medications, foods, or liquids","Eat plenty of iron rich foods such as beans and green leafy vegetables"],"a":3,"r":"Iron-rich foods enhance treatment of anemia.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":685,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has instructed a female client on how to collect a midstream urine specimen. Which of the following client statements indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["Since I am right handed, I will use my left hand to spread the labia apart","I will use the provided towelette to cleanse the area using a back and forth motion","To collect the specimen I will urinate in the container and then finish urinating in the toilet","I will hold the specimen container against the skin while collecting the urine sample"],"a":0,"r":"The nondominant hand should spread the labia while the dominant hand collects the specimen.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":686,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client who has lung cancer and is receiving external radiation therapy. Which of the following statements by the client indicates a need for further teaching?","o":["I will use a washcloth with an antibacterial soap and water to cleanse the radiation area every day","I will need to prevent direct skin exposure to the sun during treatment and for at least 1 year afterward","I should avoid applying any moisturizers, creams, lotions, or powders to the radiation area unless prescribed by my doctor","It will be safe for me to be around my daughter, who is pregnant, and my grandchildren while I am receiving treatments."],"a":0,"r":"Radiation sites should be washed gently with mild soap; antibacterial soaps may irritate skin.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":687,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"A nurse preceptor is working with a newly hired nurse who is preparing to perform a sterile dressing change. Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse requires interventions by the nurse preceptor?","o":["Opening the sterile tray by first unfolding the flap closest to the nurse’s body","Putting on clean gloves prior to opening the outer package","Placing a sterile dressing at least 2 inches from the border on the sterile field","Holding a sterile item at just above the waist level"],"a":0,"r":"The flap closest to the nurse should be opened last to avoid contamination.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":688,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for an antepartum client whose laboratory test results reveal a negative rubella titer. The client asks the nurse what this laboratory test result means. Which of the following statement is correct for the nurse to make to the client?","o":["You are not experiencing an infection caused by rubella at this time","You will require the rubella immunization following the delivery of your baby","You will need to receive the rubella immunization at your next prenatal visit","You are immune to the rubella virus and cannot pass rubella on to your baby"],"a":1,"r":"A negative rubella titer indicates nonimmunity; vaccination is given postpartum.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":689,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse manager preparing an inservice regarding legal issues and client care. Which of the following is a correct example for the nurse manager to give guring the in service?","o":["Battery is having security keep a client in their room when they want to leave against medical advice (AMA).","Using a recliners attached lap tray to keep the client in the chair is considered assault","Malpractice is applying restraints without an order from the primary health care provider (PHCP)","Defamation is posing inaccurate information regarding a coworker on social media"],"a":3,"r":"Posting false information about a coworker is defamation (libel).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":690,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who weighs 150lbs (68kg) and has developed hepatic encephalopathy. It is priority for the nurse to notify the primary health care provider if the client develops","o":["A decrease in urine output to 450ml/day","A change in pupil size from 7 to 5mm","The presence of hand flapping","The development of musty odor to the breath"],"a":0,"r":"Urine output less than 30 mL/hr (720 mL/day) indicates worsening hepatic dysfunction and requires provider notification.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":691,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has received report for the following clients. The nurse should firs t asses the client who","o":["Had a stroke 24hrs ago and has become restless and irritable","Has meningitis and reports a headache and photosensitivity","Has an abdominal aneurysm and has mild lower back pain","Has epilepsy and remains drowsy 30 minutes after a seizure"],"a":0,"r":"Restlessness and irritability after stroke may indicate increased ICP and require immediate assessment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":692,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) is precepting a newly hired nurse. It requires intervention by the preceptor if the newly hired nurse","o":["Gently massages the fingers of a client with frostbite","Refuses to give prescribed lorazepam to a client who is anxious and needs to sign a consent form","Removes the pants of a client with chemical burns to the lower extremities by cutting them off","Places a client who has a temperature of 104.5 degrees F on a cooling blanket"],"a":0,"r":"Frostbitten tissue should not be massaged.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":693,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is teaching a parent of the newborn about infant nutrition. Which of the following statements, if made by the parent, requires follow up by the nurse","o":["“I will give my 2-month-old infant a supplement of plain water if my infant develops loose stools”","“The fatty acids contained in breast milk are important for neurological development and visibility function”","“Iron from human milk is better absorbed than iron from fortified formula and infant cereals”","“I can begin introduce rice cereal to my baby at 6 months of age”"],"a":0,"r":"Infants younger than 6 months should not receive water supplementation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":694,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is admitting a client following a sexual assault. The client has cuts and bruises on the face, hands, and chest. What should the nurse initially say to the client.","o":["“I am sorry this happened”","“You are safe now”","“I will take some pictures of your injuries when you are ready”","“Do you have a family member or a friend I can call for you”"],"a":1,"r":"Establishing safety is the priority after sexual assault.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":695,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in a local health department is preparing to administer an influenza vaccine via IM injection. The nurse should question the administration of the vaccine to an individual who is","o":["27yr old female who is eight weeks pregnant and has a toddler at home","35yr old female who lives with a family member who has HIV","42yr old female who is allergic to eggs and has had a fever for 3 days","79yr old male who has a history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus (type 2)"],"a":2,"r":"Fever and severe egg allergy may contraindicate influenza vaccination depending on vaccine type and severity.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":696,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has a prescription to administer subcutaneous enoxaparin to an assigned client. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Aspirate the blood prior to injecting the medication","Check the most current prothrombin time (PT)","Retain the air bubble while injecting the medication","Insert the needle in the skin at a 15-degree angle"],"a":2,"r":"The air bubble should be retained when administering enoxaparin to ensure full medication delivery.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":697,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has sustained severe burn injuries to the anterior portion of the right and left arms, and the entire front torso. Based on the rules of nines, the nurse calculated the estimated percentage of burned total body surface area (TBSA) as","o":["27%","36%","45%","54%"],"a":1,"r":"Anterior right arm (4.5%) + anterior left arm (4.5%) + anterior trunk (18%) = 27%? Actually both entire anterior arms count 9% total plus anterior torso 18% = 36%.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":698,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a pediatric client who is postoperative following a VP shunt placement. The nurse should position the client","o":["In Trendelenburg","Prone","On the nonproductive side","In high-Fowler"],"a":2,"r":"Following VP shunt placement, positioning on the nonoperative side helps prevent pressure on the shunt.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":699,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer prescribed medications to the following clients. The nurse should questions the prescription for the client who is receiving the","o":["Angiotensin-receptor blocker, losartan, and has a blood pressure of 168/94 mm Hg","Calcium-channel blocker, diltiazem, and has 1+ nonpitting edema","Alpha blocker, terazosin, and is reporting a headache","Thiazide diuretic, hydrochlorothiazide, and is reporting leg cramps"],"a":3,"r":"Leg cramps may indicate hypokalemia caused by hydrochlorothiazide and warrant evaluation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":700,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is 4 hours postpartum following a vaginal delivery. The client has saturated a perineal pad in a timeframe of 10 minutes, which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Measure the blood pressure","Assess for a distended bladder","Massage the fundus","Administer oxytocin"],"a":2,"r":"Excessive bleeding postpartum suggests uterine atony; fundal massage is the first action.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":701,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in the labor and delivery unit is caring in labor whose membranes just spontaneously ruptured. Which of the following findings would be a priority for the nurse to report to the primary health care provider?","o":["Contractions lasting 60 seconds","Four contractions in a 10 minute period","A maternal temperature of 99.5 degree F","Appearance of bloody fluid from the vagina"],"a":3,"r":"Bloody vaginal fluid after membrane rupture may indicate placental abruption or vasa previa.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":702,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is teaching a client who has multiple sclerosis (MS) about a new prescription for baclofen. Which of the following instructions should the nurse include in the teaching?","o":["“Do not take diphenhydramine with this medication”","“Be sure and take this medication on an empty stomach”","“Stop taking the medication if you develop headaches”","“You can expect some diarrhea during the first week of taking this medication”"],"a":0,"r":"Baclofen causes CNS depression; diphenhydramine can worsen sedation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":703,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care who is receiving continuous tube feedings via a g-tube. Which of the following actions by the newly newly hired nurse requires immediate intervention by the nurse preceptor?","o":["Discarding an old irrigation set that is 24 hours old.","Maintaining the head of bed at 40-45 degree angle","Adding 2 drops of food coloring to the feeding tube","Checking the g-tube residual every 4 hours."],"a":2,"r":"Food coloring should never be added to enteral feedings because aspiration may go undetected.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":704,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department is triaging clients. Which of the following should the nurse classify as non urgent?","o":["A client who has a fractured femoral artery","A client who has multiple fractures to both arms","A client who has a red rash over the abdomen","A client who reports left flank pain"],"a":2,"r":"A localized abdominal rash is considered nonurgent.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":705,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for a client who is receiving a transfusion of packed red blood cells. The client develops chills, flushing of the skin, and reports back pain. The newly hired nurse stops the transfusion and implements the following actions. Which action requires immediate intervention by by the nurse preceptor.","o":["Initiating a 0.9% sodium chloride infusion through new intravenous tubing","Collecting a urine and blood specimen to send to the laboratory","Disposing of the blood transfusion bag and tubing in the biohazard container","Checking that the information on the blood transfusion bag matches the clients information"],"a":2,"r":"The blood bag and tubing must be saved for investigation, not discarded.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":706,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has a new prescription for knee-high embolism stockings. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Fold the top of the stockings over if they are too long","Remove the stockings once each day for 1 hour","Make sure the stockings are tight around the client’s toes","Measure the circumference and length of the client’s calf"],"a":3,"r":"Proper fitting requires calf measurements.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":707,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is assessing assigned clients for hospice services. The nurse should recommend hospice care for a client who","o":["Has diabetes mellitus and has developed CKD","Has terminal cancer and needs assistance with pain management at home","Has dysphasia due to a stroke and needs administration of gastric g tube feedings","Has dementia and needs assistance with activities of ADLS"],"a":1,"r":"Hospice is appropriate for terminal illness with comfort-focused care needs.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":708,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The registered nurse is planning care for clients and is assigning tasks to a licensed practical nurse(LPN) in an unlicensed assisted personnel (UAP). Which of the following tasks is best for the RN to assign to the LPN?","o":["Collection of past medical history for a client who has admitted with cellulitis","Recording the output for a client who has just urinated in the collection container of the toilet","Assisting with the delivery of breakfast trays to the client","Development of a plan of care for a client who is postoperative from a cholecystitis."],"a":0,"r":"LPNs may collect data such as health histories; UAPs cannot perform assessments.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":709,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is preparing to administer an intramuscular injection to an adult client. Which of the following actions is appropriate for the nurse to take?","o":["Limit the amount of medication to 4 mL when injecting into the deltoid muscle","Hold the skin taut when using the Z-track method","Use the deltoid site if the client is an adult who requires 3 mL of medication","Draw up an air bubble into the syringe after the medication has been prepared"],"a":3,"r":"An air-lock technique may be used with IM injections to prevent medication tracking.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":710,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse has provided dietary teaching to a client who has a history of atherosclerosis and a recent laboratory test, indicating an increased cholesterol level. Which of the following statement by the client indicates a need for follow up.","o":["I flavor my fish with lemon juice","I cook my food with canola oil","I take an omega-3 supplement daily","I eat two poached eggs for breakfast every morning"],"a":3,"r":"Two eggs daily contribute significant dietary cholesterol and require follow-up.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":711,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is teaching a car seat safety to the parents of a month old infant. Which of the following statements by the parent indicates the correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["We will place the car seat rear facing in the middle of the back seat","We will be sure that a hand can fit between the infant and the car seat harness","The car seat we have is a booster seat that is anchored in with the car lap belt","The car seat shoulder harness straps should be below the level of the infant’s armpits."],"a":0,"r":"Infants should ride rear-facing in the center of the back seat whenever possible.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":712,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse is providing discharge teaching to a young adult female client who has been newly prescribed carbamazepine for seizures. Which of the following client statements indicates an understanding of the teaching?","o":["I will weigh myself every week to check for weight gain","If I want children, I should not delay starting a family","I will use condoms as a method for birth control","A low sodium diet will need to be followed while on this medication"],"a":1,"r":"Carbamazepine is teratogenic; clients desiring pregnancy should discuss family planning early.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":713,"t":17,"s":38,"q":"The nurse working in an ambulatory healthcare center has assessed the following clients who are waiting to see the PCP. The nurse should immediately remove removed from the waiting room and place in an examining room the client who has.","o":["Reports of burning and arthritis with a linear rash of vesicles, extending around the right lower rib cage","A 1 mm area that is flat and red at the site of a Mantoux test which was administered 48 hours ago","Congestive heart failure and reports gaining 3 pounds in the last three days","A fever of 99.6°F and is reporting generalized pain and malaise and a history of borderline personality disorder."],"a":0,"r":"A painful vesicular rash in a dermatomal pattern suggests shingles; the client should be isolated from susceptible individuals immediately\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":714,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is assisting with discharge planning for assigned clients. Which of the following referrals is appropriate for the nurse to make?","o":["A social worker referral for a client who has diabetes mellitus (type 2) and has neuropathy.","A physical therapy (PT) referral for a client who had a total knee replacement (TKR) and needs instruction on the use of a walker.","An occupational therapy (OT) referral for a client who had an amputation and requires long-term IV antibiotics.","A speech therapy referral for a client who is scheduled to undergo an outpatient esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD)."],"a":1,"r":"Physical therapy assists clients with ambulation devices like a walker after a total knee replacement (TKR).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":715,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse has attended a continuing education program on legal issues in nursing practice. Which of the following statements by the nurse indicates a correct understanding of the conference?","o":["“Failure to communicate concerns about an older adult client who is confused and is being discharged home is an example of neglect.”","“Giving clients false assurance about a health care condition or expected outcome is an example of libel.”","“Threatening to touch a client without the client’s consent is an example of battery.”","“Posting information about a client on social media without the client’s permission is an example of slander.”"],"a":0,"r":"Neglect includes failure to communicate necessary client concerns, especially safety issues.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":716,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working in a medical-surgical unit is caring for assigned clients. Which of the following tasks is appropriate to delegate?","o":["Asking an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) to turn off the tube feeding for a client who is receiving continuous tube feedings and is vomiting.","Asking a licensed practical nurse (LPN/VN) to obtain a capillary blood glucose reading on a client who has become diaphoretic.","Asking an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) to flush the closed-wound drainage system (JP) drain of a client who is 2 days postoperative from an open appendectomy.","Asking a licensed practical nurse (LPN/VN) to teach a client who has a newly created ileostomy how to change the appliance."],"a":1,"r":"LPN/VNs can obtain capillary blood glucose readings. Teaching and flushing drains require an RN.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":717,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working on the pediatric unit is caring for the following clients. It is most appropriate for the nurse to initiate an interdisciplinary conference for a client who is:","o":["7 months old, has intussusception, is vomiting, has colicky abdominal pain, and is having jelly-like stools.","6 months old, has respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), is wheezing, and has copious nasal discharge.","2 years old, has impaired fine and gross motor skills, and was just diagnosed with cerebral palsy.","2 months old, has developmental dysplasia of the hip, unequal leg lengths, internal abduction of the left hip, and asymmetry of the gluteal folds."],"a":2,"r":"Cerebral palsy requires multidisciplinary planning early for coordination of therapies.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":718,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working on the medical-surgical unit is caring for assigned clients. The nurse should initially assess the client who:","o":["Has pericarditis with a temperature (T) of 101.8 F and chest pain that is relieved when the client leans forward.","Has atrial fibrillation (AFib) with a pulse (P) that varies from 110 to 140 and is refusing to take the scheduled dose of warfarin.","Has status asthmaticus, had wheezing on inspiration and expiration upon admission 30 minutes ago, received a nebulizer treatment, and now has vesicular breath sounds.","Had thoracic surgery 12 hours ago with a chest tube to water seal drainage and has drained 175 mL of serous sanguineous fluid in the past hour."],"a":3,"r":"Chest tube drainage of 175 mL in 1 hour is excessive and could indicate hemorrhage.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":719,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who weighs 150 lbs (68 kg) and has developed hepatic encephalopathy. It is a priority for the nurse to notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if the client develops:","o":["The development of flaccidity to the lower extremities.","An increase in urine output to 1,450 mL/day.","A change in pupil size from 7 to 5 mm.","An increase in alanine aminotransferase (ALT) levels."],"a":0,"r":"Flaccidity indicates worsening encephalopathy and possible coma. Immediate action needed.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":720,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) has received the following prescriptions for newly admitted clients. Which prescription should the nurse implement first?","o":["Initiate an IV of 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) for a client who was admitted with a urinary tract infection (UTI) and has a blood pressure (BP) of 122/68 mm Hg.","Administer a second unit of packed red blood cells (PRBCs) to a client who has a hemoglobin (Hgb) of 8 mg/dL.","Administer labetalol IV to a client who has a blood pressure (BP) of 192/98 mm Hg and a pulse (P) of 138.","Administer lactulose to a client who has portal hypertension and is reporting nausea."],"a":2,"r":"Hypertensive emergency (BP 192/98 with tachycardia) requires priority intervention with labetalol.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":721,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a teenage client who has chronic streptococcal infections of the throat and is now reporting swelling around the eyes. Which of the following is the priority action for the nurse?","o":["Test the urine for glucose and ketones.","Administer an antihistamine to decrease swelling.","Inspect for generalized edema.","Check the blood pressure for hypotension."],"a":3,"r":"Periorbital swelling could indicate acute glomerulonephritis; monitor blood pressure first. (Source wording says “hypotension”; glomerulonephritis normally causes high blood pressure.)\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":722,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing care provided by a newly hired nurse. The nurse preceptor identifies that follow-up is needed if the newly hired nurse:","o":["Preps an IV insertion site with alcohol using a back-and-forth pattern.","Primes IV tubing with 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) before starting a blood transfusion.","Uses clean gloves when collecting a 24-hour urine specimen.","Administers an influenza vaccine using the Z-track method."],"a":3,"r":"Influenza vaccine should be IM, not using the Z-track method. Z-track is for irritating IM injections.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":723,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse manager has provided a continuing education session to nursing staff regarding client rights and responsibilities. Which of the following statements by a participant indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“Clients who have a mental health diagnosis waive the right to confidentiality once hospitalized.”","“Clients are not allowed to review their medical record during hospitalization.”","“Clients will be provided a list of home health agencies to contact for any ordered follow-up care.”","“Clients have a right to know about alternatives to treatment and hospitalization.”"],"a":3,"r":"Clients have the right to know alternatives to treatment and hospitalization.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":724,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a child who has impetigo and has provided home instructions to the parents. Which of the following should the nurse include in the instructions?","o":["Towels should be washed separately from other family members.","Seal non-washable items in an airtight plastic bag for 14 days.","Soak combs and hairbrushes in boiling water for 10 minutes.","Spray household surfaces with an over-the-counter (OTC) insecticide."],"a":0,"r":"Separate towels prevent the spread of impetigo, which is highly contagious.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":725,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for the following assigned clients who have restraints in place. Which client situation requires immediate follow-up by the nurse preceptor?","o":["The client whose wrist restraints are removed every 4 hours.","The client whose safety vest is tied in a half bow to the bedframe.","The client who received alprazolam and has all 4 side rails up.","The client whose roll belt is loose enough to allow for movement in the bed."],"a":2,"r":"Alprazolam (sedative) with 4 side rails up is a restraint without an order and needs immediate intervention.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":726,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client and administers the wrong drug. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Determine if the client is experiencing any adverse effects.","Notify the client’s primary health care provider (PHCP).","Complete an incident report.","Inform the client to report the development of any unusual symptoms."],"a":0,"r":"First, assess for harm to the client after a medication error.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":727,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is assessing clients at the scene of a mass casualty disaster and prioritizing care based on the disaster triage tag system. Which of the following clients should be transported to the healthcare facility first?","o":["The client who has a closed fracture to the leg and a 2+ pedal pulse.","The client who is confused and has an open wound on the right lower leg.","The client who has burns to the anterior chest and neck.","The client who has a head injury with sluggish pupil response and is apneic."],"a":2,"r":"Burns to chest and neck compromise the airway; priority in disaster triage.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":728,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is discussing the plan of care for a client who has disseminated herpes zoster. It is appropriate for the nurse to state:","o":["“I will use both airborne and contact precautions when caring for the client.”","“I do not need to wear a mask when caring for this client because I have had the varicella vaccine.”","“I will inform the client’s visitors that they can only be in the room for 30 minutes per visit.”","“I will remind the client’s family not to bring in food from home.”"],"a":0,"r":"Disseminated herpes zoster requires both airborne and contact precautions.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":729,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse has been made aware of the following situations. The nurse should complete an incident report for which situation?","o":["A nurse has a client who was admitted with jewelry and wants to keep the jewelry with them during their stay.","A licensed practical/vocational nurse (LPN/VN) has a client who refuses to participate in their physical therapy (PT) session.","An unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) identifies that the blood pressure machine in a client’s room does not work correctly.","A nurse realizes they missed giving a medication 2 hours ago and proceeds with administering the medication."],"a":3,"r":"Medication errors (missed/delayed medication) require an incident report.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":730,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working on a surgical unit is assessing clients for the risk of developing heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following clients should the nurse identify as being at greatest risk?","o":["65-year-old male who is taking enoxaparin for the treatment of a deep vein thrombosis (DVT).","45-year-old female who has had an anaphylactic reaction following a transfusion of packed red blood cells (PRBCs).","A client who has been receiving heparin 5,000 units twice per day for the past 10 days.","20-year-old African male who has received cryoprecipitate for the treatment of hemophilia."],"a":2,"r":"Long-term subcutaneous heparin therapy increases risk of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":731,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for an antepartum client whose laboratory test results reveal a positive rubella titer. The client asks the nurse what this test result means. Which of the following statements is correct for the nurse to make to the client?","o":["“You are not experiencing an infection caused by rubella at this time.”","“You will need to receive the rubella immunization at your next prenatal visit.”","“You will require the rubella immunization following the delivery of your baby.”","“You are immune to the rubella virus and can fight off exposure to the virus.”"],"a":3,"r":"A positive rubella titer means immunity; no immediate action needed.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":732,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is performing a pre-college physical assessment on a client who is 18 years old. Which of the following immunizations should the nurse be prepared to administer to the client?","o":["Varicella vaccine.","Pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine.","Meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine.","Hepatitis B vaccine."],"a":2,"r":"Meningococcal vaccine is critical for college-aged students living in close quarters.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":733,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse has provided discharge teaching to a client who has a fiberglass cast to the lower extremity. Which of the following indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I can expect to have numbness of the toes in my left foot for the first 24 hours.”","“I should report any warm areas of the cast and odor to my doctor.”","“I should avoid applying ice to my left lower leg.”","“I can resume normal activities immediately as long as I use my crutches.”"],"a":1,"r":"Warm areas and odor indicate infection under the cast; requires reporting.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":734,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is developing a plan of care for a client who has bipolar disorder and is experiencing severe depression. Which of the following is a priority nursing intervention?","o":["Improve the client’s interactions.","Restore the client’s nutritional status.","Promote sufficient stimulation for the client.","Set firm limits and boundaries for the client."],"a":1,"r":"Nutrition is vital during severe depression to prevent physical decline.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":735,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who was recently diagnosed with terminal cancer. Which of the following client statements indicates the client is in the denial stage of grieving?","o":["“I am looking to see if there are any cancer treatment trials that I can enter.”","“I have been such a good person, and this is what God does to me.”","“I just want to be alive long enough to see my son graduate from college.”","“I want to get another opinion from the cancer institute.”"],"a":3,"r":"Denial often includes seeking a second opinion despite diagnosis.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":736,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a newly admitted client who has obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Provide a structured activity schedule for the client to help prevent compulsive behavior.","Instruct the client on the use of relaxation techniques to help decrease anxiety.","Identify precipitating factors for ritualistic behaviors.","Discuss alternative coping strategies with the client."],"a":2,"r":"Identifying triggers for rituals comes before intervention strategies.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":737,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working on a crisis hotline is speaking with a client who states, “I just took an entire bottle of amitriptyline.” Which of the following responses should the nurse initially make to the client?","o":["“It must have been difficult to call and report an attempted overdose.”","“Were you trying to kill yourself by taking an overdose?”","“What was happening at the time you decided to take the pills?”","“I’m glad you called, and I want to send an ambulance to help you.”"],"a":3,"r":"Immediate safety is the priority; send an ambulance right away.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":738,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is preparing to transfer a client from the bed to the chair. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Assist the client to a seated position.","Position the bed at an appropriate height.","Determine if the client can bear weight.","Place a transfer belt on the client."],"a":2,"r":"Weight-bearing status must be determined first before transferring a client.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":739,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working on a medical-surgical unit is caring for assigned clients. When considering dietary requirements, the nurse should recognize that a client who has:","o":["Dumping syndrome should consume a low-protein diet.","Crohn’s disease should consume a high-residue diet.","Peptic ulcer disease (PUD) should drink fluids 1 hour after eating.","Diverticulosis should consume a high-fiber diet."],"a":3,"r":"Clients with diverticulosis need a high-fiber diet to prevent complications.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":740,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving chemotherapy. The client is not eating well but denies any nausea or vomiting. Which of the following meals is best for the nurse to suggest to the client?","o":["Baked pork chop and potato with cheesecake.","Dry toast, yogurt, and gelatin.","Oat bran cereal with milk and strawberries.","Broiled chicken, green beans, and cottage cheese."],"a":3,"r":"Broiled chicken, green beans, and cottage cheese are high protein and easy to digest.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":741,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse has instructed a client who recently received hearing aids. Which of the following client statements indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?","o":["“I will turn the hearing aid off before removing it from my ear.”","“I will buy batteries only as I need them to prevent them from expiring.”","“I will use a toothpick to clean debris from the opening of the hearing aids.”","“I will keep the volume at the highest setting possible to avoid any feedback sounds.”"],"a":0,"r":"Turn off hearing aids before removing to protect the device.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":742,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working on a telemetry unit is caring for a client who has unstable angina and is reporting chest pain with a severity of 6 on a 0 (no pain) to 10 (severe pain) scale. The nurse administers a sublingual nitroglycerin tablet. After 5 minutes, the client states the chest pain is now a severity of 3. Which of the following actions should the nurse be prepared to take?","o":["Notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) that the client’s pain has decreased.","Apply oxygen at 2 L per nasal cannula.","Administer another sublingual nitroglycerin tablet.","Obtain an electrocardiogram (ECG) stat."],"a":2,"r":"If chest pain persists after 5 minutes, administer another nitroglycerin dose.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":743,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working in an assisted living facility is caring for the following assigned clients who have mental health disorders. The nurse should immediately report to the primary health care provider (PHCP) the client who is taking:","o":["Venlafaxine and experiencing daytime drowsiness.","Aripiprazole and experiencing dizziness when standing up.","Clozapine and having flu-like symptoms.","Fluphenazine and reporting nausea and vomiting."],"a":2,"r":"Flu-like symptoms with clozapine may indicate agranulocytosis, which is life-threatening.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":744,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly licensed nurse prepare to insert a peripheral IV on an assigned client. Which of the following actions by the newly licensed nurse requires the nurse preceptor to intervene?","o":["Stabilizing the vein below the area where the IV catheter will be inserted.","Applying pressure above the insertion site while retracting the stylet.","Removing the tourniquet once blood is observed in the flashback chamber.","Preparing the left forearm of a client who had a left mastectomy 5 years ago."],"a":3,"r":"Never use an arm with a history of mastectomy for IV insertion.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":745,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving IV magnesium sulfate. Which of the following client findings indicates the client is experiencing a side effect of the medication?","o":["A change in blood pressure (BP) from 128/76 to 108/66 mm Hg.","A urinary output of 125 mL over the past 4 hours.","A respiratory rate (RR) of 12.","An elevated calcium level."],"a":2,"r":"A respiratory rate of 12 is low; magnesium toxicity causes respiratory depression.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":746,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse has received a hand-off report on a client who is receiving nitroglycerin 50 mg/250 mL 5% dextrose in water at a rate of 20 mcg/min. The nurse observes that the client’s IV pump is set at a rate of 3 mL/hr. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Increase the infusion rate to 6 mL/hr.","Decrease the infusion rate to 2 mL/hr.","Request the pharmacy to send nitroglycerin 25 mg/250 mL 5% dextrose in water.","Continue the infusion at the rate of 3 mL/hr."],"a":0,"r":"The rate should be 6 mL/hr (50 mg/250 mL is 200 mcg/mL, so 20 mcg/min is 1,200 mcg/hr, or 6 mL/hr).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":747,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving total parenteral nutrition (TPN). Which of the following requires follow-up by the nurse?","o":["The client is receiving 10% dextrose in water (D10W) until a new TPN bag is delivered to the unit.","The client has experienced a weight gain of 1 lb in 48 hours.","The client’s TPN has been infusing continuously for 8 hours.","The client’s temperature (T) is greater than 100.8 F orally."],"a":3,"r":"Fever (100.8°F) in a client on TPN can signal sepsis and requires urgent evaluation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":748,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving desmopressin acetate. Which of the following findings indicates a therapeutic effect?","o":["An increase in blood pressure (BP) from 88/62 to 120/72 mm Hg.","A decrease in urine output from 5,000 to 2,000 mL in 24 hours.","An increase in serum glucose from 50 to 110 mg/dL.","A decrease in apical pulse (P) rate from 120 to 86."],"a":1,"r":"Desmopressin decreases urine output in conditions like diabetes insipidus.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":749,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has an infection and has been prescribed gentamicin intermittent IV bolus every 8 hours. A peak and trough level is required with the next dose. Complete the following sentence using the options below. The nurse should draw the peak ____ (Option 1) after completing the infusion and the trough ____ (Option 2) prior to the infusion. Option 1 / Option 2 choices: 1. Immediately. 2. 15 minutes. 3. 30 minutes. 4. 1 hour.","o":["3; 1.","4; 2.","2; 3.","1; 4."],"a":0,"r":"Peak levels should be drawn 30 minutes after, and trough levels immediately before the infusion.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":750,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse insert an indwelling catheter into a female. Which of the following actions performed by the newly hired nurse requires follow-up by the preceptor?","o":["Inflating the catheter balloon as soon as a return of urine is noted in the tubing.","Selecting a 16F catheter with a 10-mL balloon.","Cleaning the meatus with an antiseptic swab after cleaning both labia folds.","Immediately obtaining a urine specimen from the drainage bag."],"a":0,"r":"Balloon inflation should only occur after confirming the catheter is placed in the bladder.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":751,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for a client who had a bronchoscopy 1 hour ago. Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse requires follow-up?","o":["Maintaining the client in a semi-Fowler’s position.","Encouraging the client to cough and deep breathe every 30 minutes.","Assessing the face and neck for development of crepitus.","Monitoring the client for any hemoptysis."],"a":1,"r":"Coughing immediately after bronchoscopy risks aspiration; the nurse must assess gag reflex first.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":752,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who had abdominal surgery with placement of a nasogastric (NG) tube 3 days ago. The client has a pulse (P) of 110 and is reporting muscle cramps with tingling in the legs. Which of the following arterial blood gas (ABG) results are consistent with this client’s current assessment?","o":["pH = 7.36; PaO2 = 63 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 42 mm Hg; HCO3 = 26 mEq/L; SaO2 = 84%.","pH = 7.45; PaO2 = 70 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 38 mm Hg; HCO3 = 28 mEq/L; SaO2 = 86%.","pH = 7.50; PaO2 = 79 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 35 mm Hg; HCO3 = 30 mEq/L; SaO2 = 88%.","pH = 7.32; PaO2 = 72 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 50 mm Hg; HCO3 = 22 mEq/L; SaO2 = 85%."],"a":2,"r":"Alkalosis (high pH 7.50) from NG suction causes muscle cramps and tingling; matches ABGs.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":753,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse working in the post anesthesia care unit (PACU) of an ambulatory surgery center has just received a postoperative hand-off report on a client who had endoscopic carpal tunnel release (ECTR) surgery. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?","o":["Determine if the client needs pain medication.","Check the surgical dressing.","Check the client’s vital signs (VS).","Auscultate the client’s breath sounds."],"a":2,"r":"Vital signs must be checked first postoperatively to assess for immediate complications.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":754,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has been taking a large amount of antacids. Which of the following arterial blood gas (ABG) results should the nurse expect the client to have?","o":["pH = 7.24; PaO2 = 80 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 50 mm Hg; HCO3 = 24 mEq/L; SaO2 = 89%.","pH = 7.47; PaO2 = 85 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 38 mm Hg; HCO3 = 32 mEq/L; SaO2 = 92%.","pH = 7.36; PaO2 = 93 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 42 mm Hg; HCO3 = 26 mEq/L; SaO2 = 86%.","pH = 7.25; PaO2 = 90 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 35 mm Hg; HCO3 = 14 mEq/L; SaO2 = 95%."],"a":1,"r":"Excessive antacids cause metabolic alkalosis: high pH and high bicarbonate (HCO3).\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":755,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who has hypovolemic shock and has a pulse (P) of 116. The nurse has initiated IV 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline). Which of the following actions should the nurse perform next?","o":["Obtain a prescription for a vasodilator.","Document and continue to monitor the client.","Insert an indwelling urethral catheter.","Initiate oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula."],"a":3,"r":"Oxygenation is the immediate intervention for hypovolemic shock.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":756,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who is in labor and has developed the following manifestations: pallor, dizziness, breathlessness, P 115, BP 94/58 mm Hg, and fetal distress. Which of the following actions should the nurse take immediately?","o":["Prepare the client for surgery.","Administer oxygen at 2 L/minute via nasal cannula.","Initiate an IV infusion of hypertonic fluids.","Turn the client to the left side."],"a":3,"r":"Left side positioning improves uteroplacental blood flow and relieves fetal distress.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":757,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is assessing an infant who has hyperbilirubinemia and is receiving phototherapy. Which of the following findings indicates an adverse response to the therapy?","o":["Lanugo to the back and shoulders.","Urine output that has a dark-gold or brown appearance.","An increased amount of loose, green stools.","Fontanels that are sunken upon palpation."],"a":3,"r":"Sunken fontanels suggest dehydration, a serious adverse effect of phototherapy.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":758,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is planning care for a client who is on bedrest. The client has end-stage cirrhosis of the liver with hepatorenal failure and abdominal ascites. Which of the following interventions should the nurse be prepared to implement?","o":["Provide the client a low-sodium diet.","Administer vitamin K.","Encourage the client to cough and deep breathe every hour.","Administer acetaminophen for pain."],"a":0,"r":"Low-sodium diet is critical for clients with ascites to reduce fluid retention. Sodium restriction helps manage fluid accumulation.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":759,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is talking with a client who has multiple sclerosis (MS). Which of the following statements by the client indicates correct understanding of the condition?","o":["“I participate in high-impact aerobics at least 5 days a week.”","“I take a hot bath every evening as a way to relax.”","“I try and drink at least 2 liters of fluid every day.”","“I eat plenty of low-fiber, low-potassium foods every day.”"],"a":2,"r":"Increasing fluid intake is important for clients with MS to prevent urinary tract infections and maintain hydration. High-impact exercise and hot baths can worsen fatigue and symptoms.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":760,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is performing an initial assessment on a client who had a colostomy created 6 hours ago. The colostomy stoma is dark, red, and flaccid. The only drainage in the ostomy bag is a small amount of mucus. Which of the following actions is a priority for the nurse to take?","o":["Notify the surgeon of the findings.","Determine if the client has been passing flatus.","Assess the client’s bowel sounds.","Ensure proper placement of the pouch."],"a":0,"r":"A dark, flaccid stoma suggests compromised blood supply (ischemia) and is a surgical emergency. Healthy stomas should be pink or beefy red and moist.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":761,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who recently sustained a head injury. The client has the following data: Potassium 3.5 mEq/L; Sodium 110 mEq/L; Serum osmolality decreased; Urine output 20 mL/hr, tea-colored; Urine specific gravity increased; Lethargy, disorientation, headache; Loss of appetite, nausea; Exertional dyspnea; Deep tendon reflexes decreased; Apical P 110, bounding; T 98 F. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?","o":["Administer IV vasopressin.","Raise the head of bed (HOB) to high-Fowler’s.","Start an infusion of 0.45% sodium chloride (half-strength saline).","Provide a calm and quiet environment."],"a":3,"r":"The client shows signs of SIADH (low sodium, concentrated urine, decreased serum osmolality, lethargy). The priority is to reduce stimulation by providing a calm and quiet environment.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1},{"id":762,"t":17,"s":39,"q":"The nurse is caring for a client who had lumbar spinal surgery 8 hours ago. The client reports development of a headache and the nurse notes clear drainage on the surgical dressing. The nurse should immediately:","o":["Lower the client’s head of bed (HOB).","Empty the Hemovac drain.","Remove the sequential compression devices.","Reposition the client using the log rolling method."],"a":0,"r":"Clear drainage from the surgical site after spinal surgery suggests a possible cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak. Lowering the head of the bed reduces intracranial pressure and decreases further leakage while waiting for provider intervention.\n\nNot from Galen materials: this question comes from an outside study set. Check it against your Galen slides and ATI.","x":1}]};